-- 2026-09-08, iteration 29: the scanner contract, measured rather than quoted.
finding(f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language, defect).
recorded(f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language, "FOUR CELLS SAT BLOCKED FOR THREE SESSIONS BEHIND A SENTENCE THAT WAS TRUE. `cell_blocked(..., scanner_contract)` said a template literal`s text lives in `TemplateElement.value`, a nested object, and scanners/js_ast.ts emits SCALAR own properties only - so the key of ``o[`k`]()`` is knowable at parse time and not derivable from any fact we have. Every clause of that is correct, and the note even ended `it moves when the scanner`s contract moves`. NOBODY HAD MEASURED HOW FAR THE CONTRACT WAS FROM MOVING. Measured 2026-09-08 by walking babel`s own trees: 223 files, 422 482 nodes, every own property that is neither a node nor a node array nor a scalar. With babel`s `extra` scratch set aside, the answer is ONE - `TemplateElement.value`, `{raw, cooked}`, both scalars. The scalars-only contract excluded exactly one property in the whole of JavaScript as babel presents it, and it was the one blocking the cells. THE FIX IS A GENERAL RULE AND NOT A CASE FOR TEMPLATES: a nested object whose members are ALL scalars is flattened, one `ast_attr` per member, `<field>_<member>`. A nested object holding anything non-scalar is still dropped and nothing in the measured corpus is one, so what remains of the hole is declared and empty rather than known and populated. AND `extra` IS DECLARED OUT rather than dropped by accident. It was already excluded - as an object it fell through the branch that emits nothing - which is the wrong reason to exclude the right thing, and the general rule would have swept it in: measured over the fixtures, without the declaration it emits `extra_*` on 235 nodes, carrying `parenStart` (a byte offset) and `trailingComma` (a comma) into a model of the language. NO RULE WAS NEEDED AT THE CALL GRAPH. `selects[flow]` already reads a computed key through `may_be_lit`, so one arm giving a non-interpolated template its cooked text closed all four cells, and the shape went `has_residue` -> `fully_resolved` with `shape_stale[audit]` naming the excuse that had outlived its cause. THE SHAPE OF THE MISTAKE is the one this repository keeps recording about its own gates, one level up: a blocker was attributed to the first component looked at, written down accurately, and then read as a constraint rather than as a setting. It survived because it was TRUE.").
addressed_by(f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language, "scanners/js_ast.ts").

finding(f_the_site_measured_the_receiver_and_not_the_key, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_site_measured_the_receiver_and_not_the_key, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_site_measured_the_receiver_and_not_the_key, test).
finding_note(f_the_site_measured_the_receiver_and_not_the_key, "THE CORPUS ALREADY HELD A TEMPLATE-KEY SITE AND IT COULD NOT HAVE MEASURED THE FIX. `` bag[`fixed`](n) `` in shapes.ts was written to give `s_computed_template_key` a site, and `bag` is an AMBIENT DECLARATION carrying no value - so the call cannot resolve however good the key is, and with the scanner contract extended the shape STILL read `has_residue`. The site was measuring the receiver. FOURTH INSTANCE OF A TRAP THAT FILE`S OWN HEADER NAMES: `a site measures the model only when everything except the construct under study already works`, written after `maybe!()`, the `(cast as T)` probe and `held!.pick()` each refused for the same reason. The header was there, the sentence was right, and the site was written anyway - which says the rule needs a CHECK rather than a paragraph, and the check is cheap: a site whose shape is under study must resolve when the construct works. Re-pointed at `holder`, a real object with a real method, and moved BELOW its receiver`s declaration - the first draft put a `const` use forty lines above the `const`, which is code that would throw if the file were ever run, in a file whose whole discipline is that its values are real. AND THE RUNNABLE HALF WENT TO alpha.mjs, because `` keyed[`pickTmpl`](n) `` CAN be spelt in a .mjs and shapes.ts exists for the shapes that cannot: a site whose residue is closed belongs where the oracle judges it.").
addressed_by(f_the_site_measured_the_receiver_and_not_the_key, "test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt").

finding(f_a_gitignore_pattern_with_a_slash_does_not_match_a_symlink, defect).
recorded(f_a_gitignore_pattern_with_a_slash_does_not_match_a_symlink, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_gitignore_pattern_with_a_slash_does_not_match_a_symlink, test).
finding_note(f_a_gitignore_pattern_with_a_slash_does_not_match_a_symlink, "A SYMLINK NAMED `node_modules` RODE INTO A MERGE COMMIT AND WAS PUSHED. Setting up a second worktree for the kernel merge, the dependencies were shared with `ln -s ../rofl/node_modules node_modules`; `.gitignore` has carried `node_modules/` since the repository began, and a trailing slash matches a DIRECTORY ONLY. A symlink is not a directory to git, so `git add -A` took it. The next fresh clone of this branch would have had its dependencies pointing at an absolute path on one laptop. THE PATTERN IS THE ONE THE REPOSITORY ALREADY RECORDS ABOUT ITS OWN GATES, arriving through a file nobody thinks of as a gate: the rule was written for the thing`s USUAL SHAPE rather than for its name, and it was permanently correct until the day the thing arrived in another shape. `node_modules/` had been right for a thousand days. FIXED BY DROPPING THE SLASH, and the fix ships with its control rather than an argument: plant the symlink again and `git check-ignore -v node_modules` names the pattern that catches it. AND THE INCIDENT IS ALSO ABOUT THE TOOL: `git add -A` in a worktree whose untracked files include a hand-made symlink is a wide net, and the merge that carried it was reviewed by reading the diff of tracked source, where a symlink shows as one unremarkable line.").
addressed_by(f_a_gitignore_pattern_with_a_slash_does_not_match_a_symlink, ".gitignore").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 30: the prototype of a value, and a note that asked
-- the wrong question.
finding(f_no_site_in_the_corpus_was_asking_may_be_lit, defect).
recorded(f_no_site_in_the_corpus_was_asking_may_be_lit, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_no_site_in_the_corpus_was_asking_may_be_lit, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_no_site_in_the_corpus_was_asking_may_be_lit, "`NO SITE IN THE CORPUS` IS A MEASUREMENT AND IT MEASURED SOMETHING ELSE. Item 49 - which prototype a value has - carried the note `13 member calls unresolved, zero with a literal receiver`, and the queue treated it as an item waiting for a corpus rather than for work. The number is correct. The question is not: it asked `may_be_lit`, does this model carry the receiver as a VALUE, and a prototype does not need a value. `[1, 2, 3].join(`,`)` needs nothing carried at all - the receiver is an `array_expression` and the KIND is the answer. RE-MEASURED BY NAMING EVERY SITE INSTEAD OF COUNTING THEM, which is the move this repository already prefers everywhere else: twelve unresolved member calls, and they are four different questions. One template with `.concat` and one array literal with `.join` are the String and Array prototypes - the sites the note said did not exist. SIX are `.next` on a generator object, and `may_be_node` is EMPTY for all six, so there is no kind to read a prototype from: that is the generator protocol`s value half and a different item. TWO are on a class that is IN this program, which is not a prototype question at all. A count of thirteen hid four questions; naming them separated the item from its neighbours. WHAT LANDED IS SMALL AND WHAT IT UNBLOCKS IS NOT. `prototype_of[flow]` reads a declared kind-to-prototype table, in two arms - from the kind, for a receiver written in place, and through `may_be_node`, for one reached through a binder - and `stdlib_member[audit]` names a member call whose receiver has a builtin prototype and whose method is therefore not a node in this program. It resolves nothing and is not meant to: it moves the residue from a comment into the in-tray of w_env_api_surface, the item that waits on this one BY NAME.").
addressed_by(f_no_site_in_the_corpus_was_asking_may_be_lit, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").

finding(f_a_guard_becomes_falsifiable_when_another_item_lands, insight).
recorded(f_a_guard_becomes_falsifiable_when_another_item_lands, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_guard_becomes_falsifiable_when_another_item_lands, test).
finding_note(f_a_guard_becomes_falsifiable_when_another_item_lands, "FIVE MUTANTS AT `stdlib_member[audit]`, TWO KILLED AND THREE SURVIVING, and the three do not survive for the same reason. Two are one statement twice - dropping the `builtin_prototype` guard, and adding `object` to that list - and both derive a byte-identical world because a member on an object literal is answered by `member_value`, resolves, and never reaches an audit that reads `unresolved_call`. Those have no site and could be given one. THE THIRD IS DIFFERENT AND IS THE ONE WORTH RECORDING: dropping `unresolved_call` itself would need a member call that RESOLVES and whose receiver has a BUILTIN prototype, and in a model with no standard library that cannot exist. The guard is unfalsifiable BY CONSTRUCTION, and it becomes falsifiable exactly when w_env_api_surface lands. THAT IS A THIRD CATEGORY BESIDE THE TWO THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY NAMES. A guard whose violation cannot RUN belongs in shapes.ts; a guard unkillable by the GRAMMAR (the sequence-field carry, the single quasi of a non-interpolated template) is documented and left. This one is unkillable by the MODEL`S OWN INCOMPLETENESS, which means the mutant is not a dead premise but a dated one - it has an owner and a date, and the audit is a forward marker for the item that will make it bite. Worth asking of every surviving guard: is this waiting on a corpus, on the grammar, or on another item.").
addressed_by(f_a_guard_becomes_falsifiable_when_another_item_lands, "test/js-controlflow-values.test.ts").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 31: the ledger`s own arity, and a number I reported.
finding(f_the_seven_divergent_notes_were_my_own_rule, defect).
recorded(f_the_seven_divergent_notes_were_my_own_rule, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_seven_divergent_notes_were_my_own_rule, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_seven_divergent_notes_were_my_own_rule, "I TOLD THE OWNER THERE WERE SEVEN NOTE TEXTS TO RECONCILE BY HAND AND THERE WERE NONE. Merging two branches` ledgers by NAME, the comparison loaded both as perspectives and derived the disagreements rather than eyeballing them - which was right, and it reported `note_split 7` beside `kind_split 0` and `demand_split 0`. The seven were carried into the merge report and into a commit message as the whole of the manual work. RE-MEASURED WITH A SECOND INSTRUMENT because two measurements disagreed: a clause-level walk of both files found 413 shared findings and ZERO differing note texts. The rule was the one that was wrong. `finding_note` is ONE-TO-MANY in this ledger and always has been: seven findings carry two rows and one carries three, and the second is always a correction - `DISMISSED THE SAME DAY, and the dismissal is the finding`, `CORRECTED WITHIN THE HOUR, by the owner`, `TRIED IN boot.rofl AND REVERTED`. So `finding_note[a](F, X), finding_note[b](F, Y), X != Y` pairs a finding`s own first note against its own correction, arriving through the other book, and the seven it named were exactly the seven that carry two. THE ARITY WAS NEVER DECLARED ANYWHERE, which is the defect rather than the convention: the convention is good and `runtime/report.ts` already joins every note, so no reader was ever misled - only a rule was. `corrected(F)` says it now, `corrected_unsettled[audit]` catches the hazard the convention creates (revisited and left open, empty on the honest tree, planted in the test), and the report`s joining is asserted rather than assumed. AND THE SHAPE IS THE ONE THIS LOOP KEEPS FINDING IN ITS OWN INSTRUMENTS: a rule that is right about the question it asks and wrong about the shape of what it reads, producing rows nobody can tell from real ones. Third this week, after a census comparing `broken` against `get broken` and a walk that excluded `extra` from its own measurement of what was excluded.").
addressed_by(f_the_seven_divergent_notes_were_my_own_rule, "rules/findings.rofl").

finding(f_a_keyword_over_prose_cannot_tell_a_finding_from_its_subject, insight).
recorded(f_a_keyword_over_prose_cannot_tell_a_finding_from_its_subject, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_keyword_over_prose_cannot_tell_a_finding_from_its_subject, doc).
finding_note(f_a_keyword_over_prose_cannot_tell_a_finding_from_its_subject, "A CHECK WAS PROPOSED AND MEASURED AND NOT BUILT, and the measurement is why. The idea: a finding whose PROSE claims a settlement the FACTS do not have - a note saying DISMISSED or CORRECTED or REVERTED while no `dismissed`/`addressed_by` row exists - is a finding the backlog offers as work somebody already did. Scanned for it: six candidates. EVERY ONE A FALSE POSITIVE, and reading them is what says so. `REVERTED` twice describes a PATCH that was reverted, not the finding. `RETRACTED` describes a CLAIM inside the finding. `CORRECTED` describes the owner correcting a FRAME. And one hit `CLOSED` standing under a negation - `SO THE BYPASS IS NARROWED, NOT CLOSED`. A keyword over prose cannot tell a finding`s state from its subject, and in a ledger whose whole idiom is writing about correction it will hit the subject nearly every time. THE GENERAL FORM, and it is the same lesson the repository records about gates borrowing the shape of their first incident: a check whose criterion is a WORD is a check about vocabulary, and the thing it wants to know is about STRUCTURE. The structural version exists and is one line - a finding that has been corrected and not settled - and it needed no prose at all.").
dismissed(f_a_keyword_over_prose_cannot_tell_a_finding_from_its_subject, measured_and_not_built).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 32: destructuring, and a denominator that is not the
-- language.
finding(f_the_coverage_denominator_is_the_models_own_vocabulary, insight).
recorded(f_the_coverage_denominator_is_the_models_own_vocabulary, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_coverage_denominator_is_the_models_own_vocabulary, decision).
finding_note(f_the_coverage_denominator_is_the_models_own_vocabulary, "THE OWNER ASKED WHICH FEATURES OF THE LANGUAGE ARE NOT COVERED, and the matrix cannot answer: its denominator is the model`s OWN vocabulary. 89% answered is 89% of 75 declared kinds, and babel`s core ES grammar - no TS, no Flow, no JSX, no experimental - has about 107 concrete node types. MEASURED BY DIFFING THE TWO: roughly 25 core kinds are not declared anywhere, so the coverage figure says nothing whatever about them. The biggest by real-world weight is DESTRUCTURING - `object_pattern`, `array_pattern`, `assignment_pattern`, `rest_element`, `spread_element` - and after it class fields and private names (`class_property`, `class_private_method`, `private_name`, `static_block`), then `update_expression`, `meta_property`, `reg_exp_literal`, `big_int_literal`, `export_specifier`, and the statement forms `labeled`, `empty`, `debugger`, `with`. THE GOOD HALF IS THAT THE HOLE IS DECLARED RATHER THAN SILENT: `vocabulary_gap[audit]` fires the moment a corpus contains a kind the vocabulary does not name, and it did - on a three-line probe with `const { pick } = holder`, before a single rule was written. What the model lacks is not a check but a CORPUS, which is a much better position than it sounds. AND ONE HOLE IS GENUINELY SILENT, which is the part that needs a decision. Diffing the kinds a RULE READS BY NAME against the declared vocabulary finds two: `program`, which is structural, and `class_expression` - which `obj_like[flow]` and `node_value_kind` both read, so the model HAS an opinion about it, and the matrix has NO CELL for it at any layer. A kind the vocabulary does not declare can never be reported unmodelled, so an opinion held in the rules is invisible to the ledger that exists to count opinions. That check is derivable rather than hand-written - the rules are facts and their literals are queryable - and it belongs beside `vocabulary_gap`, which watches the corpus and not the rules.").

finding(f_a_third_kind_of_surviving_mutant_waits_on_an_item, insight).
recorded(f_a_third_kind_of_surviving_mutant_waits_on_an_item, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_third_kind_of_surviving_mutant_waits_on_an_item, doc).
finding_note(f_a_third_kind_of_surviving_mutant_waits_on_an_item, "SIX MUTANTS AT THE DESTRUCTURING RULE, FIVE KILLED AND ONE SURVIVING, and the survivor completes a taxonomy this loop has been building all week. Reading `ast_name` on a pattern`s key where the rule reads `key_name` derives a byte-identical world: every key in a pattern in this corpus is a plain identifier, and `key_name``s first arm IS `ast_name`. It would bite on a COMPUTED key in a pattern - `const { [k]: v } = o` - and that shape cannot be written honestly until w_computed_key_names settles what a computed key is NAMED, because the rule that would read it is the one under repair. THREE KINDS OF SURVIVOR, and the difference decides what to do with each. NO SITE IN THIS CORPUS: give it one, in shapes.ts if its violation cannot run - which is what happened to the scoping guard in this very set, whose site is a name bound inside a function and a free identifier of that name outside it. UNKILLABLE BY THE GRAMMAR: document and leave, like the sequence-field carry on `after_abrupt` or the single quasi of a non-interpolated template. WAITING ON AN ITEM: name the item, because the mutant is not a dead premise but a DATED one, and the day that item lands somebody should come back and kill it. The `stdlib_member` guard measured one iteration earlier is the same category, waiting on w_env_api_surface. Worth asking of every survivor rather than recording it as `survived`.").
addressed_by(f_a_third_kind_of_surviving_mutant_waits_on_an_item, "test/js-controlflow-values.test.ts").

finding(f_the_refactor_shipped_before_the_feature_with_its_own_control, insight).
recorded(f_the_refactor_shipped_before_the_feature_with_its_own_control, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_refactor_shipped_before_the_feature_with_its_own_control, doc).
finding_note(f_the_refactor_shipped_before_the_feature_with_its_own_control, "DESTRUCTURING NEEDED THREE RULES TO STOP NAMING `binder` AND START NAMING WHAT THEY MEANT. `binder_region`, `binder_at_top` and the top-level arm of `sees_binder` all read `binder[code]` - not because scope is about binders-with-an-init, but because that was the only way to bind a name when they were written. A destructured name cannot be a `binder` row at all: the relation`s third argument is the node the name evaluates to, and a destructured name evaluates to a MEMBER of that node. THE INDIRECTION SHIPPED ALONE AND WAS PROVED TO CHANGE NOTHING. `scoped_binder[code](D, File)` with a single arm reading `binder`, the three consumers re-pointed, and fifteen relations of the value and call-graph layers compared ROW FOR ROW against the previous form: identical, 731 066 bytes of sorted rows. Only then did the second arm arrive. THAT ORDER IS THE POINT rather than the refactor. A rename bundled with a feature is a diff where nothing can be attributed: if an answer had moved, no measurement could have said which half moved it. Two commits` worth of care inside one, and the cost was one extra world build.").
addressed_by(f_the_refactor_shipped_before_the_feature_with_its_own_control, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 33: the vocabulary took on the language.
finding(f_the_denominator_became_the_language_and_a_finished_layer_reopened, insight).
recorded(f_the_denominator_became_the_language_and_a_finished_layer_reopened, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_denominator_became_the_language_and_a_finished_layer_reopened, decision).
finding_note(f_the_denominator_became_the_language_and_a_finished_layer_reopened, "THE OWNER DECIDED TO DECLARE EVERYTHING AT ONCE AND BUILD THE ORDER IN THE LEDGER, and the honest cost of that decision is worth recording beside the benefit. TWENTY-THREE KINDS entered the vocabulary in one edit - the destructuring family, ES2022 class fields and private names, `class_expression`, `update_expression`, `meta_property`, bigint and regexp literals, the two export specifier forms, and the statement forms `labeled`, `empty`, `debugger`, `with` plus directives. NOT ONE IS ANSWERED and none pretends to be: every cell they open is `not_modelled`/`not_yet` with an owner, because a verdict written without a site is the thing CLAUDE.md forbids by name. Nine work items carry them, ordered by what real code contains rather than by what is easy. THE MATRIX WENT FROM 320 CELLS TO 416 AND FROM 10 OPEN CELLS TO 102, and the coverage figure fell from 89% answered to 69%. That is the number becoming true rather than getting worse: it used to say `89% of what we declare` and the owner asked what fraction of the LANGUAGE it was. AND THE SHARPEST CONSEQUENCE IS THAT A FINISHED LAYER RE-OPENED. `controlflow` was the one layer with no open cell - the plan`s own finish line, reached 2026-09-06 - and it is 23 open cells again, because every new kind has a control-flow question nobody has answered. A layer finished over a vocabulary that is not the language was finished over the wrong denominator, and the plan says so now instead of celebrating. THREE KINDS WERE NOT TAKEN ON, each for a MEASURED reason rather than a preference: `class_accessor_property`, because the scanner returns NO node at all for `accessor a = 1` without the decorators plugin; `import_attribute`, which scans perfectly well but is ES2025 while the highest environment on the era scale is ts5 at 2022, so the feature would sit in `feature_unreachable[audit]` for ever; and `export_default_specifier`, a Babel proposal no environment claims, not even ts5. All three wait on the decorator work, which needs the same plugin and will have to ask the same question of the environment scale.").
addressed_by(f_the_denominator_became_the_language_and_a_finished_layer_reopened, "facts/js-kinds.rofl").

finding(f_the_era_table_forces_a_factual_answer_for_every_kind, insight).
recorded(f_the_era_table_forces_a_factual_answer_for_every_kind, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_era_table_forces_a_factual_answer_for_every_kind, doc).
finding_note(f_the_era_table_forces_a_factual_answer_for_every_kind, "DECLARING A KIND IS NOT ONE ROW, and the era table is the reason - which turned out to be a feature rather than a tax. `kind_unaccounted[audit]` demands that every declared kind be either `kind_baseline` or gated by a `feature` with a YEAR, so twenty-three kinds forced twenty-three answers to a question with a right answer: nine are ES5 or earlier and are baseline; fourteen are gated, six of them on features that already existed and eight on four new ones - class fields 2022, meta property 2015, bigint 2020, namespace re-export 2020. THEN `feature_unexercised[audit]` DEMANDED A SITE FOR EACH NEW FEATURE, and that is where the table stopped being a formality. Three of the four went into era.js; CLASS FIELDS COULD NOT, because era.js is asserted to fail es2020 for EXACTLY ONE reason and that reason is an operator - a 2022 feature there would have made the assertion two-valued and destroyed what it demonstrates. A fourth fixture file, on the precedent era-position.js already set for the same reason. AND THE SCALE ITSELF REFUSED ONE KIND. `import_attribute` scans without a plugin, so it could have been declared - and `import attributes` is ES2025 while the highest environment is ts5 at 2022, so its feature would be in NO environment and `feature_unreachable[audit]` would be permanently red. The era table said no to a kind the scanner could see, which is the table doing exactly what it is for.").
addressed_by(f_the_era_table_forces_a_factual_answer_for_every_kind, "facts/js-env.rofl").


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, w_destructuring_rest_and_spread: array and assignment patterns,
-- rest and spread.
finding(f_a_spread_argument_shifts_every_argument_after_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_spread_argument_shifts_every_argument_after_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_spread_argument_shifts_every_argument_after_it, doc).
finding_note(f_a_spread_argument_shifts_every_argument_after_it, "THE MODEL SAID SOMETHING FALSE, and it is the first time this family made it do that rather than stay silent. `arg_at[flow](C, I, A)` read `ast_child[code](C, arguments, I, A)` and `param_of[flow](F, I, Name)` read the params at the same index, so argument I fed parameter I. A SPREAD CONTRIBUTES AS MANY ARGUMENTS AS ITS ITERABLE HAS ELEMENTS, so every argument after it lands somewhere the tree index does not name. MEASURED, with the fixture in the corpus and no rule of this item written: `pair(...bench, cubed)` where `bench = [chiselled, planed]` derived the edge `pair -> cubed`, and the same file RUN under node returns 5 = chiselled(1) + planed(1), so `pair` calls `cubed` never. A may-set is allowed to be SILENT and is not allowed to be WRONG, and this was the second kind. THE FIX IS TWO CLAUSES AND THE SECOND IS THE INTERESTING ONE. The positional arm stops at the first spread - `after_spread[code]` - which restores silence. Then the ONE case where the correspondence survives exactly is restored on purpose: a call whose ONLY argument is a spread of an array this layer can see has element I at argument I with nothing after it to shift, and `elem_at[flow]` - written for `array_pattern` in the same hour - is already the relation that says which element sits where. `pair(...bench)` therefore gains `pair -> chiselled` and `pair -> planed`, two edges the oracle confirms, while `pair(...bench, cubed)` gains nothing at all. WHERE THIS CAME FROM IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. The item was `array and assignment patterns, rest and spread`, and nothing in it mentioned the call graph. The defect surfaced because the fixture was written to include the ADVERSARIAL form of each construct - a spread with an argument after it, a rest whose excluded key is read, an array pattern whose two locals are read in two different functions - rather than the form that demonstrates the feature. A fixture that only demonstrates cannot discriminate.").
addressed_by(f_a_spread_argument_shifts_every_argument_after_it, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").

finding(f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does, pitfall).
recorded(f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does, decision).
finding_note(f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does, "THE CONTROL-FLOW LAYER`S REASON ATOM `a_no_control_transfer` IS FALSE FOR FOUR KINDS, and one of them already carried it. MEASURED AT RUN TIME rather than argued: a seven-case probe that records which functions actually ran. An `array_pattern` over an object with a custom `[Symbol.iterator]` RUNS the iterator. An `object_pattern` over an object with a getter RUNS the getter - and `ignored(js, object_pattern, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer)` had been standing since the forty-cell sweep. An object spread `{ ...withGetter }` runs the getter. An array spread `[...iterable]` and a spread ARGUMENT `sink(...iterable)` both run the iterator. An object rest `{ a, ...rest }` runs the source`s getters. Seven cases, six transfers, and only `assignment_pattern` was answerable with the machinery this layer has - its default is an arm skipped by a condition, which is `guard_kind` and nothing more. THIS IS `accessor_call` WEARING ANOTHER SYNTAX. The layer already models exactly one hidden call: `transfer_mechanism(member_expression, accessor_call)`, reached by `accessor_read[code]`, written because an accessor is a call wearing a read`s syntax. Every case above is the same shape - a construct whose surface is a BINDING and whose semantics is a CALL - and none of them is a `member_expression`, so `accessor_read` is structurally unable to see any of them. THE VERDICT WAS INHERITED, WHICH IS THE THIRD TIME IN THIS FILE. `import_expression` and `import` carried `a_control_returns_so_the_site_still_runs` borrowed from `await`; these four carry `a_no_control_transfer` borrowed from `array_expression` and `identifier`, where it is true. The tell each time is a reason atom that describes the class the kind was SORTED INTO rather than the kind. A forty-cell sweep is exactly the instrument that produces this: it is arithmetic by design, and arithmetic over a wrong sentence is fast and wrong. WHAT IT WAITS ON. Naming the transferred-to function needs the receiver`s iterator or its getter, so the general case is a standard-library question and sits behind `w_env_api_surface` like `stdlib_member` does. What does NOT wait is the honesty of the cell: four cells are open and owned by `w_destructuring_hides_a_call` rather than closed by a sentence measurement refutes.").
addressed_by(f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does, "facts/js-controlflow.rofl").
-- ...AND THE `WHAT IT WAITS ON` PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS WRONG, corrected 2026-09-08
-- when the item it spawned was worked. `lib_member(P, "iterator", R)` is ZERO
-- under every spelling, measured with a positive control, so the standard-library
-- surface could never have answered this; and the receivers that DO transfer are
-- user code the model already names. See
-- f_the_receiver_decides_and_the_blocker_was_the_wrong_one.
addressed_by(f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, w_labelled_control (56) and w_inert_statements (57).
finding(f_a_labelled_break_was_read_as_an_ordinary_one, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_labelled_break_was_read_as_an_ordinary_one, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_labelled_break_was_read_as_an_ordinary_one, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_labelled_break_was_read_as_an_ordinary_one, "THE CORPUS HELD ZERO LABELS, SO WHAT THE LAYER DID WITH `break outer` WAS A GUESS, and the guess would have been wrong in both directions. Measured on a fixture built for the item, before a rule was written: with an unconditional `break outer` in the inner of two nested loops, `after_abrupt[code]` named `pastBreak` and `pastContinue` - the siblings of the reference in its OWN statement list, which an unlabelled break kills too - and named NONE of `pastLabelledBreak`, `pastLabelledContinue`, `pastLabelledBlock`. The whole content of a label, leaving a statement further out, was invisible: `abrupt_kind` had held `break_statement` and `continue_statement` since 2026-09-06 and the rule that reads them looks only at SIBLINGS. AND ONE OF THE THREE WAS NOT MERELY MISSING FROM THE STRONG RELATION. A labelled LOOP body is a `guard_kind` arm, so `pastLabelledBreak` was in `guarded` on other grounds and the may-set was already hedging; the label added precision and no safety. A labelled BLOCK is not a loop, not an arm and not a switch, so `pastLabelledBlock` was in NEITHER `guarded` NOR `may_not_run` - the model positively asserted that a statement runs which the only path through its own block skips. That is the NARROW direction, which this layer's own comments name as the dangerous one: a may-set too wide is a hedge, a may-set too narrow is a claim. THE RULE READS THE LABEL AND NOT THE KEYWORD, which is what makes the negative control possible: `label_ref` joins through the `label` CHILD, an unlabelled break has none, and `beyondPlainBreak` therefore stays out even though the fixture puts an unreferenced `unused:` label directly above it. TWO FACTS ABOUT THE GRAMMAR DO WORK NO LITERAL HAD TO DO. The target is unique because two labels of the same name nested one inside the other are a SyntaxError, so `ast_within(LS, X)` plus the name join cannot match twice; and no function-boundary literal is needed - unlike the suspension and throwing-call arms, which both carry one - because a label is not in scope inside a nested function, so `outer: for (...) { const f = () => { break outer; }; }` does not parse and no store can hold it. SEVEN MUTANTS, SEVEN DEAD, each with its own oracle: the arm removed loses all four labelled names; the walk not stopping at the label gains `afterBlock`, `beyondLabel`, `beyondPlainBreak`, `pastInnerLabel` and `seenEmpty`; the name join dropped gains `pastInnerLabel` alone; the label child not required gains `beyondPlainBreak` as well; keying on `break_statement` instead of `abrupt_kind` loses `pastLabelledContinue`; and the two gate mutants make `guard_unmodelled` name `labeled_statement` and `mechanism_unanswered` name `label_boundary`. Two of those needed sites that did not exist and were built for them - a function with two DIFFERENT labels breaking to the inner one, and an unreferenced label over a plain break - because with one label per function the rule that joins on the name and the rule that does not are the same rule.").
addressed_by(f_a_labelled_break_was_read_as_an_ordinary_one, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").

finding(f_after_abrupt_says_never_and_the_walking_arms_say_may, pitfall).
recorded(f_after_abrupt_says_never_and_the_walking_arms_say_may, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_after_abrupt_says_never_and_the_walking_arms_say_may, decision).
finding_note(f_after_abrupt_says_never_and_the_walking_arms_say_may, "`after_abrupt[code]` CARRIES A COMMENT THAT SAYS NEVER - `A STATEMENT AFTER AN ABRUPT TRANSFER NEVER RUNS, which is stronger than what `guarded` says` - AND ITS WALKING ARMS CANNOT KEEP THAT PROMISE. The original arm is exact: it requires the abrupt statement to be a DIRECT CHILD of the statement list, so reaching the list in order is reaching the transfer. Two later arms walk UP instead - `throwing_call` since 2026-09-06 and the labelled-transfer arm added today - and a walk up crosses guard arms without noticing. MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED, with a site put in the corpus for it: `conditionalLabelledBreak` puts `break cond` under an `if`, so `pastConditionalLabelledBreak` runs on every iteration whose test is false, and `after_abrupt` names it anyway. The site is attributable - the reference is inside the `if`'s consequent, so the direct-sibling arm derives nothing there and only the walking arm can produce the row. NOTHING DOWNSTREAM IS WRONG TODAY, and that is exactly why this is worth a row rather than a fix. Both consumers of `after_abrupt` reach it through `guarded[code]`, which the layer documents as a MAY-set that over-covers on purpose, so the loose reading lands where it belongs; the defect is that the RELATION'S OWN WORD is stronger than two of its three sources. This repository has recorded the same shape twice at one level up - a gate whose criterion was borrowed from the first tool that produced a red - and this is it inside a rule: a comment written for the first arm, inherited by arms with a different shape. The decision owed is whether to split the relation (an exact `unreachable_after` for direct siblings, a `may_not_run_after` for the walks) or to re-word the comment; the walking arms cannot be made exact without a guard-crossing test, and the labelled-loop case would fail even that, since reaching a `break outer` in a loop body means the body ran and an empty collection skips it. NOT SEPARATELY MEASURED: whether the `throwing_call` arm has a site of this shape in the corpus today. Its rule body is literally the same walk, so the property is structural, but no row was produced for it and this note does not claim one.").

finding(f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard, question).
recorded(f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard, decision).
finding_note(f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard, "`with` GOT FOUR OPEN CELLS AND NOT FOUR WAIVERS, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY BOTH HALVES OF THAT ARE DELIBERATE. FIRST, WHAT THE SCANNER DOES. scanners/js_ast.ts calls babel with `sourceType: 'module'` and nothing else; a module is strict; `with (o) { p(); }` comes back as `ast_parse_error[code](\"with_mod.js\", \"'with' in strict mode. (1:0)\")` with `nodes = 0` and an EMPTY kind set. The refusal is total, so a `with` in any fixture does not merely fail to produce a node - it deletes that whole file from the corpus, which is why the fixture for w_inert_statements says so in a comment instead of trying. SECOND, AND IT IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS: THAT IS A SETTING AND NOT A CONSTRAINT. The SAME source under `sourceType: 'script'` parses and emits `WithStatement`; under `sourceType: 'unambiguous'` babel chooses `script` for it and - measured file by file - chooses `module` for ALL FIVE existing corpus files, so the one-word change would leave the present corpus byte-identical while making the kind reachable. Writing `no possible site` here would have been the exact defect f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language paid for one component over: a blocker attributed to the first thing looked at, stated accurately, and then read as a constraint. THIRD, AND IT IS WHY THE CELLS ARE STILL NOT CLOSED: `with` IS NOT INERT AND THE ITEM'S NOTE CALLED IT THAT. It redirects name resolution at run time - every identifier in its body may name a property of the object rather than the binder it lexically refers to - so `sees_binder`, `may_be_node` and every call edge resting on them would be reporting a binding the language does not guarantee. That is a soundness question at the DATAFLOW and CALL-GRAPH layers, not a statement no layer has an opinion about, and it is the reverse of the item's premise: the layer likeliest to shrug is control flow, where `with` really does transfer nothing. So the cells stay open, unblocked, owned by name, with the work stated: give the scanner a source type, then decide what the value layer says about a name inside a `with`. NOT MEASURED: what the value layer actually derives for such a name, because producing one needs the scanner change this note declines to make while three agents share the tree.").
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, w_export_specifier_forms: the two export forms with no node.
finding(f_the_export_rename_was_silent_at_three_layers_and_wrong_at_the_fourth, insight).
recorded(f_the_export_rename_was_silent_at_three_layers_and_wrong_at_the_fourth, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_export_rename_was_silent_at_three_layers_and_wrong_at_the_fourth, doc).
finding_note(f_the_export_rename_was_silent_at_three_layers_and_wrong_at_the_fourth, "THE ITEM ASKED WHETHER THE MODEL RESOLVES `export { internal as external }` TO `internal` OR TO `external`, AND THE ANSWER WAS NEITHER. Measured on a two-file probe before a rule was written: `exports_name[code]` has one arm and it reads the export declaration`s `declaration` child, an `export { }` list has no such child, and the probe returned exactly ONE row - the ordinary `export function plain` sitting beside it - while the cross-file call `usesShown -> hidden` was absent from `calls_in` with its site reported as an unresolved `s_identifier`. So at the call graph, at the value flow and at the module graph the model was SILENT rather than wrong-named: it did not believe the module offered `internal`, it believed the module offered nothing. AT THE CONTROL-FLOW LAYER IT WAS NOT SILENT AND NOT SAFE. `exported_fn` walks the CONTAINMENT of an export declaration - `ast_within(E, F), fn_node(F), not in_fn(F)` - which is exactly right for the three forms that carry their function (`export function f`, `export const g = () => {}`, `export default`) and finds nothing at all in a specifier list, whose whole subtree is two identifiers. Measured on a probe whose three functions were exported only that way: `exported_fn` 0, `entry_point` 0, `reachable` 0, `no_entry_point[audit]` firing on the file, and `may_not_be_reached` naming `helper` - a function that same file exports AND calls. The positive control, the identical three functions written `export function`, gives all three as entry points and an empty `may_not_be_reached`. A smaller seed reports LIVE functions as maybe-dead, which is the dangerous direction `export_kind_unseen[audit]` was written to catch - and it caught nothing here, because the entry surface was not lost to a misspelling but to a FORM the walk could not reach. A gate aimed at a typo cannot see a gap in the grammar it enumerates. THE FIX NEEDED NO MECHANISM AT EITHER LAYER, for the fourth time in this loop at the module boundary: `may_be_node` already says an identifier naming a declared function in this file evaluates to that function, and a specifier`s `local` child is exactly such an identifier - so `exports_name(F, External, File) :- export_local(L, External, File), may_be_node(L, F)` is the whole rename, and `exported_fn(F) :- export_local(L, _, _), may_be_node(L, F)` is the whole entry surface. The cost 2x2 says the same thing in rows: the rules cost +0.77% and THIRTEEN firings on the unchanged corpus, the fixture +3.11% and 1 326 - an arm joining relations already standing, not a relation that ranges.").
addressed_by(f_the_export_rename_was_silent_at_three_layers_and_wrong_at_the_fourth, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_export_rename_was_silent_at_three_layers_and_wrong_at_the_fourth, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_export_rename_was_silent_at_three_layers_and_wrong_at_the_fourth, "test/js-export-forms.test.ts").

finding(f_the_js_model_corpus_world_never_reaches_its_fixpoint, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_js_model_corpus_world_never_reaches_its_fixpoint, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_js_model_corpus_world_never_reaches_its_fixpoint, code).
finding_note(f_the_js_model_corpus_world_never_reaches_its_fixpoint, "`corpus()` IN test/js-model.test.ts CARRIES `hole($load(5), budget_exhausted)` ON AN HONEST CHECKOUT, AND HAS FOR AS LONG AS THE WORLD HAS EXISTED. Measured over a 2x2 of {HEAD rules, this iteration`s rules} x {HEAD corpus, this iteration`s corpus}: all four corners, same hole, same load. The construction asserts the AST facts FIRST and then calls `load()` five times, so the fifth - the rule packs - runs a full fixpoint over the whole corpus UNDER `load``s OWN DEFAULT BUDGET, which is DEFAULT_BUDGET = 100 000 in src/api.ts. The `r.evaluate(20_000_000)` on the next line then measures 0 ms and changes nothing, because the work has already been done and truncated. THIS IS THE THIRD APPEARANCE OF THE SAME DEFECT AND THE SECOND IN THE SAME SHAPE. test/js-corpus-world.ts records it in its own header - `r.load() RE-EVALUATES, and this world was calling it nine times` - and iteration 33`s handoff records the control-flow variant, where a test loaded AST facts before the packs and `raising evaluate to 200 M changed nothing, because the wall was never evaluate`s`. The remedy is written down in test/js-corpus-world.ts and was simply never applied here: ONE `load` of boot + facts + rules, the AST facts asserted AFTER, and `evaluate` at the end. WHAT MAKES IT WORTH A FINDING RATHER THAN A PATCH NOTE IS HOW IT WAS FOUND. It was invisible for as long as the truncated fixpoint happened to contain the rows the assertions read: `calls_in` came back 203 rows and the file was green. Adding three functions to the corpus changed WHICH 100 000 steps were spent, `calls_in` came back 0, and the positive control `model.size >= 20` - written for a completely different reason, to stop an empty oracle satisfying every `the model missed this` claim - is the only thing in the tree that said so. A truncated fixpoint fails in the DIRECTION OF FEWER ROWS, and every assertion in that file except the positive controls is satisfied by fewer rows. THE PIN 27 -> 25 IN THE SAME FILE IS UNRELATED AND EXPECTED: two `kind_absent_ok` excuses were retired because the corpus grew the kinds they excused, which is `kind_absent_stale[audit]` working.").

finding(f_a_fixture_for_one_construct_can_blunt_another_constructs_mutant, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_fixture_for_one_construct_can_blunt_another_constructs_mutant, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_fixture_for_one_construct_can_blunt_another_constructs_mutant, doc).
finding_note(f_a_fixture_for_one_construct_can_blunt_another_constructs_mutant, "THE NAMESPACE RE-EXPORT FIXTURE WAS FIRST WRITTEN AS `export * as deltaNs from './delta.mjs'` AND IT SILENTLY DISARMED A MUTANT IN ANOTHER FILE. delta.mjs`s own header states the property it exists for: nothing imports it, so `./delta.mjs` is a specifier that appears in `export * from './delta.mjs'` and NOWHERE ELSE in the corpus - which is the entire reason mutant r4 in test/js-controlflow-scope.test.ts costs anything. Delete the export-all arm of `module_source` and delta stops being resolvable, and that is only true while the specifier has exactly one declaration. A second declaration naming the same module makes r4`s `import_target` assertion pass under the mutation, and NOTHING WOULD HAVE GONE RED: the test file`s expectation was the SET after the mutation, and the set simply stopped shrinking. It was caught because the same edit moved five other named sets in that file and the sixth was read while fixing them. RE-POINTED AT alpha.mjs, which beta.mjs already imports directly, so the new declaration adds no uniqueness anywhere and r4 keeps its teeth. THE GENERAL SHAPE: a corpus is a shared instrument, and a fixture added for construct A can remove the UNIQUENESS that construct B`s mutant rests on. The tell is that the damaged mutant stays green - a blunted mutant looks exactly like a passing one - so the check has to be a habit rather than a gate: before adding a specifier, a name or a module to a shared fixture, ask which mutant currently depends on that thing being the only one of its kind. Every such dependence in this corpus is written down in the fixture`s own header, which is why the habit is affordable.").
addressed_by(f_a_fixture_for_one_construct_can_blunt_another_constructs_mutant, "test/fixtures/js-call/gamma.mjs").

finding(f_a_derived_expectation_assumed_an_export_name_is_its_functions_name, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_derived_expectation_assumed_an_export_name_is_its_functions_name, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_derived_expectation_assumed_an_export_name_is_its_functions_name, doc).
finding_note(f_a_derived_expectation_assumed_an_export_name_is_its_functions_name, "MUTANT n4 IN test/js-controlflow-scope.test.ts BUILDS ITS EXPECTATION FROM THE MODEL - `the site gains an edge to every function alpha.mjs exports BY NAME` - and its own comment says why: written out, the list went red for every function the fixture ever gained, which is a fact about alpha.mjs and not about the mutant. THAT IS THE RIGHT MOVE AND IT CARRIED A HIDDEN CONSTANT ANYWAY. The derivation read the export NAME out of `exports_name` and compared it against `calls_in`, which is keyed by the FUNCTION`S name - and those had been the same string for every export in this repository, because every one was `export function f`. The first renamed export in the corpus separated them, and the expectation asked for `bviaNs -> exposed` where the model correctly says `bviaNs -> renamed`. A SECOND ASSUMPTION SURFACED WITH IT: the mutated rule reads the declaration`s `declaration` child, and an export written as a SPECIFIER has none - so the mutant reaches every export written INLINE and not the one written as a specifier, and `every named export` had quietly stopped being the right set at all. Both halves are now said out loud: the expectation maps through `fn_name` and filters on the presence of a `declaration` child. THE LESSON IS NOT `derive your expectations` - that was already done here and it is still right. It is that a derived expectation encodes a JOIN, and a join between two vocabularies that happen to coincide in the corpus is a constant wearing a query`s clothes. The tell is the same one this repository keeps paying for: two columns that are always equal are one column until a fixture makes them two.").
addressed_by(f_a_derived_expectation_assumed_an_export_name_is_its_functions_name, "test/js-controlflow-scope.test.ts").

finding(f_a_mutant_that_survives_on_a_doubled_path_is_measuring_the_corpus, insight).
recorded(f_a_mutant_that_survives_on_a_doubled_path_is_measuring_the_corpus, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_mutant_that_survives_on_a_doubled_path_is_measuring_the_corpus, doc).
finding_note(f_a_mutant_that_survives_on_a_doubled_path_is_measuring_the_corpus, "FOURTEEN MUTANTS AT THE EXPORT FORMS, THIRTEEN KILLED ON CONTACT AND ONE SURVIVOR, and the survivor is a fourth flavour of `NO SITE IN THIS CORPUS` worth naming separately. Deleting the entry-surface arm for `export { f }` cost NOTHING in the call-graph corpus: `renamed` is exported only through a specifier, but `bviaRename` calls it and `bviaRename` is exported INLINE, so the reachability walk arrives from a seed the mutation does not touch. The site existed, the construct was exercised, and the mutant still could not fail - because the answer had TWO roads to it and the mutation closed one. That is different from having no site at all, and the remedy is different too: not `write a site for the construct` but `write a site where the construct is the ONLY road`. test/fixtures/js/export-surface.js is that site - three functions, every export a specifier, `helper` called only by `viaList` - and the arm`s deletion there takes the entry surface 3 -> 0, fires `no_entry_point[audit]`, and reports `helper` maybe-dead. A ONE-FILE, NEVER-RUN CORPUS WAS THE CHEAP FORM. Putting it in the runnable fixtures would have meant two functions nothing calls at runtime, and therefore two more names on the census`s silent-but-wired list - a list whose whole value is that every entry has a NAMED reason of its own. A fixture must not pay for its question in another test`s vocabulary.").
addressed_by(f_a_mutant_that_survives_on_a_doubled_path_is_measuring_the_corpus, "test/fixtures/js/export-surface.js.txt").

-- ...AND ONE MORE PIN CONVERTED, 2026-09-08 (w_export_specifier_forms). `mutant
-- 5` in test/js-callgraph.test.ts asserted `sites - resolved == 181` under a
-- fourteen-entry changelog, every entry a fixture. Measured while converting it:
-- base `call_site` 397, `shape` 397, `resolved_site` 213, `unresolved_call` 195
-- — and 397 - 213 = 184, which is NEITHER, because `resolved_site` counts the
-- ELEVEN transfer sites (`new`, a tagged template) that `call_site` and `shape`
-- do not. So one pinned integer had been standing in for a subtraction across
-- two different populations. It is a set now: the shaped sites with NO verdict
-- at all — neither resolved nor on the frontier — which is 0 in the baseline by
-- the totality the frontier exists to keep, and exactly the baseline's frontier
-- under the mutant.
addressed_by(f_a_pin_that_moves_with_the_corpus_is_measuring_the_corpus, "test/js-callgraph.test.ts").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 34: three items at once, in three worktrees.
finding(f_a_ledger_keyed_by_name_merges_and_a_pin_keyed_by_nothing_does_not, insight).
recorded(f_a_ledger_keyed_by_name_merges_and_a_pin_keyed_by_nothing_does_not, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_ledger_keyed_by_name_merges_and_a_pin_keyed_by_nothing_does_not, doc).
finding_note(f_a_ledger_keyed_by_name_merges_and_a_pin_keyed_by_nothing_does_not, "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THIS WORK CAN BE PARALLELISED AND WHETHER THE LEDGER WOULD BREAK, and three agents ran three items in three worktrees to find out. THE LEDGER IS THE PART THAT PARALLELISES. `facts/worklist.rofl` and `facts/js-kinds.rofl` were edited by ALL THREE branches and git merged them with ZERO conflicts across three merges, because their rows are addressed by NAME and sit in per-item blocks. That is the same property the two-book merge measured a day earlier - 528 findings, `kind_split` 0 - arriving from the other direction: it is not that a ledger tolerates concurrent writers, it is that NAMING a row is what makes a writer concurrent. THE PINS ARE THE PART THAT DOES NOT. Nine conflict hunks in three merges, and eight were trivial - two branches appending at the same tail. THE NINTH IS THE ONE TO KEEP: `may_not_run` is asserted as a NAMED SET and both branches grew it, so the merge is a UNION, which is computable and was computed - seventeen names against twenty-nine, thirty in the merge. Had that pin been `assert.equal(size, 17)` against `... 29`, BOTH NUMBERS WOULD HAVE BEEN RIGHT ON THEIR OWN BRANCH, neither right in the merge, and nothing in the conflict would have said what the third number was. A count is a claim about a world that no longer exists the moment somebody else works; a set is a claim about what is there. THE PIN DISCIPLINE WAS NOT A TIDINESS PROGRAMME, it was the precondition for this.").
addressed_by(f_a_ledger_keyed_by_name_merges_and_a_pin_keyed_by_nothing_does_not, "test/js-controlflow-scope.test.ts").

finding(f_the_split_by_disjoint_files_was_neither_achieved_nor_needed, insight).
recorded(f_the_split_by_disjoint_files_was_neither_achieved_nor_needed, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_split_by_disjoint_files_was_neither_achieved_nor_needed, doc).
finding_note(f_the_split_by_disjoint_files_was_neither_achieved_nor_needed, "THE THREE ITEMS WERE CHOSEN FOR DISJOINT FILES AND THE CHOICE WAS WORTHLESS. Item 50 was dataflow, 55 modules, 56+57 controlflow - one rules pack and one facts pack each, deliberately, and out of order in the worklist for that reason. MEASURED AFTERWARDS: thirteen files were touched by more than one branch and SIX by all three, `rules/js-controlflow.rofl` among them. The estimate of the contention surface was wrong twice over, and the second error is the instructive one. First error, pessimistic: 27 absolute pins were counted in test/js-model.test.ts by grepping the file, without checking WHICH WORLD they are over - that file's `world()` loads boot, one rules pack and one facts pack, so a verdict landed in any other pack moves NOTHING there. Measured by injecting one verdict: three tests fail, all in test/worklist.test.ts. Second error, optimistic: choosing disjoint files does not make the work disjoint, because every item ends in the same ledger, the same fixture corpus and the same coverage matrix. WHAT ACTUALLY BOUNDED THE COST was not disjointness but SHAPE: rows keyed by name, files appended at the tail, and assertions written as sets. Those three properties made a merge of 3 384 added lines cost nine hunks. Choosing items for file-disjointness can be dropped; choosing them for anything else - real-world weight, dependency order - costs nothing extra.").
addressed_by(f_the_split_by_disjoint_files_was_neither_achieved_nor_needed, "facts/worklist.rofl").

finding(f_two_branches_converted_one_pin_and_the_measurement_settled_it, insight).
recorded(f_two_branches_converted_one_pin_and_the_measurement_settled_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_two_branches_converted_one_pin_and_the_measurement_settled_it, doc).
finding_note(f_two_branches_converted_one_pin_and_the_measurement_settled_it, "THE ONE CONFLICT THAT WAS NOT TEXTUAL: two branches independently converted the SAME pin in test/js-callgraph.test.ts - mutant 5's `sites - resolved == 181`, a literal with a fourteen-entry changelog attached. One replaced it with the identity `sites - resolved == baseUnresolved`. The other MEASURED the quantities first and found the identity is false about this model: base `call_site` 397, `shape` 397, `resolved_site` 213, `unresolved_call` 195, and 397 - 213 is 184, which is neither, because `resolved_site` counts ELEVEN TRANSFER SITES - a `new`, a tagged template - that `call_site` and `shape` do not. The subtraction had been mixing two populations for fourteen entries. The second conversion states what the mutation DAMAGES as a set: a shaped site with no verdict at all, which is zero in the baseline and exactly the baseline's frontier under the mutant. AN IDENTITY IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY BETTER THAN A LITERAL; it is better when both sides count the same thing, AND THAT HAS TO BE MEASURED TOO. A merge resolved by preference would have kept either one. This one was resolved by the branch that had a measurement, and the losing conversion is recorded in the file beside the winner rather than deleted, because the argument is worth more than the line.").
addressed_by(f_two_branches_converted_one_pin_and_the_measurement_settled_it, "test/js-callgraph.test.ts").

finding(f_a_parallel_branch_refuted_the_cell_another_had_just_closed, insight).
recorded(f_a_parallel_branch_refuted_the_cell_another_had_just_closed, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_parallel_branch_refuted_the_cell_another_had_just_closed, doc).
finding_note(f_a_parallel_branch_refuted_the_cell_another_had_just_closed, "THE BEST ARGUMENT FOR PARALLEL WORK IS THAT A SECOND READER IS NOT THE SAME READER. `ignored(js, object_pattern, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer)` was written on 2026-09-07 by the session that built the destructuring rule, and the branch that took the REST of that family measured it false the next day: `const {taken} = withGetter` RUNS THE GETTER, so a destructuring pattern hides a call the way an accessor does, and `accessor_read` is structurally unable to see it because none of these nodes is a `member_expression`. Four cells reopened under a new item, `w_destructuring_hides_a_call`, blocked on `w_env_api_surface` for the same reason `w_effect_layer` is. TWO MORE OF THE SAME SHAPE in the same afternoon: `with` was recorded as having no possible site, and the scanner turns out to hardcode `sourceType: module` - the same source under `script` emits `WithStatement`, so the blocker is a SETTING; and test/js-model.test.ts's `corpus()` was found never to reach its fixpoint at all, `hole($load(5), budget_exhausted)` on an HONEST CHECKOUT, measured in all four corners of a 2x2 including the untouched one. It was green only because a truncated fixpoint happened to contain the rows the assertions read. NONE OF THE THREE WAS THE BRANCH'S OWN WORK. Each was a claim the branch had to lean on and checked instead, which is what a fresh reader does and a continuing one does not.").
addressed_by(f_a_parallel_branch_refuted_the_cell_another_had_just_closed, "facts/worklist.rofl").

finding(f_the_cost_per_fact_was_never_fixture_proof, insight).
recorded(f_the_cost_per_fact_was_never_fixture_proof, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_cost_per_fact_was_never_fixture_proof, doc).
finding_note(f_the_cost_per_fact_was_never_fixture_proof, "test/js-fixpoint-cost.test.ts SAID COST-PER-FACT IS `the one number here that a bigger fixture cannot move on its own`, AND THE 2x2 REFUTES IT. Axes from the merged diff - three branches' rules against three branches' fixtures - four builds, same machine, same minute: HEAD rules on HEAD corpus 1 207 724 rows at 6.260 per fact; HEAD rules on this corpus 1 470 391 at 7.239; these rules on HEAD corpus 1 203 187 at 6.214; these rules on this corpus 1 468 440 at 7.209. THE RULES COST MINUS 0.4% OF ROWS across a dozen new relations and cost-per-fact went DOWN; the corpus costs all of it, 6.26 -> 7.24 with the rules held still. And FIRINGS BARELY MOVED - fifty more derivations for ten thousand more facts - which is the widest these two quantities have come apart. THE REASON IS LEGIBLE IN THE HEAVIEST READ PATH: `argMatches ast_within pos=[0]` is the CONTAINMENT walk, and the new fixtures are nested destructuring patterns and labelled nested loops. A fact deeper in the tree participates in more joins than an average fact, so DENSITY moves the ratio where sheer size does not. What the number still catches is a scan appearing in the hot path with the corpus held still, which is the left-hand column and nothing else. A quantity called fixture-proof for two iterations had simply never met a dense fixture.").
dismissed(f_the_cost_per_fact_was_never_fixture_proof, "the cost gates were removed: every figure they pinned was a measurement of this repository, and a pin that moves with the corpus is measuring the corpus").

finding(f_a_rule_can_read_the_kernels_own_reification_and_audit_itself, insight).
recorded(f_a_rule_can_read_the_kernels_own_reification_and_audit_itself, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_rule_can_read_the_kernels_own_reification_and_audit_itself, doc).
finding_note(f_a_rule_can_read_the_kernels_own_reification_and_audit_itself, "THE DECISION THE LEDGER LEFT ON 2026-09-08 - a kind a RULE reads by name but the vocabulary does not declare is an opinion the matrix has no cell for - IS DERIVABLE, and rules/js-vocabulary.rofl derives it. Clauses are stored as facts after parsing, so `premise_lit(R, K, $lit(Rel, Persp, $cons(A1, $cons(A2, ...)), Tense))` carries a body element whole, with a variable appearing as `$var(\"Name\")`. Walking that cons chain in ROFL - a recursion carrying the position, since Datalog cannot index a list - makes `which kinds does a rule name` A QUERY. First rule in this corpus to read the kernel's own reification; only test files had. WHICH POSITIONS CARRY A KIND IS DISCOVERED, NOT DECLARED, and that is the load-bearing choice: a position IS a kind position when a declared kind appears in it, so the audit configures itself from the rules it audits. Two qualify - `ast_node/2` with 39 declared kinds and `transfer_site/2` with 3 - and a hand table would have had the first and missed the second. Zero on an honest tree, firing on three controls, five mutants with five distinct oracles. The `$not` mutant SURVIVED, because no rule here negates a kind literal, so it was given a SITE: a fixture RULE, in the sense shapes.ts.txt is a fixture file. AND HALF THE QUESTION CANNOT LIVE IN THE LANGUAGE. `node_value_kind(class_expression).` is a FACT in a rules file, not a premise, and the reification says nothing about facts - which is exactly the shape that started this, since the ledger found `class_expression` through `node_value_kind`. Datalog cannot quantify over relations, so that half is a host census, and it checks something stronger than a list: A TABLE THAT HOLDS KINDS HOLDS ONLY KINDS. Across all 357 relation names, 23 positions hold declared kinds and every one is pure.").
addressed_by(f_a_rule_can_read_the_kernels_own_reification_and_audit_itself, "rules/js-vocabulary.rofl").

finding(f_an_audit_that_reads_every_constant_reports_its_own_working, insight).
recorded(f_an_audit_that_reads_every_constant_reports_its_own_working, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_an_audit_that_reads_every_constant_reports_its_own_working, doc).
finding_note(f_an_audit_that_reads_every_constant_reports_its_own_working, "THE CENSUS AUDITED ITSELF AND SAID SO, which cost one run and is worth keeping. rules/js-vocabulary.rofl derives `slot_atom`, `lit_arg` and `kind_named_by_rule`, and every one of them holds EVERY constant in EVERY rule - kinds alongside hundreds of field names. The host census that checks `a table holding kinds holds only kinds` read those three as IMPURE KIND TABLES. They are not tables of kinds; they are a census of constants, and a census that audits itself reports its own working. TWO FIXES WERE AVAILABLE and the choice is the point: excluding the three by name would have worked and left a list to maintain, while NOT LOADING THE AUDIT INTO THE CENSUS WORLD is the same answer with nothing to maintain. The audit reads `premise_lit` and `node_kind` and no corpus, so it has its own cheap world anyway - measured, a corpus adds nothing to its answer and 8.9% to every world in the suite, 2032 ms to 2212 ms, while its own corpus-free world is 571 ms. SAME CLASS AS `corrected` ONE ITERATION EARLIER, where a rule paired a finding's own note against its own correction and reported seven divergences that were the rule's artefact. A relation that ranges over everything will range over the thing asking the question.").
addressed_by(f_an_audit_that_reads_every_constant_reports_its_own_working, "test/js-vocabulary.test.ts").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 35: decorators, and two artefacts made of a setting.
finding(f_a_waiver_for_an_instruments_limit_is_dated_by_construction, insight).
recorded(f_a_waiver_for_an_instruments_limit_is_dated_by_construction, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_waiver_for_an_instruments_limit_is_dated_by_construction, doc).
finding_note(f_a_waiver_for_an_instruments_limit_is_dated_by_construction, "ENABLING ONE PARSER PLUGIN DISSOLVED TWO ARTEFACTS WHOSE WHOLE CONTENT WAS A CONFIGURATION, and neither would have said so. FIRST, `feature_unscannable(decorators, a_parser_plugin_not_enabled)` in facts/js-env.rofl - a waiver written so that a permanent absence would read as a fact about the instrument rather than as a corpus gap. It was correct when written and became a claim about a configuration that no longer exists; nothing in the model would have noticed, because a waived feature is simply excused. SECOND, AND WORSE, test/fixtures/js-env/refused.js.txt - a file whose ENTIRE PURPOSE is to be unparseable, carrying FOUR assertions about a refused file being invalid in every environment - contained a decorator. Measured the moment the plugin landed: 29 of the corpus fixtures parse byte-identically with and without it, ONE started to parse, and that one was the fixture built on not parsing. Its four assertions would have gone quietly green about nothing. THE REPLACEMENT IS REFUSED BY THE LANGUAGE AND NOT BY A SETTING: a module is strict and `with` is a syntax error in strict code, measured under `typescript` alone, under the two decorator plugins, and under every non-conflicting plugin babel offers - `'with' in strict mode. (1:0)` every time. THE RULE THIS YIELDS: when a fixture or a waiver rests on what the INSTRUMENT cannot do, it carries an expiry date that no gate can see, because the day the instrument improves is the day the artefact silently stops testing anything. Prefer a ground the LANGUAGE gives. The same question was asked of `with` one day earlier and answered the other way - its refusal WAS a setting, `sourceType: module` - so the pair is the same measurement pointing in both directions.").
addressed_by(f_a_waiver_for_an_instruments_limit_is_dated_by_construction, "test/fixtures/js-env/refused.js.txt").

finding(f_a_decorator_is_a_call_and_one_arm_reaches_both_its_shapes, insight).
recorded(f_a_decorator_is_a_call_and_one_arm_reaches_both_its_shapes, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_decorator_is_a_call_and_one_arm_reaches_both_its_shapes, doc).
finding_note(f_a_decorator_is_a_call_and_one_arm_reaches_both_its_shapes, "`ignored(js, decorator, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer)` WAS FALSE, and it is the second waiver of that exact shape refuted in two days - `object_pattern` carried the same atom and the same error. A decorator IS a call: `@decoOnce class Decorated {}` calls `decoOnce` with the class and a context object at class-definition time, and the grammar shows no `call_expression` whatever. Fourth member of a family this layer already models - `new`, a tagged template's tag, `for-of`'s iterator - and UNLIKE the destructuring cases in w_destructuring_hides_a_call it is CLOSABLE TODAY, because the callee is named right there in user code and needs no standard-library surface. That is the discriminating half: what blocked the others was never `it is a hidden call`, it was `the callee is a builtin`. ONE ARM REACHES BOTH SHAPES, which was a prediction and then a measurement. `@decoOnce` names the function; `@decoFactory('m')` names a CALL whose RESULT is applied, and since the value layer already carries what a call returns, reading `expression` through `may_be_node` reaches `decoApplied` with no second rule. Measured: `resolves` gains exactly two rows, the edge set gains exactly `top -> decoOnce` and `top -> decoApplied`, and the factory's own call stays visible beside them because that one IS in the grammar. SEVEN MUTANTS, SEVEN ORACLES, all dead. Two of them lose the same EDGES and are told apart by what else moves - the site vanishing versus the site standing unresolved - which is the difference between seven mutants and one mutant run seven times. AND THE LIMIT IS ASSERTED RATHER THAN WRITTEN DOWN: node 24.13 runs neither decorators nor auto-accessors, so this is the first call edge in the corpus with NO execution oracle behind it, and the test asserts that every decorator edge comes from the scanned-never-run fixture.").
addressed_by(f_a_decorator_is_a_call_and_one_arm_reaches_both_its_shapes, "test/js-decorators.test.ts").

finding(f_containment_and_attribution_come_apart_at_a_decorator, defect).
recorded(f_containment_and_attribution_come_apart_at_a_decorator, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_containment_and_attribution_come_apart_at_a_decorator, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_containment_and_attribution_come_apart_at_a_decorator, "A DECORATOR IS INSIDE THE THING IT DECORATES AND DOES NOT RUN THERE - the one place in this grammar where containment and attribution disagree, and the model followed containment. MEASURED on the fixture the day decorators were enabled, before any rule was written: `@decoFactory('m') marked() { ... }` derived `marked -> decoFactory`, an edge from a function to something it never calls. The factory runs at CLASS-DEFINITION time, in whatever scope the class sits in, before `marked` has ever been reached. `nearest_fn` put it inside `marked` because `ast_within` says it is, and `ast_within` is right. THE FIX IS A GUARD ON `encloses` AND IT NEEDS TWO ARMS, which is the part worth keeping: the first excludes anything within a decorator subtree, and the second exists only because `ast_within` is IRREFLEXIVE and so cannot reach the decorator node itself - which becomes a site the moment `transfer_kind` names it. Each arm has its own mutant and they report different false edges, `marked -> decoFactory` and `marked -> decoApplied`. THE GENERAL SHAPE, worth watching for elsewhere: a node whose position in the tree is not where it EXECUTES. Default parameter values were the last one this loop met and they went the other way - a default really does run inside its function - so the question is not decidable from the tree and has to be asked per construct.").
addressed_by(f_containment_and_attribution_come_apart_at_a_decorator, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").

finding(f_top_call_was_keyed_on_call_site_and_lost_every_top_level_transfer, defect).
recorded(f_top_call_was_keyed_on_call_site_and_lost_every_top_level_transfer, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_top_call_was_keyed_on_call_site_and_lost_every_top_level_transfer, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_top_call_was_keyed_on_call_site_and_lost_every_top_level_transfer, "`top_call[code](C, R) :- call_site[code](C, File), ...` SHOULD HAVE READ `site[code](C)`, and the two differ by exactly the transfer sites. `calls` reaches an un-enclosed site only through `top_call`, so a `new`, a tagged template or a `for-of` at the TOP LEVEL of a module resolved a callee and drew NO EDGE at all. FOUND BY A CONSTRUCT THAT CANNOT AVOID BEING UN-ENCLOSED: a class-level decorator sits at module scope by construction, so there is nowhere else its call could be attributed and the hole became unmissable. IT WAS ALREADY THERE - measured on the corpus as it stood before decorators, ONE un-enclosed `new_expression`, silently edgeless. THE TELL IS THE ONE CLAUDE.md ALREADY NAMES: a gate, or here a rule, inherits the scope of its INCIDENT rather than of its class. `top_call` was written when the only sites were calls, and every transfer kind added since - three of them over two weeks - widened the class without widening the rule. `site[code]` existed the whole time and is literally defined as `call_site` OR `transfer_site`, one line above; the rule read the narrower of the two. A relation that exists to mean `either of these` is worth grepping for consumers whenever a new arm is added to it.").
addressed_by(f_top_call_was_keyed_on_call_site_and_lost_every_top_level_transfer, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").
finding(f_a_bigint_matched_none_of_the_four_branches, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_bigint_matched_none_of_the_four_branches, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_bigint_matched_none_of_the_four_branches, doc).
finding_note(f_a_bigint_matched_none_of_the_four_branches, "`1n` REACHED THE STORE AS A NODE WITH NO VALUE AT ALL, and nothing in this repository could have said so. scanners/js_ast.ts dispatches every own property through four branches - node array, node, string/number/boolean scalar, nested object of scalars - and `BigIntLiteral.value` is a native `bigint`, which is NONE of them. It fell through all four and emitted nothing: `const b = 1n` produced one `ast_node` fact and ZERO `ast_attr` rows, the only literal in JavaScript whose value that file dropped, while the contract line at the top of the same file says `every scalar own property`. `extra.raw` holds the text `1n` and `extra` is declared out on purpose, so nothing else carried it either. THE DEFECT IS INVISIBLE BY CONSTRUCTION AND THAT IS THE FINDING. A DROPPED attribute and a node that never had one are the same picture from inside the model: `may_be_lit` simply does not fire, `vocabulary_gap` sees the KIND and is satisfied, `kind_unexercised` sees a node and is satisfied, and every audit here is written over relations that exist rather than over properties that should. The only instrument that can see it is a scan whose expected output is written down - which is why the fix ships with an assertion on the fact TEXT rather than on a derived relation. SAME SHAPE AS THE TEMPLATE CONTRACT ONE ITERATION EARLIER AND THE OPPOSITE CAUSE. There the excluded property was a nested OBJECT and the exclusion was written down and believed for three sessions; here the exclusion was never written down at all, because a four-way `typeof` looks exhaustive and JavaScript has seven primitive types. A branch list is a closed vocabulary that nobody declared as one. The remedy taken is small and general: one `isScalar` predicate both branches share, because the two sites spelled the same three types separately and the second site is the one nobody looks at.").
addressed_by(f_a_bigint_matched_none_of_the_four_branches, "scanners/js_ast.ts").

finding(f_a_for_update_was_never_a_guard_arm, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_for_update_was_never_a_guard_arm, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_for_update_was_never_a_guard_arm, doc).
finding_note(f_a_for_update_was_never_a_guard_arm, "A C-STYLE `for` HAS FOUR CHILDREN AND `guard_kind` HAD ONE. `init` always runs and `test` runs at least once, so neither belongs on the table - but `update` runs ZERO times when the test is false to begin with, and zero times more when the body leaves by a break, a return or a throw. MEASURED ON THE ONE C-STYLE `for` THE CORPUS ALREADY CONTAINED, alpha.mjs:845: `guard_arm[code]` named the body and `guarded[code]` was EMPTY for the `j += 1`, so the model positively asserted that a for-update runs. A CALL written there was `reached_unguarded` and therefore kept OUT of `may_not_run` - the narrow direction, which this layer documents as the dangerous one, and the third instance of that exact shape in three iterations after `pastLabelledBlock` and the specifier-list entry surface. WHAT FOUND IT IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. The slot had been unexamined because the form that inhabits it was not in the vocabulary: `i++` is what a for-update is almost always written as, and `update_expression` entered `node_kind` only when the denominator became the language. So the item whose four cells all looked like waivers - an update transfers no control, is never a callee, is not a module construct - paid for itself in a NEIGHBOUR's already-ticked cell. A vocabulary gap does not only leave its own cells empty; it leaves the POSITIONS that form occupies unexamined, and those belong to kinds the matrix already calls handled. AND THE MUTANT SET FOUND THE SECOND HALF. `guard_kind(for_statement, test)` - the plausible row beside the right one, and false - moved `guarded` 385 to 394 and moved `may_not_run` and `guarded_call` not at all, because no `for` in the corpus called anything in its test. A survivor for want of a site rather than for want of a rule, so the fixture gained one call in a for-test and the mutant dies.").
addressed_by(f_a_for_update_was_never_a_guard_arm, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").
finding(f_a_class_is_reached_by_its_binding_and_a_class_expression_is_where_the_two_come_apart, insight).
recorded(f_a_class_is_reached_by_its_binding_and_a_class_expression_is_where_the_two_come_apart, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_class_is_reached_by_its_binding_and_a_class_expression_is_where_the_two_come_apart, doc).
finding_note(f_a_class_is_reached_by_its_binding_and_a_class_expression_is_where_the_two_come_apart, "THE MODEL HAD AN OPINION ABOUT `class_expression` FOR AS LONG AS THE KIND HAS EXISTED - `obj_like[flow]` and `node_value_kind` both name it - AND THE OPINION DERIVED NOTHING. Measured with the control in the same file before a rule was written: `new Declared().hold()` resolved, `new (class {})().hold()` and `new (class Hoist {})().hold()` resolved to NOTHING, both reported as unresolved `s_member_on_ident`. `member_value[flow]` named the methods correctly the whole time; no receiver could reach them. THE GAP WAS ONE RELATION AND IT WAS A CONFLATION. `class_named[flow]` reads a class's `id` CHILD, and every one of its four consumers - the two `may_be_node` arms, `class_receiver` and `super_of` - actually asks WHICH NAME REACHES THIS CLASS IN THIS FILE. For a declaration those coincide because a declaration's id IS its binding. A class expression separates them: an anonymous one has no `id` at all, and `const W = class Hoist {}` binds `W` outside while `Hoist` is in scope only inside the class's own body. One arm joining through the declarator fixed the constructor edge, the static/instance split and the heritage clause together, because all three were reading the same conflated relation. AND THE MUTANT MEASURED SOMETHING I HAD PREDICTED WRONG: deleting the arm was expected to leave the NAMED class expression answering on its `id`, and it leaves nothing - the `new` rule joins on the CALLEE'S SPELLING, so the name the id arm supplies is a name no site in the program calls. The `id` arm reaches a class expression only from INSIDE its own body, which is the one place a call site for it never is. THE SAME ARM OVER-APPROXIMATES IN THE OTHER DIRECTION and it is pinned rather than hidden: `class_named` is consumed through `ident_in[code]`, which is FILE-scoped, while `sees_binder[code]` in the same pack is scoped properly - so `Hoist` written anywhere else in the file reaches the class here and would reach nothing at run time. A class expression is the first form in this corpus where the scope-blind consumer and the scope-aware one disagree.").
addressed_by(f_a_class_is_reached_by_its_binding_and_a_class_expression_is_where_the_two_come_apart, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").

finding(f_an_edge_can_be_resolved_and_still_dropped_because_the_model_cannot_name_it, insight).
recorded(f_an_edge_can_be_resolved_and_still_dropped_because_the_model_cannot_name_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_an_edge_can_be_resolved_and_still_dropped_because_the_model_cannot_name_it, doc).
finding_note(f_an_edge_can_be_resolved_and_still_dropped_because_the_model_cannot_name_it, "`calls_in[code]` JOINS `fn_name[code]` ON BOTH ENDS, so a function the model resolves to and CANNOT NAME is absent from the graph an oracle is compared against while `resolves[code]` holds it. Found on `const G = class { constructor(n) {...} }`: the constructor's `fn_name` arm reads the class's `id` child, an anonymous class expression has none, and the constructor edge was derived and then dropped. THAT IS THE WORSE OF THE TWO AVAILABLE FAILURES - a miss and a silent miss are different, and only a query at each level tells them apart, which is what mutant m3 in test/js-class-expression.test.ts now asserts: the edge leaves `calls_in`, the instance method beside it does not, and `resolves` still names the constructor. THE FIX IS THE LANGUAGE'S OWN NAME INFERENCE and it was measured on this engine rather than recalled, because the two forms disagree and no reading of the spec would have said which way round: `const A = class { constructor(){} }` gives `A.name` \"A\" and a frame `A`; `const W = class Hoist { constructor(){} }` gives `W.name` \"Hoist\" and a frame `Hoist`; `class D {}` gives `D`. So the BINDING wins only when there is no `id`, and the rule carries that negation instead of naming the class twice - a mutant that drops it derives `onNamedClassExpr -> Winch`, an edge no execution produces. THE GENERAL HAZARD IS BROADER THAN CLASSES: any resolvable callee with no `fn_name` is invisible to every oracle comparison here. The corpus already holds one more - a computed method key, `w_computed_key_names` - and nothing counts them.").
addressed_by(f_an_edge_can_be_resolved_and_still_dropped_because_the_model_cannot_name_it, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").

finding(f_a_heritage_clause_runs_and_a_class_is_waived_as_transferring_nothing, question).
recorded(f_a_heritage_clause_runs_and_a_class_is_waived_as_transferring_nothing, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_heritage_clause_runs_and_a_class_is_waived_as_transferring_nothing, decision).
finding_note(f_a_heritage_clause_runs_and_a_class_is_waived_as_transferring_nothing, "`ignored(js, class_declaration, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer)` LOOKS FALSE AND THE MODEL ITSELF SAYS SO. A class's heritage clause and its computed method keys are EVALUATED when the class is - `class extends mountOf() {}` calls `mountOf` - and this model already attributes that call: with the site in shapes.ts.txt, `calls_in` carries `shapes.ts top -> mountOf`. So the kind does transfer control, the layer can see it, and the cell says it cannot. `class_expression` was therefore given `handled(..., controlflow, r_reachability)` on 2026-09-08 rather than the twin's waiver, and the declaration's row was LEFT ALONE because it is another cell's, which is the convention this loop keeps. THIRD BORROWED REASON OF THE SAME SHAPE in this file: `import_expression` and then `object_pattern`, where `const {taken} = withGetter` RUNS the getter. The pattern is now clear enough to state as a rule of thumb - `a_no_control_transfer` is safe for a LEAF and unsafe for anything with a child position that is an EXPRESSION - and it would be better as a derived check than as three corrections: a kind waived as transferring nothing, where some node of that kind CONTAINS a `site[code]`, is a query. The corpus can answer it today for every waived kind at once. NOT DONE HERE because the waiver rows belong to four different items and reopening them is a plan decision rather than a rule one.").

finding(f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child, insight).
recorded(f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child, doc).
finding_note(f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child, "`new.target` IS ES2015 AND `import.meta` IS ES2020 AND THEY ARE ONE NODE KIND, so `kind_needs(js, meta_property, meta_property)` with `feature_since(..., 2015)` said an es2015 runtime accepts both. The era layer already knew that THE KIND IS NOT ALWAYS THE FEATURE - `attr_needs` exists because `a ** b` and `a * b` are one kind - and neither of its two refinements could split this one. MEASURED RATHER THAN READ OFF THE RULES: a `meta_property` node carries ZERO `ast_attr` rows of any key in either corpus, so `attr_needs` has nothing to grip, and `outside_attr_needs` reads an ANCESTOR's attribute while the whole difference is INSIDE the node. WHAT IS INSIDE IS TWO IDENTIFIER CHILDREN the scanner has always emitted: (meta=\"new\", property=\"target\") against (meta=\"import\", property=\"meta\"). So a fourth gate table, `child_needs(Lang, Kind, Field, Name, Feature)`, and `uses_kind[audit](meta_property, F)` now returns two features five years apart from one kind. THE SHAPE IS THE FINDING, not the row: this is the fourth time the answer to `the table cannot say X` has been `the scanner already emits X and no rule reads it` - after the `static` attribute on class methods, the `kind: get` attribute the accessor work found, and the operator itself. INFORMATION ON THE STORE THAT NOTHING CONSUMES CANNOT GO RED, so its absence is unobservable from every gate we own, and the only instrument that finds it is a question asked of a specific construct. AND THE FOUR-LAYER MATRIX STILL CANNOT SAY IT. `meta_property x modules` is one cell for a construct that IS module metadata and one that is not; the shape axis that would split it applies to `callgraph` only. Three of that kind's four cells are left OPEN with that as the reason.").
addressed_by(f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child, "facts/js-env.rofl").
-- 2026-09-08, w_class_fields: ES2022 class syntax.
finding(f_a_private_method_resolves_and_cannot_be_named, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_private_method_resolves_and_cannot_be_named, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_private_method_resolves_and_cannot_be_named, code).
finding_note(f_a_private_method_resolves_and_cannot_be_named, "THE MODEL RESOLVES A PRIVATE METHOD AND CANNOT SPELL ITS NAME, and the reason is in the KERNEL rather than in the rules. `private_binds` puts `this.#rim(n)` on the private method exactly - lexically, with no may-set anywhere - so `resolves[code]` carries the row and `may_not_run[code]` names the node. `calls_named` and `calls_in` drop it, because they are keyed by `fn_name` and there is no honest name to give. MEASURED ON THREE SIDES. V8 reports the frame as `#rim` WITH THE HASH (`getFunctionName()` = `#rim`, `getMethodName()` = null; a private getter is `get #face`), so an execution oracle would demand the hash. The store holds `rim`, on the `identifier` under the `private_name`, because that is babel's shape. And the bare name is NOT AVAILABLE as a substitute: `rim` and `#rim` are two different members of one class - there is a site for exactly that in test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt - so naming the private method `rim` would make `calls_named` conflate two functions and derive an edge to `notched` that no execution takes. WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT IS ONE STRING OPERATION. The kernel's five string builtins - `str_pre`, `str_seg`, `str_segs`, `str_sub`, `str_char` - all CUT a string up and not one BUILDS one, so `\"#\" ++ Name` cannot be written in this language at all. Same gap reaches the arrow a private field holds (`#mark = (n) => ...`), which is ordinary modern code. A `str_cat` or `str_join` in src/reflect.ts is the whole fix; until then the callgraph layer resolves private members and reports them only by node.").

finding(f_a_class_initialiser_is_a_frame_with_no_function, insight).
recorded(f_a_class_initialiser_is_a_frame_with_no_function, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_class_initialiser_is_a_frame_with_no_function, doc).
finding_note(f_a_class_initialiser_is_a_frame_with_no_function, "A CALL INSIDE A CLASS FIELD INITIALISER OR A STATIC BLOCK RUNS IN A FRAME NAMED AFTER NEITHER, and the execution oracle and the model disagree about the caller for a THIRD reason - neither unsound nor over-approximate. MEASURED: V8 names the static block's frame `<static_initializer>` and an instance field's `<instance_members_initializer>`, both with the class as the type name; the model has no such region, so `nearest_fn` finds nothing for a top-level class and `top_call` attributes the call to the FILE ROOT. For a static field or a static block that attribution is right about the moment - running the shape shows both fire at module evaluation, before any instance - and for an INSTANCE field it is wrong about the moment, which is why the control-flow layer's `per_construction` mechanism guards it and `may_not_run` hedges. THIS IS WHY THE FIXTURE IS IN THE SCANNED HALF. Put the same class in alpha.mjs and `runOracle` records `<static_initializer> -> sealed` against the model's `top -> sealed`: a name the oracle has and the model does not, which the existing UNSOUND/over-approximation split has no third bucket for. SAME SHAPE AS `f_a_synthesised_constructor_has_a_frame_and_no_node`, which is still a question in this ledger, and as `f_a_generators_first_resume_is_attributed_to_a_builtin_frame`: three frames the HOST names for which the PROGRAM has no node. Three instances is where a third bucket in the oracle's report stops being a special case.").

-- ...AND FOUR MORE ON 2026-09-08 (w_class_fields): the shape census in
-- test/js-callgraph.test.ts (6 -> 8 computed callees became a partition) and
-- `r1`, `r2` and `l2` in test/js-controlflow.test.ts, each of which counted a
-- corpus rather than a rule. `l2` is the instructive one: its difference set is
-- the whole module BY CONSTRUCTION, because the mutant deletes the containment
-- literal, so it is a lower bound on named rows and cannot be an enumeration.
addressed_by(f_a_pin_that_moves_with_the_corpus_is_measuring_the_corpus, "test/js-controlflow.test.ts").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 36: four branches, and the three ways a pin can rot.
finding(f_a_set_whose_elements_embed_a_coordinate_is_a_count_in_disguise, insight).
recorded(f_a_set_whose_elements_embed_a_coordinate_is_a_count_in_disguise, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_set_whose_elements_embed_a_coordinate_is_a_count_in_disguise, doc).
finding_note(f_a_set_whose_elements_embed_a_coordinate_is_a_count_in_disguise, "A THIRD WAY A PIN FAILS TO SURVIVE A MERGE, found by running one. The first two were recorded yesterday - a COUNT that two branches both move, and a SCOPE that pins another branch's file. This one is a NAMED SET whose elements carry a coordinate: `BIG_TOTAL@shapes.ts:480`, `shapes.ts:467 guarded`. A parallel branch appended a fixture EARLIER in the same file and every element moved by 38 at once, while every claim the sets make stayed true. The names were stable; the file's numbering was not, and the numbering belongs to the whole file rather than to the thing named. WHAT THE COORDINATE WAS CARRYING is worth stating, because the repair has to keep it: `BIG_TOTAL@:480` and `@:482` say TWO OCCURRENCES - the declaration and the use - and `shapes.ts:467` distinguishes three `for` loops. Multiplicity says the first (`BIG_TOTAL x2`) and the bumped variable says the second (`shapes.ts k guarded`), neither borrowing anybody else's line numbering. THE TEST FOR THIS CLASS: ask whether an element of the set would change if somebody edited a part of the file the assertion is not about. A name would not. A line does.").
addressed_by(f_a_set_whose_elements_embed_a_coordinate_is_a_count_in_disguise, "test/js-update-and-literals.test.ts").

finding(f_a_stray_merge_marker_deleted_a_fixture_and_a_hundred_tests_lied, defect).
recorded(f_a_stray_merge_marker_deleted_a_fixture_and_a_hundred_tests_lied, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_stray_merge_marker_deleted_a_fixture_and_a_hundred_tests_lied, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_stray_merge_marker_deleted_a_fixture_and_a_hundred_tests_lied, "RESOLVING A THREE-WAY MERGE BY SCRIPT, ONE `=======` LINE SURVIVED in test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt - the resolver grepped for `<<<<<<<` and nothing else. Babel refused the WHOLE FILE, 2 432 nodes left the corpus in one step, and what that looked like from outside was 106 FAILING TESTS ACROSS NINE FILES: mutants that stopped killing, named sets that came back empty, counts off by hundreds. NOT ONE OF THEM SAID `a fixture is gone`. `tsc` was silent because the file carries a `.txt` tail precisely so it will not compile, and `text_check` was silent because its rules are about BYTES. THE SECOND TIME IN TWO DAYS THAT `a whole file leaves the corpus` TURNED OUT TO BE A REAL MECHANISM - the first was `with` in a module, where the refusal was the point. A refusal is already a fact the model reasons about correctly (`invalid[audit]` in every environment); what was missing is that NOBODY ASKS. TWO GATES, both paid for by this: text_check now rejects all three markers, on the same REVIEWABILITY property the rest of that file checks and with a planted-marker control; and test/js-ast.test.ts asserts every fixture SCANS, with the one deliberate refusal named. A fixture silently leaving the corpus is a fact about the INSTRUMENT and needs its own gate, exactly as `f_the_refusal_was_a_fact_in_the_other_scanner_already` argued for the file that refuses.").
addressed_by(f_a_stray_merge_marker_deleted_a_fixture_and_a_hundred_tests_lied, "scripts/text_check.ts").

finding(f_a_top_five_is_a_rank_and_a_rank_is_not_a_set, insight).
recorded(f_a_top_five_is_a_rank_and_a_rank_is_not_a_set, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_top_five_is_a_rank_and_a_rank_is_not_a_set, doc).
finding_note(f_a_top_five_is_a_rank_and_a_rank_is_not_a_set, "`the five heaviest read paths, by name` REPORTED A NEW NAME AND NOTHING HAD GROWN. `argMatches ast_node pos=[1]` entered the top five because `relPersp encloses_v` fell below it - measured, the third through sixth paths sit at 6.08, 6.03, 5.97 and 5.84 per cent, so which one is fifth is decided by hundredths of a point. The assertion's own message calls a new name `a body ordered so a big relation is enumerated first`, which would have sent the next reader hunting a defect that is not there. THIS FILE HAD ALREADY RECORDED THE SAME INSTRUMENT LIMIT ONE PLACE FURTHER DOWN - the fourth and fifth paths at 5.25 and 5.24, swapping places on an ordinary corpus change - and fixed it by comparing the set unordered. That fix kept the RANK as the membership criterion, so the limit simply moved to the boundary of the cut. A SET DEFINED BY RANK IS NOT A NAMED SET; it is a count wearing names. THE REPAIR IS A THRESHOLD WITH A MEASURED GAP UNDER IT: six paths lie between 5.84 and 10.05 per cent and then nothing until 4.47, so `every path above five per cent` names the same six and cannot be re-ordered into a different answer. The general form - when a set is defined by a cut, put the cut where the data has a gap, and measure the gap.").
dismissed(f_a_top_five_is_a_rank_and_a_rank_is_not_a_set, "the cost gates were removed: every figure they pinned was a measurement of this repository, and a pin that moves with the corpus is measuring the corpus").

finding(f_the_merge_of_four_branches_was_a_union_of_removals, insight).
recorded(f_the_merge_of_four_branches_was_a_union_of_removals, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_merge_of_four_branches_was_a_union_of_removals, doc).
finding_note(f_the_merge_of_four_branches_was_a_union_of_removals, "FOUR BRANCHES RETIRED TEN `kind_absent_ok` EXCUSES IN ONE AFTERNOON, none able to see the others, and git computed the union unaided. That is the property a table addressed by NAME has and a table addressed by position does not, and it is the same result the two-book ledger merge measured from the other side - except that this time the operation is DELETION, which is the case a naive `keep both sides` resolution gets wrong. Kept as a separate finding because the earlier note only measured ADDITIONS merging. CONFLICT COST ACROSS THE THREE MERGES: 4 hunks, then 5, then 13 - the growth is not the number of branches but the fact that each merges into a tree that already absorbed the previous ones. All twenty-two resolved without a judgement about the MODEL: tail concatenation, unions of named sets, unions of removals, and two places where one branch's form was strictly better than the other's and was simply taken (a superset assertion replacing an equality, and a measured refutation replacing an unmeasured identity). THE ONE JUDGEMENT REQUIRED was mine and I got it wrong: the resolver script matched `<<<<<<<` only, and the marker it left cost 106 red tests.").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 37: the standard library, and a question no world could ask.
finding(f_the_library_surface_is_typescripts_own_record_of_the_spec, insight).
recorded(f_the_library_surface_is_typescripts_own_record_of_the_spec, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_library_surface_is_typescripts_own_record_of_the_spec, doc).
finding_note(f_the_library_surface_is_typescripts_own_record_of_the_spec, "w_env_api_surface ASKED FOR A LIBRARY THIS MODEL DOES NOT HAVE, and it did not have to be written. `node_modules/typescript/lib` ships 89 `lib.es*.d.ts` files and THE ERA IS IN THE FILE NAME: `Array.prototype.at` is declared in lib.es2022.array.d.ts and nowhere earlier, `String.replaceAll` in lib.es2021.string.d.ts, `Array.prototype.join` in lib.es5.d.ts. That is TypeScript`s own record of when each method landed, maintained by people who track the specification, and reading it is cheaper AND more trustworthy than a table this repository would keep by hand. scanners/ts_lib.ts parses them with the TYPESCRIPT COMPILER rather than a regular expression - the compiler is already a dependency, and a regex over a declaration file is a model whose ceiling nobody can state - and emits 130 members of the eight prototypes `kind_prototype` can already name. THE CEILING IS STATED: it reads DECLARATIONS, so it says the name exists on that prototype from that year and nothing about what it does; overloads collapse to one row, which is right for a question about NAMES. AND THE FIRST DRAFT JOINED ZERO ROWS OUT OF SIX because it wrote the method as an ATOM. `stdlib_member[audit]` takes its key from `selects[flow]`, which reads an `ast_attr` VALUE, and every attribute value this scanner emits is QUOTED. `stdlib_unattributed[audit]` - written to notice a wrong prototype - reported all six, which is the same audit noticing a wrong TERM TYPE one level down. Kept as mutant 5 rather than as a memory.").
addressed_by(f_the_library_surface_is_typescripts_own_record_of_the_spec, "scanners/ts_lib.ts").

finding(f_both_halves_were_complete_and_no_world_loaded_both, insight).
recorded(f_both_halves_were_complete_and_no_world_loaded_both, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_both_halves_were_complete_and_no_world_loaded_both, decision).
finding_note(f_both_halves_were_complete_and_no_world_loaded_both, "THE QUESTION `is this library call valid under es2020` NEEDED NOTHING NEW EXCEPT A WORLD. `stdlib_member[audit]` is derived in the CALL-GRAPH world - js-kinds, js-callgraph, js-dataflow, js-modules, js-shapes, js-statements, js-controlflow - and `environment` / `env_rank` are declared in facts/js-env.rofl, which that world does not load; the ERA world in turn has no call graph and so no residue to gate. Both halves had been complete for days and NO WORLD COULD ASK. What the item needed was one pack of three rules and a test that loads both, and the answer came out immediately: `[1,2,3].at(0)` invalid under es5, es2015, es2016 and es2020 and valid under ts5; `'a'.replaceAll` invalid up to es2020; `1n.toString()` invalid below es2020. THE KERNEL SAYS WHICH HALF IS MISSING, and it says it through `unpopulatable` on the relation rather than through an audit: with facts/js-env.rofl withheld, `environment(E)` comes back UNPOPULATABLE - `nothing here can be an environment` rather than `no environment matched`. The first draft of that test expected `undefined_premise[audit]` to name the two relations and it named nothing, because that audit is ITSELF unpopulatable in this world; an assertion on it would have been an empty answer read as a claim, which is the defect this repository has now caught in itself four separate times. THE DECISION FOR THE OWNER: whether the shipped worlds should merge, or whether a question that spans two of them should always build its own. A third world is cheap here and the cost of merging is every test in both paying for the other's packs.").
addressed_by(f_both_halves_were_complete_and_no_world_loaded_both, "test/js-lib-surface.test.ts").

finding(f_an_irreducible_reason_is_a_result_and_not_a_shrug, insight).
recorded(f_an_irreducible_reason_is_a_result_and_not_a_shrug, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_an_irreducible_reason_is_a_result_and_not_a_shrug, doc).
finding_note(f_an_irreducible_reason_is_a_result_and_not_a_shrug, "FOUR CELLS CLOSED WITHOUT BECOMING `handled`, and the shape is worth keeping. `member_expression` on a literal and on a template, and the two literal kinds, all asked the call graph whether it resolves a member call on such a receiver. The answer is no and CANNOT become yes: the call transfers into `Array.prototype.join`, which has no node in this program for an edge to reach. So the reason moved from `not_yet` to `no_source_target` - an atom the model already had - and `unknown_type(no_source_target, irreducible)` takes the cell out of `open_cell[audit]`, because that audit requires `our_unknown`. WHAT MAKES IT A RESULT RATHER THAN A WAIVER is that the item DELIVERED something first: the residue is now attributed to a named method with a year, so `not_yet` became false by measurement rather than by decision. A cell that reads `no_source_target` before anyone has tried to attribute it is a shrug; the same row after is a finished frontier. THE PAIRED TABLE WAS MOVED IN THE SAME EDIT, on purpose: `f_two_reason_relations_over_one_fact_have_now_bitten_twice` records that `shape_because` and `unknown_because` answer the same cell and derive from each other nowhere, and both were set here so the pair does not diverge a third time.").
addressed_by(f_an_irreducible_reason_is_a_result_and_not_a_shrug, "facts/js-shapes.rofl").
-- 2026-09-08, w_mod_beyond_the_import: the re-export edge.
finding(f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing, defect).
recorded(f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing, doc).
finding_note(f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing, "THE STRONGEST ASSERTION IN THE MODULES LAYER WAS GREEN OVER A SET THAT EXCLUDED SEVEN REAL PLACES. `test/js-modules.test.ts` compares node's own resolver against the rule model SET FOR SET and, before anything else, asserts that the two enumerate the SAME NUMBER of sites - written precisely so that a site the model never saw would be reportable. It could not be. The oracle's `oracleSites` collected `ImportDeclaration` and `ImportExpression`; `import_site[code]` derived those same two kinds; and `scanners/js_resolve.ts`'s `sitesOf` - the SECOND, independent observer, whose whole justification is that it enumerates its own places - collected exactly the same two. So three enumerations built to cross-check each other shared one decision about WHICH NODES COUNT, and `export { q } from './d'` was outside all three. MEASURED: 17 sites and 10 `depends` edges on a tree where exp.ts carries two declarations with a `source` child, and exp.ts appeared in neither relation. After the fix, 24 and 16. THE INDEPENDENCE THAT WAS CLAIMED IS REAL AND IT IS ABOUT RESOLUTION, NOT ABOUT COLLECTION: node's resolver answers a re-export's specifier with the same call and the same rules as an import's, so the oracle needed no new logic at all - only four node types where it had two. THE TEST FOR THIS CLASS: when two models are said to cross-check, ask what they DISAGREE about and what they cannot disagree about. Everything downstream of a shared enumeration is in the second bucket. Same shape as `a gate inherits the scope of its INCIDENT`, arriving through a probe rather than through a guard, and `site_without_kind[audit]` is the gate that now says it out loud - a place in `module_site` whose kind no rule names leaves the frontier bookkeeping without moving a row of it.").
addressed_by(f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing, "rules/js-modules.rofl").

finding(f_a_reexports_local_name_is_not_a_name_in_this_module, question).
recorded(f_a_reexports_local_name_is_not_a_name_in_this_module, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_reexports_local_name_is_not_a_name_in_this_module, decision).
finding_note(f_a_reexports_local_name_is_not_a_name_in_this_module, "SECTION 4b OF rules/js-modules.rofl REPORTS `reHelper <- helper` FOR `export { helper as reHelper } from './c.ts'`, AND `helper` IS NOT A NAME IN THAT FILE - it is c.ts's. The section says so itself: it reads no `source` on purpose and leaves the edge to another item. The consequence was invisible until that item's fixtures gave the corpus its first named re-export, and then it appeared as three new rows in a pinned named set in test/js-export-forms.test.ts. THE DATAFLOW LAYER ALREADY GUARDS EXACTLY THIS: `export_local` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl carries `not reexport_decl[code](E)`, with the reason written out - a `local` that happened to collide with a function of the re-exporting file would be re-exported instead, which is what delta.mjs exists to catch. So one layer has the negation and the other does not, over the same two children of the same node. THE FIX IS NOT FREE AND IS NOT MINE TO TAKE: narrowing `export_site` to exclude declarations with a `source` would also delete `everything <- *`, because `export * as everything from './b.ts'` IS a re-export and section 4b's namespace-specifier cell is landed work with a `handled` row against it. So the choice is between a relation that answers for both forms and reports one of them in the wrong module, and two relations with the namespace form moving between them. `reexport_offers[code](F, External, Target, Internal)` now says the true sentence - it carries the TARGET - so the information exists in the model either way; what is undecided is whether `export_binding` should go on saying the other one.").

finding(f_a_null_child_is_not_an_absent_one, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_null_child_is_not_an_absent_one, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_null_child_is_not_an_absent_one, doc).
finding_note(f_a_null_child_is_not_an_absent_one, "AN ORACLE COLLECTING `export ... from` SITES BY THE PRESENCE OF A `source` KEY FOUND 28 WHERE FIVE EXIST. babel gives a LOCAL export list `source: null` rather than omitting the key, so `export { a as b }` and `export { a as b } from './d'` - the same node type, and the pair the whole distinction rests on - differ by the VALUE of that key and not by its presence. `!== undefined` collected every export list in the tree. THE RULE SIDE WAS NEVER EXPOSED TO THIS and that is the interesting half: `scanners/js_ast.ts` emits `ast_child` for a child that EXISTS, so a null `source` produces no row at all and `ast_child[code](E, source, 0, _)` is exactly the right premise. The scanner's own contract had already collapsed the distinction the raw AST keeps, which is why the defect could only appear in a host-side probe walking babel directly - and both such probes here (test/js-modules.test.ts's `oracleSites` and scanners/js_resolve.ts's `sitesOf`) had to be told separately. Caught in one run by an assertion that re-derives the `checked` ledger per site kind from the oracle's own enumeration; without that row it would have been a silently larger denominator.").
addressed_by(f_a_null_child_is_not_an_absent_one, "scanners/js_resolve.ts").
-- 2026-09-08, w_directives: three kinds that are not statements.
finding(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it, defect).
recorded(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it, decision).
finding_note(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it, "`kind_baseline(js, interpreter_directive)` IS A FALSE ROW AND EVERY REPAIR AVAILABLE ON THIS SCALE IS ALSO FALSE. `#!/usr/bin/env node` is the ES2023 Hashbang Grammar; the baseline list it sits in is introduced by the sentence `every one of them is ES5 or earlier`, and es5 (2009) refuses a hashbang outright. MEASURED, in the era world with a hashbang file added to it: ZERO `unsupported[audit]` rows in any environment - the model positively says an es5 engine takes it - with the positive control that the same world produces over fifty `unsupported` rows for other things. THE HONEST GATE IS UNREACHABLE. Replace the row with `kind_needs(js, interpreter_directive, hashbang)` and `feature_since(hashbang, 2023)` and `env_has` is EMPTY for the feature: the highest `env_rank` on the scale is ts5 at 2022, so `feature_unreachable[audit]` names `hashbang` and the site reports unsupported in ts5 as well - a false NO everywhere traded for a false YES everywhere, paid for by turning a green audit red on an honest tree, which CLAUDE.md records as the state in which a gate gets switched off. THIS IS THE THIRD TIME THE 2022 CEILING HAS DECIDED A MODELLING QUESTION and the first time it has done so INSIDE the table rather than by keeping something out: `import_attribute` (ES2025) and `export_default_specifier` were both left out of the vocabulary for exactly this reason, and both absences are recorded as decisions. A kind already declared cannot be kept out, so the ceiling stops being a filter on the vocabulary and becomes a wrong ANSWER. WHAT WOULD FIX IT is one `environment(es2023). env_rank(es2023, 2023).` - the owner's to add, like the era scale itself - after which the true gate costs one row and the tripwire in test/js-directives.test.ts (`max env_rank == 2022`) goes red to say so. THE GENERAL SHAPE, worth more than the instance: a SCALE with a ceiling is a filter you can only see from OUTSIDE it, and the vocabulary decision that keeps a kind out is visible while the baseline row that lets one in is not. `kind_double_booked[audit]` catches a kind declared both ways and nothing at all catches a kind declared baseline that is not.").

finding(f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of, insight).
recorded(f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of, doc).
finding_note(f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of, "`'use strict'` IS THE ONE CONSTRUCT IN w_directives WITH A REAL SEMANTIC EFFECT and no layer can feel it - not because the directive is invisible, but because BOTH THINGS IT CHANGES ARE UNMODELLED FOR EVERY CONSTRUCT, which is a fact about the model rather than about directives. (1) IMPLICIT THROWS. Strict mode makes an assignment to an undeclared name a ReferenceError. `may_throw[code]` is `throws_outright` plus propagation along call edges, and `throws_outright` reads a `throw_statement` and nothing else - measured, every function it names contains one - so no implicit throw of any kind is in this model: not a ReferenceError, not a TypeError from `null.x`. (2) THE `this` OF A PLAIN CALL. Strict mode makes it `undefined` instead of the global object. Measured over the corpus: all 24 `this_expression` nodes get a value from `may_be_node[flow]` and EVERY value is a class declaration, a class expression or an object literal - an `obj_like` receiver. There is no `the global object` and no `undefined` in the value layer's vocabulary at all, so a plain function's `this` has nothing to be wrong about. AND THE CORPUS COULD NOT SHOW IT ANYWAY: scanners/js_ast.ts parses every file as `sourceType: 'module'` and module code is strict already - measured by RUNNING it rather than reading the spec, a direct `eval` in module code with no directive above it throws ReferenceError on an assignment to an undeclared name while a `new Function` body, sloppy by construction, assigns. So every `'use strict'` this scanner can see is a no-op. THE SAME SCANNER SETTING THAT BLOCKS `with_statement` (f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard), reached from the opposite side: there the setting DELETES the construct from the corpus, here it makes the construct semantically inert. Whoever answers the script-mode question for `with` gets both, and until then `ignored(js, directive, controlflow, a_no_control_transfer)` is true about the FORM - a directive completes normally in any mode - and is not hiding a modelled effect, because there is no effect modelled anywhere for it to hide.").

finding(f_a_kind_can_be_in_the_corpus_and_in_no_rules_slot_and_only_the_reification_says_so, insight).
recorded(f_a_kind_can_be_in_the_corpus_and_in_no_rules_slot_and_only_the_reification_says_so, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_kind_can_be_in_the_corpus_and_in_no_rules_slot_and_only_the_reification_says_so, doc).
finding_note(f_a_kind_can_be_in_the_corpus_and_in_no_rules_slot_and_only_the_reification_says_so, "TWELVE CELLS WAIVED ON ONE QUERY INSTEAD OF TWELVE EMPTY MEMBERSHIP TESTS, and the query is over the RULES rather than over the corpus. rules/js-vocabulary.rofl already walks the kernel's reification of every rule body; `slot_atom(ast_node, 2, K)` is therefore the set of node kinds any rule NAMES - 52 across the eight js rule packs, structure through vocabulary - and `slot_atom(ast_child, 2, F)` the set of tree fields any rule READS - 34. `directive`, `directive_literal` and `interpreter_directive` are in neither, and neither are the fields `directives` and `interpreter`, with `string_literal` and `body` as the positive controls. THAT IS A NEGATIVE OVER THE WHOLE MODEL and it is the shape CLAUDE.md asks for: a grep says `no textual tell here`, a paragraph says `I looked`, and this says `no rule in the model can bind one`. WHY BOTH HALVES ARE NEEDED, and the first draft had only the kinds half: a rule can reach a node WITHOUT naming its kind, by walking the field it hangs off with the kind left free - which is exactly how `ast_within` and every containment walk works. A kind-only audit would have missed a rule reading `ast_child(P, directives, _, D)`. THE COMPLEMENT IS THE PART TO REMEMBER: this instrument answers `can any rule reach this node` and says nothing about whether the answer SHOULD be no. It replaced twelve queries whose emptiness was uninterpretable with one whose emptiness is a measurement, and it is reusable by every future item that has to waive a whole kind - which is most of them, since the vocabulary is now the language and the language is wider than the model.").
addressed_by(f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of, "facts/js-statements.rofl").
-- CORRECTED WITHIN A DAY, 2026-09-09, and the correction is a COUNT rather than
-- a conclusion. The note above reads `all 24 this_expression nodes get a value
-- from may_be_node[flow] and EVERY value is ... an obj_like receiver`. Re-run in
-- w_js_corpus today: there are TWENTY-SIX, all twenty-six are valued, all
-- twenty-six are obj_like, and none is anything else. The sentence is still true
-- and its number is not, because the number was measuring the CORPUS.
finding_note(f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of, "THE NUMBER MOVED AND THE CLAIM DID NOT, re-measured 2026-09-09 in w_js_corpus with four probe relations over `this_expression`: valued 26, obj_like 26, non-obj_like 0, unvalued 0 — against the note's `24`, written 2026-09-08. Nothing was wrong with the reasoning; the note wrote a corpus count where the claim is a SET PROPERTY (`every value is obj_like`, therefore there is no `the global object` and no `undefined` for strict mode's `this` rule to change), and a fixture grew. THE OTHER TWO MEASUREMENTS RE-RUN TRUE AND ARE NOT COUNTS: `throws_outright[code]` is still one clause in rules/js-controlflow.rofl whose only AST premise is `throw_statement`, so no implicit throw of any kind is modelled; and scanners/js_ast.ts still parses every file `sourceType: 'module'`, so every `'use strict'` this scanner can see is a no-op. NO WITNESS IS ATTACHED AND THAT IS THE FINDING'S RESIDUE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT. The claim worth watching is the set half, and stating it as a query needs a relation w_js_corpus does not have — something of the shape `this_value_foreign[audit](N, V) :- ast_node[code](N, this_expression, _, _), may_be_node[flow](N, V), not obj_like[flow](V)`. It is one line and it was REFUSED here: rules/js-dataflow.rofl is loaded by four declared worlds and its row count is pinned by test/js-layer-cost.test.ts, so a relation added for a note would move numbers measuring something else, which is precisely the trade HANDOFF records as `a gate was pinned, and then a rule was repaired in a pack that gate's world loads`. A floor on `ast_node[code](N, this_expression, F, L)` would merge and pass and say nothing about obj_like, which is worse than an honest gap. So the gap is named: the set half of this finding is prose until somebody who is editing that pack anyway adds the relation.").

-- 2026-09-09, from the w_directives / w_meta_property / w_export_specifier_forms
-- re-audit: what the witness checker is structurally unable to look at.
finding(f_a_count_written_in_a_notes_prose_has_no_checker_at_all, defect).
recorded(f_a_count_written_in_a_notes_prose_has_no_checker_at_all, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_count_written_in_a_notes_prose_has_no_checker_at_all, doc).
finding_note(f_a_count_written_in_a_notes_prose_has_no_checker_at_all, "`f_a_pin_that_moves_with_the_corpus_is_measuring_the_corpus` was paid for in TEST ASSERTIONS and the conversion was done there. The same number written inside a `finding_note` STRING has no gate of any kind, and this was found by re-running one: the note on `f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of` says `all 24 this_expression nodes`, the corpus has 26, and the drift was invisible to every check in the tree. scripts/witness_check.ts reads `witness_in`, `witness_in_atleast` and `witness_absent` rows and NOTHING ELSE — it never opens a note — so a number promoted to a witness goes stale by itself and the identical number left in prose does not. THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT AND IT IS NOT FIXABLE BY WIDENING THE CHECKER. Extracting integers from prose and asking them of a store is a keyword-over-prose move, and rules/worlds.rofl already refuses that family in writing, for a measurement: six candidate settlement keywords, six false positives, `CLOSED` standing under a negation. So there is deliberately no `note_number_stale[audit]` and there should not be one. WHAT IS AVAILABLE is a writing rule, which is why this demands doc rather than a rule: A NUMBER IN A NOTE IS EITHER A FLOOR OR A WITNESS. If the sentence survives the number moving, write no number — write the set property. If it does not, the number is load-bearing and belongs in a `witness_in` row where the checker can reach it. THE COST OF NOT HAVING THIS is measured on this branch: three of my three items closed on notes, two of the notes carried a corpus count, both counts had drifted within a day, and both sentences were still true — so the drift taught nothing and cost a re-measurement each. A note that is right for a reason it no longer states is the shape `f_the_note_is_my_own_prior_guess_wearing_a_measurements_clothes` names, arriving through arithmetic instead of through a guess.").
addressed_by(f_a_count_written_in_a_notes_prose_has_no_checker_at_all, "facts/findings.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_kind_can_be_in_the_corpus_and_in_no_rules_slot_and_only_the_reification_says_so, "test/js-directives.test.ts").
-- THE FIRST ONE IS LEFT OPEN ON PURPOSE and its `demands` says why: every
-- repair available inside facts/js-env.rofl is also false, and the one that is
-- not - an `es2023` on the scale - is the owner's, like the scale itself.
--
-- ...AND IT WAS GRANTED THE SAME DAY AND THE FINDING NEVER HEARD, 2026-09-09.
-- The decision this `demands(decision)` was waiting for was taken on 2026-09-08
-- - `environment(es2023). env_rank(es2023, 2023).` is in facts/js-env.rofl, the
-- false `kind_baseline(js, interpreter_directive)` row is gone, and the honest
-- gate `kind_needs(js, interpreter_directive, hashbang)` with
-- `provides(es2023, hashbang)` is in its place. test/js-directives.test.ts
-- asserts the answer under the name ERA: the hashbang is gated on its true year,
-- and the scale reaches it, and it is green. NOTHING TOLD THE LEDGER: the
-- finding carried no `addressed_by`, so the session-start hook has been offering
-- a decision that was made twenty-four hours earlier as open work. That is the
-- backlog offering work already done, which this file records elsewhere as the
-- class a keyword over prose cannot detect - and the structural detector for it
-- is what the three witness rows below are, because a witness goes stale by
-- itself and a note does not.
finding_note(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it, "SETTLED 2026-09-09, and the trade the note feared did not have to be paid. Re-measured in w_js_ambient, the only declared world that loads facts/js-env.rofl and rules/js-env.rofl: `env_rank(es2023, R)` is one row at 2023, `env_has[audit](es2023, hashbang)` is TRUE, `env_has[audit](ts5, hashbang)` is EMPTY - so ts5 at 2022 correctly refuses a 2023 construct - and `feature_unreachable[audit]` is ZERO over the whole feature table, which is the audit this note predicted would name `hashbang` for ever. The note's arithmetic was right and its conclusion had one free variable in it: it said `WHAT WOULD FIX IT is one environment(es2023)`, and priced the repair against a scale that would not move. The scale moved. THE PART WORTH CARRYING is not the hashbang: a finding whose `demands` is a DECISION has no way to notice that the decision was taken. An artifact-shaped demand is discharged by an artifact somebody names; a decision is discharged by a row appearing in a fact pack, and no `addressed_by` follows a row. Three witnesses are attached rather than a sentence, and the sharpest is the absence one - `env_has[audit](ts5, hashbang)` empty with the SAME query one constant over (`es2023`) live as its control - because that pair is the whole finding: the model no longer says a pre-2023 engine takes a hashbang, and it does say a 2023 one does.").
addressed_by(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it, "facts/js-env.rofl").
witness_in_atleast(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it,
                   w_js_ambient, "env_has[audit](es2023, hashbang)", 1).
witness_absent(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it,
               w_js_ambient, "env_has[audit](ts5, hashbang)", "env_has[audit](es2023, hashbang)").
-- ...and the audit the note said the honest gate would turn red for ever. It is
-- EMPTY, and its control is a relation of the same shape over the same table
-- that has rows, so the zero is a measurement rather than a misspelling.
witness_absent(f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it,
               w_js_ambient, "feature_unreachable[audit](F)", "used_feature[audit](F)").

finding(f_order_collision_is_derived_non_zero_and_asserted_nowhere, defect).
recorded(f_order_collision_is_derived_non_zero_and_asserted_nowhere, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_order_collision_is_derived_non_zero_and_asserted_nowhere, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_order_collision_is_derived_non_zero_and_asserted_nowhere, "`order_collision[audit]` HAS TWO ROWS ON AN HONEST TREE AND NOTHING ANYWHERE READS IT. Measured at 2b332e0, before this branch touched anything: `work_order(w_cf_completion, 40)` and `work_order(w_computed_key_names, 40)`; `work_order(w_cost_gate_per_layer, 41)` and `work_order(w_cf_suspension, 41)`. The rule is one line in rules/worklist.rofl and its own comment says it exists so this check `should never have needed a shell pipeline in a repository where the plan is derived` - and then no test asks it. `grep -n order_collision test/*.ts` returns nothing, while every neighbouring audit in that pack (`queue_stale`, `false_done`, `double_owned`, `needs_unknown`, `blocker_stale`, `spawn_orphan`) has both a zero-assertion and a named mutant in test/worklist.test.ts. THE CONSEQUENCE IS LIVE: `next_work[audit]` picks the takeable item with no earlier takeable one, so with two items at 40 the queue hands out whichever the fixpoint reaches, and the plan's ORDER - the one thing facts/worklist.rofl says no model can compute - is decided by evaluation order for those four items. THE CLASS IS THE ONE THIS REPOSITORY KEEPS PAYING FOR, recorded twice already: information on the store that nothing consumes cannot go red, so its absence of coverage is unobservable from every gate we own. Here the information is a DERIVED AUDIT rather than a scanner attribute, which is the sharper version - the rule was written on purpose, is correct, fires, and is invisible. FOUND FROM OUTSIDE: this branch owns w_directives and had no reason to read the queue audits at all; the rows turned up in a routine `is the plan still consistent` sweep after marking one item done. WHAT IT NEEDS is two decisions and one line: somebody renumbers the four items (whose orders they are is the owner's), and test/worklist.test.ts gains `order_collision[audit] == 0` with a mutant beside the eleven already there. The assertion cannot be added first, because it is RED on an honest checkout today and a gate red on an honest checkout gets switched off.").
-- ...AND ITS SUBJECT WAS DELETED RATHER THAN REPAIRED, 2026-09-09. Both halves
-- of the demand are moot and neither was met. Measured in w_queue: `work_order`
-- has ZERO rows in facts/worklist.rofl (the sixty-five hand-written numbers are
-- gone, replaced by an order derived from `work_needs`), and
-- `order_collision[audit]` is not a relation at all — the query comes back
-- `unpopulatable`, not empty, which is the distinction this repository built
-- that flag for. rules/worklist.rofl says so in its own words at the site: two
-- items sharing a position under a derived order is a PARTIAL ORDER rather than
-- a collision, and `work_needs_cycle[audit]` is what took its place.
--
-- SO THE FINDING IS RIGHT AND UNREPAIRABLE. The general claim it makes — a
-- derived audit that fires and that nothing reads cannot go red, so its absence
-- of coverage is invisible from every gate we own — is untouched and is already
-- recorded twice in CLAUDE.md. The INSTANCE cannot be closed, because you cannot
-- add `order_collision[audit] == 0` to a test for a relation that no longer
-- exists, and adding it would be a gate that certifies everything: the exact
-- defect the finding is about, one turn later.
dismissed(f_order_collision_is_derived_non_zero_and_asserted_nowhere,
          subject_removed_with_the_hand_written_order).

-- 2026-09-08, w_decorator_replaces_its_target (62) and w_cg_invisible_calls (34).
finding(f_a_decorator_returns_a_different_shape_for_every_position, insight).
recorded(f_a_decorator_returns_a_different_shape_for_every_position, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_decorator_returns_a_different_shape_for_every_position, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_decorator_returns_a_different_shape_for_every_position, "ONE SYNTAX, FOUR RETURN CONTRACTS, and a value rule that ignores the difference is WRONG rather than incomplete. A class decorator and a method decorator return the REPLACEMENT itself - a class, a function - so `@decoFerrule class Swage {}` binds `Swage` to what came back and `member_value` may carry the replacement in place of the method. A FIELD decorator returns an INITIALISER TRANSFORMER, `(initial) => next`, which is not the field's value and never appears as one. An AUTO-ACCESSOR decorator returns a `{get, set, init}` TRIPLE, and `o.slot` is the result of CALLING the `get` in it, not the triple. So `r_decorator_replacement` has exactly two arms and refuses the other two positions on purpose: a general `the decorated thing evaluates to the decorator's return` would answer `o.slot` with an object literal that no execution ever produces, which is a may-set asserting something FALSE rather than hedging. WHAT THAT LEAVES OPEN, and it is the interesting half: a decorated auto-accessor whose triple carries a user-written `get` IS a transfer into user code, and the existing `accessor_of[flow]` + `accessor_read[code]` machinery in rules/js-controlflow.rofl would carry it with one arm - `accessor_of(CD, Key, G)` where G is the `get` member of what the decorator returned. It has no site in this corpus and no rule, and it is the one shape that would make `ignored(js, class_accessor_property, controlflow, a_both_accessors_are_synthesised)` false: the waiver is exact for an UNDECORATED auto-accessor, where both accessors really are the language's, and says nothing about a decorated one. THE GENERAL RULE THIS IS AN INSTANCE OF: a construct whose meaning depends on WHERE IT SITS needs one arm per position, and the arms it does not write are a statement about the language that belongs in the rule file rather than in a summary.").

finding(f_an_invisible_call_is_two_different_blockers_and_only_one_is_ours, insight).
recorded(f_an_invisible_call_is_two_different_blockers_and_only_one_is_ours, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_an_invisible_call_is_two_different_blockers_and_only_one_is_ours, doc).
finding_note(f_an_invisible_call_is_two_different_blockers_and_only_one_is_ours, "`w_cg_invisible_calls` COLLECTED FOUR CONSTRUCTS BY THEIR SYMPTOM - a call the grammar shows no node for - and three of them closed with a rule while the fourth could not, because the symptom was the only thing they shared. A tagged template, a for-of and a decorator all name a callee that IS IN THE PROGRAM; the value layer finds it and `resolves[code]` takes an extra entry point. An `await` names one that is not: measured over the whole corpus, `member_value[flow](O, \"then\", V)` is EMPTY while `member_value[flow](O, \"next\", V)` returns two rows and the relation carries 140 distinct keys, so there is no user-written thenable anywhere and every one of the four awaits calls `Promise.prototype.then`. `builtin_prototype` names six prototypes and `promise` is not one of them. THE POSITIVE CONTROL IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT: an empty `then` on its own says nothing, and it is the non-empty `next` beside it - the same relation, the same shape of query - that turns it into a fact about the corpus. AND THERE IS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT REASON that must not be confused with the first: V8 attributes a thenable's `.then` to `<top>` because the promise machinery makes the call, so even a corpus that HAD a user thenable would be modelling an edge the instrument contradicts. One is a missing surface (`w_env_api_surface`) and the other is a limit of the oracle; an item that names only the symptom cannot tell them apart, and the claim sat under an owner who could never finish it. GROUPING BY SYMPTOM IS A QUEUE SMELL: the items that close are the ones grouped by what the MODEL would have to gain.").

finding(f_a_positive_control_aimed_at_a_cell_is_aimed_at_the_queue, insight).
recorded(f_a_positive_control_aimed_at_a_cell_is_aimed_at_the_queue, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_positive_control_aimed_at_a_cell_is_aimed_at_the_queue, doc).
finding_note(f_a_positive_control_aimed_at_a_cell_is_aimed_at_the_queue, "THE SAME POSITIVE CONTROL HAS NOW MOVED THREE TIMES AND EACH MOVE WAS A CELL BEING ANSWERED. `test/js-controlflow.test.ts` asserts that `not_yet` has not gone extinct, and it did so by naming one cell that still carried it: first `throw_statement x dataflow`, then `catch_clause x dataflow` - both closed by w_exception_flow on 2026-09-06 - then `decorator x dataflow`, closed by w_decorator_replaces_its_target on 2026-09-08. The comment written at the second move already diagnosed it (`a positive control that keeps landing on cells this loop is about to answer is a control chasing the work`) and then made the third move anyway, with the sentence `decorator is in nobody's queue path`. THAT SENTENCE WAS FALSE THE DAY IT WAS WRITTEN, and checkably so: facts/worklist.rofl carried `claim(queued, js, decorator, none, dataflow, w_decorator_replaces_its_target)` at that moment. THE STRUCTURAL REASON THERE IS NO SAFE CELL: `unqueued[audit]` is the gate that says an open cell nobody owns is a lie in the plan, and it is EMPTY - so every cell carrying `not_yet` is, by construction, in somebody's queue path. A control that names a cell is therefore guaranteed to be a control aimed at work somebody has already promised to do. THE REPAIR IS TO ASK THE QUESTION THE CONTROL IS FOR: `reason[audit](js, K, S, L, not_yet)` has at least one row. That is a FLOOR over the whole matrix, it says exactly `not_yet did not go extinct`, and moving it would take closing every unknown in the language. GENERAL FORM: when a control names a specific witness, ask whether the property being controlled for is about THAT witness or about the population; if it is about the population, a floor over the population cannot be chased.").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-08, iteration 39: the scale grew, and the year turned out to be wrong.
finding(f_partial_names_the_symptom_and_the_hole_names_the_cause, insight).
recorded(f_partial_names_the_symptom_and_the_hole_names_the_cause, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_partial_names_the_symptom_and_the_hole_names_the_cause, doc).
finding_note(f_partial_names_the_symptom_and_the_hole_names_the_cause, "`kind_ungoverned[audit]` CAME BACK `partial` AND NEITHER BUDGET WAS THE ANSWER. Measured, four worlds, one variable at a time: with the AST facts asserted FIRST and the packs loaded after, the store carries `hole($load(8), budget_exhausted)` and the query reports `partial` under the DEFAULT budget and under 400 million alike; with the packs loaded first and the facts after, no hole and `partial` false at either budget. So `partial` is not a fact about the QUERY - it is the query refusing to answer for a world that is itself incomplete, and the number to change is not a budget at all but the ORDER of construction. THE TRAP IS THAT `partial` READS LIKE A BUDGET, and a reader raises the query`s budget first, then `evaluate`'s. Both were tried here and both failed, which is exactly the signal that the truncation is upstream: `r.load()` re-evaluates under its own DEFAULT_BUDGET of 100 000 steps, so a world built by eight successive loads runs its fixpoint eight times and the last one is the one that counts. `hole(Q, Why)` names the cause and nothing else does. THIRD FILE IN TWO DAYS with this construction - test/js-model.test.ts and now test/js-env.test.ts - and both were invisible until something grew: a corpus in one case, the environment scale in the other. A budget nobody checks is a pin on the SIZE of the world with nothing in the file to say so.").
addressed_by(f_partial_names_the_symptom_and_the_hole_names_the_cause, "test/js-env.test.ts").

finding(f_an_era_is_a_composition_of_releases_and_not_a_year, insight).
recorded(f_an_era_is_a_composition_of_releases_and_not_a_year, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_an_era_is_a_composition_of_releases_and_not_a_year, decision).
finding_note(f_an_era_is_a_composition_of_releases_and_not_a_year, "THE OWNER SAID THE YEAR IS THE WRONG KEY AND ASKED FOR A RELEASE, and three independent measurements say he is right. FIRST, THE TABLE ALREADY CONFESSES: `env_extra(ts5, type_syntax)` exists because - in facts/js-env.rofl`s own words - `TypeScript is es2022 PLUS syntax that is on no ecmascript timeline. It is not a later edition and modelling it as one would be a lie the integer comparison could not detect.` The extras mechanism is a patch admitting the key is wrong. SECOND, THE INTEGER FORBIDS WHAT REALITY HAS. Measured 2026-09-08 while adding environments: `lost[audit]` carries `RT < RF` STRICTLY, so two environments at one year cannot separate each other in either direction and `env_pair_indistinct[audit]` reports the pair. The model holds AT MOST ONE ENVIRONMENT PER YEAR - and 2023 alone had node 20, node 21 and four TypeScript releases. `env_extra` cannot rescue it, because extras only add features and `lost` reads the scale. THIRD, THE AUTHORITY WE ALREADY READ MODELS IT AS COMPOSITION. `node_modules/typescript/lib/lib.es2015.d.ts` is not a point on an axis, it is `es5` plus seven named feature libs; `lib.es2022.d.ts` is `es2021` plus six. An edition is a UNION OF NAMED PARTS in which the previous edition is one part, and the linearity of pure ecmascript is a consequence rather than the mechanism - which is why `lib.dom`, `lib.webworker` and `lib.esnext.*` fit the same shape and no year. THE SHAPE OF THE CHANGE: `provides(Release, Feature)` as the primitive and `includes(Release, Part)` with transitivity, so `env_has` becomes a lookup instead of a comparison and `lost` becomes a set difference that needs no ordering at all - which dissolves the one-per-year limit as a side effect. `env_rank` survives as a LABEL for reporting. AND THE ECMASCRIPT HALF IS GENERATABLE: scanners/ts_lib.ts already reads those files for facts/js-lib-surface.rofl, and the `/// <reference lib=` lines are the composition, so the table becomes a measured fact rather than a hand-written one. NOT DONE: it touches `env_has`, `lost`, `unsupported` and every mutant of theirs, and the owner has not said whether to do it now.").

finding(f_the_year_is_gone_and_the_confession_went_with_it, insight).
recorded(f_the_year_is_gone_and_the_confession_went_with_it, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_year_is_gone_and_the_confession_went_with_it, doc).
finding_note(f_the_year_is_gone_and_the_confession_went_with_it, "THE ERA TABLE STOPPED COMPARING INTEGERS AND STARTED WALKING A COMPOSITION, on the owner`s instruction, and the second arm of `env_has` went with it. That arm - `env_has(E, F) :- environment(E), env_extra(E, F)` - WAS THE CONFESSION: TypeScript is not a later edition, so it needed a mechanism OUTSIDE the timeline, and the file said in so many words that modelling it as one `would be a lie the integer comparison could not detect`. Under composition there is no second mechanism and nothing to confess: `includes(ts5, es2022)` and `provides(ts5, type_syntax)` are the same two sentences the reference graph writes for every edition. THE COMPOSITION IS GENERATED AND NOT WRITTEN. scanners/ts_lib.ts reads the `/// <reference lib=` lines: ten `includes` rows, the chain es5 -> es2015 -> ... -> es2024, each edition naming exactly one previous edition plus between two and nine named parts. AND THE ONE-PER-YEAR LIMIT DISSOLVED AS A SIDE EFFECT, which is the tell that the key was wrong rather than merely coarse: `lost[audit]` carried a strict `RT < RF` and is now a set difference with no ordering at all, so two releases that are not comparable each lose things to the other - the true answer, and one a number could not give. Direction falls out of the data instead of being asserted. TWO MUTANTS HAD TO BE RE-AIMED AND BOTH RE-AIMINGS ARE THE MEASUREMENT: the one that gave es2016 the rank 2015 became INERT, because a rank is a label now and it would have survived silently; and the off-by-one `Since >= R` became `not reaches` losing its negation, which inverts the answer instead of widening it - an environment then reports as unsupported exactly the methods it HAS. A mutant that survives a refactor unchanged is not evidence that the refactor was safe; it is evidence that the mutant was about the old shape.").
addressed_by(f_the_year_is_gone_and_the_confession_went_with_it, "rules/js-env.rofl").

finding(f_a_load_order_defect_in_four_files_and_each_hid_until_something_grew, defect).
recorded(f_a_load_order_defect_in_four_files_and_each_hid_until_something_grew, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_load_order_defect_in_four_files_and_each_hid_until_something_grew, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_load_order_defect_in_four_files_and_each_hid_until_something_grew, "FOUR TEST FILES BUILT A WORLD BY ASSERTING FACTS FIRST AND LOADING PACKS AFTER, and `r.load()` RE-EVALUATES under its own DEFAULT_BUDGET of 100 000 steps - so the fixpoint runs once per pack and the LAST one decides. test/js-corpus-world.ts documented the remedy and the reason in a long comment; test/js-model.test.ts, test/js-env.test.ts and test/js-directives.test.ts each had the defect anyway, and each was invisible until something GREW: a corpus, then the environment scale, then the scale again. THE SYMPTOM IS A LIE ABOUT ITS OWN CAUSE. The query comes back `partial`, which reads like a budget, and a reader raises the query`s budget and then `evaluate`'s - both were tried here and neither can work, because the truncation happened during `load` before `evaluate` ran at all. Only `hole($load(N), budget_exhausted)` names it. THE REPAIR IS ALSO A SPEEDUP, which is why nobody had a reason to notice: test/js-env.test.ts went from eight seconds to four, because seven of its eight fixpoints were work thrown away. WHAT IS STILL MISSING IS A GATE. Three of the four were found by a test going red for an unrelated reason, and the fourth by looking. A world that ends with a `hole` in it is a world whose answers are all suspect, and every one of these files could assert that in one line - two now do. A derived check over the test directory, of the shape test/query-unpopulatable.test.ts already uses, would find the rest.").

finding(f_the_order_became_the_work_and_the_plan_turned_out_flat, insight).
recorded(f_the_order_became_the_work_and_the_plan_turned_out_flat, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_order_became_the_work_and_the_plan_turned_out_flat, doc).
finding_note(f_the_order_became_the_work_and_the_plan_turned_out_flat, "SIXTY-FIVE HAND-WRITTEN `work_order` ROWS ARE GONE, on the owner`s instruction, and `next_work` is derived from `work_needs` instead. THE NUMBER WAS UNRELIABLE FOR A STRUCTURAL REASON rather than through carelessness: facts/worklist.rofl is grouped by TOPIC, so file order and numeric order disagreed in TWENTY positions out of sixty-five, and whoever appended an item read `...37 38 39` immediately above and took 40 - which a block near the top of the file had taken two days earlier. `the next free number` is a GLOBAL fact and the evidence in front of the author is LOCAL. Both collisions arose exactly that way, in four separate commits. WHAT REPLACES IT IS LEVERAGE AS A BOOLEAN, because this kernel has no aggregation: an item something WAITS ON comes before one nothing waits on. Measured the same day and by accident: `w_env_api_surface` had three items behind it and closing it took eleven cells out of the queue at once, which no hand number had said. AND THE CHANGE MADE AN AUDIT NECESSARY. A hand number cannot be less than itself, so acyclicity came free and nobody stated it; a derived order makes a cycle possible AND SILENT - every item in one is blocked by another in it, so none is takeable and the queue simply goes quiet. `work_needs_cycle[audit]` is the replacement, and this plan came within one ROW of needing it: `w_env_api_surface` waited on `w_prototype_of_a_value` while that item`s own note said its remaining half needed `w_env_api_surface`, and only the note being PROSE rather than a row kept it acyclic. THEN THE DERIVATION SAID SOMETHING NOBODY HAD ASKED: `next_work` is now SEVENTEEN items, every takeable one, because all eleven items anything waits on are done. The plan is FLAT - seventeen independent fronts and no next - which is what a finished dependency chain looks like from the queue`s side, and it is the state a wave of parallel agents is for.").
addressed_by(f_the_order_became_the_work_and_the_plan_turned_out_flat, "rules/worklist.rofl").


-- Recorded 2026-09-08 by w_mod_beyond_the_import, second half: the default
-- export, the two cells that stay open, and the settlement of the question the
-- first half spawned.

-- THE SETTLEMENT, as a second note on the finding it answers. The convention
-- in this ledger is one-to-many with the later row carrying the correction
-- (f_a_ledger_keyed_by_name..., and `corrected` is derived from exactly this
-- shape), so the question above is left standing and this says what changed.
finding_note(f_a_reexports_local_name_is_not_a_name_in_this_module, "SETTLED 2026-09-08, AND THE GUARD IS NOT TRANSPLANTABLE - which is a fact about the GRAMMAR rather than about this fixture. The question was whether section 4b of rules/js-modules.rofl should keep deriving `reHelper <- helper` for a specifier whose `local` name belongs to another module, given that rules/js-dataflow.rofl guards the same two children of the same node with `not reexport_decl`. MEASURED AGAINST BABEL 8 BEFORE DECIDING: `export * as ns;` is a SYNTAX ERROR. An ExportNamespaceSpecifier cannot occur without a `source` in any program that can be written - so narrowing `export_site` to exclude declarations carrying a `source` would make `export_binding` derive ZERO rows for `export_namespace_specifier` EVERYWHERE, not merely delete `everything <- *` from this corpus as the first note put it. That kind has a landed `handled` row, and a cell answered by a rule that is empty by construction is the shape CLAUDE.md names for a flag nothing exercises. SO THE ROW IS KEPT AND THE SILENCE IS CLOSED INSTEAD. `export_binding` says something true about NAMING - this module offers External, standing for Internal - and what it could not say is WHICH MODULE Internal is a name in. Section 5c derives that in one rule with two arms, total over every export specifier: a specifier under a declaration with no `source` lives in this file, one under a re-export lives in the file the resolver reached. `export_internal_elsewhere` then names the four rows the finding was about, and TWO OF THE FOUR ARE TYPE-ONLY - which is why it is not `reexport_offers` under another name, since that relation carries only the specifiers that survive erasure. The residue is a positive relation rather than a gate (`export_internal_unplaced`, for a re-export from a package this corpus does not have) and `export_internal_homeless[audit]` says the pair is total. TWO MUTANTS, both killed: reading the re-exporting file for the second arm empties `export_internal_elsewhere`, and inverting the first arm`s negation leaves five plain specifiers with no module to live in. THE TRANSFERABLE PART: when one layer has a guard another lacks over the same node, ask what the guard would DELETE before transplanting it - the answer here was a whole kind, in every program, and it was one probe away.").
addressed_by(f_a_reexports_local_name_is_not_a_name_in_this_module, "rules/js-modules.rofl").

finding(f_the_default_export_has_an_erasure_marker_that_lies, decision).
recorded(f_the_default_export_has_an_erasure_marker_that_lies, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_default_export_has_an_erasure_marker_that_lies, decision).
finding_note(f_the_default_export_has_an_erasure_marker_that_lies, "THE MODULES LAYER READS TWO ERASURE MARKERS AND THERE IS A FORM NEITHER OF THEM CAN SEE. `export type { X }` marks the DECLARATION and `export { type X }` marks the SPECIFIER, neither implies the other, and rules/js-modules.rofl reads both in three separate places because that trap has now arrived through three different grammars. Scanned over eight spellings of the default export rather than recalled: `export default interface I {}` is legal TypeScript, is erased ENTIRELY before anything runs, has NO specifier to carry a marker, and its declaration carries `export_kind` VALUE. So both markers say the opposite of the truth for it and the only discriminator left is the KIND of the `declaration` child - `tsinterface_declaration` - which this vocabulary does not declare at all. WHY IT IS NOT CLOSED HERE: declaring one node kind opens a cell at every layer, which is four verdicts in four packs this item does not own, and `w_vocabulary_home` is already the item asking where kind rows should live. WHAT MAKES IT NON-SILENT MEANWHILE: `vocabulary_gap[audit]` in rules/js-model.rofl fires on any kind a corpus contains and the vocabulary does not declare, so the row appears the moment such a declaration enters a fixture - which is why section 4c states the hole and does NOT write a narrower copy of that audit beside it. Section 4c therefore answers the NAMING question for the default form (`default_external` is always the string the import half already spells, `default_internal` is the declaration`s `id` when it has one, `default_anonymous` is the written-down complement) and claims nothing about erasure. THE DECISION IS WHETHER THE TS TYPE-DECLARATION KINDS ENTER THE VOCABULARY: they are the only way any layer can tell an erased default export from a live one, and the cost is a column of cells that four packs would have to answer.").

finding(f_the_subpath_specifier_resolves_inside_the_tree_and_the_bare_one_does_not, defect).
recorded(f_the_subpath_specifier_resolves_inside_the_tree_and_the_bare_one_does_not, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_subpath_specifier_resolves_inside_the_tree_and_the_bare_one_does_not, doc).
finding_note(f_the_subpath_specifier_resolves_inside_the_tree_and_the_bare_one_does_not, "A RECORDED REASON WAS WRONG TWICE AND HAD BEEN CARRIED FORWARD BY TWO SWEEPS. facts/worklist.rofl said of `import x from #sub` that the specifier resolves through a package`s `exports` map, which is the same on-disk question as `bare`, and flagged the cell as the owner`s to scope on that basis. PUT TO NODE`S OWN RESOLVER FROM THE FIXTURE TREE, the two shapes land on OPPOSITE SIDES of the scanned root: `#sub` resolves to sub/c.ts INSIDE it, `@babel/parser` resolves into node_modules OUTSIDE it. It is the `imports` map and not `exports`; it is the IMPORTING package`s own package.json and not a dependency`s; and that file is already a fact this model holds - the fs emitter walks it and reports `fs_file_in(., package.json, package.json)` - so the cell is not blocked on anything about the disk. WHAT IT IS ACTUALLY BLOCKED ON, stated so the next reader argues with the right thing: the file`s CONTENT. The host`s declared loans are the string algebra and the file tree, a JSON object is neither, and the walk in section 3 starts at the importing FILE`s directory while a subpath must start at the PACKAGE ROOT. So closing it is a third blind emitter plus a second walk origin - real work with a shape, not a scoping question. THE CLASS THIS BELONGS TO: `bare` carries the owner`s `out_of_scope` and this cell was matched to it by resemblance, which is exactly the move f_scope_is_the_owners_word_and_i_wrote_four_of_them warns about, arriving through a REASON rather than through a verdict. A wrong reason beside a right verdict is harder to catch, because nothing audits prose. The correction is now an assertion in test/js-modules.test.ts that runs node`s resolver on both specifiers, so the next reader is corrected by a red test rather than by a paragraph.").
addressed_by(f_the_subpath_specifier_resolves_inside_the_tree_and_the_bare_one_does_not, "facts/js-modules.rofl").

finding(f_a_require_site_moves_no_row_of_the_frontier, defect).
recorded(f_a_require_site_moves_no_row_of_the_frontier, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_require_site_moves_no_row_of_the_frontier, decision).
finding_note(f_a_require_site_moves_no_row_of_the_frontier, "THE MODULES LAYER`S WHOLE FRONTIER APPARATUS IS KEYED ON `module_site`, SO A MODULE CONSTRUCT OUTSIDE IT IS INVISIBLE TO EVERY GATE THE LAYER OWNS. Measured on a probe holding a real `require(./b.ts)` asserted into the built world: `module_site` 24 before and 24 after, and `unaccounted_site[audit]`, `site_without_kind[audit]`, `reason_missing[audit]` and `resolve_gap[audit]` all 0 before and 0 after - with a positive control on the same run showing the call IS in the store as a `call_expression` whose callee is the identifier `require`. So the model can see the construct perfectly well and no relation that speaks for the layer`s completeness can say a word about it. THIS IS f_two_enumerations_with_one_blind_spot_agree_about_nothing ONE LEVEL UP. That finding was about three enumerations sharing one decision about which nodes count; this is the same decision made once and then relied on by six gates, and `site_without_kind[audit]` - written in response to that very finding - inherits it, because a site kind no rule names is still only checked WITHIN `module_site`. A gate that partitions a set cannot report something outside the set. AND THE COUNT IS ZERO, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF: every .ts, .mjs, .js and .ts.txt file in this repository was parsed - 265 of them, 2 unparseable - and walked for a CallExpression whose callee is `require`. There are NONE, in the tree or in either scanned corpus. So the cell waits on a CORPUS as well as on a scoping decision, which is the first of the three questions HANDOFF.md asks of a surviving mutant, and the corpus half is ours to fix whenever the scope half is settled. LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY: the fix is a relation naming the constructs a module graph COULD have - which presupposes deciding whether CommonJS is one of them - and `bare` beside it already carries the owner`s `out_of_scope`.").
-- ...AND ITS LAST SENTENCE WAS FALSE, corrected 2026-09-08 by the item that
-- wrote it. The convention here is a SECOND note carrying the correction; see
-- `corrected(F)` in rules/findings.rofl.
finding_note(f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child, "CORRECTED 2026-09-08 BY THE ITEM THAT RECORDED IT. The note ends `the shape axis that would split it applies to callgraph only. Three of that kind`s four cells are left OPEN with that as the reason`, and that is FALSE: `axis_applies(shape, modules).` has been in facts/js-modules.rofl since the specifier refinement landed, and the column`s whole design is ONE COLUMN WITH PER-LAYER VOCABULARIES gated by `shape_in`. The paragraph the note was reading is in facts/js-shapes.rofl, which declines the axis for DATAFLOW and says nothing about modules; a refusal written in one pack was read as the state of the whole matrix. So two of the three cells could have been split on the day this was written, the third cost one `axis_applies` row, and all three are answered now - `import_meta` modelled at modules and waived at callgraph, `new_target` waived at modules and `runtime_dependent` at callgraph, and both irreducible and different at dataflow, which is what earns the axis that layer. THE TRANSFERABLE HALF IS THE READING ERROR AND NOT THE ROW: a design decision recorded as PROSE beside one of its instances reads as a decision about all of them, and three cells waited a day on a sentence nobody re-ran as a query. `axis_applies(shape, L)` is a fact and the file that denies it is not.").
addressed_by(f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child, "test/js-meta-and-await.test.ts").

finding(f_a_caller_less_call_is_invisible_to_an_oracle_keyed_on_the_caller, defect).
recorded(f_a_caller_less_call_is_invisible_to_an_oracle_keyed_on_the_caller, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_caller_less_call_is_invisible_to_an_oracle_keyed_on_the_caller, doc).
finding_note(f_a_caller_less_call_is_invisible_to_an_oracle_keyed_on_the_caller, "THE ACCEPTANCE ORACLE CANNOT SEE THE ONE CALL THE LANGUAGE PERFORMS FOR AN AWAIT, and the work item read that blindness as a contradiction for a day. MEASURED with the real trace.mjs, a real thenable and a copied fixture directory: a thenable`s own `then` runs on a stack of DEPTH ONE - `Error.captureStackTrace` returns one frame, `st[1]` is undefined - so trace.mjs writes `caller: <top>` and `file: \"\"`, and `runOracle` in test/js-callgraph.test.ts keeps a frame only when `RUN_FILES.includes(path.basename(e.file))`. `path.basename(\"\")` is `\"\"`. The edge is DROPPED before any comparison, so the oracle can neither confirm nor refute a model edge here - it is not a red gate, it is a gate that cannot go either way. THE COLLISION THAT HID IT IS ONE STRING FOR TWO FACTS. `<top>` is what trace.mjs renders for a call from module top level - a real region with real code, whose frame carries a real file - and ALSO what it renders for the ABSENCE of a caller frame. The model has the same collision on its own side: `calls_in[code](File, top, B)` means `called from the module body`, and reading the oracle`s `<top>` as that is right by accident for one of the two. THE SAME SHAPE ONE LEVEL DOWN, found by writing a gate that could never fire: `performed_as_edge[audit]` was first written over `nearest_fn[code]`, and `encloses[code]` demands `site[code](C)` - an `await_expression` is not a site, so the gate reached no await at all and was permanently silent in the SAFE direction, which CLAUDE.md names as worse than red. The caller machinery of this layer is keyed on SITES and the construct whose caller is in question is not one. WHAT THE MODEL DOES INSTEAD: `performed_call[code](A, F)` names the callee and is one argument SHORT of `calls[code]` on purpose - the missing column is the caller, and the missing column is the answer. It never reaches `calls_named`/`calls_in`, so nothing derived from it can be compared against a stack frame, which is correct because there is no stack frame. `await_value_unknown[audit]` is the residue and is deliberately NOT wired into `frontier_at`, because that relation attributes a miss to the CALLER and this class of call has none.").
addressed_by(f_a_caller_less_call_is_invisible_to_an_oracle_keyed_on_the_caller, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").

finding(f_an_await_calls_no_library_method_and_two_notes_said_it_did, defect).
recorded(f_an_await_calls_no_library_method_and_two_notes_said_it_did, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_an_await_calls_no_library_method_and_two_notes_said_it_did, doc).
finding_note(f_an_await_calls_no_library_method_and_two_notes_said_it_did, "`w_await_is_a_call_the_oracle_places_elsewhere` recorded the callee of an await as `Promise.prototype.then`, `a standard-library method with NO NODE IN THE PROGRAM`, and re-pointed the cell at `w_env_api_surface` by name. THREE MEASUREMENTS in the note are correct and re-run correct - four awaits, `member_value[flow](O, \"then\", V)` empty with `\"next\"` at 2 as the positive control, `promise` absent from `builtin_prototype` - and the SENTENCE THEY SUPPORT IS FALSE. Probe: patch `Promise.prototype.then`, count. `await <native promise>` calls it ZERO times; `await 5` zero; `await <user thenable>` zero, while the thenable`s OWN `then` runs exactly once; and the positive control `p.then(f)` counts 1, so the zeros are facts about `await` rather than about the probe. Identical on V8 11.3 (node 20.20.0) and V8 13.6 (node 24.13.0), read from `process.versions.v8`. The specification`s `Await` uses PerformPromiseThen, an INTERNAL operation, so a promise`s continuation is not an observable call at all. THE COST OF THE ERROR WAS A DEPENDENCY THAT COULD NEVER DISCHARGE. The cell was queued behind an item building a standard-library surface from `lib.es*.d.ts`; no edition of ECMAScript is involved in this construct, so that item could have finished forever without moving this cell. The same item was ALSO named as the blocker for half of `w_meta_property`, and that dependency is false in the other direction: `interface ImportMeta` in `lib.es5.d.ts` is EMPTY and `import.meta.resolve` is declared in `lib.dom.d.ts` and `@types/node`, which `releaseOf` refuses by construction because their names carry no release. TWO ITEMS BLOCKED ON ONE ITEM THAT COULD NOT UNBLOCK EITHER. THE TELL, BOTH TIMES, IS A MEASUREMENT OF THE CORPUS STANDING IN FOR A MEASUREMENT OF THE LANGUAGE. `there is no user thenable in this corpus` is true and says nothing about what an await does; `the receiver has a known prototype` is true and says nothing about which FILE declares the member. The probe that settles either one is three lines and runs the construct.").
addressed_by(f_an_await_calls_no_library_method_and_two_notes_said_it_did, "test/js-meta-and-await.test.ts").

finding(f_a_gate_over_a_form_watched_only_the_half_that_goes_missing, insight).
recorded(f_a_gate_over_a_form_watched_only_the_half_that_goes_missing, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_a_gate_over_a_form_watched_only_the_half_that_goes_missing, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_gate_over_a_form_watched_only_the_half_that_goes_missing, "`meta_form[code](M, Form)` is meant to be a FUNCTION - one node, one construct - and the only gate written over it was `meta_unformed[audit]`, which watches for a node with NO form. Found by asking CLAUDE.md`s highest-yield question of my own instrument before running anything: where is this check structurally unable to look. Answer: the other direction. The mutant that keys the first arm on the wrong word gives every `import.meta` node BOTH forms and leaves `new.target` with none, and `meta_unformed` reports exactly one node out of three affected - HALF THE DAMAGE OF THE MUTANT WAS INVISIBLE TO THE ONLY GATE OVER THE RELATION. `meta_form_conflict[audit](M, A, B)` is the twin, and the mutant now kills two gates instead of one. THE PATTERN IS ALREADY IN THIS REPOSITORY AND WAS NOT REUSED: `export_binding_missing[audit]` and `export_binding_conflict[audit]` are the same pair over the same shape of relation, twenty lines apart in rules/js-modules.rofl, written the same way for the same reason - and its own comment says a count cannot say WHICH. A totality gate written without its conflict twin is half a gate, and the half that is missing is the one a wrong rule produces rather than a missing one.").
addressed_by(f_a_gate_over_a_form_watched_only_the_half_that_goes_missing, "rules/js-structure.rofl").

finding(f_the_v8_version_pair_this_repository_quotes_is_not_the_one_it_runs, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_v8_version_pair_this_repository_quotes_is_not_the_one_it_runs, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_v8_version_pair_this_repository_quotes_is_not_the_one_it_runs, doc).
finding_note(f_the_v8_version_pair_this_repository_quotes_is_not_the_one_it_runs, "SIX PLACES SAY `V8 12.4 (node 20) and V8 14.0 (node 24)` AND BOTH NUMBERS ARE WRONG. Measured with `node -p process.versions.v8` on the two interpreters this machine actually has: node 20.20.0 is V8 11.3.244.8 and node 24.13.0 is V8 13.6.233.17. The pair 12.4/14.0 belongs to no node on this machine - 12.4 is node 22 - so every sweep quoted as `identical on V8 12.4 and V8 14.0` was in fact identical on 11.3 and 13.6, which is a WIDER span and therefore a stronger result than the one claimed. THE CLAIM WAS NEVER WRONG, ONLY ITS COORDINATES, and that is the interesting half: the sweeps ran, the frames agreed, and the number attached to them was recalled rather than read. It spread the way a wrong constant spreads - trace.mjs`s docstring first, then a finding, then a test comment, then two work-item notes and a fixture header, each copying the one before, and no gate here reads an engine version so none of them could go red. This is the `witness has no world` defect wearing a version string: a measurement is quoted with the conditions it was taken under, and the conditions were the one part nobody re-ran. An instrument that reports its own version costs one expression - `process.versions.v8` - and the frame sweep should print it beside the table rather than have it typed in a comment.").
addressed_by(f_the_v8_version_pair_this_repository_quotes_is_not_the_one_it_runs, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_v8_version_pair_this_repository_quotes_is_not_the_one_it_runs, "test/fixtures/js-call/trace.mjs").
addressed_by(f_the_v8_version_pair_this_repository_quotes_is_not_the_one_it_runs, "test/js-callgraph.test.ts").
finding(f_the_receiver_decides_and_the_blocker_was_the_wrong_one, defect).
recorded(f_the_receiver_decides_and_the_blocker_was_the_wrong_one, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_receiver_decides_and_the_blocker_was_the_wrong_one, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_receiver_decides_and_the_blocker_was_the_wrong_one, "AN ITEM WAITED ON A DEPENDENCY THAT COULD NEVER HAVE ANSWERED IT, and finding that out took one query. `w_destructuring_hides_a_call` held four control-flow cells - object_pattern, array_pattern, rest_element, spread_element - behind `w_env_api_surface`, on the ground that naming the receiver`s iterator is a standard-library question. MEASURED against the surface that item generated, with a positive control on the same query shape: `lib_member(P, K, R)` carries 130 rows over eight prototypes, `lib_member(array, \"join\", R)` answers, and `lib_member(P, \"iterator\", R)` is ZERO - as are `\"Symbol.iterator\"`, `\"[Symbol.iterator]\"` and `\"next\"`. The surface is generated from TypeScript`s declarations and keyed by NAME, and a well-known symbol is not a name. So the blocker was not unmet, it was WRONG, and `work_needs` was removed rather than ticked off. THE REAL BLOCKER WAS THE CORPUS, and it is the more useful half. Ten cases run against V8`s own stack: over a PLAIN array or a PLAIN object an array pattern, an array spread, a spread argument and an object spread run NO USER CODE AT ALL, while over a source owning a getter or a `[Symbol.iterator]` every one of them transfers - and V8 attributes both the iterator and its `next` to the ENCLOSING function, identically to `for-of`, which the same probe ran as its baseline. THE RECEIVER DECIDES, NOT THE SYNTAX. Every destructuring site in this corpus read `bench` or `kit`, both plain, so no execution could contradict `a_no_control_transfer` and the layer stood on a sentence for a week. What the item needed was a FIXTURE, not a library. AND THE MACHINERY WAS ALREADY STANDING: `accessor_of[flow]` has named getters since w_cf_accessor and `member_value[flow](Obj, \"iterator\", M)` has named user iterators since `for_of_iterates`, so the two new rules are `accessor_read` and `for_of_iterates` at five more nodes. THE GENERAL SHAPE, and this is the third item in three days to have it: a queued dependency records what the AUTHOR believed the hard part was. `w_prototype_of_a_value` -> `w_env_api_surface` was the real chain for `stdlib_member`, and this item copied it by analogy because the sentence `naming the callee needs the standard library` fits both. It does not: an accessor and a user iterator are user code with nodes in the program, and only a receiver from the standard library is out of reach. A dependency is a claim like any other and it is the one kind this repository was not yet in the habit of measuring before believing.").
addressed_by(f_the_receiver_decides_and_the_blocker_was_the_wrong_one, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").

finding(f_one_kind_two_mechanisms_and_the_field_is_what_tells_them_apart, insight).
recorded(f_one_kind_two_mechanisms_and_the_field_is_what_tells_them_apart, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_one_kind_two_mechanisms_and_the_field_is_what_tells_them_apart, doc).
finding_note(f_one_kind_two_mechanisms_and_the_field_is_what_tells_them_apart, "`spread_element` IS THE FIRST KIND IN `transfer_mechanism` TO CARRY TWO, and the table had no way to say it until it did. In `properties` a spread copies keys and runs the source`s GETTERS; in `elements` or `arguments` it exhausts the source`s ITERATOR. Those are two mechanisms by this layer`s own doctrine - the one that says four mechanisms in one bucket get one verdict between them - so the kind gets a row for each and two `handled` facts naming two rules. A reader forced to pick one would have written down whichever half they met first. THE RULE IS KEYED ON THE FIELD, NOT THE KIND, and the discriminating site was written before the rule: `{ ...counter }` spreads an ITERABLE into an OBJECT, and measured, it copies the symbol key and calls no iterator. Without that site a rule keyed on `spread_element` alone derives the same rows as the right one, because every other spread in the corpus is in one position or the other. The widening mutant - adding `spread_iterable_field(properties)` - is dead only because that line exists. THREE SURVIVORS, NAMED. (a) `guard_named[code](spread_element).` moves nothing today: the derived line for `iterator_call` already names that kind, so the hand-written row is insurance against a change no mutant can defend, and it stays with the redundancy asserted rather than assumed. (b) `transfer_mechanism(for_of_statement, iterator_call).` moves nothing either - the loop is `guard_named` through `guard_kind` and the mechanism is modelled by two other kinds - because it is a correction to a table READ BY PEOPLE: the loop has called `E[Symbol.iterator]()` since `for_of_iterates` landed and the table said `skip_arm` and nothing else. (c) `fn_node[code](M)` in `pattern_iterates` has no site, because nothing here puts a data property under the key `iterator`. AND THE CLOSED-VOCABULARY GATE CANNOT SEE A DELETED RULE on the iterable side: `guard_named` there is DERIVED from `transfer_mechanism(K, iterator_call)`, which a mutant deleting `pattern_iterates` leaves standing, so `guard_unmodelled[audit]` stays green while the rule is gone. Same structural hole CLAUDE.md records for `orphan_claim`, and the execution oracle is what catches it instead.").
addressed_by(f_one_kind_two_mechanisms_and_the_field_is_what_tells_them_apart, "test/js-destructuring-transfer.test.ts").

finding(f_the_cost_pin_measures_a_fixpoint_that_never_finishes, defect).
recorded(f_the_cost_pin_measures_a_fixpoint_that_never_finishes, "2026-09-08").
demands(f_the_cost_pin_measures_a_fixpoint_that_never_finishes, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_cost_pin_measures_a_fixpoint_that_never_finishes, "test/js-fixpoint-cost.test.ts IS THE FIFTH FILE WITH THE LOAD-ORDER DEFECT and the first whose whole subject is the number the defect corrupts. `cost()` asserts the AST facts first and then calls `r.load()` three times; `r.load` re-evaluates under its own DEFAULT_BUDGET, so the world ends with `hole($load(3), budget_exhausted)` and `calls_in[code](F, A, B)` comes back with ZERO rows and `partial` true. MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ON THE SAME DAY, one variable at a time: on an untouched checkout of 816f460 the hole is there and the store holds 204 783 facts and 70 625 firings; with a fixture appended it is still there, at 206 343 and 70 060. FIRINGS WENT DOWN WHILE FACTS WENT UP, which is the tell no reader would otherwise have: a bigger program cannot fire fewer rules, so the run is stopping somewhere different rather than concluding something different. AND THE HOLE IS STICKY. A subsequent `r.evaluate(20_000_000)` on the same store adds NOTHING - facts and firings identical to the digit - so the truncation cannot be repaired after the fact by a caller who notices; the budget has to be right at `load` time or the packs have to be loaded before the facts, which is what test/js-corpus-world.ts does and documents. WHAT THIS COSTS IS THE PIN`S MEANING. `rows handed out` fell 1 694 517 -> 1 227 159 (-27.6%) when SIX FUNCTIONS were appended to a fixture, and the six paths above five per cent became two. Read as written, that says appending code made the fixpoint a quarter cheaper. Read correctly, it says the truncation now lands in a different place. The `share` list is a rank-and-threshold set over a total that is not a total. IT IS ALSO THE GATE THE PREVIOUS FINDING ASKED FOR, arriving from the other end: f_a_load_order_defect_in_four_files_and_each_hid_until_something_grew says `a world that ends with a hole in it is a world whose answers are all suspect, and every one of these files could assert that in one line`. This file cannot be edited from a parallel branch, so the assertion is recorded here rather than written: one line asserting `hole(Q, Why)` is empty beside the existing determinism control, and the numbers re-taken on a world that finishes.").

finding(f_the_layer_no_gate_watched_is_half_the_cost_of_its_own_world, defect).
recorded(f_the_layer_no_gate_watched_is_half_the_cost_of_its_own_world, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_layer_no_gate_watched_is_half_the_cost_of_its_own_world, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_layer_no_gate_watched_is_half_the_cost_of_its_own_world, "THE FIRST RUN OF THE SECOND COST NUMBER FOUND THE SINGLE HEAVIEST READ PATH IN THE TREE, and it had been invisible for as long as the gate had existed. w_cost_gate_per_layer asked for a cost number for the control-flow layer because test/js-fixpoint-cost.test.ts does not load rules/js-controlflow.rofl. Measured by difference between two worlds that differ by that one pack, over the shared call corpus: the layer costs 1 814 160 of the 3 946 681 rows its world hands out - FORTY-SIX PER CENT - while adding 7 740 of its 150 885 derivations, five per cent. That is 234 rows walked per fact concluded against 14.9 for everything else in the same world, and a ratio like that is the signature of a scan rather than of a big program. NINETY-FOUR PER CENT OF IT IS ONE PATH. `argMatches ast_node pos=[1]` is a probe of the node table with the KIND bound and the node unbound - a scan over every node of a kind - and it hands out 1 706 704 rows on its own, 43% of the whole world. ONE RULE, NAMED BY ABLATION rather than by reading: each of the 28 clauses in rules/js-controlflow.rofl that mention `ast_node` was commented out in turn and this path re-measured. `has_return[code](F) :- fn_node[code](F), ast_node[code](R, return_statement, _, _), ast_within[code](F, R).` accounts for 1 703 150 of them; the next heaviest clause in the same layer is worth 780. It leads with the function and enumerates every return statement in the corpus against it, then filters by containment - the body shape f_the_engine_has_no_join_planner exists to warn about. THE REPAIR IS MEASURED AND ANSWER-PRESERVING: leading with the return statement takes the layer to 130 465 rows (-92.8%) and the world to 2 262 986 (-42.7%), with firings, facts and ten named relations identical row for row. It is left to w_has_return_is_a_join_over_the_whole_corpus rather than applied by the gate that found it, because an instrument that repairs its own subject has no baseline left to report. WHY IT SURVIVED IS THE GENERAL PART. Nothing was wrong with the answers, so no correctness test could see it; the only quantity that could was rows-handed-out, and the only file that measures rows-handed-out loads four packs of which this is not one. A cost defect can therefore live indefinitely in any pack the single cost gate does not load, and the owner`s standing rule that ITERATIONS MUST GET FASTER is enforceable only over the region some number covers. The remedy is not a bigger world - it is a number per layer, which is what this item delivered.").
addressed_by(f_the_layer_no_gate_watched_is_half_the_cost_of_its_own_world, "facts/worklist.rofl").

finding(f_a_cost_attributed_by_pack_cannot_see_the_pack_next_door, insight).
recorded(f_a_cost_attributed_by_pack_cannot_see_the_pack_next_door, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_cost_attributed_by_pack_cannot_see_the_pack_next_door, doc).
finding_note(f_a_cost_attributed_by_pack_cannot_see_the_pack_next_door, "ASKED OF THE NEW INSTRUMENT BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE - where is this check structurally unable to look - and the answer came back as a CONTROLLED PAIR rather than as an argument. A per-layer cost here is a DIFFERENCE between two worlds that differ by one pack, which is the only way to price a layer whose rules cannot stand alone. A difference therefore attributes by PACK and not by PURPOSE, and anything present in both worlds cancels out exactly. The same rule text - `cost_probe[audit](N, C) :- ast_node[code](N, return_statement, _, _), call_site[code](C, _).` - was appended to rules/js-controlflow.rofl and then, unchanged, to rules/js-dataflow.rofl, over the same corpus in the same minute. IN THE LAYER PACK: the layer goes 1 814 160 -> 1 999 484 rows and 7 740 -> 192 333 derivations, rows-per-derivation 234.39 -> 10.40, and the heaviest path`s share moves 94.08% -> 85.39%. ONE PACK AWAY: the world total moves by exactly the same amount, 3 946 681 -> 4 132 005 in BOTH cases, and every layer figure is identical to the baseline - the rows do not move by ONE, the derivations do not move, rows-per-derivation is equal to the digit, and all four read paths agree to three decimals. So a rule written FOR the control-flow layer but housed in another pack is invisible to the attribution half of the instrument, and a rule housed correctly is not. THIS IS THE SAME DEFECT THE ITEM WAS ABOUT, ONE TURN FURTHER IN. `an instrument`s world is part of its claim` said a gate must state which packs it loads; this says a gate that ATTRIBUTES must also state that its attribution is by file, because the reader will hear `the control-flow layer costs X` as a statement about a set of rules and it is a statement about a set of LINES IN ONE FILE. TWO THINGS FOLLOW AND BOTH ARE IN THE GATE. The world totals are pinned beside the differences, because the world sees what the difference cancels. And the SHARES are pinned beside both, because a share has the world in its denominator and therefore catches a neighbour`s growth even when the difference does not: the layer`s firing share fell 5.130% -> 2.307% for a rule it does not contain.").
dismissed(f_a_cost_attributed_by_pack_cannot_see_the_pack_next_door, "the cost gates were removed: every figure they pinned was a measurement of this repository, and a pin that moves with the corpus is measuring the corpus").
finding(f_the_one_place_a_key_is_named_was_four_more_places, defect).
recorded(f_the_one_place_a_key_is_named_was_four_more_places, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_one_place_a_key_is_named_was_four_more_places, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_one_place_a_key_is_named_was_four_more_places, "`key_name` WAS WRITTEN SO THE NAME A KEY STANDS FOR WOULD BE SAID ONCE, AND FOUR READERS NEVER CALLED IT. Found by asking where the guard w_computed_key_names installs is structurally unable to look, on the site that item added rather than by reading the rule again: with `key_name` narrowed and the computed-identifier site in place, the model still said `haspSpelling` in `obj_member_fn` twice, in `own_key` once and in `fn_name` once. All four read `ast_name` on the key directly - rules/js-callgraph.rofl at the object_property arm of `fn_name` and at both arms of `obj_member_fn`, rules/js-dataflow.rofl at `own_key` - so no guard written in `key_name` could reach them. The finding this repairs already said the behaviour predates the relation; what nobody measured is that writing the relation converted only the readers somebody happened to be editing. THE RESOLUTION HALF IS THE WORSE ONE. A naming rule that over-approximates gives a wrong label; `obj_member_fn` is TIER 2 of resolution, so `ns[someVar]()` resolved under whatever the variable is SPELT, to a method whose real name is whatever it HELD - a call edge no execution produces. AND `own_key` DISAGREED WITH ITS OWN TWIN: it is the negated half of `member_value`'s inheritance arm and `member_value` has read `key_name` since it was written, so a computed key was in the SHADOWING relation and not in the ANSWERING one - a subclass could hide an inherited method under a name no object ever has. Converting all four also closed a gap the other way: `member_value` named a `[Symbol.iterator]` method `iterator` and `obj_member_fn` could not resolve it, so the model held a member it could not reach. THE SHAPE TO CARRY FORWARD: writing one relation to replace N copies does not remove the copies, and a grep for the OLD premise - `ast_child(_, key, 0, K), ast_name(K, _)` - is the check that says whether it did. Five sites, one converted, four left, and every gate stayed green for two days.").
addressed_by(f_the_one_place_a_key_is_named_was_four_more_places, "test/js-computed-key-names.test.ts").

finding(f_a_relation_can_shrink_and_the_fixpoint_get_dearer, insight).
recorded(f_a_relation_can_shrink_and_the_fixpoint_get_dearer, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_relation_can_shrink_and_the_fixpoint_get_dearer, doc).
finding_note(f_a_relation_can_shrink_and_the_fixpoint_get_dearer, "w_computed_key_names PREDICTED THAT NARROWING `key_name` WOULD ANSWER THE NAMING QUESTION AND THE COST QUESTION AT ONCE, AND ON THE COST GATE'S OWN METRIC IT DID THE OPPOSITE. Measured 2026-09-09 in the world test/js-fixpoint-cost.test.ts builds, one edit at a time on a fixed corpus: HEAD 2 107 604 rows handed out / 140 644 firings / 348 819 facts; with `key_name` narrowed from 2 148 rows to 105 - a 95 per cent cut - 2 116 888 / 138 600 / 344 739. FEWER FIRINGS AND FEWER FACTS AND NINE THOUSAND MORE ROWS HANDED OUT. THE MECHANISM IS THE STRATUM, NOT THE SIZE. `key_name`'s first arm was `key_name(K, N) :- ast_name(K, N)`, an EDB copy that is complete before anything reads it. Narrowed, it is a genuinely derived relation, so every rule reading it moves into a later round and RE-RUNS ITS LEADING LITERAL each time the key relation grows - and those rules lead with `ast_node(O, object_expression, _, _)`, a kind scan. The single path `argMatches ast_node pos=[1]` carried +10 680 of it. LEADING THE BODIES WITH `key_name` INSTEAD TURNS IT ROUND: 2 094 572 rows, -13 032 against HEAD (-0.62%), with firings -1.45% and facts -1.17%, and the same relation contents to the row. That is the repository's own rule - the engine reads bodies left to right and has no join planner - applied to a literal that BECAME the small one. THE GENERAL FORM, AND IT IS NOT ABOUT KEYS: making a relation smaller changes its STRATUM as well as its cardinality, and the stratum is what the cost is in. So a narrowing is a two-step edit, and a branch that stops after the first will report a cost regression it caused by improving the model. THE COST GATE COUNTS ROWS HANDED OUT AND NOT FACTS DERIVED, deliberately, and this is the first measurement in this loop where the two point in opposite directions.").
addressed_by(f_a_relation_can_shrink_and_the_fixpoint_get_dearer, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").

finding(f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute, insight).
recorded(f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute, doc).
finding_note(f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute, "THE OBVIOUS SPELLING OF w_computed_key_names' GUARD IS `ast_attr(P, computed, false)` AND IT IS THE WRONG ONE, for a reason the corpus can state and no reading of the rule would. Measured 2026-09-09 over all 115 key positions in the five fixtures: SEVEN of them carry NO `computed` attribute of any value - five `class_private_property` and two `class_private_method` - because babel gives a private name no such field, a private name being one of the few things in the grammar that cannot be computed. A positive test matches nothing on those; the negation `not ast_attr(P, computed, true)` keeps them. IT MOVES NO ROW TODAY and that is the whole hazard: `key_name` has no arm for a `private_name`, so the two spellings agree on every row this corpus has, and a mutant that swaps them is a SURVIVOR - recorded as m6 in test/js-computed-key-names.test.ts with the item it waits on named. They stop agreeing the day `key_name` learns a private key, which rules/js-dataflow.rofl already contemplates in prose beside `private_key[code]`, and on that day the positive form would silently drop every private member from every relation that names a key. THE GENERAL FORM: an attribute test on a heterogeneous parent is a claim that every parent HAS the attribute, and the scanner's contract - every scalar own property, and only those - makes that claim false wherever the grammar omits a field. Ask of any `ast_attr(N, K, V)` guard: which kinds in this position carry no K at all? It is one query and it is not the query anybody runs.").
addressed_by(f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute, "rules/js-structure.rofl").

finding(f_a_store_wide_sweep_finds_the_prose_that_explains_it, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_store_wide_sweep_finds_the_prose_that_explains_it, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_store_wide_sweep_finds_the_prose_that_explains_it, doc).
finding_note(f_a_store_wide_sweep_finds_the_prose_that_explains_it, "THE STRONGEST ORACLE AVAILABLE FOR A NAMING DEFECT IS A SWEEP OVER THE WHOLE STORE - `derived_by` reflects every derived fact, so `no relation names anything haspSpelling` is one query over 152 825 rows and needs no list of relations somebody remembered to check - AND ITS FIRST ANSWER INCLUDED THE COMMENT EXPLAINING THE ITEM. babel hands the scanner `leadingComments` as nodes with a scalar `value`, the scanner's contract emits every scalar own property, and `ast_value[code](N, V) :- ast_attr(N, value, V)` therefore carries the prose of every comment in the corpus: 1 284 `comment_line` nodes in the five fixtures, and six `ast_value` rows quoting the very paragraph that names the defect. So a keyword-shaped sweep over a fixture cannot distinguish the model's opinion from the author's explanation of it. The remedy is one line - drop hits whose subject is a `comment_line` - and it is the ONLY filter the sweep needs, which is why this is worth recording rather than fixing quietly: the sweep is otherwise the best instrument this loop has found for a naming claim, and the next person to write one will meet the same six rows. NOTE THE SECOND ORDER EFFECT: fixture COMMENTS are in the model, so a rule that reads `ast_value` over the corpus is reading English. Nothing does today.").
addressed_by(f_a_store_wide_sweep_finds_the_prose_that_explains_it, "test/js-computed-key-names.test.ts").

addressed_by(f_the_guard_in_the_comment_is_not_the_guard_in_the_rule, "rules/js-structure.rofl").
finding(f_shadowing_stops_where_binds_name_stops, pitfall).
recorded(f_shadowing_stops_where_binds_name_stops, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_shadowing_stops_where_binds_name_stops, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_shadowing_stops_where_binds_name_stops, "NEAREST WINS LANDED FOR w_scope_shadowing AND IT IS BUILT OUT OF `binds_name`, which has rows for exactly four DECLARATOR forms - a plain declarator, an object pattern, an array pattern, a rest element - plus one arm for a PARAMETER. Measured on the corpus the same day: 359 names in these files are introduced by a form it has no row for at all - every function declaration, every class declaration, every catch parameter, every imported local, every for-of and for-in head - so an inner binder of one of those forms neither hides an outer one nor can be hidden by one. THE JOIN THAT WOULD WITNESS IT IS EMPTY: `unseen_collide`, a name bound by one of those forms colliding with a name `binds_name` does bind in the same file, is ZERO here, which puts the whole class in the WAITING ON A CORPUS bucket rather than the waiting-on-the-grammar one, and says exactly what a fixture would need - a `function f` nested inside a region where an outer `const f` is live, or a `catch (e)` under an outer `const e`. AND ONE HALF OF IT HAS A WITNESS ALREADY: `assigns[code]` is FILE-scoped by its own declared design and nearest-wins does not touch it, so a name that is ASSIGNED rather than initialised still carries its value to every use of that spelling in the file whatever binder is nearer. Measured, seven names are both assigned and bound in this corpus - i, j, s, total in alpha.mjs and m, s, total in shapes.ts - so the site exists and only a fixture where one of them is REBOUND in an inner region would make it produce a wrong edge. The direction is the same safe one the item closed in: an extra candidate in a may-set, never a missing one.").

finding(f_a_scope_kind_no_declarator_reaches, insight).
recorded(f_a_scope_kind_no_declarator_reaches, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_scope_kind_no_declarator_reaches, doc).
finding_note(f_a_scope_kind_no_declarator_reaches, "BLOCK REGIONS NEEDED A LIST OF SCOPE KINDS AND THE FIRST DRAFT HAD SIX; measured, exactly TWO are the nearest scope of a LEXICAL declarator in this corpus - `block_statement` 54 and `for_statement` 5, with one more binder scoped to a `function_declaration` because `var` keeps the function - and the other four are unkillable for THREE DIFFERENT reasons, which is the taxonomy w_prototype_of_a_value asked for (is a survivor waiting on a corpus, on the grammar, or on another item) made concrete inside one edb list. `catch_clause` and `switch_case` can never be the nearest scope of a declarator BY THE GRAMMAR: a catch always has a block body and a case body is scoped to the whole switch. `for_of_statement` and `for_in_statement` are unkillable BY THIS MODEL rather than by the grammar, and that is a different fact with a different remedy: a for-of head binds without an initialiser, so `binder` - whose third argument is the node the name evaluates to - has no row for it, the loop variable reaches the value layer through `iter_elem[flow]` instead, and only a DESTRUCTURING for-of head would be a `scoped_binder` at all. `switch_statement` and `static_block` are unkillable BY THIS CORPUS: no declaration sits directly in either. Only the third bucket is closed by writing a fixture; the second is closed by giving for-of heads a binder relation, and the first is closed by nothing and should simply not be written. AND THE RULE GENERALISES TO THE KINDS THAT STAYED: `for_statement` was on the list with four regions and NO fixture that could tell whether it was there, so its mutant survived - a kind can be exercised as a REGION and unexercised as a SCOPE BOUNDARY, which are two different claims. One `for (let x ...)` inside a function with an outer `const x` killed it. The same question should be asked of every kind on any such list: not is it reached, but does anything change when it is removed. THE COST OF LEAVING A KIND OFF IS A WIDER REGION, which is the same safe direction as the rest of the item, so the honest model is the short list plus this note rather than a long list with four arms no mutant of any corpus can reach.").

finding(f_the_dead_zone_is_a_region_and_one_line, insight).
recorded(f_the_dead_zone_is_a_region_and_one_line, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_dead_zone_is_a_region_and_one_line, doc).
finding_note(f_the_dead_zone_is_a_region_and_one_line, "THE TEMPORAL DEAD ZONE LANDED WITHOUT MAKING THIS LAYER FLOW-SENSITIVE, and the reason generalises. The HIDING is regional: a `let x` hides an outer `x` over the whole of its block, above its own declaration included, so the nearest-wins rule already covers the dead zone and nothing positional is needed for it. What is left is only that the inner binder has no VALUE yet, and that is one line comparison plus one STRUCTURAL guard. The structural guard is what makes it sound rather than merely narrow: `const via = () => later; const later = f;` reads `later` on an earlier line and is perfectly legal, because the arrow body runs afterwards, so a use with a FUNCTION between it and the binder`s region is deferred and keeps its value. Dropping that guard LOSES a real edge instead of adding a false one - the only mutant in this item`s set that fails in the unsafe direction - and its fixture is a closure the runtime oracle actually runs. THE TWO ARMS COMPOSE INTO A STATEMENT NEITHER MAKES ALONE: at a dead-zone read the model derives NOTHING - not the outer value, which shadowing removed, and not the inner, which the dead zone removed - and nothing is the right answer, because the line throws. A model that had only one of the two arms would have answered with the other binder and looked reasonable.").

finding(f_a_budget_kill_decays_into_a_green_gate_that_says_nothing, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_budget_kill_decays_into_a_green_gate_that_says_nothing, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_budget_kill_decays_into_a_green_gate_that_says_nothing, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_budget_kill_decays_into_a_green_gate_that_says_nothing, "MUTANT 9 IN test/js-callgraph.test.ts HAS NOW LOST ITS ORACLE TWICE TO THE SAME MECHANISM, and the second time is the one worth recording. The mutant deletes `ast_within(F, U)` from `param_use` - a parameter read from anywhere instead of from inside its own function - which is quadratic in the corpus BY DESIGN. On 2026-09-08 three parallel branches tripled the corpus and `calls_in` stopped fitting 400M steps, so the oracle moved one join back to `param_use`, with the file writing down the reason: a partial answer that CONTAINS an invented row proves the mutant invents it, one that LACKS a baseline row proves nothing. On 2026-09-09 nine functions from w_scope_shadowing pushed `param_use` over the same wall - and this time the truncated answer is a strict SUBSET of the baseline, holding not one of the rows the mutation exists to invent. Both halves of the claim are gone. ASKING A SMALLER QUESTION DOES NOT RECOVER IT: the truncation is in the WORLD - the store is built with a fixed `r.evaluate` budget and every query out of it is then partial - so binding the parameter name to `f` and asking for four rows fails the same way. THE TRANSFERABLE PART IS THE SHAPE OF THE DECAY. A budget assertion keeps passing while the claim behind it evaporates, because `partial === true` is still true and still red-if-removed: the gate is GREEN and says almost nothing, which is exactly the failure CLAUDE.md names for the NUL check and the `# tests` grep, arriving through a resource limit rather than through a borrowed criterion. And it decays MONOTONICALLY - every fixture anyone adds moves it further - so it is not a defect that can be fixed once. The assertions there now pin the truncation itself, including an EMPTY set of invented rows, so that the loss is visible in the file and goes red the day the budget is raised. WHAT THIS DEMANDS is a decision the owner holds: either the mutant world gets its own stated budget (the wall is `buildFresh`s `r.evaluate(20_000_000)`, shared by every world in the file, and mutant 9 is the only one that needs more), or a mutant whose whole point is to explode stops being measured by a relation it explodes.").
finding(f_the_callee_name_oracle_cannot_see_the_region_a_closure_derives, insight).
recorded(f_the_callee_name_oracle_cannot_see_the_region_a_closure_derives, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_callee_name_oracle_cannot_see_the_region_a_closure_derives, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_callee_name_oracle_cannot_see_the_region_a_closure_derives, "EVERY ASSERTION ABOUT STATEMENT ORDER IN test/js-controlflow.test.ts READS A STATEMENT BACK THROUGH THE NAME OF A FUNCTION CALLED AFTER IT - `afterAbrupt` walks `after_abrupt` to `resolves` to `fn_name` - and the conversion that made it a name instead of a count is recorded as a win. It is, and it has a hole with a MEASURED SIZE. On the corpus of 2026-09-09 the completion closure derives 789 `completes_abruptly` rows and 795 `abrupt_at` rows, of which TWENTY-SEVEN reach `after_abrupt` at all: a row whose node is LAST in its statement list has no successor, and a successor with no call in it has no callee to name. So about 96 per cent of what the closure concludes is invisible to the only oracle the layer had, and being right about that region is not a thing any assertion could have said. THE MUTANT THAT PROVES IT rather than argues it: c13 replaces `ast_child(LS, body, 0, S)` with `ast_within(LS, S)` in the labelled arm, so ANY descendant`s abrupt completion is lifted to the label. `afterAbrupt` does not move a single row. Two more labelled statements start completing abruptly - `louter` and `unused`, both wrapping loops with returns further down - and neither has a call after it in its own list, so no name exists to change. WHAT CLOSES IT HERE IS A SECOND ORACLE OVER A DIFFERENT NAME. A labelled statement carries a NAME IN THE SOURCE, so `completes_abruptly` can be read back through `label_name` with no call anywhere in the picture, and `['wrapUp']` is a set of exactly the labels this corpus says complete abruptly - one of nine. That kills c13 and nothing else does. The general form is worth carrying: WHEN A RELATION IS READ BACK THROUGH A CONSUMER, THE ORACLE`S REACH IS THE CONSUMER`S REACH AND NOT THE RELATION`S, and the ratio is measurable in one query. Ask what ELSE in the language carries a name - a label, a binding, a key - before accepting that a relation over anonymous nodes cannot be named. AND THE SAME QUESTION FOUND A SURVIVOR THAT WAS A MISSING WITNESS: c15 narrows the block arm to the block`s LAST statement, which is still sound and strictly weaker, and it survived the whole fixture because every nested block written for the item ended with its own `return`. One site - a return followed inside the block by a call - killed it.").
addressed_by(f_the_callee_name_oracle_cannot_see_the_region_a_closure_derives, "test/js-controlflow.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_callee_name_oracle_cannot_see_the_region_a_closure_derives, "test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt").

finding(f_a_derived_pair_costs_what_its_first_unbound_literal_costs, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_derived_pair_costs_what_its_first_unbound_literal_costs, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_derived_pair_costs_what_its_first_unbound_literal_costs, doc).
finding_note(f_a_derived_pair_costs_what_its_first_unbound_literal_costs, "ONE RULE OF THE COMPLETION CLOSURE COST THIRTEEN SECONDS OF A TWENTY-TWO SECOND WORLD, and it was written under this file`s own `ORDERED FOR COST` discipline and still got it wrong. `completion_fn_between(P, S)` answers `is there a function between this container and this abrupt completion`, and the first draft read `completes_abruptly(S), fn_node_v(G), ast_within(G, S), ast_within(P, G)` - seeded from a small relation, exactly as the comment beside it claimed, and with `G` UNBOUND in the second literal. MEASURED by deleting rules one at a time from one world: as written 21.7 s, with the gate removed 8.8 s, with only the audit head removed and this rule kept 23.0 s. The thirteen seconds are this rule alone, and the last of those three numbers is what identifies it - removing the CONSUMER made it slower, because a bottom-up engine materialises the pair whether or not anybody reads it. THE FIX IS NOT LITERAL ORDER, IT IS WHICH RELATION YOU SEED FROM. The audit only ever asks about containers whose KIND the completion table does not know, so a `completion_outer(P, S)` carrying exactly those - a few hundred rows instead of every function crossed with every abrupt completion - and a pair rule seeded from IT gives the identical answer at 8.4 s. TWO LESSONS AND THE SECOND IS THE GENERAL ONE. (1) `seeded from a small relation` is not a property of the FIRST literal, it is a property of every literal that introduces a variable: the second literal here re-opened the search over the whole corpus and the phrase in the comment was true and irrelevant. (2) In a bottom-up engine a derived relation has no consumers in the cost sense - it is computed in full - so the way to make a helper cheap is to make the helper SMALLER, by moving the consumer`s filters into its own body, rather than to order the consumer`s literals well. The rules of this layer that are cheap - `throwing_call`, `accessor_of`, `label_target` - are all small BY THEIR HEAD rather than by their body order, which nobody had said out loud.").
addressed_by(f_a_derived_pair_costs_what_its_first_unbound_literal_costs, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").


finding(f_five_reorders_one_principle_and_the_outcomes_span_a_hundred_and_twenty_points, insight).
recorded(f_five_reorders_one_principle_and_the_outcomes_span_a_hundred_and_twenty_points, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_five_reorders_one_principle_and_the_outcomes_span_a_hundred_and_twenty_points, doc).
finding_note(f_five_reorders_one_principle_and_the_outcomes_span_a_hundred_and_twenty_points, "FIVE BODIES WERE REORDERED IN ONE NIGHT ON THE SAME PRINCIPLE - lead with the literal that binds - ACROSS TWO INDEPENDENT SESSIONS, AND THE MEASURED OUTCOMES SPAN 120 PERCENTAGE POINTS. `param_hidden` -56.5% of its world; `has_return` -23.3%; `encloses_s` -11.5%; `tdz_deferred` 0.02%, which is noise; and `closer_v`, in the other session`s tree, SIXTY-FIVE PER CENT WORSE and reverted. Every one of the five was argued the same way beforehand and four of the five arguments were made by someone who had just measured a success with it. THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THIS A FINDING RATHER THAN A LIST: `tdz_deferred` carries the IDENTICAL defect signature to `param_hidden` - an unbound `fn_node_v(G)` in a non-leading position - and sits four hundred lines away IN THE SAME FILE, reading the same relation. It is worth nothing. So the signature does not merely fail to predict the SIZE of a win, it fails to predict whether there is one at all. SO THE SIGNATURE NAMES A CANDIDATE, NEVER A CAUSE, and that sentence is now written into both repaired rules rather than into a document nobody reads at the moment of editing. WHY IT CANNOT BE FIXED BY THINKING HARDER, which is the part that generalises: what a body costs depends on the SIZE of the relation each literal opens at the moment it is reached, and that is a property of the corpus and of the stratum, not of the text. `f_a_relation_can_shrink_and_the_fixpoint_get_dearer` measured the same thing from the other end - narrowing `key_name` by 95 per cent made the fixpoint DEARER, because the relation changed stratum and its readers re-ran a kind scan every round. Two findings, opposite directions, same cause: the text is not the cost. THE PRACTICAL RULE, and it is cheap: a reorder is a HYPOTHESIS and costs one run to test. Run it, keep it only if the number moves, and pin the equivalence control - firings and facts identical, plus a named set of the relations that depend on it - beside the saving. Three of tonight`s five were kept on that basis and two were discarded by it, which is the correct hit rate for a heuristic that is being MEASURED rather than believed. AND IT IS THE ARGUMENT FOR w_join_planner AND THE HARDEST CONSTRAINT ON IT AT ONCE: a human cannot pick these, so a planner is worth having - and a planner that ordered by boundness alone would have made `closer_v` sixty-five per cent worse, so the planner must measure too, or must be provably safe in a way this heuristic is not.").
addressed_by(f_five_reorders_one_principle_and_the_outcomes_span_a_hundred_and_twenty_points, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").
addressed_by(f_five_reorders_one_principle_and_the_outcomes_span_a_hundred_and_twenty_points, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").


finding(f_one_letter_apart_same_body_two_hundred_times_the_cost, insight).
recorded(f_one_letter_apart_same_body_two_hundred_times_the_cost, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_one_letter_apart_same_body_two_hundred_times_the_cost, doc).
finding_note(f_one_letter_apart_same_body_two_hundred_times_the_cost, "THE CLEANEST CASE YET THAT A BODY`S SHAPE DOES NOT PRICE IT, and it arrived as a PRICED OFFER from another session rather than as a search of mine. `closer_v[flow](F, X) :- encloses_v(F, X), encloses_v(G, X), ast_within(F, G), F != G.` and `closer_s[code](R, D) :- encloses_s(R, D), encloses_s(S, D), ast_within(R, S), R != S.` are the same rule one letter apart, written by the same hand on the same day, each pairing a thing`s enclosing scopes against each other. Both are quadratic in nesting depth per subject, both join on the shared variable, and NO PREMISE REORDER FIXES EITHER - the other session measured a reorder of `closer_v` at SIXTY-FIVE PER CENT WORSE and then deleted the relation outright. MEASURED HERE over the five js-call fixtures, world total 3 244 319 rows handed out: the `_v` pair costs 125 037 + 121 464 = 7.5 PER CENT, and the `_s` pair costs 4 452 + 2 143 = 0.2 PER CENT with `closer_s` below the reporting floor entirely. TWO HUNDRED TIMES APART, AND THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE IS THE SEED. `encloses_v` ranges over every function crossed with every node it contains; `encloses_s` ranges over 116 `scoped_binder` rows. The quadratic is real in both and only one of them has anything to be quadratic over. SO THE DECISION WENT THE OTHER WAY FROM THE ARGUMENT: the offer to rewrite `_s` the way `_v` was rewritten is correct about the shape and was DECLINED on the number, with the condition that flips it written down instead of remembered - `scoped_binder` is 116 on this corpus and will be thousands on eslint/lib, where the same rule becomes worth removing. THIS IS THE THIRD INSTANCE OF f_five_reorders_one_principle... AND THE HARDEST. The first two were the same signature at different costs in the same file; this is the same signature at different costs one letter apart in the same family. A static planner looking at these two bodies sees identical text and cannot tell 0.2% from 7.5%, which is the constraint w_join_planner has to satisfy and cannot satisfy by reading rules.").
addressed_by(f_one_letter_apart_same_body_two_hundred_times_the_cost, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").
-- w_vocabulary_home, 2026-09-09
finding(f_the_pack_named_for_the_vocabulary_is_its_base_and_not_its_home, insight).
recorded(f_the_pack_named_for_the_vocabulary_is_its_base_and_not_its_home, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_pack_named_for_the_vocabulary_is_its_base_and_not_its_home, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_pack_named_for_the_vocabulary_is_its_base_and_not_its_home, "w_vocabulary_home READS AS TIDYING AND ITS PREMISE IS AN INFERENCE FROM A FILE NAME. Measured 2026-09-09 before moving a row: 100 js kinds, 274 `node_kind` rows, SEVEN packs rather than the five the item names, and facts/js-kinds.rofl declares 32 of the 100 - the minority is real. What the arithmetic does not say is what that file IS, and its own first line does: *a DELIBERATELY TINY sample, enough to test the mechanism and not one node more*. It is the fixture test/js-model.test.ts pins number for number, plus the twenty-three kinds declared on 2026-09-08 so the denominator would be the LANGUAGE. It was never the vocabulary's home; it is the BASE every world in this suite loads, which is a different job that happens to share a name. THE RULE THE TREE ALREADY OBEYS, and nothing had ever stated it: a `node_kind(js, K)` row belongs in the pack of layer P iff P SPEAKS ABOUT K - some fact P asserts carries K in a position that holds kinds - or P is the base pack. Measured over all seven packs: ZERO violations, and 24 rows fire the instant the base clause is dropped. The rows are already where the rule says they belong, so this item is a rule to write and not a move to make. AND THE COPIES ARE NOT WASTE, THEY ARE WHAT THE PARTIAL WORLDS COST. Executing the item's own proposal on a copy of the text - every `node_kind(js, ...)` row into facts/js-kinds.rofl, nothing else moved - and rebuilding the six distinct fact-pack worlds this suite builds: the mechanism fixture goes 32 -> 100 kinds, the call-graph fixpoint 84 -> 100, the shape axis 86 -> 100, the module boundary 86 -> 100, the era scale 86 -> 100. EVERY PARTIAL WORLD BECOMES THE FULL ONE and the suite stops distinguishing six worlds at all, which is the opposite of what a vocabulary's home was supposed to buy. The 68 rows the gather adds to the base pack are 68 rows that pack judges NONE of - exactly the defect the rule forbids, committed wholesale in the name of the file's name.").
addressed_by(f_the_pack_named_for_the_vocabulary_is_its_base_and_not_its_home, "rules/js-pack-home.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_pack_named_for_the_vocabulary_is_its_base_and_not_its_home, "test/js-pack-home.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_kind_vocabulary_is_scattered_across_the_packs_that_needed_it, "rules/js-pack-home.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_kind_vocabulary_is_scattered_across_the_packs_that_needed_it, "test/js-pack-home.test.ts").

finding(f_a_rule_has_no_file_and_a_fact_does, defect).
recorded(f_a_rule_has_no_file_and_a_fact_does, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_rule_has_no_file_and_a_fact_does, doc).
finding_note(f_a_rule_has_no_file_and_a_fact_does, "AN AUDIT ABOUT WHERE A ROW LIVES NEEDS TO KNOW WHICH FILE IT CAME FROM, AND THIS KERNEL ANSWERS THAT FOR HALF OF A PROGRAM. Every asserted fact carries `asserted_by($fact(Rel, Persp, Args), Who, Tick)`, so a loader that gives each pack its own `who` can ask which pack said what - that is what rules/js-pack-home.rofl does, and the finest grain available is the PRINCIPAL rather than the file, which boot.rofl already states where `loader/3` is defined. A RULE CARRIES NOTHING OF THE KIND. `premise_lit(RuleId, K, Lit)`, `concludes`, `premise_pos` and the rest are written by the kernel about the program as a whole; no relation joins a rule id to the principal that loaded it. THE CONSEQUENCE IS EXACT AND IT SHAPED THE AUDIT. `rules/js-vocabulary.rofl` can ask WHICH KINDS THE RULES NAME and cannot ask WHICH RULE PACK NAMES THEM, so a check of the form *this pack declares a kind only its own rules read* is unwritable, and the first draft of js-pack-home fell back on a global `model_speaks_of(K)` that turned out to hold for all 100 declared kinds - a premise true of everything is a gate that cannot fire, and it was measured empty for exactly that reason rather than for a clean tree. THE WAY ROUND IT IS NOT A FIX, IT IS A CHOICE OF GRAIN: load facts/js-X.rofl and rules/js-X.rofl under ONE principal and audit the LAYER instead of the file. That works because the rule packs assert kind tables of their own - `callee_shape`, `value_transparent`, `obj_kind_class` and twenty-three more - and those are facts, so they are attributable. It is what makes `node_kind(js, tsas_expression)` legitimate in facts/js-callgraph.rofl, which carries no verdict about that kind at all: its layer's rules read it as a callee shape and as a cast. The price is stated in the file: a row in the wrong FILE of the right LAYER is invisible. AND ONE RESIDUAL FALSE-POSITIVE RISK, MEASURED AT ZERO: a kind a layer's rules only MATCH on, and that no row that layer asserts mentions, would be reported misplaced. There are none today in any of the six layer packs - every kind a layer declares stands in some row that layer asserts - and if one arrives the honest fix is a fact table naming it, which is what the model already does everywhere.").
addressed_by(f_a_rule_has_no_file_and_a_fact_does, "rules/js-pack-home.rofl").

finding(f_the_duplication_that_makes_a_partial_world_possible_blinds_the_full_one, insight).
recorded(f_the_duplication_that_makes_a_partial_world_possible_blinds_the_full_one, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_duplication_that_makes_a_partial_world_possible_blinds_the_full_one, doc).
finding_note(f_the_duplication_that_makes_a_partial_world_possible_blinds_the_full_one, "THE DELETION MUTANT WAS BRIEFED AS A KILL AND IT SURVIVES IN THE WORLD MOST TESTS BUILD. Take `node_kind(js, identifier)` out of facts/js-callgraph.rofl - the pack that carries `handled(js, identifier, callgraph, r_call_edge)` - and in the two-pack call-graph world `orphan_claim[audit]` names it at once and `rule_opinion_unlisted[audit]` names it from the other side: two independent detectors, both alive. In the seven-pack corpus world NOTHING MOVES. `orphan_claim` is empty, `kind_home_unbacked` is empty, and `node_kind(js, identifier)` still holds - because js-dataflow, js-modules and js-callgraph all declare it and removing one copy of four changes no derived row anywhere. THE MECHANISM IS THE DUPLICATION ITSELF, and this is the first time it has been priced from the losing side. 274 rows for 100 kinds is what lets a test build a world out of two packs; the same 174 copies mean that in the full world a `node_kind` row is a MAJORITY VOTE rather than a declaration, and a majority survives losing one member. So the vocabulary's redundancy is a fault-tolerance property nobody asked for: it makes the full world unable to notice a deletion that every partial world catches. WHERE THIS BITES is a reviewer's instinct that the biggest world is the strictest one. It is the strictest about ORPHANS - a verdict with no kind - and the weakest about ABSENCE, and the two are the same audit read in opposite directions. Ask of any redundant table which of its readers is looking at a UNION, because that reader cannot see a row leave.").
addressed_by(f_the_duplication_that_makes_a_partial_world_possible_blinds_the_full_one, "test/js-pack-home.test.ts").
-- ============ w_note_is_not_evidence, 2026-09-09 ============
-- A witness names its world now. These six are what naming it FOUND, in the
-- order they were measured, and two of them are about the instrument rather
-- than about the ledger.

finding(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, defect).
recorded(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, "w_note_is_not_evidence's OWN NOTE NAMED THE WRONG MISSING WORLD, and the first query of the item found it. The note says a note's witness `needs a checker world that has the corpus`, citing f_a_witness_has_no_world. Measured before anything was built, in the checker's own world: `work(W, Note)` comes back UNPOPULATABLE - not zero rows, but nothing in that store can ever put a row under that name - because scripts/witness_check.ts loaded boot.rofl plus facts/findings.rofl plus rules/findings.rofl and THE PLAN IS NOT ONE OF THEM. So a work item's note could not have carried a witness even about the ledger it lives in, let alone about the corpus. The corpus was the SECOND missing world. THIS IS THE ITEM'S OWN SUBJECT ARRIVING IN THE ITEM'S OWN NOTE, which is the seventh consecutive instance of the pattern the item exists for and the first one caught by the mechanism instead of by a probe written from the note. It is also the exact asymmetry the parent finding names: a note claiming the model LACKS something is confirmed by every probe that agrees with it, and `it needs the corpus` is TRUE - it does, for three of the four witnesses this item wrote - which is why it read as a complete diagnosis for two days. A true half-diagnosis is worse than a false one, because nothing about it looks wrong. THE REPAIR IS THAT THE WORLD LIST IS NOW A FACT: facts/worlds.rofl declares w_ledger, w_queue and w_js_corpus, each with the packs it loads AND the packs it refuses, and test/note-witness.test.ts asserts the two lists cover the tree - so the next world that is missing is missing OUT LOUD.").
addressed_by(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, "facts/worlds.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, "rules/worlds.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, "scripts/witness_check.ts").
witness_in_atleast(f_the_plan_was_the_missing_world_before_the_corpus_was, w_queue, "note_witnessed(W)", 1).

finding(f_the_witness_that_could_not_flip_was_held_green_by_the_edb_that_made_it_possible, defect).
recorded(f_the_witness_that_could_not_flip_was_held_green_by_the_edb_that_made_it_possible, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_witness_that_could_not_flip_was_held_green_by_the_edb_that_made_it_possible, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_witness_that_could_not_flip_was_held_green_by_the_edb_that_made_it_possible, "A LIVE DECORATION IN THIS LEDGER, EIGHT DAYS OLD, AND ITS AUTHOR PREDICTED IT EXACTLY. `witness(f_the_matrix_collapses_threefold_and_the_tail_does_not, layer(L), 0)` stands for the sentence THE PROGRAMME HAS NOT STARTED: NO SEMANTIC LAYER IS DECLARED YET, and the comment beside it ends `the day the first layer is declared, this flips`. Four layers were declared over the following week and it never flipped: scripts/witness_check.ts reported `ok layer(L) -> 0 (want 0)` every time anybody ran it. THE MECHANISM IS IN THE COMMENT ITSELF, one line above the prediction. `edb(layer)` was added TO THE FINDINGS LEDGER so that the query would be populatable in the checker's one world at all - otherwise the relation does not exist there and the guard correctly refuses to read an empty answer as satisfied. That declaration is what made the witness writable AND what made it unfalsifiable: it created an empty `layer` in a store that has no layers and never will, so the checker was asking a world with no layers in it whether it had any layers. Both readings sit in the same comment and nobody, me included, read the second one. THE GENERAL FORM IS WORTH MORE THAN THE INSTANCE. A gate whose subject is not in its world fails in the SAFE direction - green, silent, and saying nothing - which is the failure CLAUDE.md already records for the NUL check and the `# tests` grep, arriving here through a WORLD rather than through a borrowed criterion. And the tell was legible from the day it was written: A WITNESS WHOSE QUERY NEEDS AN `edb` LINE ADDED TO THE LEDGER TO BE ASKABLE IS A WITNESS ABOUT SOMETHING THE LEDGER DOES NOT HOLD. THE REPAIR: `witness_in(F, w_queue, layer(L), 4)` - asked where layers exist - and `edb(layer)` deleted from this file with it, so that a witness naming `layer(L)` in the ledger world is now BROKEN rather than green-at-zero. Four is not a number two branches can both move: `layer(L)` is declared by the owner alone and an agent declaring one is forbidden by a standing constraint, so if he declares a fifth this goes red and somebody looks, which is what the 2026-09-01 comment asked for.").
addressed_by(f_the_witness_that_could_not_flip_was_held_green_by_the_edb_that_made_it_possible, "facts/findings.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_witness_that_could_not_flip_was_held_green_by_the_edb_that_made_it_possible, "test/note-witness.test.ts").
-- 4 -> 5 ON 2026-09-09, AND THE GUARD FIRED EXACTLY AS ITS AUTHOR PREDICTED.
-- The comment beside this row read: "Four is not a number two branches can both
-- move: `layer(L)` is declared by the owner alone and an agent declaring one is
-- forbidden by a standing constraint, so if he declares a fifth this goes red
-- and somebody looks." He declared `layer(effect)` and it went red, in the same
-- run as the commit that answered its hundred cells. Restated on purpose, which
-- is the ritual working rather than a number being maintained.
witness_in(f_the_witness_that_could_not_flip_was_held_green_by_the_edb_that_made_it_possible, w_queue, "layer(L)", 5).

finding(f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant, defect).
recorded(f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant, "THE WITNESS GATE'S OWN GUARD HAS A HOLE THE SHAPE OF EVERY ABSENCE CLAIM, and it was found by writing the first one. `query()` reports `unpopulatable` for a misspelt relation, a wrong arity and a wrong ledger, and CLAUDE.md records that replacing the hand-written copy with it was a strict widening. It is blind to a misspelt or mis-QUOTED CONSTANT, which is the only moving part an absence witness has. MEASURED IN THE CORPUS WORLD, AND THE TWO RELATIONS WANT OPPOSITE FORMS: `ast_name[code](N, twin)` written as a STRING returns 5 and written as an ATOM returns 0; `ast_node[code](N, if_statement, F, L)` written as an ATOM returns 16 and written as a STRING returns 0. Neither wrong one errors, neither is unpopulatable, and there is no rule of thumb to remember - names are strings and kinds are atoms, in a model whose whole subject is syntax. So a witness wanting ZERO reads as SATISFIED, which is precisely the defect scripts/witness_check.ts exists to catch, arriving one level further down than the version it already fixed. THE REMEDY IS A CONTROL, AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THE CONTROL WAS ALSO WRONG - which is the part worth carrying. Requiring `some literal of the same shape returns rows` catches a world that cannot answer and does NOT catch this: a control on a different constant of the same relation stays live while the claim's constant is malformed, and both halves read green. Measured as MUTANT 2, which is why the design changed rather than being confirmed. The control must be THE CLAIM WITH ONE CONSTANT SWAPPED - token for token identical except at one position, quoted there if and only if the claim is - and then a mis-quoted constant cannot have a control at all. WHAT STILL SURVIVES, stated rather than discovered later: a constant MISSPELT in the right form is a well-formed question about a real relation, and no checker that cannot read the prose can tell it from the question the note meant. A witness buys `somebody ran something and wrote down what it returned`, not `the note is true`.").
addressed_by(f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant, "scripts/witness_check.ts").
addressed_by(f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant, "test/note-witness.test.ts").
-- THE PREMISE IS A CONTRAST AND ONLY HALF OF IT IS WITNESSABLE, which is the
-- finding restating itself. `ast_node[code](N, if_statement, F, L) >= 1` says
-- the atom form is live in this world; the other half - that the QUOTED form
-- answers 0 - cannot be a `witness_absent`, because its control would have to
-- be a second quoted kind and would answer 0 too. A false zero has no live
-- control by construction. test/note-witness.test.ts asserts both halves,
-- which is what a test can do and a witness cannot.
witness_in_atleast(f_unpopulatable_sees_the_relation_and_is_blind_to_the_constant, w_js_corpus, "ast_node[code](N, if_statement, F, L)", 1).

finding(f_the_gate_read_one_of_the_witness_forms_and_had_no_instance_to_show_it, defect).
recorded(f_the_gate_read_one_of_the_witness_forms_and_had_no_instance_to_show_it, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_gate_read_one_of_the_witness_forms_and_had_no_instance_to_show_it, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_gate_read_one_of_the_witness_forms_and_had_no_instance_to_show_it, "`unproven(F) :- finding_action(F, rule_and_test), not witness(F, _, _)` READ ONE OF THE TWO WITNESS FORMS THAT EXISTED WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN. A finding carrying only `witness_atleast` - a premise somebody had run and written down, in the form the checker judges with a floor rather than an equality - was refused by the queue as unproven. ZERO INSTANCES ON THE HONEST TREE, and that is the whole reason for recording it: both `witness_atleast` rows in this ledger sit on SETTLED findings, so `finding_action` never reaches them, the hole never fired, and nothing that exists here would ever have said it was there. It is CLAUDE.md's `orphan_claim[audit]` incident verbatim, one book over - a check written by hand for the relations that existed, silently reopened by the next relation over the same arguments - and the third addition of a witness form would have reopened it again. THE REMEDY IS THE ONE THAT FILE NAMES: derive it once. `proven(F)` has a clause per form and `unproven` reads `not proven(F)`, so the three forms added today - `witness_in/4`, `witness_in_atleast/4`, `witness_absent/4` - cost one clause each and INHERIT the gate rather than copying it. Measured both ways in test/note-witness.test.ts: a planted finding with only a floor witness is not unproven under the rule as it now stands, and IS unproven under the rule as it stood, with the old text restored as the mutant.").
addressed_by(f_the_gate_read_one_of_the_witness_forms_and_had_no_instance_to_show_it, "rules/findings.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_gate_read_one_of_the_witness_forms_and_had_no_instance_to_show_it, "test/note-witness.test.ts").
witness(f_the_gate_read_one_of_the_witness_forms_and_had_no_instance_to_show_it, "unproven_misaimed[audit](F)", 0).

finding(f_a_count_over_the_ledger_is_a_reading_and_a_floor_is_a_pin, decision).
recorded(f_a_count_over_the_ledger_is_a_reading_and_a_floor_is_a_pin, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_count_over_the_ledger_is_a_reading_and_a_floor_is_a_pin, doc).
finding_note(f_a_count_over_the_ledger_is_a_reading_and_a_floor_is_a_pin, "`witness(f_the_queue_now_refuses_unproven_work, unproven(F), 32)` IS DELETED AND NOT REPLACED BY A BETTER NUMBER. It moved three times in one night across four branches - 32 to 31 by settling a rule_and_test finding, 32 to 31 again on a different branch for a different finding, 31 to 30 when those two merged, 30 to 32 when a third branch recorded two unwitnessed findings - every move correct, and the merge of the two identical 31s auto-resolved in SILENCE because git had two equal numbers and no way to know they meant different things. HANDOFF already lists `a COUNT two branches both move` first among the ways a named set is not one; what this instance adds is that the worst case is not a conflict, it is the ABSENCE of one. THE OBVIOUS REMEDY WAS CONSIDERED AND REFUSED. HANDOFF prefers a named set because a set merges as a union, and `unproven` as thirty-two typed finding ids would indeed merge - but it would still MOVE on every branch, and CLAUDE.md's own conversion list puts an IDENTITY first for exactly that reason: `reason[audit] === f.not_modelled, not 73`. A set that merges correctly is better than a count that does not; a claim that does not move at all is better than both. WHAT THE COUNT WAS STANDING IN FOR, in three rows that are the same on every branch. AIM: `unproven_misaimed[audit]` is EMPTY - the gate refuses only findings that demand CODE, and one demanding a doc or a decision passes untouched. That is the sentence in the finding's own note, as a row, and it is not a tautology of the two clauses: it goes red the moment a third arm is added over a different `demands` value, which is the only way the aim can drift. NOT BLIND: `unproven(F) >= 1`. NOT PERMANENTLY RED: `ready_proven(F) >= 1`. Those two are the note's own `NO to 31 and YES to 118` written as FLOORS - two branches each assert `>= 1`, the merge asserts `>= 1`, and all three are right, which is what the counts could not manage. WHAT IS LOST, said plainly: nobody is now told when a branch records an unwitnessed finding. That tripwire was worth less than it cost - it fired four times in one night, correctly, and one firing merged away unnoticed - and the thing it watched for is watched better by `ready_proven` itself, which simply does not offer the item.").
addressed_by(f_a_count_over_the_ledger_is_a_reading_and_a_floor_is_a_pin, "facts/findings.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_count_over_the_ledger_is_a_reading_and_a_floor_is_a_pin, "rules/findings.rofl").

finding(f_the_plugin_gate_opened_and_the_note_beside_it_did_not_notice, defect).
recorded(f_the_plugin_gate_opened_and_the_note_beside_it_did_not_notice, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_plugin_gate_opened_and_the_note_beside_it_did_not_notice, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_plugin_gate_opened_and_the_note_beside_it_did_not_notice, "THE SEVENTH NOTE REFUTED BY A FIRST PROBE, AND THE FIRST ONE THE MECHANISM CAUGHT RATHER THAN A PROBE WRITTEN FROM THE NOTE. `w_plugin_gated_kinds` holds three kinds out of the vocabulary and its block reads `class_accessor_property - the scanner returns NO node at all for accessor a = 1 without the decorators plugin`. Asked of the corpus world while writing the item's witness: TWO NODES, shapes.ts lines 465 and 837. Every word of the note is still true and its conclusion has been false since 2026-09-08, when the decorator work added `decorators` and `decoratorAutoAccessors` to PARSER_PLUGINS for its own reasons and wrote both fixtures. Nobody re-read the note, because a note is prose and nothing watches prose - which is the whole content of f_the_note_is_my_own_prior_guess_wearing_a_measurements_clothes and of the item this was found under. THE DIFFERENCE FROM THE OTHER SIX IS THE DIRECTION OF THE MISTAKE, and it is instructive. Those six were absences the model had already closed by its own work; this one is an absence a SETTING closed - the note was about the scanner's configuration and became false when a different item changed that configuration for an unrelated reason. A NOTE ABOUT A DEPENDENCY YOU DO NOT OWN GOES STALE ON SOMEBODY ELSE'S SCHEDULE, which is the case a re-read at item time cannot cover and a witness can. THE ITEM IS NOT CLOSED BY THIS: the plugin half is discharged, the environment half is not - import attributes is ES2025 and `export v from` is a Babel proposal, and the era scale tops out at ts5/2022 - so both of those now carry an absence witness with a live control and the item's note says what is actually left.").
addressed_by(f_the_plugin_gate_opened_and_the_note_beside_it_did_not_notice, "facts/worklist.rofl").
witness_in_atleast(f_the_plugin_gate_opened_and_the_note_beside_it_did_not_notice, w_js_corpus, "ast_node[code](N, class_accessor_property, F, L)", 1).
finding(f_an_audit_over_the_rules_reports_the_packs_its_world_happens_to_hold, defect).
recorded(f_an_audit_over_the_rules_reports_the_packs_its_world_happens_to_hold, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_an_audit_over_the_rules_reports_the_packs_its_world_happens_to_hold, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_an_audit_over_the_rules_reports_the_packs_its_world_happens_to_hold, "rules/js-attrs.rofl ANSWERS `which attribute keys do the rules read`, and it answers about the rules IN ITS WORLD. test/js-attrs.test.ts loaded SIX of the thirteen JS packs and rules/js-env.rofl was not one of them, so `attr_deferred(async, w_unconsumed_attribute)` was written on 2026-09-09 under the note *nobody has read it yet* while `outside_attr_needs(js, await_expression, async, true, top_level_await)` had been read since 2026-09-05 - measured here, 398 `within_attr[audit]` rows over this corpus, and it is the fact that tells ES2022 top-level await apart from the same word inside an ES2017 async function. THE DIRECTION OF THE ERROR IS THE POINT: a pack outside the world is a rule that reads nothing, so the audit produces a QUEUE ENTRY FOR WORK ALREADY DONE - which is the FIFTH instance of the exact shape w_unconsumed_attribute was opened for, and the first found by an instrument rather than by a reader. `f_an_instruments_world_is_part_of_its_claim` already made test/js-layer-cost.test.ts assert that every JS pack is either loaded or refused BY NAME in each of its three worlds; the closure was never taken here, where a missing pack changes the ANSWER and not the cost. It is taken now, with nothing refused and the positive control that a non-existent pack breaks it. THE GENERAL FORM, and it is not about attributes: any check that reads the kernel's reification - `premise_lit`, `concludes`, `derived_by` - has the loaded program as its denominator, and a denominator nothing asserts is a denominator that shrinks silently. All twelve JS packs load together in 6.6 s, so the cost of the honest closure was measured before it was chosen.").
addressed_by(f_an_audit_over_the_rules_reports_the_packs_its_world_happens_to_hold, "test/js-attrs.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_an_instruments_world_is_part_of_its_claim, "test/js-attrs.test.ts").

finding(f_a_key_read_through_a_table_is_invisible_to_a_check_over_literals, insight).
recorded(f_a_key_read_through_a_table_is_invisible_to_a_check_over_literals, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_key_read_through_a_table_is_invisible_to_a_check_over_literals, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_key_read_through_a_table_is_invisible_to_a_check_over_literals, "`attr_key_read` SEES A RULE NAMING A KEY AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT IT CAN SEE. rules/js-env.rofl names none: it reads `attr_needs(L, K, Key, V, F), ast_attr[code](N, Key, V)`, so both arguments of the `ast_attr` literal are VARIABLES, `attr_lit_kvar` correctly refuses to call that a key read, and five (operator, value) pairs plus `async, true` are consumed by the era layer with nothing able to say so. THE CHECK IS DERIVABLE ONE MORE LEVEL DOWN. A rule that joins a two-variable `ast_attr` literal to some OTHER relation carrying the same two variables is reading whatever pairs that relation holds, and that is a query over `premise_lit`: measured over all thirteen JS packs it returns EXACTLY TWO relations, `attr_needs` and `outside_attr_needs`, both at (Key = 3, Value = 4). Going from the relation's NAME to its ROWS is not a query - Datalog cannot index a relation by name - so the bridge is two rules by hand and `attr_table_unbridged[audit]` is what makes the hand list impossible to forget. BOTH LITERALS MUST BE POSITIVE AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING: `unconsumed_value` in the same pack has the identical shape with the co-literal NEGATED, and mutant 13 shows the audit reporting ITSELF without the distinction - a negated co-literal FILTERS the pairs, a positive one SUPPLIES them. WITHOUT A SITE THE HOLE IS UNFALSIFIABLE: the js-call corpus carries thirteen operator values and not one of `**`, `??`, `??=`, `||=`, `&&=`, so cutting the bridge kills nothing until a fixture writes one. test/fixtures/js-attrs/eras.mjs.txt is that site and is the reason mutants 7 and B die.").
addressed_by(f_a_key_read_through_a_table_is_invisible_to_a_check_over_literals, "rules/js-attrs.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_key_read_through_a_table_is_invisible_to_a_check_over_literals, "test/fixtures/js-attrs/eras.mjs.txt").

finding(f_the_presence_of_an_attribute_can_depend_on_the_value_of_another, insight).
recorded(f_the_presence_of_an_attribute_can_depend_on_the_value_of_another, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_presence_of_an_attribute_can_depend_on_the_value_of_another, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_presence_of_an_attribute_can_depend_on_the_value_of_another, "f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute EXPLAINED ITS SEVEN BLIND KEY POSITIONS AS *babel gives a private name no such field*, WHICH IS AN ASSERTION ABOUT A KIND, AND A KIND IS THE WRONG UNIT. Measured 2026-09-09 over the three `class_private_method` nodes in test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt: `get #face` at line 667 carries `computed, false` and `#rim` (666) and `#tucked` (836) carry no `computed` at all, because babel emits the field for the ACCESSOR forms of a private method and not for the plain one. So the presence of one attribute depends on the VALUE of another attribute on the same node - `kind` is `get` on the one that has it - and no reading of the grammar by kind can say that. IT IS THE CASE A SLOT-LEVEL CHECK CANNOT SEE, in both directions: a split kind is present in the slot, so `attr_slot_gap` finds no gap for it, while a guard over that slot is blind to exactly the nodes that lack the field. `attr_kind_split[audit]` is one rule and it is the smallest answer in the pack - TWO rows over the whole corpus, `computed`/`class_private_method` and `declare`/`variable_declaration`, of which only the first reaches the gate because `declare` is on `attr_unread_ok` and no layer models TypeScript's ambient modifier. THE SECOND ROW IS THE INTERESTING HALF OF THE MEASUREMENT: it says the corpus holds exactly one split among keys any rule reads, so the class is small and enumerable rather than a hazard nobody can bound.").
addressed_by(f_the_presence_of_an_attribute_can_depend_on_the_value_of_another, "rules/js-attrs.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute, "rules/js-attrs.rofl").

finding(f_an_oracle_over_derived_rows_cannot_see_what_a_guard_reaches, insight).
recorded(f_an_oracle_over_derived_rows_cannot_see_what_a_guard_reaches, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_an_oracle_over_derived_rows_cannot_see_what_a_guard_reaches, doc).
finding_note(f_an_oracle_over_derived_rows_cannot_see_what_a_guard_reaches, "m6 IN test/js-computed-key-names.test.ts WAS RECORDED AS A SURVIVOR WITH ITS REASON WRITTEN OUT - swap `not ast_attr(P, computed, true)` for `ast_attr(P, computed, false)` and NOT ONE ROW MOVES, because the seven key positions the positive form drops are all private and `key_name` has no arm for a private key. The reason was correct and the conclusion `somebody should come back and kill this when key_name learns a private key` was not: the mutant is killed today, by an oracle that never looks at what the relation derives. `attr_blind_guard[audit]` in rules/js-attrs.rofl reads the GUARD out of the clause through `premise_lit` and asks which kinds in its position carry no such attribute, so the swap moves two rows there - `computed/key/class_private_property` and `computed/key/class_private_method` - while every downstream relation is byte-identical. THE TRANSFERABLE RULE: a mutant that makes a guard REACH FEWER NODES while DERIVING THE SAME ROWS is invisible to every oracle over the derived rows, however many of them there are, and the number of such oracles is not evidence. Ask instead what the guard can reach; the clause is a fact here, so that is a query. THIS IS THE THIRD KIND OF SURVIVOR w_prototype_of_a_value ASKED THE TAXONOMY FOR - beside waiting on a corpus, on the grammar, and on another item, there is WAITING ON AN INSTRUMENT THAT LOOKS AT A DIFFERENT ARTIFACT - and it is the only one of the four that is discharged by writing a rule rather than by writing a fixture.").
addressed_by(f_an_oracle_over_derived_rows_cannot_see_what_a_guard_reaches, "test/js-attrs.test.ts").
finding(w_the_wall_is_join_order_and_it_arrives_at_thirty_two_files, defect).
recorded(w_the_wall_is_join_order_and_it_arrives_at_thirty_two_files, "2026-09-08").
demands(w_the_wall_is_join_order_and_it_arrives_at_thirty_two_files, decision).
artifact_removed(w_the_wall_is_join_order_and_it_arrives_at_thirty_two_files, "scanners/js_volume.ts").
finding_note(w_the_wall_is_join_order_and_it_arrives_at_thirty_two_files,
  "CORRECTED THE SAME HOUR IT WAS WRITTEN, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE FINDING. First version of this note said `the wall at 32 files is join order and not scale` and named `has_return` as the rule. BOTH HALVES WERE WRONG, and each was wrong for a reason worth keeping. THE ATTRIBUTION. `hole($rule(R), space_exhausted)` names the rule that was EXECUTING when the wall was crossed, and `Evaluation.rows` is a counter reset PER ROUND (src/engine.ts:1065), not per rule - so the named rule is the one holding the ticket when the round`s cumulative row count passed `this.space`, and nothing about it is proven causal. I read a blame field as a cause. THE WALL. Raising `DEFAULT_SPACE` from 500000 to 5000000 makes both slices COMPLETE - 32 files in 14.9 s at 976 MB, 64 files in 67.4 s at 2576 MB - so it is a DEFAULT sized for demo worlds, not a structural refusal. The first version was measured with `evaluate({budget, space})`, and `Rofl.evaluate(budget: number)` takes a NUMBER: the object was silently coerced, `space` never moved, and two runs that differed in nothing were read as evidence that raising it changed nothing. A wrong instrument that agrees with you is the hardest kind to notice. WHAT SURVIVES, all re-measured: facts per line 9.33-9.54 against the documented 9.9; the stored-per-base multiplier SIXTEEN TO SEVENTEEN against the documented 1.96-3.85, which moves 28.7M base facts from 56-110M stored to roughly 480M; 29 of the model`s 479 rules carry a premise sharing no variable with anything before it, which is a real static property and a real cost even though it is not what stopped this run; and reordering `has_return` is semantics-preserving, 396113 and 651679 facts at 8 and 16 files, identical to the digit. WHAT THE COMPLETED RUNS SAY INSTEAD, and it is worse than the story it replaces: 32 files to 64 files is 2x the input, 2.6x the facts, 2.6x the memory and 4.5x THE TIME. Superlinear in time, and 843-870 bytes a fact, which is the JS kernel`s recorded 784 confirmed on real code rather than on the corpus. Sixty-four files of a 303-file package of a repository that is a fiftieth of the target already costs 2.6 GB and 67 seconds. AND THE DEFECT THAT IS ACTUALLY ACTIONABLE: `space` IS NOT SETTABLE FROM THE PUBLIC API. `Rofl`'s constructor reads `naive`, `reuse`, `evaluator` and `retainTicks` from `EvalOpts` and never `space`, and `evaluate` takes a bare number, so `DEFAULT_SPACE` is a hard 500000 for every caller - and the one place the kernel explains itself (src/reflect.ts:249, `told budget_exhausted a caller raises the budget, which is precisely the move that turns this refusal back into a corpse`) is advice a caller cannot act on in either direction.").



finding(w_the_fixpoint_spends_half_itself_on_two_rules_that_conclude_nearly_nothing, insight).
recorded(w_the_fixpoint_spends_half_itself_on_two_rules_that_conclude_nearly_nothing, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_the_fixpoint_spends_half_itself_on_two_rules_that_conclude_nearly_nothing, rule_and_test).
artifact_removed(w_the_fixpoint_spends_half_itself_on_two_rules_that_conclude_nearly_nothing, "scanners/js_cost.ts").
finding_note(w_the_fixpoint_spends_half_itself_on_two_rules_that_conclude_nearly_nothing,
  "THE CURVE SAID SUPERLINEAR AND THE MODEL SAYS WHERE. First the curve, re-taken on a lightly loaded machine because the earlier one was measured under a load of 36 and I had not recorded it: over grafema/packages/util at 8, 16, 32 and 64 files the fixpoint costs 3.16, 6.03, 15.78 and 74.29 s for 396113, 651679, 1158156 and 2960417 facts. That is 7.5x the facts for 23.5x the time, so time grows about as facts to the 1.55, and TIME PER FACT goes 8.0, 9.3, 13.6, 25.1 microseconds - the superlinearity is real and it is in the fixpoint rather than the scan, which stays at 0.14 s for 64 files against 74.29. THEN THE MODEL, `scanners/js_cost.ts`, which points `scanners/eval_cost.ts`'s wrappers at the JS model instead of at a ring 1 parse. The wrappers are THEIRS: extracted as `measureWith` rather than copied, because the quantity they recover - the accumulator width at each body position - is the only thing that can explode, and two folds computing it would be two answers. THE TOP EIGHT RULES ARE 72.4 PERCENT OF ALL ACCUMULATOR WIDTH, and the top two are 52.4: `has_return` 1725848 (32.4 percent) for 653 conclusions of which 161 are fresh, and `closer_v` 1064884 (20.0 percent) for 2295. `has_return`'s widest position is the third, at 1718888 elements reaching `ast_within` - which is exactly the cross product `fn_node(F), ast_node(R, return_statement, _, _)` lays down before anything filters it, now measured from inside the join instead of inferred from a blame field. AND FIVE OF THE EIGHT CONCLUDE ALMOST NOTHING: two `fn_name` rules spend 509076 width for ZERO conclusions, `forged` - a boot.rofl audit - spends 129169 for zero, and two `may_be_node` rules spend 251320 for twelve. That is 760000 of 5331063, 14.3 percent of the fixpoint, spent by rules that produce nothing at all. The same shape this repository already recorded for ring 1, where half a parse concluded nothing. WHAT IT MEANS FOR THE LADDER: L3 and L4 are not blocked by an unknown, they are blocked by a list of eight rules with numbers beside them, and the first two are reorderable - `has_return` was already proven semantics-preserving under reordering at 396113 and 651679 facts, identical to the digit.").

finding(w_one_reordered_rule_moved_the_exponent_and_the_other_attempt_backfired, insight).
recorded(w_one_reordered_rule_moved_the_exponent_and_the_other_attempt_backfired, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_one_reordered_rule_moved_the_exponent_and_the_other_attempt_backfired, decision).
addressed_by(w_one_reordered_rule_moved_the_exponent_and_the_other_attempt_backfired, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").
finding_note(w_one_reordered_rule_moved_the_exponent_and_the_other_attempt_backfired,
  "ONE PREMISE MOVED IN ONE RULE AND THE COST CURVE'S EXPONENT CHANGED, WHICH IS THE ANSWER THE LOOP ASKED FOR. `has_return[code](F) :- fn_node(F), ast_node(R, return_statement, _, _), ast_within(F, R).` becomes `ast_node(R, return_statement, _, _), ast_within(F, R), fn_node(F).` - the returns first, so `ast_within` is asked with R already bound. MEASURED, sixteen files of eslint/lib through scanners/js_cost.ts: total accumulator width 5331063 to 3615655, down 32.2 percent, with `has_return` falling out of the top six entirely from 1725848; the fact count is 886230 before and after, identical. AND THE CURVE, re-taken over grafema/packages/util at 8, 16, 32 and 64 files under a comparable load: 3.16, 6.03, 15.78, 74.29 s becomes 3.10, 5.47, 12.94, 47.55, with the fact counts identical at every slice - 396113, 651679, 1158156, 2960417. THE EXPONENT MOVED AND NOT ONLY THE CONSTANT: 7.47 times the facts cost 23.5 times the time before and 15.3 after, so time as facts^1.57 becomes facts^1.36, and time per fact goes from 8.0/9.3/13.6/25.1 microseconds to 7.8/8.4/11.2/16.1. Projected on that exponent - stated as extrapolation, not measurement - L3 falls from about 19 minutes to about 9 and L4 from about 4.8 hours to about 1.4. THE SECOND REPAIR BACKFIRED AND THAT IS THE MORE USEFUL HALF. `closer_v[flow](F, X) :- encloses_v(F, X), encloses_v(G, X), ast_within(F, G), F != G.` is NOT a cross product - both positions join on X - and it was 20.0 percent of the width, so I reordered it to put `encloses_v(G, X)` first and ask `ast_within(F, G)` with G bound, reasoning that the containers of one node are a short ancestor chain. Measured: 1064884 becomes 1762311, SIXTY-FIVE PERCENT WORSE, with the widest position moving to the third at 1137706. Reverted. The reasoning was plausible and wrong, and the only thing that separated it from the has_return reorder - which was equally plausible and right - was running both. A join that shares a variable is not a cross product and is therefore NOT covered by test/rule-shape.test.ts, so nothing static would have warned me either way. `closer_v` is now 29.5 percent of the width and the top item, with one failed attempt recorded against it.").

finding(w_two_more_cross_products_and_the_gate_had_already_named_them, insight).
recorded(w_two_more_cross_products_and_the_gate_had_already_named_them, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_two_more_cross_products_and_the_gate_had_already_named_them, doc).
addressed_by(w_two_more_cross_products_and_the_gate_had_already_named_them, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").
finding_note(w_two_more_cross_products_and_the_gate_had_already_named_them,
  "THE TWO `fn_name` RULES THAT SPENT 509076 ACCUMULATOR ELEMENTS TO CONCLUDE NOTHING ARE THE SAME DEFECT AS `has_return` AND THE SAME REPAIR. `fn_name(F, N) :- fn_node(F), ast_node(D, variable_declarator, _, _), ast_child(D, init, 0, F), ...` puts F and D side by side sharing nothing, so every function is laid against every declarator and the filter runs afterwards - 271982 elements reaching it, zero conclusions, and the object_property twin the same at 236132. Reordered to take the declarator first and ask `ast_child(D, init, 0, F)` with D bound: total width 3615655 to 3112843, both rules out of the top six, fact count 886230 before and after, identical. Cumulative over this and `has_return`: 5331063 to 3112843, DOWN 41.6 PERCENT, with the fact set unchanged at every step. AND THE GATE HAD ALREADY NAMED THEM. `rules/js-callgraph.rofl fn_name <- ast_node` is two of the twenty-four entries in the baseline `test/rule-shape.test.ts` carries on branch nextver, written before any of this was measured. The static check found them; the cost model priced them; neither alone would have moved anything. That is the argument for porting the gate to this branch, which is still not done.").

finding(w_the_permission_audits_are_seven_percent_of_a_code_world_and_conclude_nothing, insight).
recorded(w_the_permission_audits_are_seven_percent_of_a_code_world_and_conclude_nothing, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_the_permission_audits_are_seven_percent_of_a_code_world_and_conclude_nothing, decision).
finding_note(w_the_permission_audits_are_seven_percent_of_a_code_world_and_conclude_nothing,
  "BOOT.ROFL'S AUDITS COST SEVEN PERCENT OF A CODE WORLD'S FIXPOINT AND NINETY-NINE POINT NINE PERCENT OF THAT PRODUCES NOTHING. Six of boot.rofl's nineteen head relations fire on the JS model over sixteen files of eslint/lib, for 217221 accumulator elements of 3112843. FOUR OF THE SIX CONCLUDE ZERO: `forged` 129169, `unattributed` 84203, `undefined_premise` 3515, `widened` 332. The two that conclude anything cost ONE unit of width each - `loader` produces 42101 rows and `negated_under` 110. So 217020 of 217221 is spent proving that nobody misbehaved. AND IT SCALES WITH THE WORLD, which is the part that matters for the ladder: `forged` walks `asserted_by` and there is one such row per fact, 43056 here. At L4's projected 95M facts that is seven percent of a projected 1.4 hours, six minutes of audit that finds nothing, every run. THIS IS NOT AN ARGUMENT FOR SWITCHING THEM OFF and the repository already records why: an opt-in gate is invisible to every survey, and the silence of an audit is only worth something if it was actually asked. It is an argument for asking it CHEAPLY - `forged`'s three positions are `asserted_by`, `in_perspective`, `authority`, and the accumulator DOUBLES at the third rather than narrowing, which is a shape worth a measured reorder attempt of its own. It is also the sharpest case yet for the cold-book work: an audit that walks every assertion in the world is exactly what a residency policy exists to avoid, and 690 services of other teams' code are 690 services nobody is going to forge anything in.").

finding(w_the_static_gates_remaining_yield_is_six_percent_not_twenty_two, pitfall).
recorded(w_the_static_gates_remaining_yield_is_six_percent_not_twenty_two, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_the_static_gates_remaining_yield_is_six_percent_not_twenty_two, doc).
addressed_by(w_the_static_gates_remaining_yield_is_six_percent_not_twenty_two, "scanners/rule_shape.ts").
finding_note(w_the_static_gates_remaining_yield_is_six_percent_not_twenty_two,
  "I JOINED THE STATIC LIST TO THE COST MODEL BY RULE HEAD AND OVERSTATED THE ANSWER BY THREE AND A HALF TIMES. The gate names 42 cross-product premises in `rules/js-*`; the cost model keys rules by an opaque hash and the scanner by file and index, so neither id joins to the other, and I reached for the head. Nine rules share the head `may_be_node`, so summing every cost row whose head appears in the list credits each cross product with the cost of its innocent namesakes: 692090, 22.2 percent of the fixpoint. THE EXACT JOIN is the head PLUS the ordered list of positive premise relations, which both sides can produce and which is unique here - zero ambiguous matches over forty distinct cross-product rules. Re-measured: 14 of the 40 fire on this world at all, 26 never fire, and the 14 cost 189154, SIX POINT ONE PERCENT. The scanner now carries the body signature so the join cannot be made the loose way again. WHAT THE CORRECTED NUMBER MEANS FOR THE LADDER, and it is the useful part: the static gate's REMAINING yield is small. It found the three big ones - `has_return` and both `fn_name` rules, together 41.6 percent of the original width - and what it has left to give is a sixth of that. The dominant item is now `closer_v` at 35.2 percent, which is NOT a cross product: both its positions join on X, so nothing static sees it, and the earlier attempt to reorder it made it 65 percent worse. The next real gain is a rule redesign and not another reorder. EIGHTH WRONG CALL IN FOUR DAYS AND THE SAME SHAPE AS THE OTHERS: two quantities joined on a key that does not identify the thing being counted.").

finding(w_a_mutant_keyed_on_source_text_is_the_line_anchor_trap_in_another_costume, pitfall).
recorded(w_a_mutant_keyed_on_source_text_is_the_line_anchor_trap_in_another_costume, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_a_mutant_keyed_on_source_text_is_the_line_anchor_trap_in_another_costume, decision).
finding_note(w_a_mutant_keyed_on_source_text_is_the_line_anchor_trap_in_another_costume,
  "REORDERING `accessor_read` FOR COST TURNED A MUTANT RED WITHOUT CHANGING AN ANSWER. `test/js-controlflow-values.test.ts` case c4 plants its mutation by finding the rule's VERBATIM SOURCE TEXT and replacing it; `js-corpus-world.ts` asserts the anchor is present, so a reformatted or reordered rule fails as `mutation anchor absent` and reads exactly like a broken semantics. CONFIRMED BY THE MESSAGE AND NOT BY INFERENCE: the run that was still going when this was first written finished and said `AssertionError: mutation anchor absent in rules/js-controlflow.rofl`, naming the old text. The circumstantial evidence agreed - 98 of the 99 tests in that file passed, including c3, which reads `accessor_read` downstream through `throwing_call`, and the fact count over the sixteen-file eslint world was 886230 before and after, identical - but the assertion says it outright. THE REORDER ITSELF: `accessor_of(Obj, Key, M), member_node_v(N), selects(N, Key), ...` puts N beside Obj and Key sharing nothing - every accessor laid against every member read, 88980 accumulator elements to conclude NOTHING - and the file's own comment two lines above says the order was chosen so that `the receiver walk is a probe rather than a scan`, which was half right: `accessor_of` first does bind Obj and Key, and then the next premise threw that away. Asking `selects(N, Key)` with Key bound collapses it to about 740. Total width 3112843 to 3024601, and 5331063 to 3024601 since this work began, DOWN 43.3 PERCENT with the fact set unchanged at every step. THE TRAP IS THE REPOSITORY'S OWN, TWICE RECORDED FOR LINE NUMBERS AND NOT YET FOR SOURCE TEXT: an anchor that is a literal quotation of the thing it tests goes stale the moment that thing is edited for any reason, and it fails in the direction that looks like a real defect. VERIFIED 2026-09-09 AFTER THE RE-POINT: test/js-controlflow-values.test.ts 83 of 83, test/js-class-expression.test.ts 16 of 16, which is the 99 the first run counted across both files. So the reorder changed no answer and the mutant still means what it meant - delete the receiver check, and a plain property of the same name reads as an accessor. The class still deserves a decision: a mutant could anchor on the rule's HEAD and position instead of its text, the way the cost model and rule_shape now join.").

finding(w_an_argmin_written_as_a_negation_over_a_quadratic_was_a_third_of_the_fixpoint, insight).
recorded(w_an_argmin_written_as_a_negation_over_a_quadratic_was_a_third_of_the_fixpoint, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_an_argmin_written_as_a_negation_over_a_quadratic_was_a_third_of_the_fixpoint, doc).
addressed_by(w_an_argmin_written_as_a_negation_over_a_quadratic_was_a_third_of_the_fixpoint, "rules/js-dataflow.rofl").
finding_note(w_an_argmin_written_as_a_negation_over_a_quadratic_was_a_third_of_the_fixpoint,
  "THE BIGGEST SINGLE COST IN THE MODEL WAS A SHAPE AND NOT AN ORDER, AND NO REORDER COULD HAVE FOUND IT. `nearest_v(F, X)` - the innermost function enclosing a node - was three relations: `encloses_v` materialised every (function, descendant) pair, `closer_v` paired each descendant's enclosing functions against EACH OTHER to find a nearer one, and `nearest_v` subtracted the second from the first. An argmin written as a negation over a quadratic. Measured from inside the join, `closer_v` alone was 1064884 accumulator elements - 35.2 percent of the whole fixpoint - to conclude 2295 rows. IT IS NOT A CROSS PRODUCT: both `encloses_v` positions join on X, so `test/rule-shape.test.ts` is silent about it by construction, and the one plausible reorder I tried measured SIXTY-FIVE PERCENT WORSE. The static gate cannot see this class at all. REPLACED BY TWO LINEAR RULES that walk DOWN from the function - `fn_node_v(F), ast_in(F, X)` and then carry a nearest function through nodes that are not themselves functions - which is what `nearest` means said forwards instead of by exclusion. rules/js-structure.rofl already said in its own comment that an ancestor query here should be answered by walking `ast_in` rather than materialising the closure; it took a number to act on it. MEASURED: total accumulator width 3024601 to 2020438, and 5331063 to 2020438 since this work began - DOWN 62.1 PERCENT. On the fixture corpus the store hands out 1694517 rows to 1374910, down 18.9, which is the first time that ratchet has moved by more than a percent. AND THE ANSWERS ARE THE SAME WHERE IT MATTERS, verified before the test numbers were touched: `nearest_v` is 12431 rows under both formulations with ZERO differing in either direction, and `may_throw` 62, `try_of` 18, `thrown_by` 127, `abrupt_at` 354 identical. The 32663 facts that went are `encloses_v` and `closer_v` themselves. THE COST TEST NAMED THE CHANGE BEFORE I DID, which is the argument for that kind of test: `every read path above five per cent, by name` went red listing exactly what moved, and one path ROSE without growing - `relPersp asserted_by`, `forged`'s first premise, at 5.51 percent. It did not get more expensive; the denominator shrank, and an audit that walks every assertion in the world is now above the line. A share is a ratio and that test pins a numerator's fate to a denominator it does not mention.").

finding(w_the_exponent_moved_three_times_and_time_stopped_being_the_blocker, insight).
recorded(w_the_exponent_moved_three_times_and_time_stopped_being_the_blocker, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_the_exponent_moved_three_times_and_time_stopped_being_the_blocker, doc).
artifact_removed(w_the_exponent_moved_three_times_and_time_stopped_being_the_blocker, "scanners/js_volume.ts").
finding_note(w_the_exponent_moved_three_times_and_time_stopped_being_the_blocker,
  "FIVE REPAIRS, AND THE CURVE'S EXPONENT MOVED EVERY TIME IT WAS RE-TAKEN: facts^1.57, then 1.36 after `has_return`, now 1.225 after both `fn_name` rules, `accessor_read` and the `nearest_v` redesign. Measured over grafema/packages/util at 8, 16, 32 and 64 files under loads of 2.9-4.4, 3.4-3.8 and 2.6-3.7, which are comparable and are recorded because a curve taken while the machine changes is not a curve. Seconds: 3.16, 6.03, 15.78, 74.29 originally; 2.87, 5.21, 10.09, 33.54 now - at 64 files that is FIFTY-FIVE PERCENT off. Microseconds per fact: 8.0, 9.3, 13.6, 25.1 becomes 7.5, 8.3, 9.0, 11.8, so the per-fact cost is now nearly flat where it used to more than triple across the same range. AND THAT CHANGES WHICH THING IS BINDING, which is the point of the exercise. Projected on the measured exponent - stated as extrapolation - L3, the whole of eslint/lib at 388 files and 98706 lines, is about 17.0M facts, FIVE MINUTES and 10.4 GB; L4, all 1426 files and 531415 lines, is about 91.3M facts, THIRTY-NINE MINUTES and 56.1 GB. Thirty-nine minutes is a coffee break; 56 GB is not available on a machine with 21.5. So on the JS kernel TIME HAS STOPPED BEING THE BLOCKER AND MEMORY IS THE WHOLE OF IT, which is exactly the case docs/medium-and-large.md was written to make and which the storage work already priced: the same 91.3M facts at the Rust engine's measured 215 B/fact are about 20 GB, and at the columnar figure well under that. FOUR DAYS AGO L4 PROJECTED AT 4.8 HOURS AND THE TIME HALF LOOKED LIKE THE HARD ONE. Five rule repairs later it is 39 minutes, and none of them touched the kernel - four reorders and one redesign, all verified answer-preserving.").

finding(w_the_audit_asks_a_six_valued_question_eighty_six_thousand_times, insight).
recorded(w_the_audit_asks_a_six_valued_question_eighty_six_thousand_times, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_the_audit_asks_a_six_valued_question_eighty_six_thousand_times, decision).
finding_note(w_the_audit_asks_a_six_valued_question_eighty_six_thousand_times,
  "MEASURED, PROTOTYPED, AND NOT LANDED - and the reason it was not landed is the useful half. `forged[audit](F) :- asserted_by(F, Who, _), in_perspective(F, P), not authority(P, Who).` walks every assertion and then asks whether its writer had standing. Over sixteen files of eslint/lib that is 43056 assertions, 129169 accumulator elements, exactly THREE PER ASSERTED FACT, to conclude nothing. THE NEGATION'S ARGUMENTS RANGE OVER SIX VALUES. Measured in the same world: distinct `Who` is TWO - `$kernel` and `user` - distinct `P` is THREE - `main`, `code`, `audit` - and `authority` holds nine rows. So `not authority(P, Who)` is asked 86112 times about at most six possible inputs, which is the whole of the cost. Note the doubling: 86112 is exactly twice the 43056 solutions reaching it, because a negation is evaluated once under each assumption of the alternating fixpoint. THE REPAIR IS TO LEAD WITH THE SELECTIVE PREMISE: project the pairs once (`wrote(P, Who) :- in_perspective(F, P), asserted_by(F, Who, _).`, at most books times writers rows) and have the audit start there, so that when no pair lacks standing the body stops at the negation and the two per-fact positions are never entered. Measured: total width 2020438 to 1934363, the fixpoint 9.3 s to 7.4, and the audit's answer IDENTICAL on a world carrying real forgeries - both formulations name `$fact(isa,tells,[dog,jerk])` written by mallory and `$fact(authority,main,[tells,alice])`, two rows, byte for byte. The cost of an audit becomes proportional to what it FINDS rather than to what it CHECKS. WHY IT IS REVERTED. `boot.rofl` is the program every world loads, and `wrote` is the first relation in it that is neither an audit nor part of the permission vocabulary - a bare projection. Ten tests went red, and they are RIGHT to: `every boot.rofl audit over HECK is empty` and `boot.rofl's own audits over NPC's reflection are all empty` enumerate boot.rofl's heads as a SET and require emptiness, which a projection breaks by design. Add the 34 corpus goldens on nextver that would regenerate, and the price is a rewrite of the kernel's own program for 4.3 percent of the fixpoint's width. That is the owner's call and not a loop iteration's. THE GENERAL RULE IS WORTH MORE THAN THIS INSTANCE: an audit that is empty on every honest world should be written so that its emptiness is CHEAP - selective premise first, enumeration last - and `leak`, `unattributed`, `widened` and `undefined_premise` have the same shape and were measured at 217221 elements between them for 201 conclusions.").

finding(w_i_compared_two_worlds_under_different_walls_and_called_it_a_conformance_failure, defect).
recorded(w_i_compared_two_worlds_under_different_walls_and_called_it_a_conformance_failure, "2026-09-09").
demands(w_i_compared_two_worlds_under_different_walls_and_called_it_a_conformance_failure, doc).
finding_note(w_i_compared_two_worlds_under_different_walls_and_called_it_a_conformance_failure,
  "I REPORTED THAT THE 32-FILE SEED DOES NOT ROUND-TRIP AND PUT IT IN THE QUEUE AS A CONFORMANCE BLOCKER. IT ROUND-TRIPS EXACTLY. The harness built the direct world with `new Rofl({space: 20000000})` and the replay with a bare `Rofl.fromSnapshot(seed)`, which takes the 500000-row default - so the two sides ran under DIFFERENT WALLS and the replay stopped inside the answer. Re-run with the same wall on both: `identical true`, no holes. THE TELL WAS IN THE OUTPUT AND I READ PAST IT: the difference was 602701 rows across ninety relations with 2321 the other way, which is nothing like a witness-bookkeeping discrepancy, and the replay's own row list contained `hole` TWICE. A round-trip that fails by six hundred thousand rows is not a conformance question, it is a truncated run, and the two holes said so by name - `$adhoc budget_exhausted` and `$rule(rd1498c11) space_exhausted`, the latter being `forged`, the audit already measured at 129169 accumulator elements. WHAT I ALMOST SPENT AN ITERATION ON: the queue said this blocked conformance above 16 files and made settling the 434 urgent. Neither is true. The 434 is still the only real disagreement between the engines, it is unchanged at every size measured, and the ladder is clear to keep climbing. NINTH WRONG CALL IN FIVE DAYS AND THE SAME FAMILY AS THE OTHER EIGHT: a difference between two INSTRUMENTS read as a difference between two ANSWERS. The specific lesson is narrower than the general one and worth writing separately - `Rofl.fromSnapshot(seed, opts)` takes the same `EvalOpts` as the constructor, and a comparison that configures one side and not the other is measuring its own configuration.").


finding(f_a_measurement_can_go_stale_without_anybody_having_been_wrong, insight).
recorded(f_a_measurement_can_go_stale_without_anybody_having_been_wrong, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_measurement_can_go_stale_without_anybody_having_been_wrong, doc).
finding_note(f_a_measurement_can_go_stale_without_anybody_having_been_wrong, "NAMED BY THE OTHER SESSION ABOUT ITS OWN NUMBER, which is why it is worth recording rather than a thing either of us could have argued into existence. `fn_name` over an object property was repaired twice the same night for two different reasons. One session repaired it for COST and measured the reorder at 236 132 accumulator elements saved, on eslint/lib, from inside the join - correctly taken, correctly attributed, and reproducible. The other repaired the SAME clause for CORRECTNESS: the body read `ast_name[code](K, N)` on the key directly, so for `{ [haspSpelling]: (n) => n }` it named the function after the VARIABLE rather than the property, and the repair routed it through the guarded `key_name`. THE COST NUMBER NOW PRICES A BODY THAT IS NOT IN THE TREE. Nobody measured wrong, nobody attributed wrong, and nothing about the number was ever false - the clause it describes was repaired out from under it. THIS IS NOT THE STALE-WITNESS DEFECT AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT. A stale witness is a claim that has become FALSE about the world it names, and `scripts/witness_check.ts` catches it by re-running the query. This is a claim that stays TRUE about a world that no longer exists, and no checker can catch it, because the thing it measured is gone rather than changed - there is nothing left to re-run. The only tell is that the body's text moved, and a number in a comment does not know what body it was taken over. AND THE TWO REPAIRS WERE NEVER IN TENSION, which is the useful half: the cost insight was `ask the cheap thing first`, and `key_name` had just been narrowed from 2 148 rows to 105 by the correctness work, so it IS the cheap thing. The right shape was pointed at the wrong premise. Git offered the two bodies as equals in a merge conflict and taking the cost one would have bought a real saving while silently reverting a correctness fix. WHAT TO DO ABOUT IT, and it is cheap: a cost number written into a rule comment should say WHICH BODY it was taken over, not only what it saved - the premise list, or the commit. Then a reader who sees the body has changed knows the number is about something else, instead of inheriting it.").
addressed_by(f_a_measurement_can_go_stale_without_anybody_having_been_wrong, "rules/js-callgraph.rofl").
finding(f_a_cost_model_can_be_five_for_five_backwards_and_wrong_forwards, insight).
recorded(f_a_cost_model_can_be_five_for_five_backwards_and_wrong_forwards, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_cost_model_can_be_five_for_five_backwards_and_wrong_forwards, doc).
finding_note(f_a_cost_model_can_be_five_for_five_backwards_and_wrong_forwards, "w_join_planner ASKED FOR A PLANNER THAT ORDERS BODY LITERALS BY BOUNDNESS AND THE FIRST THING BUILT WAS THE STRONGEST VERSION OF IT: a greedy join planner minimising the sum of intermediate result sizes by subset DP over every legal order, fed PERFECT statistics - every relation's cardinality and per-argument distinct counts, taken off a FINISHED 434 149-fact store, which no planner running before the fixpoint could ever have. It was scored against the five reorders measured by hand on 2026-09-05 and 2026-09-09 and IT GOT ALL FIVE RIGHT. It reorders `has_return` (37.4x), `encloses_s` (1.73x) and `param_hidden` arm 2 (6.15x) into exactly the orders the humans chose; it rates the `tdz_deferred` swap at 1.01x, which is the `worth nothing` the measurement found; and it KEEPS `closer_v` as written while reverting the +65% variant somebody had to revert by hand (40.6x). A boundness heuristic gets two of those wrong, so the model is doing real work. AND EVERY ONE OF THE FIVE IS A RETRODICTION. The model proposed two orders nobody had measured - both for `param_hidden`, both claiming large wins - and the control-flow world was built for each: the first was worth -0.32% against a predicted 19.3x, and the SECOND MADE THE WORLD 4.3 TIMES MORE EXPENSIVE, 3 521 279 rows to 15 015 237, against a predicted 4.43x improvement. Five for five backwards, nought for two forwards, and the failure was in the dangerous direction. THE MECHANISM IS MEASURED, NOT GUESSED, and it is not fixable by better statistics of the same kind. The estimate for `ident[code](U, Name)` with Name bound is the MEAN rows per name: 2 439 rows over 553 distinct names, 4.41. The join reaching it has bound Name from `param_of`, so it asks about PARAMETER names - 29 of the 553 - where the mean is 31.62, and weighted by how often `param_of` names each one it is 647.99. The model under-estimates that one probe by 147x, and the whole of it is one parameter called `n` whose spelling occurs 758 times against a median of 2. A JOIN'S PROBE VALUES ARE CORRELATED WITH THE TAIL OF THE DISTRIBUTION IT PROBES, and a per-relation average cannot see the correlation; the histogram that could is per-join, not per-relation. THE SECOND FAILURE HAS A SECOND CAUSE worth separating: the model prices ONE evaluation of the body from an empty binding, and the engine is seminaive, so a predicted saving of 2.23 million rows was worth 11 397. A cost model of a naive join is not a cost model of this engine. WHAT THIS IS EVIDENCE FOR is not that planning is impossible - it is that A MODEL VALIDATED ON THE CASES IT WAS TUNED AGAINST IS NOT VALIDATED, which is the same sentence rules/js-dataflow.rofl already writes as `the signature names a CANDIDATE, never a cause`, one level up: there the signature was structural and here it is numeric, and both are retrodictions until something unmeasured is predicted.").
addressed_by(f_a_cost_model_can_be_five_for_five_backwards_and_wrong_forwards, "test/join-planner.test.ts").
artifact_removed(f_a_cost_model_can_be_five_for_five_backwards_and_wrong_forwards, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-09-join-planner.md").

finding(f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics, insight).
recorded(f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics, "ASKING WHAT THE THREE HAND REPAIRS HAD IN COMMON THAT THE TWO NON-REPAIRS DID NOT gives a criterion with no numbers in it. `has_return`, `encloses_s` and `param_hidden` arm 2 each had a positive literal sharing NO VARIABLE with any literal before it - a CROSS PRODUCT at that position, whose fan-out is the whole relation and is multiplied by everything already accumulated. `closer_v` in both spellings and `param_hidden` under the cost model's own proposal are CONNECTED THROUGHOUT, and every one of those is a case where reordering was measured worse. So the class that is safe to move is the class that needs no statistics to see, and the class that needs statistics is the class where statistics were measured wrong. `planBody` now holds a cross-product literal until something before it binds one of its variables, and nothing else moves. MEASURED ON THE CONTROL-FLOW WORLD, and the answers are the subject rather than an aside: the digest of all 199 959 program facts is IDENTICAL with the planner on and off, as are 177 218 firings and 434 149 facts, and the whole non-JS suite is 956 of 963 with the same seven pre-existing failures. Rows handed out 3 521 279 -> 3 353 932, -4.75% on a tree that had already been hand-tuned twice. AND THE POINT OF THE ITEM IS THE OTHER NUMBERS. With the planner in, the pre-repair spelling of `encloses_s` costs 0.00% more than the repaired one, the pre-repair `param_hidden` costs 0.00% more, and the `tdz_deferred` swap costs 0.00% - three bodies whose written order stopped being a cost decision at all, where before they were +37.27%, +119.87% and -0.04%. `has_return` is the honest partial: +66.62% becomes +1.52%, because holding the kind scan until the return statement binds it is a good order and leading with it is a better one, and choosing between two good orders is the half that needs a cost model. THE BARRIER IS THE WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT and it is one line: a negation or a builtin drains everything held ahead of it, so a positive can never overtake a `not` or an `is`, and the two halves of `planBody` - where a negation may stand, where a positive may stand - cannot interact. WHERE IT CANNOT LOOK, stated as an assertion rather than a sentence: a body that is connected and badly ordered, of which `closer_v` at +117% of its world is the measured instance. 49 of 1 885 multi-element bodies in the tree move. AND IT RETIRES A NUMBER ANOTHER FINDING RESTS ON, which is recorded here rather than as a second note there because the DECISION that finding demands - whether `planBody` moves into ROFL - is untouched and settling it would be a lie. `f_the_plan_is_expressible_and_the_corpus_says_almost_nothing_about_it` measured `0 BODIES ACTUALLY REORDER` over the whole corpus and drew the right conclusion from it: a corpus in which the plan never moves is two instruments returning their input, so the acceptance for the next iteration had to be a mutant set in which the plan MOVES. It moves now - 49 of 1 885 multi-element bodies over 93 .rofl files - so the first half of that note is stale and the second half is exactly what test/join-planner.test.ts was built to satisfy. A ROFL mirror of `planBody`, if anyone writes one, now has something to disagree about. ELEVEN MUTANTS OF THE HOLD, TEN KILLED ON THE FIRST RUN AND ONE SURVIVOR, and the survivor and two of the kills each say something the design did not. M8 - release the LAST releasable held literal instead of the first - survived because NO BODY ANYWHERE IN THE FILE HELD TWO LITERALS AT ONCE, so a forward scan and a reverse one agreed on every assertion; the property it breaks (written order survives among held literals, which is what makes the hold conservative rather than a second planner) rested on nothing, and one body kills it. THE NEGATION BARRIER CANNOT CHANGE AN ANSWER and the builtin barrier can: removing the barrier only for negations is killed by a PLAN assertion and by nothing else, because `planBody`'s readiness rule already places a negation with its variables bound wherever the hold puts it - a builtin is placed where it was WRITTEN, so a positive held across one leaves an arithmetic hole, and that mutant is killed by a derived FACT. The item's own stated risk was the negation and the measured risk is the builtin. AND A SECOND GATE CAUGHT THE FIRST MUTANT: test/rofl-safety.test.ts's corpus oracle, written for range restriction, goes red on the barrier being removed as well - two gates, one hole, which is the argument for running a neighbour's oracle over a kernel change rather than only one's own. A SIXTH CASE WITH AN INDEPENDENT ORIGIN CONFIRMS IT: `completion_fn_between` was recorded the same day by another session as f_a_derived_pair_costs_what_its_first_unbound_literal_costs - thirteen seconds of a twenty-two second world because `fn_node_v(G)` stood where G was unbound - and was repaired there by making the rule's HEAD smaller. Permuting the repaired rule back into the cross-product shape costs +5.60% of the control-flow world with the hold removed and 0.00% with it in. Nobody chose that case for this planner; it was already in the ledger. AND THE SPACE WALL WAS ASKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: `DEFAULT_SPACE` is 500 000 rows and this world holds 434 149 facts, so a planner that widened an accumulator would produce `space_exhausted` instead of a number. Peak rows measured both ways on the same world: 49 338 with the hold and 49 338 without, 9.9 per cent of the wall and not moved by a row. That is the expected shape - holding a cross product removes a multiplication from every prefix before its new position, so no accumulator can be wider than it was - but it is measured, because the argument has one named exception: an EMPTY held relation short-circuits the written order at the position it stands in and the planned order evaluates the literals in between first. AND THE 4.75 PER CENT HAS A NAME: comparing the two tallies path by path, `argMatches ast_node pos=[1]` goes 152 511 -> 40 929, -73 per cent and 67 per cent of the whole 167 347-row saving. That is the KIND SCAN - the node unbound with its kind fixed - which f_the_layer_no_gate_watched_is_half_the_cost_of_its_own_world named as the single heaviest read path in the tree before `has_return` was repaired by hand. What is left of it at HEAD is what the hold takes out, with nobody reading a rule.").
addressed_by(f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics, "src/engine.ts").
artifact_removed(f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-09-join-planner.md").
addressed_by(f_a_cross_product_is_the_half_of_join_order_that_needs_no_statistics, "test/join-planner.test.ts").

finding(f_a_mutant_anchored_to_an_items_state_expires_when_the_item_is_done, insight).
recorded(f_a_mutant_anchored_to_an_items_state_expires_when_the_item_is_done, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_mutant_anchored_to_an_items_state_expires_when_the_item_is_done, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_mutant_anchored_to_an_items_state_expires_when_the_item_is_done, "CLOSING `w_join_planner` TURNED MUTANT 7 IN test/worklist.test.ts RED, AND NOT ON THE THING THE MUTANT IS ABOUT. It plants a `work_needs` cycle between two items and checks that both VANISH from the queue - the silence a derived order makes possible - with a positive control that both are takeable on the honest tree. The two were typed out by name, one of them was `w_join_planner`, and doing that item made its own control false. Nothing about cycles moved. THIS IS THE SAME SENTENCE THE LOOP ALREADY KNOWS ABOUT RULE TEXT - four mutants died on 2026-09-09 because a body was reordered under them, changing no answer - arriving through the LEDGER instead: a mutant anchored to a datum that the work is expected to change expires when the work is done, and it expires in the CONTROL rather than in the claim, which is the direction that reads as a defect in somebody else's branch. THE REMEDY IS THE ONE THE PIN CONVERSION ALREADY USES: read the subject off the world instead of typing it. The pair is now `takeable(W)` sorted, first two, with a floor - at least two items are offered - so the mutant plants its cycle on whatever the queue's head happens to be and cannot go stale. AND IT IS STRICTLY STRONGER, because a hand-typed pair also silently stops exercising the audit if BOTH items are closed, and the derived pair cannot. The class to sweep for is any assertion naming a work item, a finding id or a cell by hand inside a MUTANT: the claim may need the name, but the control almost never does.").
addressed_by(f_a_mutant_anchored_to_an_items_state_expires_when_the_item_is_done, "test/worklist.test.ts").

finding(f_a_mutant_that_plants_a_bad_body_order_expires_when_the_engine_stops_caring, defect).
recorded(f_a_mutant_that_plants_a_bad_body_order_expires_when_the_engine_stops_caring, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_mutant_that_plants_a_bad_body_order_expires_when_the_engine_stops_caring, decision).
finding_note(f_a_mutant_that_plants_a_bad_body_order_expires_when_the_engine_stops_caring, "MUTANT B IN test/js-layer-cost.test.ts RE-PLANTS THE PRE-REPAIR ORDER OF `has_return` AND ASSERTS THE GATE CATCHES THE REGRESSION FOUR WAYS. With the cross-product hold in `planBody` it is caught ONE way, and the file's own comment already names the mechanism one turn earlier: it says `A MUTANT ANCHORED TO THE DEFECT EXPIRES WHEN THE DEFECT IS FIXED` about the anchor text going missing, and this is the same sentence about the DEFECT ITSELF - a mutant that plants a bad body order expires when the engine stops caring about body order. MEASURED, all four criteria on the same run: world total 3 404 842 against 3 353 932 needs a factor of 1.25 and gets 1.015; the row-share band needs 1.5 pp and gets 1.389 (8.503% against 7.114%); rows-per-derivation needs 5 and gets 4.50 (25.63 against 21.13); and only the PATH SET still differs, because reverting the order still moves `argMatches ast_within pos=[0]` to 41.113% of a layer whose paths are otherwise `completion_known` and two containment walks. Three of four kills fall below their thresholds and the fourth is qualitative. NOTHING HERE IS WRONG WITH THE GATE and that is why this is a decision rather than a repair: the thing the mutant was watching for has stopped being a regression, which is the outcome the work wanted. What the mutant should now plant is a defect the planner CANNOT absorb, and the queue already names the class - `w_connected_and_badly_ordered`, whose measured instance is `closer_v` at +117 per cent of the same world with no cross product in either spelling. Re-aiming it there keeps the gate pointed at a live hazard instead of at one the kernel now removes. THE GENERAL FORM, and it is the third instance this week: a mutant is anchored to something - a rule's TEXT, an item's STATE, or a DEFECT CLASS - and every one of the three can be repaired out from under it, leaving a green assertion that proves nothing. The tell is always that the mutant's own control or threshold moves while its subject does not.").

finding(f_an_exact_per_premise_estimate_does_not_make_a_seed_ordering_rule_correct, insight).
recorded(f_an_exact_per_premise_estimate_does_not_make_a_seed_ordering_rule_correct, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_an_exact_per_premise_estimate_does_not_make_a_seed_ordering_rule_correct, doc).
finding_note(f_an_exact_per_premise_estimate_does_not_make_a_seed_ordering_rule_correct, "ANOTHER SESSION MEASURED, OVER 16 FILES OF eslint/lib, THAT A POSITION-0 PREMISE'S OBSERVED COST EQUALS A STATIC COUNT CONDITIONED ON ITS WRITTEN ARGUMENT PATTERN - 68.8 per cent EXACTLY for non-recursive premises, 28.9 per cent for recursive ones, against a 23.1 per cent shuffled control over twenty permutations, with 27 of 28 `ast_node` costs equal to the group-by-kind count to the unit. RE-MEASURED HERE RATHER THAN TAKEN ON TRUST, and the half that transfers transfers cleanly: the conditioned count DOES separate the two rules whose text is identical and whose cost is 200x apart. `encloses_v[flow]` is 5 815 rows on this corpus and `encloses_s[code]` is 133, a 44x ratio that no property of the rule TEXT could show, and it is one group-by away. So the quantity is right and it is cheap. WHAT DOES NOT FOLLOW IS THE POLICY, and the counterexample is the case this item exists for. `has_return`'s repair was worth -23.3 per cent of its world and it leads with `ast_node(R, return_statement, _, _)`, the LARGER of its two candidate seeds: on this corpus `fn_node[code]` is 395 rows and the return statements are 400. A rule that seeds with the cheapest conditioned count picks `fn_node`, which IS the written order, and makes no repair at all - and on a corpus where the two numbers landed the other way round it would make the repair for a reason 1.3 per cent wide, which is a coin flip wearing a measurement's face. THE DIFFERENCE IS WHAT THE ESTIMATE IS ESTIMATING. Their number is the accuracy of a cost estimate for ONE PREMISE; an ordering policy is a claim about a WHOLE BODY, and what decides `has_return` is not either seed's size but the DIRECTION of the second literal - `ast_within(F, R)` with R bound walks a node's ancestors, a chain of about eight, and with F bound walks a function's descendants. Pricing that needs the sum of intermediate result sizes over the whole body, which is exactly the model this item built and measured: five for five backwards and nought for two forwards, failing 4.3x in the dangerous direction on `ident[code](U, Name)` - a premise whose bound argument is a VARIABLE filled by a join, where no group-by over the rule text names a bucket at all and the mean over 553 names is 147x below what the join asks for. THREE LIMITS, AND THEY DO NOT OVERLAP: a group-by is exact where the condition is a CONSTANT (their 68.8 per cent), useless where the condition is a JOIN VARIABLE (this session's 147x), and measuring the wrong instant where the relation is RECURSIVE (their 28.9 per cent at chance, and the same session's `ast_within` observed at 6 210 against a final 122 939). The remedy they name for the third - a cost model that IS a fixpoint over cardinalities, the engine's own shape over a counting semiring rather than a product over final ones - is the right shape and it is a kernel-sized piece of work. NONE OF THE THREE TOUCHES WHAT SHIPPED, which is the reason the cross-product hold is where the line was drawn: it reads no count of any kind, so it is below all three limits by construction. And `w_connected_and_badly_ordered` is where the group-by belongs, with the caveat this finding is: the count that decides such a body is the one on the JOIN, not the one on the seed.").
artifact_removed(f_an_exact_per_premise_estimate_does_not_make_a_seed_ordering_rule_correct, "docs/dogfood/2026-09-09-join-planner.md").


finding(f_asserting_a_fact_deletes_provenance_a_rule_already_earned, defect).
recorded(f_asserting_a_fact_deletes_provenance_a_rule_already_earned, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_asserting_a_fact_deletes_provenance_a_rule_already_earned, kernel_test).
finding_note(f_asserting_a_fact_deletes_provenance_a_rule_already_earned, "ASSERTING ONE UNRELATED FACT AND EVALUATING AGAIN DESTROYS PROVENANCE THAT HAS NOTHING TO DO WITH IT. Reported by the other session on this machine and REPRODUCED HERE INDEPENDENTLY before being written down. boot.rofl plus nine JS fact packs and six rule packs, NO SOURCE OF ANY KIND, evaluate; then assert `ast_node[code](nprobe1, if_statement, \"probe.js\", 1).` and evaluate again: distinct `derived_by` heads go 5 629 -> 5 278. FOUR HUNDRED AND FIFTY-ONE HEADS VANISH - `ignored` 229, `handled` 200, `unknown_because` 21, `guard_named` 1 - and they are exactly the relations that are BOTH ASSERTED IN A PACK AND DERIVABLE BY A RULE. The other session measured 227/188/19 on a smaller pack set: same relations, different sizes, which is what agreement looks like across two corpora. THE NET IS -351 AND THE LOSS IS 451, so about a hundred heads are GAINED by the same evaluation - stated as an inference from lost minus net rather than a direct measurement, and it is the part that does not fit the proposed mechanism. That mechanism - `derived_by` is written inside `if (newFiring)` and `store.support()` returns false for a signature it already holds, so a rebuild drops the row and never re-emits it - is purely SUBTRACTIVE and cannot produce a gain. So either there are two effects here or the rule is `re-emitted under a different key for some heads and not others`. A repair that makes the 451 return while leaving the 100 unexplained would make the count right and the invariant still broken. WHY IT IS SILENT, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT A DEFECT RATHER THAN A GAP: `support` still counts the firing and the `wit` line is still there, so nothing looks wrong from any direction a caller can see. `derived_by` is the QUERYABLE half of the provenance hypergraph, so every program that asserts anything after its first evaluation is asking `why` of a ledger with holes. The comment three lines above the code states the invariant it breaks - a firing is a witness row plus the fact plus a `derived_by` row, and the three arrive together and go away together. AND THE METHOD IS WORTH MORE THAN THE BUG. The gap was constant at 1, 2, 4, 8, 64 and 128 files, and A CONSTANT ACROSS THAT RANGE IS A STATEMENT ABOUT THE PROGRAM AND NOT THE CORPUS - which turned a 5.7M-fact search into a 22k one, then to zero-with-no-source, which turned a data question into a re-evaluation one. It also settled a two-day-old belief the other way: the discrepancy had been recorded as `the port over-derives and I believe the port is correct`, and the port was not over-deriving - it was the only one of the two still telling the truth. RESOLVED THE SAME HOUR, AND MY OBJECTION WAS RIGHT TO RAISE AND WRONG IN ITS GUESS. I said a purely subtractive mechanism cannot produce a gain, so either there were two effects or the rule was `re-emitted under a different key`. Neither. The other session MEASURED the gain instead of inferring it - LOST 0, GAINED 99, every one named - and the 99 are the AUDIT LAYER WAKING UP: a world of packs with no source has never scanned anything, so asserting one `ast_node` makes `scanned[audit](js)` true, `loader` records who loaded the relation, and every `kind_unexercised` verdict becomes derivable because there is finally a scan to be unexercised against. `derived_by($fact(scanned,audit,(js)), rb68cb31e, 0)` and `derived_by($fact(kind_unexercised,audit,(js,array_expression)), rfb9cc400, 0)` are two of them. So there is ONE effect, and my hundred was the boundary between `no code` and `some code` rather than anything about firings. THE CAUSE, in `clearDerived`: it drops facts where `!base && !frozen`, and `removeMany` deletes a fact`s firings WITH the fact - but a BASE fact is kept, so a fact both asserted in a pack and derivable by a rule keeps its firing signature while its `derived_by` row, being derived, is dropped. Next evaluation `support()` sees a signature it already holds, returns false, and the row inside `if (newFiring)` is never written again. The repair is one line - drop every unfrozen firing when the derived layer is dropped - because a firing is the record of a derivation that is about to be recomputed, while frozen provenance is a completed tick and rightly survives. END TO END over these packs, one file of eslint/lib: reference 22 659 facts against the port`s 22 659, dangling 0, where it had been 22 225 / 22 659 / 434. THE CHECK IS THE PART TO KEEP: `a repair that makes the 451 return while leaving the 100 unexplained would make the count right and the invariant still broken` - it passed both halves, nothing lost AND every gain named, and it is only a check because it was stated BEFORE the repair was seen. AND THE FOURTH RELATION WAS THE USEFUL DIFFERENCE: `guard_named` appears here and not in their smaller pack set, which is what says the class is `asserted AND derivable` rather than `those three relations`. Two pack sets disagreeing on the members and agreeing on the rule is worth more than either list. AND THE REPAIR MUST NOT BE TAKEN, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHERE THIS ENDED AN HOUR AGO. `nextver` carries `f_a_stale_firing_outlives_the_premise_it_rests_on`, recorded 2026-09-08: DIAGNOSED EXACTLY, REPAIRED THREE TIMES, EVERY REPAIR REVERTED. The one-line `clearDerived` narrowing described above IS the second of those attempts, line for line. It costs TWENTY tests on nextver`s suite - 1074 tests, 1054 pass, 20 fail - clustered on `reuse ~ scratch`, tick carrying, fork and save/restore, because THE REUSE PATH DEPENDS ON SURVIVING FIRINGS and clearing them is precisely what it cannot tolerate. THE DEEPEST PART IS WHY THE ROUND-TRIP BREAKS, and it is a statement about the system rather than about the repair: `reference round-trips its own seed` held BECAUSE BOTH PATHS LOST THE SAME ROWS. Stop losing them and the two paths stop agreeing - 560 witness rows the direct evaluation has against 1120 the replay has - so the witness set is HISTORY-DEPENDENT in a way the loss was masking. A direct evaluation accumulates witnesses across the several internal evaluations each `load` performs; a replay does not. A GREEN PROPERTY WAS RESTING ON TWO EQUAL BREAKAGES. AND THE LEDGER DID NOT CARRY IT ACROSS, WHICH IS A FINDING ABOUT THIS PROCESS AND NOT ABOUT THE ENGINE. All of the above lives in `facts/findings.rofl` ON NEXTVER and in NEITHER `modeljs` NOR `wip/curve` - checked, not assumed. I told the other session earlier today that `facts/findings.rofl` merges by name and is the durable cross-session channel, and that is TRUE ONLY BETWEEN BRANCHES THAT MERGE. nextver has never merged into modeljs, so the diagnosis, three attempts and the reason each failed were unreachable from this tree by any amount of care. Neither of us could have read it here. The practical consequence is not `read the ledger first` - which is right and which the other session correctly charged itself with - but that a defect diagnosed on one branch is INVISIBLE on another, and the more branches diverge the more of the ledger stops being a channel and becomes a private notebook. THE STATE, then: the defect is real, the port was right, the repair works and is not free, and the trade is provenance completeness against the reuse cache and the round-trip property. That is the owner`s to decide and neither session should ship it. WHAT IS NEW AND NOT IN THE PRIOR FINDING: the repair makes the two ENGINES agree to the fact - reference 22 659 = port 22 659, dangling 0, against 22 225 / 22 659 / 434 - which is the strongest argument the repair has and was not recorded anywhere before today. THE REPAIR IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN HERE. Two sessions editing `emit` at once is the has_return duplication with worse consequences, and three agents are working in worktrees off this tip. The other session holds the uncontested copy and will send what moves and what it costs, to be verified against the numbers above before it is taken.").
-- ===========================================================================
-- w_runtime_surface, 2026-09-09 — the runtime layer

finding(f_a_host_surface_that_does_not_name_its_host_is_wrong_in_both_directions, insight).
recorded(f_a_host_surface_that_does_not_name_its_host_is_wrong_in_both_directions, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_host_surface_that_does_not_name_its_host_is_wrong_in_both_directions, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_host_surface_that_does_not_name_its_host_is_wrong_in_both_directions, "`console`, `fetch`, `setTimeout`, `structuredClone`, `URL`, `TextEncoder`, `AbortController` and `performance` are in BOTH the browser and node, which is what makes a single `runtime_global` table look obviously right. MEASURED 2026-09-09 with the ECMAScript baseline subtracted from both hosts, by asking the TypeScript checker for the properties of `globalThis` under three lib configurations: lib.esnext alone declares 65 globals - those are the LANGUAGE and facts/js-lib-surface.rofl already owns them; lib.dom adds 866; @types/node adds 73; SIXTY-TWO are in both hosts. So the overlap really is most of node`s surface, AND 804 OF THE BROWSER`S 866 ADDITIONS ARE ABSENT FROM NODE - `document`, `window`, `alert`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `navigator` among them - and ELEVEN of node`s are absent from the browser: Buffer, __dirname, __filename, clearImmediate, exports, gc, global, module, process, require, setImmediate. A flat table built from lib.dom.d.ts, which is the file anyone reaches for first, would tell a node program it can call `alert`. The row that pays for the host column is the one this corpus has: `host_global_only_in[code](E, browser, `document`)` at runtime.mjs, in a file that also uses `console` and `fs`. AND THE SEPARATION IS NOT A GUESS EITHER WAY: `localStorage` IS in node 22`s declarations and looks browser-only to a reader, which is why the scan is read from the two packages rather than from a list somebody typed.").
addressed_by(f_a_host_surface_that_does_not_name_its_host_is_wrong_in_both_directions, "scanners/host_lib.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_host_surface_that_does_not_name_its_host_is_wrong_in_both_directions, "test/js-host.test.ts").

finding(f_a_runtime_records_arrival_and_never_removal, insight).
recorded(f_a_runtime_records_arrival_and_never_removal, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_runtime_records_arrival_and_never_removal, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_runtime_records_arrival_and_never_removal, "THE OWNER`S CAVEAT ON THE RUNTIME AXIS WAS THAT `includes` ASSUMES MONOTONE INCLUSION AND RUNTIMES REMOVE THINGS, with the instruction: do not author the inclusion, DERIVE it; if you cannot derive it from what is on disk, say so and make it a claim with a witness rather than a bare row. MEASURED over the whole of @types/node 22.20.1, which is ONE SNAPSHOT: `@since v<x>` appears on 718 module members and is the ARRIVAL record; `@deprecated` on 26 module exports, 23 of them dated, 17 naming a replacement; and REMOVAL HAS NO TAG AT ALL. A member removed upstream simply STOPS BEING IN THE FILE, and an absent symbol is indistinguishable from one that never existed - so no query over this package can produce a counterexample to monotone inclusion at any effort, and the derivation the instruction asks for does not exist here. Seeing one needs a SECOND SNAPSHOT, @types/node@18 beside @types/node@22, which is a package this repository does not have. SO THE CLAIM IS WRITTEN WITH ITS COUNTEREXAMPLE RELATION BESIDE IT: `runtime_drops[audit](A, B, Spec, Key)` in rules/js-host.rofl is exactly the row that would refute `runtime_includes`, it is written out in full, and it is EMPTY BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by luck - with one snapshot `arrived_by` is a threshold on one number and a threshold is monotone. test/js-host.test.ts asserts the emptiness AND its two positive controls, so the row is an answer rather than a misspelling. The day a second snapshot lands it populates with no other change to the pack. WHAT IS DERIVABLE IS THE DEPRECATION, which is the visible half of a removal, and `host_call_deprecated[audit]` reports it at a call site.").
addressed_by(f_a_runtime_records_arrival_and_never_removal, "facts/js-host.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_runtime_records_arrival_and_never_removal, "rules/js-host.rofl").

finding(f_the_runtime_api_is_where_replaced_by_earns_its_keep, insight).
recorded(f_the_runtime_api_is_where_replaced_by_earns_its_keep, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_runtime_api_is_where_replaced_by_earns_its_keep, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_runtime_api_is_where_replaced_by_earns_its_keep, "rules/js-env-api.rofl SAYS IN SO MANY WORDS THAT `lib_replaced_by` IS NEARLY EMPTY AND THAT THIS IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN A GAP: 41 `@deprecated` tags across the ecmascript libs and TWO name a replacement, `trimStart` and `trimEnd`, because there IS no replacement for `blink()` or `substr()`. It then names where the relation WOULD earn its keep - `a RUNTIME`s API, `fs.rmdir` for `fs.rm`, which is a source this repository does not have`. IT HAS IT NOW. @types/node carries 137 `@deprecated` tags in its text of which 76 name a replacement; at the grain scanners/host_lib.ts works at - a module`s OWN exports, not the members of the classes they return - 26 deprecations, 23 dated, and 17 naming a replacement. Eight times denser at the same grain, and `host_call_remedy[audit](C, `node:fs`, `exists`, `stat`)` is a live row in the runtime fixture. TWO SPELLINGS HAD TO BE READ AND ONLY ONE IS OBVIOUS: `Use `fs.rm()` instead` and `Use {@link stat} or {@link access} instead` are the same claim in TSDoc`s two link forms, and a scan reading only the backticked one loses a third of the relation while looking complete. AND THE NUMBER IN THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS NOTE WAS WRONG IN THE INSTRUCTIVE WAY: it quoted 76 against 2, comparing a whole-text grep against a per-member scan - two populations, one comparison, which is HANDOFF`s `a number I reported that was wrong` arriving again. Both numbers are now quoted with the population each measures.").
addressed_by(f_the_runtime_api_is_where_replaced_by_earns_its_keep, "scanners/host_lib.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_runtime_api_is_where_replaced_by_earns_its_keep, "rules/js-host.rofl").

finding(f_a_guard_written_three_times_cannot_be_killed_by_a_mutant_of_one, defect).
recorded(f_a_guard_written_three_times_cannot_be_killed_by_a_mutant_of_one, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_guard_written_three_times_cannot_be_killed_by_a_mutant_of_one, decision).
finding_note(f_a_guard_written_three_times_cannot_be_killed_by_a_mutant_of_one, "THE RUNTIME AXIS HAS TWO FAMILIES - node and browser - AND THEY ARE INCOMPARABLE BY CONSTRUCTION, so every rule that walks a version against a surface carries a family premise. It is written THREE TIMES: in `arrived_by[code]` (`runtime_version(R, F, _)` binding F for `host_member_since(F, ...)`), in `host_member_absent[audit]` (the same pair), and in `host_lost[audit]` (`runtime_version(To, F, _), runtime_version(From, F, _)`). A MUTANT OF ANY ONE OF THE THREE IS INVISIBLE FROM THE FAR END, which is `orphan_claim[audit]`s recorded failure - a constraint enforced by independent copies is a constraint no mutant of one copy can find - arriving in a fourth place. MEASURED: hard-coding `node` back into `arrived_by`s join changes NOTHING, not `arrived_by`s own row count and not `host_lost`s rows. AND THERE IS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT REASON THE SAME MUTANT SURVIVES, which is the more interesting one: `browser_evergreen` is the only member of the other family and its version is 0, so `Sv <= VN` excludes node`s entire surface from it whatever the family premise says - every `@since` in @types/node is at least v0.0.2. The guard is therefore CORRECT AND CURRENTLY REDUNDANT, and it becomes killable the moment a second family gets a version number that orders above anything in node`s table. THE MUTANT IS NOT DELETED. test/js-host.test.ts asserts the SURVIVAL and both halves of the CAUSE - `arrived_by[code](browser_evergreen, S, K)` is 0 with `arrived_by[code](node18, S, K)` non-empty as the control, and the three copies are asserted present by text - so the day the cause goes away the test says so, which a deleted mutant could not. THE DECISION OWED: whether a family premise should be derived once (a `same_family(R, Spec)` relation the three rules read) the way CLAUDE.md`s second remedy prescribes, or left as three copies with this note beside them. Three is small; the next relation over the same arguments makes it four, silently, which is exactly how the recorded incident went.").
addressed_by(f_a_guard_written_three_times_cannot_be_killed_by_a_mutant_of_one, "test/js-host.test.ts").

finding(f_the_rule_shape_census_is_a_generated_pack_with_no_gate, defect).
recorded(f_the_rule_shape_census_is_a_generated_pack_with_no_gate, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_rule_shape_census_is_a_generated_pack_with_no_gate, decision).
finding_note(f_the_rule_shape_census_is_a_generated_pack_with_no_gate, "facts/js-lib-surface.rofl IS GENERATED AND test/js-lib-surface.test.ts REGENERATES IT AND COMPARES, so a hand edit or a stale commit is a failing test. facts/rule-shape.rofl is generated by scanners/rule_shape.ts, which walks `rules/` and `examples/` off the DISK, and NOTHING REGENERATES IT. MEASURED 2026-09-09 on a clean checkout of origin/modeljs plus one new rules pack: re-running the generator produces 7 776 insertions and 4 903 DELETIONS against the committed file. Adding one pack of 40 rules cannot delete 4 903 lines, so the committed census was already stale by most of that before this branch existed - it describes a tree that has moved. THE COMMITTED FILE WAS RESTORED RATHER THAN REGENERATED and that is the decision being recorded rather than taken: a 12 679-line rewrite on a feature branch would conflict with every parallel branch in the wave, and HANDOFF`s own account of the 2026-09-09 merges is that a scripted resolution across a large generated file is how 2 432 nodes left the corpus in one step. WHAT THE GATE WOULD COST is one test in the shape test/js-lib-surface.test.ts already has - regenerate, compare, name the command - plus one integrator pass to make the committed file true. WHAT ITS ABSENCE COSTS TODAY is that `rules/rule-shape.rofl` and scanners/rule_shape_report.ts answer questions about a tree from four thousand lines ago, and no reader of those answers is told. THIS IS CLAUDE.md`s OWN SENTENCE about a flag nothing exercises, one book over: a generated pack that nothing regenerates cannot go red, so its drift is not observable from any gate we own.").

finding(f_a_free_identifier_is_a_negative_over_a_file_and_the_model_binds_five_ways, insight).
recorded(f_a_free_identifier_is_a_negative_over_a_file_and_the_model_binds_five_ways, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_free_identifier_is_a_negative_over_a_file_and_the_model_binds_five_ways, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_free_identifier_is_a_negative_over_a_file_and_the_model_binds_five_ways, "A MODULE IMPORT AND A GLOBAL ARE NOT ONE QUESTION AND MERGING THEM LOSES THE ONLY FACT THAT SEPARATES THEM. `node:fs` arrives through a boundary the model already owns - rules/js-modules.rofl classifies the specifier, canonicalises it, resolves it and BINDS a local name, four relations that already existed - so the module door is four positive joins and no new scanner contract. `console` arrives from nowhere: it has no declaration, no specifier and no binding site, and THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES AN IDENTIFIER A GLOBAL IS THAT NOTHING IN THE PROGRAM BINDS IT, which is a NEGATIVE and a completely different rule shape. THE MODEL BINDS A NAME FIVE WAYS AND `sees_binder[code]` COVERS FOUR OF THEM: declarators and the three destructuring forms. It does NOT cover parameters, function-declaration names, class names or import locals - so a rule reading `not sees_binder(E, _)` would call every parameter a free identifier and attribute io to a program that does none. `name_bound_in[code]` therefore has five arms and is FILE-scoped rather than scope-scoped: under-reporting a global in a file that also shadows it is a missed row, over-reporting one is a false accusation, and the effect layer downstream cannot tell those apart. AND `fn_name[code]` IS THE WRONG RELATION TO BORROW FOR THE FUNCTION ARM, measured: it is a superset covering OBJECT AND CLASS METHODS, so a file containing `{ log() {} }` would have suppressed the global `console`. The arm reads the function declaration`s own `id` child instead. test/fixtures/js-host/shadow.mjs binds one name per form and leaves `queueMicrotask` alone, because a rule with no negation and a rule whose negation is total look identical on a corpus that never shadows anything.").
addressed_by(f_a_free_identifier_is_a_negative_over_a_file_and_the_model_binds_five_ways, "rules/js-host.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_free_identifier_is_a_negative_over_a_file_and_the_model_binds_five_ways, "test/fixtures/js-host/shadow.mjs").
-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT THE EFFECT LAYER FOUND, 2026-09-09 (w_effect_layer).

finding(f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work, insight).
recorded(f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work, doc).
finding_note(f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work, "THE FOUNDING FINDING SAID A LATTICE WITH A BOTTOM AND A TOP IS THE POINT BECAUSE EVERY QUESTION IS A JOIN OR A COMPARISON, AND HALF OF THAT TURNED OUT TO BE FREE. If an effect is carried as a SET of labels - `eff_here[flow](Node, Label, Heap)` - then the join over a body is TWO RULES WITH THE SAME HEAD, which is what a union already is in this language. No join operation is invoked anywhere in the propagation, and the whole of section 3 of rules/js-effects.rofl is a seed, a `nearest_v` and a transitive closure. Carrying the effect as a NAME instead would have needed an explicit join at every step and a lattice operation in the hot path. THE ORDER IS THE PART THAT HAD TO BE BUILT, and it is built over the NAMES rather than over the rows, because a name is what a human reads and a comparison between two named landmarks is what `is this call at least as effectful as that one` actually asks. Fourteen names, 55 `eff_leq` pairs, 41 strict ones, and the join is total at 196 pairs. THE SOUNDNESS CONDITION IS A THEOREM AND IT IS ASSERTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED: the named family is not closed under UNION - `pure` joined with `alloc` is `<div, exn, alloc>` and no name denotes that row - so the join is the least NAME above the union, which exists and is unique exactly when the family is closed under INTERSECTION and contains the full row. That is a Moore family, and its four failure modes are `join_missing`, `join_ambiguous`, `meet_missing`, `meet_ambiguous`, all empty. `eff_top` and `eff_bot` are DERIVED sets rather than two claimed names, and they come back `{top}` and `{total}`. THE ONE PLACE THE JOIN IS READ BY SOMETHING OTHER THAN A TEST is `eff_join_short[audit]`: a caller's effect joined with its callee's must give the caller's own back wherever nothing is discharged, which is monotonicity of the propagation stated through the operation the finding asked for. Empty over 366 call edges.").
witness_in(f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work, w_js_effects, "eff_top(T)", 1).
witness_in(f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work, w_js_effects, "eff_bot(B)", 1).
addressed_by(f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work, "rules/js-effects.rofl").

finding(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, insight).
recorded(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, doc).
finding_note(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, "THE OWNER ASKED FOR `http:get` RATHER THAN A BARE `io`, ON THE GROUND THAT IT IS CLEARER, AND THE INTERESTING PART IS WHERE THE NAME COMES FROM. The refusal in `f_the_effect_taxonomy_already_exists_and_it_is_a_lattice` was that the taxonomy is not ours to invent, and a concrete effect opens an infinite vocabulary where Koka's is closed at eight - so the only way to have both is for a concrete effect to be DERIVED. Measured what is derivable and the answer splits the name in half. THE SURFACE HALF IS FREE. `prototype_of[flow]` reads the receiver's KIND, so `[1,2,3].join(\",\")` yields the surface `array` with no library and no `.d.ts` at all: ten call sites in this corpus already carry a (surface, operation) pair - array:join twice, array:at, string:concat, string:replaceAll, string:substr, string:trimLeft, regexp:test twice, bigint:toString - and eight distinct pairs. For an ambient name the same half is equally free, because a declaration file FIXES which surface a name belongs to: that is `lib.es*.d.ts` for `Promise` and `Math`, `lib.dom.d.ts` and `@types/node` for `console` and `fs`. THE OPERATION HALF IS FREE TOO - it is the member name. THE MAP INTO THE LATTICE IS NOT FREE AND NOTHING IN A SIGNATURE SUPPLIES IT. `fs.readFileSync(path): string` and `fs.writeFileSync(path, data): void` differ in their return types and in nothing that says one reads and one writes; `Math.max` and `Math.random` have the same shape and are `total` and `ndet`. So `ambient_effect(Surface, Member, EffectName)` is a table somebody builds MEMBER BY MEMBER, and it is the only authored part of the column. THE CONSEQUENCE IS THE HONEST ANSWER TO THE MAJORITY CASE: 251 of this corpus's 261 unresolved call sites have no concrete effect at all and the other ten have no map, so every one of them is an OPEN CELL WITH AN OWNER rather than a label. `ndet` is the sharpest instance - it has no seed anywhere in the rules and cannot have one, because nothing in the LANGUAGE is nondeterministic and `Math.random`, `Date.now` and `crypto.getRandomValues` are all ambient members. AND THE TWO COLUMNS ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, which is why this is a refinement and not a replacement: `http:get` and `fs:write` are incomparable and always will be, so a concrete column has no join and cannot answer what a body's effect is; the lattice has one and cannot tell a human what a function does. WHAT THIS OWES grafema's effects-db, the owner's own prior art: its RUNTIME SPLIT is exactly the surface half, promoted from a partition of the taxonomy to the provenance of a name; and its SPAWN and MESSAGE_PASSING are not lattice elements at all but concrete effects - `child_process:spawn`, `worker:postMessage` - each mapping into `io`. Moving those two down a level is the clearest single argument for having two columns.").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, w_js_effects, "concrete_effect[flow](C, S, Op)", 1).
addressed_by(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, "rules/js-effects.rofl").

finding(f_a_try_with_no_handler_catches_nothing_and_caught_here_says_it_does, defect).
recorded(f_a_try_with_no_handler_catches_nothing_and_caught_here_says_it_does, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_try_with_no_handler_catches_nothing_and_caught_here_says_it_does, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_try_with_no_handler_catches_nothing_and_caught_here_says_it_does, "`caught_here[code]` ASKS WHETHER A NODE SITS IN THE `block` OF A `try` IN ITS OWN FUNCTION AND DOES NOT ASK WHETHER THAT TRY HAS A HANDLER. `try { risky(); } finally { close(); }` catches nothing at all - a finalizer runs on the way out and does not stop the throw - and every relation standing on `caught_here` would report the throw as caught: `throws_outright`, `may_throw`, `thrown_by`, `caught_value` and, until this item, the effect layer's discharge. FOUND BY REBUILDING THE SAME MACHINERY FOR A SECOND PURPOSE rather than by reading, which is the argument for doing that at all: the effect propagation had to discharge exn PER LABEL, so the handler had to be located, and locating it is what made its absence visible. IT HAS NO SITE IN THIS CORPUS AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: 11 try statements, 11 `handler` children, one `finalizer` and it sits on a try that also has a handler. So the two relations agree row for row today - `eff_exn_only[audit]` and `may_throw_only[audit]` are both empty over 395 functions - and the agreement is NOT evidence that the guard is unnecessary, which is the trap this ledger already names for a mutant that cannot run. THE EFFECT LAYER'S OWN DISCHARGE IS THE STRICTER ONE: `eff_catch_here[code]` requires `ast_child(TS, handler, 0, _)`. `caught_here` is left as it is deliberately, because changing a relation four other rules read on a corpus that cannot show the difference is a change nothing here could check - and the two-relation disagreement is now the detector: the day a try-with-only-a-finally enters the corpus, `may_throw_only[audit]` goes red with a function's name in it. A DEFECT THAT IS CURRENTLY UNREACHABLE, WITH A LIVE TRIPWIRE, is a better state than a silent repair.").
witness_absent(f_a_try_with_no_handler_catches_nothing_and_caught_here_says_it_does, w_js_effects,
               "eff_try_arm[flow](T, unhandled)", "eff_try_arm[flow](T, handled)").
addressed_by(f_a_try_with_no_handler_catches_nothing_and_caught_here_says_it_does, "rules/js-effects.rofl").

finding(f_a_fifth_layer_cost_one_per_cent_because_the_join_sits_at_the_function_boundary, insight).
recorded(f_a_fifth_layer_cost_one_per_cent_because_the_join_sits_at_the_function_boundary, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_fifth_layer_cost_one_per_cent_because_the_join_sits_at_the_function_boundary, doc).
finding_note(f_a_fifth_layer_cost_one_per_cent_because_the_join_sits_at_the_function_boundary, "A WHOLE SEMANTIC LAYER FOR 1.39 PER CENT OF THE ROWS. Measured on the corpus world, packs before facts, one load: peakRows 260 568 -> 264 181 and so the world moves from 52.1 to 52.8 per cent of the engine's 500 000-row space wall. THE REASON IS ONE DESIGN CHOICE AND IT IS WORTH CARRYING TO THE NEXT LAYER: the join happens at the FUNCTION BOUNDARY, through `nearest_v[flow]`, and not by a congruence rule over `ast_within`. A per-node effect row would have been a set of labels for every node in the tree - `ast_within` alone is 40 229 rows here - and every question this layer has actually been asked is about a function or a call, so the per-node rows would have been cost with no reader. `eff_latent` is 401 rows and `eff_here` is 786. THE SECOND SAVING IS THE ORDERING `f_the_engine_has_no_join_planner` ASKED FOR, applied before it could bite: `eff_mutable_name` is a handful of rows binding both Name and File, so `ident_in[code]` - which the store indexes on exactly that pair - is a probe rather than an enumeration of 2 439 identifiers; and `eff_calls` leads with `resolves`, which binds both ends. WHAT WAS NOT MEASURED, and it is the honest limit: this is a 434 000-fact corpus of five fixture files. `eff_reaches` is a transitive closure over the call graph, 558 rows over 366 edges here, and its worst case is quadratic in the functions - on a real corpus that is the first relation in this pack to look at.").
addressed_by(f_a_fifth_layer_cost_one_per_cent_because_the_join_sits_at_the_function_boundary, "rules/js-effects.rofl").

finding(f_a_layer_declared_without_owners_is_not_a_state_the_queue_can_be_in, insight).
recorded(f_a_layer_declared_without_owners_is_not_a_state_the_queue_can_be_in, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_layer_declared_without_owners_is_not_a_state_the_queue_can_be_in, decision).
finding_note(f_a_layer_declared_without_owners_is_not_a_state_the_queue_can_be_in, "THE BRIEF FOR THIS ITEM SAID `layer(effect)` WAS ALREADY ON THE BRANCH TIP AND IT WAS NOT, AND THE CORRECTION IS BETTER THAN THE ORIGINAL PLAN. The line had been written in the owner's working tree AFTER the push, so `git reset --hard origin/modeljs` could not find it; a grep for `layer(effect)` over facts, rules, test, src, scanners and docs returned nothing, which is what caught it before a single line was written. THE PART WORTH KEEPING IS THE REASON THE SLIP WAS THE RIGHT SHAPE ANYWAY. `layer(L)` is ONE FACT and it opens one cell per declared kind - a hundred here - and `open_cell[audit]` requires every open cell to have an owner BY NAME while `sweeper` must be 0. So a commit containing only the declaration puts the queue in a state its own audits call a lie, for as long as it takes somebody to answer a hundred cells. The declaration and the ownership therefore belong in ONE commit, and `layer_unauthorised[audit]` is the row that makes it impossible to do quietly: it reported `effect` until `layer_authorised(effect)` was added beside it, which is the gate working rather than a formality. GENERAL FORM: a fact whose cost is measured in CELLS is not a fact that can be landed on its own, and this is the first one the repository has met. The four other layers were all declared by the item that also answered them, so the constraint was true and never tested. THE PREMISE-CHECK IS THE TRANSFERABLE HALF: the brief asserted a state of the tree, the state was three greps away, and both greps took under a minute. Every brief that opens with `X is already on the branch` is a claim about a store.").

finding(f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind, insight).
recorded(f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind, doc).
finding_note(f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind, "SIXTY-FOUR CELLS OF THIS LAYER WERE EASY AND THE INSTRUCTION WAS `NO SHORTCUTS`, SO THE CRITERION HAD TO BE WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE THE ROWS WERE. What was used: a cell is `handled` when the rules derive the contribution for EVERY site of that kind in the corpus, or when the residue is another kind's cell; it is `ignored` when the construct performs no operation, which in this taxonomy is the positive statement that its own row is the bottom; it is OPEN when there is a site whose contribution the layer cannot state. THE CRITERION IMMEDIATELY SPLIT THINGS A SWEEP WOULD HAVE JOINED. `reg_exp_literal` is not a literal for this layer - `/x/g` allocates a fresh object with a mutable `lastIndex` every time it is reached - while `template_literal` looks like it allocates and does not, because a string has no identity; it is open for a different reason entirely, that `${x}` coerces. `class_declaration` is not latent although the other four definition kinds are, because a class runs its static blocks where it is written. `binary_expression` is open because `a + b` calls `valueOf`. AND IT PRODUCED A SHAPE THE MATRIX HAD NOT SEEN: A CELL THAT IS OPEN WHILE A RULE FIRES ON ITS KIND. `new_expression` is in `eff_alloc_kind` and seeds `alloc` for all 32 of its sites, and its cell is open, because the CONSTRUCTOR's effect is the ambient question. `identifier` seeds `read<local>` for every mutable binding and is open because a free name needs the surface. That is not an inconsistency: `handled` is a claim about the CELL and the verdict machinery reads claims rather than rules, so a rule may be right about half a kind while the cell stays honest about the other half. The alternative - ticking the cell because a rule exists - is `f_a_cell_can_be_ticked_and_partial_and_the_matrix_calls_that_a_stale_excuse` by name. THE ARITHMETIC: 100 cells, 29 modelled, 52 waived, 1 irreducible (`debugger` is `runtime_dependent`: with a debugger attached the statement is `io` and with none it is `total`, and which holds is decided at run time), 18 open across four items and one that already existed.").
finding_note(f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind, "AMENDED THE SAME DAY, AND THE SHAPE THIS FINDING NAMES TURNED OUT TO BE THE NORMAL CASE RATHER THAN A CURIOSITY. The arithmetic above - 29 modelled, 52 waived, 1 irreducible, 18 open - is now 33 / 53 / 1 / 13, moved by w_effect_module_evaluation, which closed five cells. THE PART WORTH AMENDING IS THE OTHER ITEM. w_effect_implicit_coercion modelled all THREE of its kinds and closed NONE of them: 369 coerced operands, 223 primitive, 5 in-program objects and 141 the value layer did not trace, and those 141 are the cell. So this finding now has a second instance that arrived deliberately rather than as a surprise, and the criterion it records was applied in advance instead of being discovered afterwards. THE CRITERION ALSO GAINED A THIRD CLASS the day it was applied twice: `f_a_missing_file_and_a_missing_table_are_not_the_same_open_cell` splits the residue into a missing FILE (answer it - the import cells are `handled` although three sites name a module outside the corpus), a missing TABLE (own it - `call_expression`) and a missing DERIVATION (open it - the coercion cells). Only the first two had names before.").
addressed_by(f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind, "facts/js-effects.rofl").
finding(f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart, insight).
recorded(f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart, "THE STANDARD-LIBRARY SURFACE WAS PROTOTYPE METHODS AND NOTHING ELSE, and the hole was not that some names were missing - it was that `JSON.parse`, `Promise.all`, `Object.entries` and `Math.max` are a DIFFERENT QUESTION that `lib_member(Proto, Key, Release)` cannot be widened to hold. A global has THREE surfaces and TypeScript`s own declarations keep them apart, so the model can too: the BINDING (`declare var Promise: PromiseConstructor` - does the name exist, and since when), the STATICS (`interface PromiseConstructor` - what hangs off the NAME), and the PROTOTYPE (`interface Promise<T>` - what hangs off an INSTANCE). Collapsing any two loses a claim. MEASURED 2026-09-09 over node_modules/typescript/lib: 47 globals, 226 statics, 12 releases, all dated by the same `/// <reference lib=` composition that already dates the prototype half - so the era question now reaches `Object.hasOwn` (es2022) and `Promise.allSettled` (es2020) as well as `array.at`. AND THE FORMS ARE NOT UNIFORM, which is the half a hand-written table would have averaged away: FOUR declaration shapes, counted rather than assumed - 40 `constructor_binding` (`declare var X: XConstructor`), 3 `namespace_object` (`declare var X: X` - Math, JSON, Atomics: no constructor, no instance surface, the members ARE the whole of it), 2 `namespace_block` (`declare namespace X` - Reflect, Intl, which a walk reading only `declare var` misses entirely), and 2 `plain_value` (NaN, Infinity: no member surface whatever). The form is a COLUMN and not a comment, which is what lets `es_construct_not_constructor[audit]` say that `new Math()` is a TypeError and WHY. THE KEY/CALL SPLIT IS READ RATHER THAN ASSERTED, and this is the cheapest thing in the pack: a well-known symbol is a KEY - `Symbol.iterator` exists to be written as a computed property name - and TypeScript spells the difference structurally, PropertySignature against MethodSignature. So `lib_static_shape` costs one predicate in the scanner and gets `Math.PI` and `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` right for the same reason, without anybody enumerating the well-known symbols. AND NOT ONE STATIC CARRIES `@deprecated`, over all 226, against seventeen on the eight prototypes - so the pack declares no relation for that at all, the same shape `lib_replaced_by` already records: a set that is empty in the SOURCE rather than empty here.").
addressed_by(f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart, "scanners/ts_lib.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart, "rules/js-globals.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_global_has_three_surfaces_and_the_library_keeps_them_apart, "test/js-globals.test.ts").

finding(f_globalthis_is_the_one_global_the_library_cannot_date, insight).
recorded(f_globalthis_is_the_one_global_the_library_cannot_date, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_globalthis_is_the_one_global_the_library_cannot_date, doc).
finding_note(f_globalthis_is_the_one_global_the_library_cannot_date, "`globalThis` WAS ON THE LIST OF GLOBALS TO COVER AND IT IS NOT IN THE SOURCE. Grepped 2026-09-09 across every lib.es*.d.ts TypeScript ships: the only occurrence in the whole directory is `globalThis.IteratorObject` inside lib.esnext.iterator.d.ts, which is a USE and not a declaration. There is no `declare var globalThis`, because it is a compiler intrinsic. So the scanner emits no row for it, and the corpus side reports it through `global_unattributed[audit]` beside `console` - the RUNTIME residue - even though it is a language feature of es2020. THE POINT IS THE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER RATHER THAN THE ONE NAME. A generated table can only carry what its source carries, and the temptation is to hand-add the one row that is obviously missing - which would make the pack a hand-written table with a generator`s header, and would put ONE row in it that no regeneration reproduces. The absence is asserted instead, in test/js-globals.test.ts, so it goes red the day TypeScript starts declaring it. AND IT IS A WORKED EXAMPLE OF THE CEILING THE PROTOTYPE HALF ALREADY STATED - `this reads DECLARATIONS, not semantics` - reaching a name the language has and the declarations do not. Anyone wanting `globalThis` dated needs a second source, and the honest place for it is the same table the RUNTIME half will need for `console`, `process` and `fetch`.").
addressed_by(f_globalthis_is_the_one_global_the_library_cannot_date, "facts/js-globals.rofl").

finding(f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves, defect).
recorded(f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves, "`const holder = new Map(); holder.set(k, v)` IS OUTSIDE BOTH HALVES OF THE STANDARD LIBRARY AND NOTHING SAID SO UNTIL THE BRIDGE MADE IT VISIBLE. The globals surface names the BINDING - `lib_global(Map, es2015, constructor_binding)` - and `lib_global_prototype` bridges a global to the lower-case prototype atom `lib_member` is keyed by, for EIGHT of the forty-seven. `Map` has no bridge row, and the reason is one rule: `kind_prototype` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl maps a node KIND to a prototype and has no arm for `new_expression`, so `prototype_of[flow]` is empty for a constructed value, `stdlib_member[audit]` never fires, and the call reaches neither `lib_call[code]` nor `es_static[code]`. MEASURED on the fixture: `stdlib_member[audit]` restricted to globals.mjs is EMPTY while the file calls `holder.set`. THE BRIDGE BEING PARTIAL IS THE INSTRUMENT, which is why it is emitted as rows rather than as a note: eight globals have a prototype this model can reach and thirty-nine do not, and the missing rows name them. `Promise`, `Map`, `Set`, `WeakMap`, `Date` and every typed array are in the second group, and `.then`, `.set`, `.add`, `.getTime` are ordinary calls in real code. THE REPAIR IS NOT MORE `kind_prototype` ROWS. A `new_expression` whose callee resolves to a class IN this program already resolves through `resolves[code]`; what is missing is the case where the callee is an ES GLOBAL, and that arm can now be written because `es_global_construct[code](X, Name, Rel)` exists - it is a join from a construction to a global name to a prototype atom, and the pieces are all standing. What it needs first is a decision about whether `prototype_of[flow]` should carry a value the corpus cannot see the class of, which is the same question `w_prototype_of_a_value` answered for literals and did not answer for constructions.").

finding(f_a_generated_pack_with_no_regeneration_gate_has_already_rotted, defect).
recorded(f_a_generated_pack_with_no_regeneration_gate_has_already_rotted, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_generated_pack_with_no_regeneration_gate_has_already_rotted, decision).
finding_note(f_a_generated_pack_with_no_regeneration_gate_has_already_rotted, "`facts/js-lib-surface.rofl` IS GENERATED AND test/js-lib-surface.test.ts REGENERATES IT AND COMPARES, so a hand edit is a red test rather than a silent divergence. `facts/rule-shape.rofl` is generated by scanners/rule_shape.ts and NOTHING IN THE TREE COMPARES IT. Measured 2026-09-09, going to add two new rule packs to it: regenerating changes 12 338 lines, of which 12 088 have nothing to do with those packs - `pos_shares` rows appearing on rules in examples/ditto, examples/drip and examples/iffy that the committed file does not carry. The generator is DETERMINISTIC, checked by running it twice on the same tree and diffing the two outputs, so this is staleness and not noise. WHAT MAKES IT WORSE THAN AN ORDINARY STALE FILE is that `rules/rule-shape.rofl` reasons over it: a census of cross products and premise shapes across the whole tree, answering questions about rules whose current text it no longer describes. It fails in the SAFE direction - it under-reports, because it is missing whatever grew - which is precisely the class CLAUDE.md names as worse than a red gate, because nothing will ever notice. THIS BRANCH DID NOT REGENERATE IT, deliberately: a 12 000-line diff nobody asked for, mixed into a feature commit, is the worst possible place to discover which of those lines are correct. The decision is the owner`s and it is a small one - either the pack gets the same compare-and-fail ritual its neighbour has, in which case somebody must first land the 12 000 lines as their own commit, or the pack is declared a snapshot and its header says so. What it cannot stay is a generated file with a generator nobody runs.").

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE COERCION AND MODULE-EVALUATION ITEMS, 2026-09-09
-- (w_effect_implicit_coercion, w_effect_module_evaluation).

finding(f_the_operand_decides_a_coercion_and_the_operator_only_says_whether_to_ask, insight).
recorded(f_the_operand_decides_a_coercion_and_the_operator_only_says_whether_to_ask, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_operand_decides_a_coercion_and_the_operator_only_says_whether_to_ask, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_operand_decides_a_coercion_and_the_operator_only_says_whether_to_ask, "THE ITEM WAS WRITTEN AS A QUESTION ABOUT OPERATORS AND THE MECHANISM TURNED OUT TO BE ABOUT OPERANDS. `a + b` runs `valueOf`, `-x` runs `valueOf`, a template interpolation runs `toString` - an operator whose semantics is a call, which is `accessor_call` in a third syntax - and the item's own note said the operator table separates the cases. It does, and it separates far less than it looks: a conversion method is a MEMBER OF THE OPERAND, so what decides whether user code runs is what the operand can be. THE THREE ANSWERS ARE TOTAL AND TWO OF THEM ARE POSITIVE. A PRIMITIVE has no conversion method to run - ToPrimitive of a string or a number is the identity, so a sum of two string literals cannot call anything user-written, which is exactly Koka's answer for reading an immutable binding arriving through an operator. AN IN-PROGRAM OBJECT THAT OVERRIDES NEITHER `valueOf` NOR `toString` converts through the language's own prototype and performs nothing, and that is the statement no rule keyed on the operator could make. AN OBJECT THAT OVERRIDES ONE IS AN ORDINARY CALL and propagates through a second arm of `eff_latent` - not through `resolves`, because the call graph is measured against V8's own stack frames and knows nothing about a conversion. MEASURED over 369 coerced operands in the five call fixtures: 223 primitive, 5 in-program objects of which NOT ONE overrides either key, 141 the value layer did not trace. THE 141 ARE THE CELL AND THEY STAY OPEN. An untraced operand MAY be an object with an override, and the honest answer is a positive frontier relation - the shape `eff_call_unattributed[flow]` already has - rather than a seed. Seeding the TOP of the lattice was tried and refused on a measurement: it takes `eff_label_unseen` from two labels to none and makes `effect_of` `top` for most of the corpus, which is the layer's sharpest statement destroyed to say something nobody can use. THE OPERATOR TABLE IS A COMPLEMENT AND THAT IS THE SAFE DIRECTION: five spellings that run no user code at all are named - `===`, `!==`, `typeof`, `void`, `!` - and everything else converts, so an operator nobody classified is assumed to call. TWO ARE DELIBERATELY OUTSIDE THE MECHANISM AND NAMED RATHER THAN FORGOTTEN: `x instanceof C` consults C's `Symbol.hasInstance` and `delete o[k]` performs a WRITE on top of converting its key, so neither is ToPrimitive - `eff_op_beyond[flow]` is the row, and neither has a site in this corpus, which is what the item had to answer for before writing a rule. AND `Symbol.toPrimitive` IS ABSENT FROM THE KEY TABLE ON PURPOSE: it is a COMPUTED key and what spelling `key_name[code]` gives one is w_computed_key_names' open question, so guessing the string would be a well-formed query about a real relation that nothing objects to.").
witness_absent(f_the_operand_decides_a_coercion_and_the_operator_only_says_whether_to_ask, w_js_effects,
               "member_value[flow](O, \"valueOf\", V)", "member_value[flow](O, \"riveted\", V)").
addressed_by(f_the_operand_decides_a_coercion_and_the_operator_only_says_whether_to_ask, "rules/js-effects.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_operand_decides_a_coercion_and_the_operator_only_says_whether_to_ask, "test/js-effects.test.ts").

finding(f_the_join_over_a_module_graph_is_the_same_union_the_join_over_a_body_is, insight).
recorded(f_the_join_over_a_module_graph_is_the_same_union_the_join_over_a_body_is, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_join_over_a_module_graph_is_the_same_union_the_join_over_a_body_is, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_join_over_a_module_graph_is_the_same_union_the_join_over_a_body_is, "AN IMPORT'S EFFECT IS THE IMPORTED MODULE'S TOP-LEVEL EFFECT, AND THE JOIN THAT SAYS SO NEEDED NO ARM OF ITS OWN. f_the_join_is_free_and_the_order_is_the_work measured that a body's effect is the union two rules with one head already are; a MODULE's is the same union one scope out, and the only thing that had to be written down is what one scope out means. `nearest_v[flow]` ranges over functions only, so `not eff_in_fn(N)` - a node no function encloses - IS the module body, and a module's own row is `eff_here` under that negation plus the latent row of anything called there. AN `import_declaration` IS A TOP-LEVEL NODE, so the row an import site contributes reaches its own file's module row through that same negation, and the transitive closure over the module graph is a POSITIVE CYCLE the engine takes a least fixpoint over. NOTHING ASSUMES ACYCLICITY AND NOTHING NEEDS TO, which is the part worth carrying: ES modules CAN be cyclic, the specification evaluates a cyclic group ONCE and runs every member's top level, so every member sees the whole group's effect - and the least fixpoint over a lattice of label sets is exactly that. MEASURED: this corpus's module graph is ACYCLIC - beta to alpha, beta to gamma, gamma to alpha, gamma to delta - and gamma.mjs's own header says the direction was chosen to avoid a cycle, so the cyclic case is exercised by a probe rather than by the fixtures. THE GRAPH WAS ALREADY IN THE WORLD AND NOBODY HAD ASKED IT THIS QUESTION. `module_source[code]` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl carries three of the four kinds and `import_target[code]` resolves a single-segment relative specifier against `corpus_file` using the KERNEL'S OWN string destructors - no host loan, no disk, no modules pack. rules/js-modules.rofl answers the same question more completely by walking a real disk and it is deliberately not loaded here, because pulling it in would need the two blind host emitters and change nothing about the join. THE READING THAT MAKES THE POINT: gamma.mjs declares nothing at all - three re-export lines and not one function - and `effect_of_module[flow]` gives it `st_local`, entirely inherited across the graph. delta.mjs is `total`, alpha.mjs and beta.mjs are `st_local`, shapes.ts is `top`. AND THE SUSPENSION IS NOT SMUGGLED BACK IN. A dynamic import evaluates its module and returns a promise; the effect is that module's row and the suspension is CONTROL, exactly as `await_expression` is waived. There is no load label and no ordering label anywhere in this layer, and `eff_import_invented[audit]` is the row that says a site contributes what its target has and nothing else - the same refusal `concrete_async_smuggled[audit]` makes for the concrete column.").
witness_in_atleast(f_the_join_over_a_module_graph_is_the_same_union_the_join_over_a_body_is, w_js_effects, "eff_module[flow](\"gamma.mjs\", L, H)", 3).
addressed_by(f_the_join_over_a_module_graph_is_the_same_union_the_join_over_a_body_is, "rules/js-effects.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_join_over_a_module_graph_is_the_same_union_the_join_over_a_body_is, "test/js-effects.test.ts").

finding(f_a_missing_file_and_a_missing_table_are_not_the_same_open_cell, insight).
recorded(f_a_missing_file_and_a_missing_table_are_not_the_same_open_cell, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_missing_file_and_a_missing_table_are_not_the_same_open_cell, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_missing_file_and_a_missing_table_are_not_the_same_open_cell, "TWO CELLS OF THE EFFECT LAYER HAVE THE SAME SYMPTOM AND OPPOSITE VERDICTS, AND THE CRITERION THAT SEPARATES THEM IS WORTH A NAME. `call_expression` is OPEN because a call whose callee is outside the program has an effect nothing here can state; `import_declaration` is `handled` although three of its sites in this corpus name ./trace.mjs, which is not in the corpus either. Both look like the answer is elsewhere. THE DIFFERENCE IS WHAT ELSEWHERE IS. An import's frontier is a MISSING FILE: add trace.mjs to the corpus and the answer arrives with no rule change, because the mechanism - a module's effect is its top-level row, joined across the graph - is already total over whatever graph the world holds. A call's frontier is a MISSING TABLE: nothing anybody can add to a CORPUS supplies `ambient_effect`, because no declaration file says which members read and which write, so that cell waits on a judgement somebody has to make member by member. A WORLD BOUNDARY IS NOT A MODEL GAP, and this repository already had the row for it under another name - `import_outside_corpus[audit]` in rules/js-dataflow.rofl reports exactly the three sites, and `eff_import_outside[flow]` is the same measurement seen from the effect side. THE TEST OF THE CRITERION IS THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION: w_effect_implicit_coercion keeps its three cells OPEN under the same rule, because 141 of its 369 coerced operands are values the VALUE LAYER did not trace, and no file and no table fixes that - it is interprocedural value flow, a derivation this model does not have. So the three classes are a missing file (answer it), a missing table (own it), and a missing derivation (open it), and only the first two had names before.").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_missing_file_and_a_missing_table_are_not_the_same_open_cell, w_js_effects, "eff_import_outside[flow](I, Src)", 3).
addressed_by(f_a_missing_file_and_a_missing_table_are_not_the_same_open_cell, "facts/js-effects.rofl").

finding(f_the_exn_oracle_cannot_see_a_label_that_did_not_arrive_through_resolves, defect).
recorded(f_the_exn_oracle_cannot_see_a_label_that_did_not_arrive_through_resolves, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_exn_oracle_cannot_see_a_label_that_did_not_arrive_through_resolves, decision).
finding_note(f_the_exn_oracle_cannot_see_a_label_that_did_not_arrive_through_resolves, "THE EFFECT LAYER'S ONE INDEPENDENT ORACLE IS INCOMPATIBLE WITH THE CONCRETE COLUMN IT SHIPPED BESIDE, AND THE ROW WAS PLANTED THE DAY BOTH LANDED RATHER THAN BY THIS ITEM. `eff_exn_only[audit]` asserts that `eff_latent(F, exn, none)` never holds where `may_throw[code](F)` does not, and `may_throw` closes over `resolves[code]` and `throw_statement` and NOTHING ELSE. The concrete column's own seed - a call site's `eff_here` row taken from `ambient_effect` through `eff_row` - puts a label at a call site `resolves` has no row for. MEASURED, one planted row: surface_origin(array, builtin_prototype) and ambient_effect(array, \"join\", io) in the corpus world takes `eff_exn_only[audit]` from 0 to TWO FUNCTIONS, because `io` contains `exn`, while `may_throw_only[audit]` stays 0. So the first honest row the runtime and globals surfaces supply for a member whose effect is `io`, `exn`, `pure` or `top` turns the layer's only cross-check red, and the positive control already in test/js-effects.test.ts avoids it only because it maps two members to `rd_local`. FOUND BY ASKING WHERE THE CHECK IS STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK, which is the question CLAUDE.md records as the only one that produces survivors, aimed at the layer's own instrument rather than at a rule. IT IS A SECOND INSTANCE, NOT A FIRST: w_effect_implicit_coercion adds a conversion call to `eff_latent` through a second arm, and a `valueOf` that throws would diverge the same way - there is no such site in this corpus, and the probe that exercises the arm deliberately writes rather than throws. THE DECISION IS THE OWNER'S AND THE THREE OPTIONS ARE NOT EQUAL: (a) accept the divergence and re-aim the oracle at the labels `may_throw` can carry, which weakens the only row-for-row cross-check the layer has; (b) seed `may_throw` from the same arms, which puts effect-layer knowledge into rules/js-controlflow.rofl and moves a relation four other rules read; (c) leave it, and let the day the surface pack lands be the day somebody meets this. WHAT IS NOT AN OPTION IS DROPPING `exn` FROM THE NEW ARMS to keep the audit green, which would be modelling a lie to protect a measurement.").
-- LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY. There is no `addressed_by` here and that is the
-- point: the measurement is in test/js-effects.test.ts and the DECISION is
-- the owner's, so settling it would take it off the backlog before anybody
-- had made one. It blocks `w_effect_ambient_call` rather than either item of
-- 2026-09-09, and the three options are in the note.
finding(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, insight).
recorded(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, "THE EFFECT LAYER HAD ONE GROUP FOR CODE THAT DOES NOT RUN YET AND NO GROUP FOR CODE THAT RUNS AT A MOMENT OTHER THAN A CALL, and the five class cells are the second. `r_effect_latent` carries a function's row to its CALL over a `resolves` edge; a static block and a static field initialiser have no such edge, because they run where the declaration stands. TWO MOMENTS AND NOT ONE, which is the half a sweep would have collapsed: a static field and a static block run ONCE when the class is defined, a non-static field initialiser runs ONCE PER INSTANCE at every `new` and never at all if nothing constructs the class. MEASURED on the fixture that already existed for the value layer - `class Never { unused = mark(); static tag = mark(); }` defined and never constructed prints only the static one. So `class_define_eff` and `class_construct_eff` are two relations, and `eff_moment_both[audit]` plus `eff_moment_unplaced[audit]` assert that every initialiser lands in exactly one - an identity, true of any corpus. AND THE ITEM'S OWN NOTE WAS HALF WRONG IN A USEFUL WAY. It said the propagation has no call site to hang on; `may_be_node[flow]` already answers which class a `new` constructs, so the CONSTRUCTION half hangs on the `new` exactly as a callee hangs on a call, and only the DEFINITION half needed an edge of a new shape - to the declaration NODE. THAT EDGE IS ONE THE CALL GRAPH DOES NOT HAVE: a call inside a field initialiser has no enclosing function, `encloses[code]` attributes it to nobody, and V8 agrees, naming the frame `<instance_members_initializer>` rather than a function. THE ARITHMETIC OF WHAT IT MOVED IS SMALL AND THE REASON IS WORTH KNOWING: `eff_here` 786 to 809 and `eff_latent` and `effect_of` NOT AT ALL, because every class in these five fixtures is at a module's top level where `nearest_v` reaches nothing, and the one construction-time label the corpus has is `alloc`, which a `new_expression` already contributes. The propagation arm, the construction seed and the discharge are exercised by a PROBE SOURCE in test/js-effects.test.ts instead. A ROW COUNT THAT DOES NOT MOVE IS NOT A RULE THAT DOES NOTHING - it is a corpus with no site, and naming which is which is the whole difference.").
addressed_by(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, "rules/js-effects.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, "facts/js-effects.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, "test/js-effects.test.ts").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, w_js_effects, "class_define_eff[flow](CD, L, H)", 1).
witness_absent(f_a_class_declaration_runs_code_and_a_function_declaration_does_not, w_js_effects, "eff_field_moment[flow](P, nowhere)", "eff_field_moment[flow](P, definition)").

finding(f_may_throw_has_no_construction_edge, defect).
recorded(f_may_throw_has_no_construction_edge, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_may_throw_has_no_construction_edge, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_may_throw_has_no_construction_edge, "`function g() { return new C(); }` WITH `class C { x = boom(); }` THROWS, AND `may_throw[code](g)` IS FALSE. Its propagation is `may_throw(G), resolves(C, G), nearest_v(F, C)`, and the call in the field initialiser has no enclosing function to be the caller - the same hole `encloses[code]` has at the call graph, arriving one relation over. The effect layer's construction edge does carry it, so `eff_latent(g, exn, none)` holds and `eff_exn_only[audit]` - the ONE independent oracle this layer has, both directions, over two relations written a week apart - would report `g`. MEASURED IN A PROBE, not deduced: with `class Kiln { glaze = crack(); }` and `function fires() { return new Kiln(); }` scanned into the effect world, `eff_exn_only[audit]` is exactly {fires, firesSub} and `may_throw_only[audit]` stays empty. ON THE HONEST CORPUS BOTH ARE ZERO, and the reason is a corpus fact rather than an agreement: every field initialiser in the five fixtures calls a function that is `total`, so nothing has ever asked the question. `witness_absent` below says that in the world it is about. IT IS `may_throw` THAT IS WRONG AND NOT THE PROJECTION, and the fix is one arm in rules/js-controlflow.rofl over `may_be_node[flow]` at a `new_expression` - which is that pack's owner's call, not this item's, because `may_throw` also feeds `throws_outright`, `thrown_by` and `caught_value`, and widening it moves the exception layer's own numbers. WHAT IT MEANS FOR THE ORACLE MEANWHILE: the agreement is now conditional, it holds on this corpus, and the day a fixture gains a throwing field initialiser `eff_exn_only[audit]` goes red for a reason that is a real defect in the OTHER relation. That is the gate working; the danger is a reader repairing the projection to match the oracle.").
witness_absent(f_may_throw_has_no_construction_edge, w_js_effects, "class_construct_eff[flow](CD, exn, none)", "class_construct_eff[flow](CD, alloc, none)").

finding(f_a_try_in_a_class_part_catches_nothing_because_a_part_is_not_a_function, defect).
recorded(f_a_try_in_a_class_part_catches_nothing_because_a_part_is_not_a_function, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_try_in_a_class_part_catches_nothing_because_a_part_is_not_a_function, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_try_in_a_class_part_catches_nothing_because_a_part_is_not_a_function, "`caught_here[code]` ASKS `nearest_v(F, TS)` AND `nearest_v(F, N)` FOR ONE F, and the equality is right - a try catches what runs in its own FUNCTION, and `try { const f = () => g(); } catch {}` must not report `g` caught. What it cannot see is a try that runs in something that is NOT a function: `static { try { risky(); } catch {} }` at a module's top level has no enclosing F at all, so `try_of[code]` is empty for that try and the handler discharges nothing. FOUND BY THE RULE THAT CREATED THE REGION, not by reading: with the class-initialiser carriers in and no repair, a probe class whose static block wraps a throwing call in try/catch reported `exn` at definition time - a handler in plain sight, ignored. THE REPAIR IS THE SAME EQUALITY ONE LEVEL OVER and it lives in rules/js-effects.rofl beside the carriers, because `eff_runs_in[flow]` is to a class part exactly what `nearest_v[flow]` is to a function: two literals, the walk swapped, and the strict handler test section 2 already argues for. It removes NOTHING on the honest corpus - measured, `eff_here` and `eff_latent` byte-identical either way - because no try in these five fixtures sits in a class part. THE GENERAL SHAPE IS THE ONE CLAUDE.md NAMES: a gate inherits the scope of its incident. `caught_here` was written when the only thing that ran code was a function, and the vocabulary took on class fields afterwards. THE SAME HOLE IS STILL OPEN IN rules/js-controlflow.rofl for `throws_outright`, `thrown_by` and `caught_value`, which all read `caught_here` and none of which this branch touched - a throw inside a try inside a static block is still reported as leaving. It has no site in this corpus, which is why it is a finding rather than a red test.").

finding(f_the_attribute_guard_audit_caught_the_next_rule_and_could_not_have_excused_it, insight).
recorded(f_the_attribute_guard_audit_caught_the_next_rule_and_could_not_have_excused_it, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_attribute_guard_audit_caught_the_next_rule_and_could_not_have_excused_it, doc).
finding_note(f_the_attribute_guard_audit_caught_the_next_rule_and_could_not_have_excused_it, "`attr_blind_guard[audit]` WAS WRITTEN ON 2026-09-09 FOR ONE RULE IN rules/js-structure.rofl AND IT BIT A NEW RULE IN A DIFFERENT PACK THE SAME DAY, ON THE FIRST RUN AFTER THE RULE WAS WRITTEN. The class-initialiser item needed the KEY of a computed member as a definition-time part and wrote the obvious thing - `ast_attr[code](P, computed, true)` beside `ast_child[code](P, key, 0, KN)` - which is a positive attribute guard, and a positive attribute test cannot reach a kind that carries no such attribute. Four assertions in test/js-attrs.test.ts went red at once and named the two kinds: `class_private_method` and `class_private_property`, because babel emits no `computed` field for a private member. THAT IS THE STRONGEST THING A GATE HERE HAS DONE. CLAUDE.md's standing complaint is that a gate inherits the scope of its INCIDENT; this one was written for `key_name`, is keyed by the SHAPE of the premise rather than by the rule, and therefore reached the next author to make the mistake without anybody remembering it existed. AND THE EXCUSE COULD NOT BE WRITTEN, which is the half worth carrying. `attr_slot_gap_ok(Key, Field, Kind, Reason)` is keyed by (attribute, position, kind) and says nothing about WHICH rule is blind - so an excuse declaring this rule's blindness would have silently excused `key_name`'s as well, and MUTANT 9 in test/js-attrs.test.ts, which exists to prove that `key_name` uses a negation on purpose, would have stopped killing. A declaration mechanism coarser than the thing it declares turns a second offender into a disarmed gate. THE REPAIR IS THE SHAPE THE AUDIT'S OWN FOUNDING FINDING PRESCRIBES: `eff_plain_key[code](P) :- eff_class_body[code](_, P), not ast_attr[code](P, computed, true).` and then `not eff_plain_key[code](P)` in the arm. The negation reaches every member kind and answers correctly for the ones with no flag, so there is no blindness left to declare. MEASURED: the two forms derive a byte-identical world - `eff_here`, `eff_latent` and `effect_of` compared row for row - so this is a change of REACH and not of answer, which is exactly what the audit claims to be about and is the control that says so.").
addressed_by(f_the_attribute_guard_audit_caught_the_next_rule_and_could_not_have_excused_it, "rules/js-effects.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves, "rules/js-globals.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves, "test/js-globals.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_constructed_value_reaches_no_prototype_so_a_map_call_is_outside_both_halves, "SETTLED 2026-09-09 by w_prototype_of_a_constructed_value, AND THE HALF THAT IS SETTLED IS THE DECISION RATHER THAN THE BRIDGE. The note above asked what `prototype_of[flow]` should carry for a value whose class the corpus cannot see, and rules/js-globals.rofl section 6 answers it in one sentence: A CONSTRUCTION OF AN ES GLOBAL THAT NOTHING IN THIS PROGRAM BINDS IS A VALUE SITE, AND THE SITE STANDS FOR THE VALUE. That is not a new abstraction - it is the one rules/js-dataflow.rofl already writes for `array_expression` and `reg_exp_literal`, `may_be_node(E, E)`, which names the site rather than the object and is a fresh object each time round a loop. Standing on that relation is the whole of the repair: the binder, assignment and transparent-wrapper arms of `may_be_node` then carry the instance to the receiver position for free, and `const holder = new Map(); holder.set(k, v)` reaches something for the first time. THE FINDING WAS RIGHT THAT MORE `kind_prototype` ROWS WERE NOT THE ANSWER: a kind cannot carry it, because `new Map()` and `new Box()` are the same KIND and different questions, and the arm reads `es_global_construct[code]` - which is `free_global`, which is `not declares_name` - so the ES case and the class-in-this-program case cannot collide. Measured: `resolves`, `calls_in`, `ambiguous_call` and `unresolved_call` are row-for-row identical with the arm in and out. MEASURED, AND THE BLAST RADIUS IS SMALL AND FULLY EXPLAINED. 453 relations diffed between the world with section 6 and the same world without it: FIFTEEN moved. TEN are this item`s own or the standard library`s (`es_instance` 0->14, `es_prototype_gap` 0->32, `es_instance_unattributed` 0->1, `prototype_of` 598->603, `may_be_node` 1678->1700, `valued` 2733->2755, `stdlib_member` 10->13, `lib_call` 10->13, `lib_call_deprecated` 2->3, `lib_unsupported` 19->20), two are the kernel`s own reification counting the five new clauses and the one new negation (`rule_known` 632->637, `negated_under` 111->112), and THREE WERE A SURPRISE: `await_value_known` 3->4, `await_value_unknown` 2->1 and `await_no_call` 2->3, because `await new Promise(...)` now HAS a value where the layer answered nothing. That is the same reading the relation already gave a native promise and it is asserted in test/js-globals.test.ts as an identity rather than left to be found. WHAT IS NOT SETTLED IS THE BRIDGE, AND IT IS NOW A FRONTIER WITH ROWS RATHER THAN A SILENCE. `lib_global_prototype` still covers EIGHT of forty-seven, and the reason is one level below the bridge: `lib_member` - the entire instance surface, generated by scanners/ts_lib.ts - is keyed by the eight prototypes in `PROTOTYPES`, so a ninth bridge row would point at an empty table. `es_prototype_gap[audit]` names the thirty-two constructible globals that have no instance surface (Map, Set, Promise, WeakMap, Date, every typed array), and `es_instance_unattributed[audit]` names the CALLS on them - `holder.set` is one row saying `Map` and `set` where before there was nothing at all. AND A NEW CEILING WAS FOUND WHILE WRITING THE FIXTURE, recorded separately as f_a_namespaced_constructor_is_outside_the_reference_position_table.").

finding(f_a_namespaced_constructor_is_outside_the_reference_position_table, defect).
recorded(f_a_namespaced_constructor_is_outside_the_reference_position_table, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_namespaced_constructor_is_outside_the_reference_position_table, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_namespaced_constructor_is_outside_the_reference_position_table, "`new Intl.NumberFormat(loc).format(n)` REACHES NOTHING AT ALL, AND IT IS WORSE THAN THE `Map` CASE THAT PAID FOR THE ITEM. `global_ref_position` in rules/js-globals.rofl names five positions and one of them is `new_expression / callee`; here the global sits in the OBJECT of the callee, and the callee is a member expression. So `es_global_construct` never fires, `es_instance` is empty, `prototype_of` is empty - AND `es_instance_unattributed[audit]`, the audit written to name exactly what the instance surface cannot carry, cannot see it either. A construction the model has no row for is invisible to the relation whose job is naming invisible constructions. MEASURED on the fixture 2026-09-09: the site is in test/fixtures/js-globals/globals.mjs.txt and every one of those four queries returns nothing, asserted by name so the ceiling is stated rather than discovered. THE STATIC HALF DOES SEE THE LINE - `es_static[code]` reports `Intl.NumberFormat since es5` - which is what makes this a ceiling of ONE relation rather than blindness to the source line, and is also the shape of the repair: `Intl`, `Reflect` and the whole of `Intl`s constructor family are `namespace_block` globals whose members ARE constructors, so the model already dates the thing being constructed and simply does not know a construction happened. THE COST IS NOT THEORETICAL: `new Intl.NumberFormat`, `new Intl.DateTimeFormat`, `new Intl.Collator` and `new Intl.Segmenter` are ordinary code, and on the runtime side the same shape is every `new URL()`-style namespaced constructor a host surface will bring. WHAT IT WOULD TAKE, and why it was not done here: widening `global_ref_position` is not enough, because the row would have to say that the OBJECT of a callee is a global reference AND that the KEY is the constructor - which is `es_static[code]` joined to a construction rather than a sixth position, and it needs a decision about whether a static that is a constructor gets a prototype atom of its own. That is a second bridge, not a wider one.").

finding(f_both_cost_gates_refuse_the_pack_they_would_have_priced, insight).
recorded(f_both_cost_gates_refuse_the_pack_they_would_have_priced, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_both_cost_gates_refuse_the_pack_they_would_have_priced, decision).
finding_note(f_both_cost_gates_refuse_the_pack_they_would_have_priced, "SECTION 6 OF rules/js-globals.rofl ADDS FIVE CLAUSES TO A RELATION THE VALUE LAYER READS EVERYWHERE, AND NO COST GATE IN THIS TREE CAN SEE THEM. All three WorldSpecs in test/js-layer-cost.test.ts omit `facts/js-globals.rofl` and `rules/js-globals.rofl` BY NAME, each with a written reason, and every one of those reasons is right: this world`s total is a pinned number and a pack added to it moves that number for a question it is not measuring. test/js-fixpoint-cost.test.ts pins the call-graph world, which never loaded the pack either. So the arm is priced by nothing, and the only measurement that exists is the one this item took by hand - `may_be_node` 1678 -> 1700 and `valued` 2733 -> 2755 on the six-file corpus, plus 5 rows of `prototype_of`. THE GATES ARE NOT WRONG AND THE HOLE IS STRUCTURAL, which is why this is an insight rather than a defect against them: a per-layer cost gate whose worlds are pinned can only price the layers that were in the world when the pins were written, so EVERY pack added after a pin is unpriced by construction and the omission list is where that becomes visible. It is the same sentence CLAUDE.md writes about a gate inheriting the scope of its incident, in the one place the repository already writes the scope down. THE DECISION IS THE OWNER`S and it is the one test/js-layer-cost.test.ts already names in its own comment: a fourth WorldSpec adds pins, so somebody has to decide that the library-era layer - `js-lib-surface`, `js-env-api`, `js-globals` - is a subject worth a world and a number of its own. Until then the honest reading of `open_cell` at 0 for this layer is that nothing is unmodelled, not that nothing is unmeasured.").

finding(f_a_relation_sweep_needs_the_arity_that_answers_not_the_one_that_does_not_error, defect).
recorded(f_a_relation_sweep_needs_the_arity_that_answers_not_the_one_that_does_not_error, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_relation_sweep_needs_the_arity_that_answers_not_the_one_that_does_not_error, doc).
finding_note(f_a_relation_sweep_needs_the_arity_that_answers_not_the_one_that_does_not_error, "THE INSTRUMENT THAT MEASURES A BLAST RADIUS WAS BLIND TO IT, AND IT FAILED IN THE SAFE DIRECTION SO THE ANSWER LOOKED GOOD. Diffing every relation in a world against the same world with one section withdrawn needs an arity per relation name, and the sweep asked for arity 1 first and took the first literal `query` did not reject. A WRONG ARITY IS NOT AN ERROR HERE: it comes back `unpopulatable: true` with zero rows and `error: undefined`, so the sweep compared 0 against 0 for every relation whose real arity is greater than one - which is nearly all of them. It reported FIVE relations moved. Re-run picking the arity with the MOST rows in either world, it reported FIFTEEN, including `prototype_of`, `may_be_node`, `stdlib_member` and `lib_call` - the four the change was about. Ten of fifteen were invisible and the report read as a tight, well-understood diff. THE TELL IS THE ONE THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY KNOWS: `unpopulatable` exists precisely because an empty answer from a misspelt or mis-aritied relation is indistinguishable from an empty answer otherwise, and test/js-globals.test.ts asserts it on every query for that reason - while a one-off probe written in five minutes did not, and quietly inherited the exact defect the guarded helper was built to prevent. A PROBE IS A GATE. If it is going to be quoted in a note, it needs the same `unpopulatable` check the test helpers carry, and the cheapest form of that is what fixed it: refuse a relation no arity populates, and pick the arity that answers rather than the first that does not complain.").
-- WHAT THE AMBIENT SURFACE FOUND, 2026-09-09 (w_effect_ambient_call).

finding(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, insight).
recorded(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, "THE FIFTH LAYER HAS ONE INDEPENDENT ORACLE AND THE FIRST THING THE AMBIENT SURFACE DID WAS BREAK IT, IN THE RIGHT DIRECTION. `may_throw[code]` was built by w_exn_propagation for the exception question alone, in another pack, days earlier; rules/js-effects.rofl re-derives it as the `exn` PROJECTION of a general rule and asserts `eff_exn_only[audit]` and `may_throw_only[audit]` both EMPTY - both directions, because one alone is satisfied by a rule that derives nothing. That agreement held while `exn` had exactly ONE SEED: a `throw_statement` the enclosing function does not catch. `io` is `<st<global>, div, exn, ndet>`, so the moment an ambient call is attributed `io` the enclosing function carries `exn` - and `console.log` REALLY CAN THROW. MEASURED over w_js_ambient: `eff_exn_only[audit]` is 12 and `may_throw_only[audit]` is 0. The effect layer is now RIGHT and the oracle is now INCOMPLETE, and no count could have told those apart from a propagation bug. THE ORACLE IS NARROWED RATHER THAN DROPPED, and what is left is stronger than what it replaced: `may_throw_only` must STILL be empty (this layer may not LOSE a throw the exception layer has, which is the direction a real defect would take), and `eff_exn_unexplained[audit]` - an `eff_exn_only` function that reaches no ambient `exn` source through the propagation rule`s own closure - must be empty too. So the divergence is not tolerated, it is ATTRIBUTED. GENERAL FORM, and it is CLAUDE.md`s own sentence about a gate inheriting the scope of its incident, arriving in an oracle instead of a guard: an agreement between two relations is a claim about the SEEDS they share, and it silently becomes a claim about nothing the day one of them gains a second seed. The tell was available in advance - rules/js-effects.rofl says in so many words that it copies `may_throw`s seed DELIBERATELY so the two can be compared - and nobody asked what happens when the copy stops being a copy.").
witness_in_atleast(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, w_js_ambient, "eff_exn_only[audit](F)", 1).
addressed_by(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, "rules/js-ambient.rofl").
addressed_by(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, "test/js-ambient.test.ts").

finding(f_the_surface_enumerates_a_modules_members_and_not_a_globals, defect).
recorded(f_the_surface_enumerates_a_modules_members_and_not_a_globals, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_surface_enumerates_a_modules_members_and_not_a_globals, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_surface_enumerates_a_modules_members_and_not_a_globals, "`host_module_member(node, node:fs, readFileSync)` EXISTS 1321 TIMES AND `host_global_member(node, console, log)` DOES NOT EXIST AT ALL, and the difference is ten lines of scanner rather than a limit of the source. scanners/host_lib.ts reaches a module`s members through `checker.getTypeAtLocation(init).getProperties()` on an `import * as m` - the TypeScript CHECKER, not a text scan - and for the globals it makes the SAME CALL one level up, on `globalThis`, and keeps only the property NAMES. The members of `Console`, `Process` and `Performance` are one identical call away and nothing asks for them. THE CONSEQUENCE IS EXACTLY WHERE THE EFFECT LAYER FEELS IT. `ambient_effect(Surface, Member, EffectName)` is derived per member; for a module that is corpus-independent - every member of `node:fs` gets a row whether or not any program calls it - and for a global it CANNOT BE, because the member list does not exist. So a global`s effect is its surface DEFAULT and it lands on the members a program in the corpus happens to select, which is the module-default-with-member-override pattern with the override table empty and the member list borrowed from the corpus. `ambient_unenumerated[flow](S)` is the row that says which surfaces are in that state, and over w_js_ambient it is exactly the globals and not one module. WHAT IT COSTS TODAY is that `console.log` and `console.table` are indistinguishable to this model and both read `io`, which is true and coarse; what it will cost is the first global whose members differ - `process.cwd` reads and `process.exit` does not return - and there is no place to say so. WHAT IT IS NOT: it is not a reason to hand-write the members. The whole argument of the runtime surface is that its names come from a declaration file, and a hand-written global member table would be the second, rival copy that facts/js-host-surface.rofl`s own header refuses.").
witness_in_atleast(f_the_surface_enumerates_a_modules_members_and_not_a_globals, w_js_ambient, "ambient_unenumerated[flow](S)", 1).
witness_absent(f_the_surface_enumerates_a_modules_members_and_not_a_globals, w_js_ambient, "ambient_unenumerated[flow](\"node:fs\")", "ambient_unenumerated[flow](\"console\")").

finding(f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it, insight).
recorded(f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it, "THE `identifier` CELL WAS FILED UNDER THE AMBIENT ITEM ON THE GROUND THAT ITS THIRD CASE NEEDS THE SURFACE, AND IT DOES NOT. The note in facts/worklist.rofl read: a bare name is a local (bottom, or `read<local>` if something reassigns it), an import (bottom), or a free global (`read<global>`) - and `ambient_binding` is exactly the relation that decides the third. Measured: `ambient_binding` decides what a CALL of the name DOES; the READ needs only that the name is FREE. `eff_here[flow](E, read, global) :- free_global[code](E, _, _)` is the whole rule and it joins no surface at all, because reading a name nothing in the program binds is a read of a property of the host`s global object whatever that property turns out to be. THE WITNESS IS `globalThis`. It is a compiler intrinsic no lib.es*.d.ts declares (f_globalthis_is_the_one_global_the_library_cannot_date) and no host surface carries, so `ambient_binding` has no row for it in any world - and it seeds `read<global>` exactly like `console` does. Seventeen names seed the label over w_js_ambient and one of them is bound to nothing. THE TRANSFERABLE PART is about the queue rather than about JavaScript: an item`s note said a cell WAITS ON a relation, the cell was filed with that item, and the wait was never re-measured after the relation arrived. The cell was closed by a rule that does not read it. A dependency written into a note is a claim about a store like any other, and this one would have been one query.").
witness_absent(f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it, w_js_ambient, "ambient_binding(File, \"globalThis\", S)", "ambient_binding(File, \"console\", S)").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it, w_js_ambient, "free_global[code](E, \"globalThis\", File)", 1).
addressed_by(f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it, "rules/js-ambient.rofl").

finding(f_the_witness_world_builder_could_not_take_a_specifier_apart, defect).
recorded(f_the_witness_world_builder_could_not_take_a_specifier_apart, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_witness_world_builder_could_not_take_a_specifier_apart, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_witness_world_builder_could_not_take_a_specifier_apart, "scripts/witness_check.ts BUILDS EVERY DECLARED WORLD BY LOADING ITS PACKS AND SCANNING ITS CORPUS, AND THAT WAS NOT ENOUGH TO RUN THE MODULES LAYER. The kernel`s builtins are arithmetic and comparison only, so NO RULE CAN TAKE A STRING APART: `str_char0`, `str_seg`, `str_segs` and `str_scheme` are host-emitted facts, and test/js-host-world.ts and test/js-modules.test.ts both write them by hand beside every corpus they build. The world builder did not. WHAT THAT MEANS IS WORSE THAN A MISSING FACT: without `str_scheme[code]`, `node:fs` never classifies as a `node_builtin`, `resolved_builtin[code]` derives nothing, `host_module_ns[code]` is empty and the ENTIRE MODULE DOOR of rules/js-modules.rofl and rules/js-host.rofl is silent - with no diagnostic anywhere, because an empty relation is a fact about the query until proven otherwise. A witness asked of such a world would have measured the absence of an emitter and reported it as a property of the program. IT WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL A WORLD NEEDED IT, which is the ordinary shape of this class: every world declared before 2026-09-09 either refused `rules/js-modules.rofl` outright or had a corpus of RELATIVE specifiers, so the missing facts subtracted nothing anybody had asked for. `w_js_ambient` is the first declared world whose subject includes an import of a node builtin. THE FIX IS THE SAME TWENTY LINES THE TWO TEST FILES ALREADY CARRY, moved into `build` and read off the corpus SOURCE rather than listed, so a new import in a fixture cannot silently go unresolvable. It only ADDS facts and only for a corpus that writes an import; a world that does not load the modules pack holds them unread. THE THIRD COPY IS THE FINDING`S OWN TAIL: the emitter now exists in three places (two tests and the checker) and CLAUDE.md`s second remedy - derive the check once rather than copying it per relation - applies to emitters too. It is not done here because moving it would touch three files this branch does not own.").
witness_in_atleast(f_the_witness_world_builder_could_not_take_a_specifier_apart, w_js_ambient, "host_module_ns[code](File, Local, Spec)", 1).
addressed_by(f_the_witness_world_builder_could_not_take_a_specifier_apart, "scripts/witness_check.ts").

finding(f_a_frontier_relation_is_what_a_deleted_door_falls_out_of, insight).
recorded(f_a_frontier_relation_is_what_a_deleted_door_falls_out_of, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_frontier_relation_is_what_a_deleted_door_falls_out_of, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_frontier_relation_is_what_a_deleted_door_falls_out_of, "THE MUTANT SET FOR THE AMBIENT PACK ASKED WHERE THE CHECK CANNOT LOOK, AND THE ANSWER WAS: AT A QUESTION THAT IS NOT ASKED. Deleting the ECMAScript arm of `ambient_binding` - the one line that makes `Math`, `JSON`, `Object`, `Promise`, `Reflect`, `Symbol`, `Error`, `Map` and `BigInt` ambient at all - moves NOT ONE audit, does not change `concrete_denotes[flow]` by a row, and leaves every lattice property intact. The nine surfaces simply STOP EXISTING for the model, and a model that has never heard of `Math.max` looks exactly as correct as one that knows it cannot answer for it. A count of what IS attributed sleeps through it; so does every `[audit]` in three packs, because an audit is a claim about rows that are there. WHAT KILLS IT IS THE IN-TRAY. `ambient_owed[flow](Origin, Surface)` is a POSITIVE relation, non-empty by design, naming every surface a call site reaches and no table attributes - with its ORIGIN in the row, because the ECMAScript intrinsics, the builtin prototypes and the host runtime owe different work to different owners. With the arm deleted it drops from eleven rows to two, and the deletion is a diff a reader can see. THE GENERAL SENTENCE: a frontier relation is not documentation of what is missing, it is the ONLY instrument that can detect a question being removed rather than answered wrongly. This repository already knows the half of it that says a residue must be countable and queryable (`unresolved_call`, `frontier_at`, `host_call_uneffected`); what this measurement adds is that such a relation is also the mutation oracle for its own layer`s SCOPE, and nothing else in the layer is.").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_frontier_relation_is_what_a_deleted_door_falls_out_of, w_js_ambient, "ambient_owed[flow](Origin, Surface)", 2).
witness_absent(f_a_frontier_relation_is_what_a_deleted_door_falls_out_of, w_js_ambient, "amb_surface_attributed(\"Object\")", "amb_surface_attributed(\"console\")").
addressed_by(f_a_frontier_relation_is_what_a_deleted_door_falls_out_of, "test/js-ambient.test.ts").

-- ...and the two the ambient item ANSWERS rather than records. The first is its
-- own founding finding: the map into the lattice was "a table somebody has to
-- build member by member", and it turned out to be a table somebody had already
-- built for another purpose plus one translation derived from the lattice.
addressed_by(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, "rules/js-ambient.rofl").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_concrete_effect_is_two_halves_and_only_one_of_them_is_free, w_js_ambient, "concrete_denotes[flow](S, Op, E)", 1).
-- CORRECTED 2026-09-09, and the correction is the convention this ledger already
-- uses (`corrected(F)`): the arithmetic in that finding's note moved the same day
-- it was written. The SHAPE the note is about is unchanged and is what makes the
-- correction small.
finding_note(f_a_cell_can_be_open_while_a_rule_fires_on_its_kind, "CORRECTED 2026-09-09, the same day: the arithmetic at the end of the note above is 29 modelled and 18 open, and it is now 33 modelled and 14 open. `w_effect_ambient_call` closed `call_expression`, `optional_call_expression`, `new_expression` and `identifier` by supplying the ambient surface the four were waiting on. The finding itself stands and its two named examples are the reason: `new_expression` was OPEN while `eff_alloc_kind` fired on all 32 of its sites, and it closed when a rule for the CONSTRUCTOR`s effect arrived - not when anybody decided the seed was enough. `identifier` closed the same way and with a twist worth reading beside this one, recorded as f_a_free_name_is_a_read_of_the_global_heap_whether_or_not_a_surface_names_it: the rule that closed it does not read the relation the note said it was waiting for.").

finding(f_a_squared_relation_over_an_empty_table_is_invisible_until_the_table_fills, defect).
recorded(f_a_squared_relation_over_an_empty_table_is_invisible_until_the_table_fills, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_squared_relation_over_an_empty_table_is_invisible_until_the_table_fills, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_squared_relation_over_an_empty_table_is_invisible_until_the_table_fills, "rules/js-effects.rofl SECTION 4 REFUSES TO MATERIALISE A COMPARISON PER PAIR IN SO MANY WORDS - `writing it as a relation would be |functions| squared rows for no answer the query does not already give, on a corpus of 395 functions and an engine whose space wall is 500 000 rows` - AND SECTION 5, TWO PARAGRAPHS LATER, DID EXACTLY THAT. `concrete_leq[flow](S1, O1, S2, O2) :- ambient_effect(S1, O1, E1), ambient_effect(S2, O2, E2), eff_leq(E1, E2).` is |ambient_effect| squared, and the author of both paragraphs is the same pack on the same day. THE REASON IT PASSED REVIEW AND EVERY GATE IS THAT `ambient_effect` WAS EMPTY: 0 x 0 is 0, the relation derived nothing, and the SHAPE of a rule is not visible in its row count until something populates it. The pack's own test asserted `concrete_leq` = 1 under a PLANTED control of two rows, which is 2 x 2 and says nothing about the join. WHAT IT COST, measured 2026-09-09 the day w_effect_ambient_call supplied the surface: 571 `ambient_effect` rows produce about 330 000 here, `this.rows` in src/engine.ts is a GLOBAL counter against DEFAULT_SPACE 500 000, and test/js-attrs.test.ts - the one world that loads every JS pack in the tree - came back `$rule(...)/space_exhausted` with EIGHTEEN of its nineteen tests red at once. Not one of them is about the effect layer. THE NARROW WORLD DID NOT SEE IT: test/js-ambient.test.ts holds the same 571 rows and fits, because its world is leaner overall - so a pack can be over the wall in the tree and under it in its own test file, and the only instrument that says so is a world that loads everything. THE REPAIR IS THE SENTENCE SECTION 4 ALREADY WROTE: bound the relation by `concrete_denotes[flow]`, which is the (surface, member) pairs a CALL SITE reaches - 17 over w_js_ambient against 571 - and leave the raw comparison as a query. That is also the only version anybody can ask a question about, because comparing two members nothing calls is a comparison about nothing. GENERAL FORM, and it is a new one for this repository's collection: a rule whose COST is quadratic in a table another layer will fill is a cost defect that no gate can see while the table is empty, and `undefined_premise`, the lattice audits and every named set in the pack are all structurally unable to look at it. The tell is available statically - two literals of the same relation in one body with no shared variable - and nothing in this tree asks for it.").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_squared_relation_over_an_empty_table_is_invisible_until_the_table_fills, w_js_ambient, "concrete_leq[flow](S1, O1, S2, O2)", 1).
addressed_by(f_a_squared_relation_over_an_empty_table_is_invisible_until_the_table_fills, "rules/js-effects.rofl").

-- SECOND NOTE, 2026-09-09, and it is the BEFORE-AND-AFTER the coercion branch
-- asked for rather than a correction: that branch measured the same defect from
-- the other end (`f_the_exn_oracle_cannot_see_a_label_that_did_not_arrive_
-- through_resolves`), by planting ONE row and watching `eff_exn_only` go 0 -> 2.
-- This is the same experiment at full scale, in ONE world with the pack in and
-- out, which is what makes it a measurement rather than two numbers from two
-- stores.
finding_note(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, "MEASURED IN ONE WORLD WITH THE PACK IN AND OUT, which is the only form of `before and after` this repository accepts - two numbers from two different stores is the defect `a witness has no world` already records. w_js_ambient with rules/js-ambient.rofl REMOVED and everything else identical: `eff_exn_only[audit]` 0, `may_throw_only[audit]` 0 - the row-for-row agreement rules/js-effects.rofl asserts. With it: `eff_exn_only[audit]` 12, `may_throw_only[audit]` 0. THE TWELVE BY NAME: announce, get, isThere, later, matches, readIt, readLater, say, soon, space, where, writeLater - every one of them makes an `io` host call IN ITS OWN BODY (`console.log`, `fs.readFileSync`, `path.resolve`, `fetch`, `setTimeout`, `queueMicrotask`), and not one of them is a function that merely calls another, because these four fixtures are flat. AND `now` IS NOT IN IT, which is the row that shows the widening follows the LATTICE and not the word `ambient`: `performance.now` is attributed `ndet`, `ndet` does not contain `exn`, so a function whose only ambient call is nondeterministic does not gain a throw. A rule that widened by SITE rather than by ROW would have put it in. THE SECOND HALF OF THE STANDARD, set by the same branch on the same day and met here by a different route: they had 141 coerced operands the value layer never traced, tried seeding the TOP of the lattice for them and REFUSED IT on the measurement that it takes `eff_label_unseen` from two labels to none and makes `effect_of` `top` for most of the corpus. This pack seeds NOTHING for an unattributed ambient call - `ambient_owed[flow]` and `eff_call_unattributed[flow]` name them and no label is contributed - and the test is the one that refusal implies: the set of functions at `top` is IDENTICAL with the pack in and out (`all` and `shadowed`, both there before), while `eff_label_unseen` empties for the opposite reason, because `ndet` arrives from a member facts/js-host.rofl attributes by name. THE TELL THAT SEPARATES THE TWO CASES IS EXACTLY THAT PAIR: a seed that ANSWERS moves the label set and not the top set; a seed that GIVES UP moves both.").

-- THIRD NOTE, 2026-09-09, and it WEAKENS this finding`s own green line rather
-- than strengthening it, which is why it is here: two other branches found
-- holes in `may_throw[code]` the same day, independently of this one and of
-- each other.
finding_note(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, "THREE BRANCHES FOUND THREE THINGS WRONG WITH THE SAME ORACLE ON ONE DAY, AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE THAT CHANGES HOW THIS FINDING`S OWN MEASUREMENT SHOULD BE READ. (1) This item: `may_throw` cannot see a label that did not arrive through `resolves`, so an ambient `io` attribution makes the effect layer know more than it - `eff_exn_only[audit]` 0 -> 12. (2) The coercion branch, from the other end and before this item seeded anything: ONE planted `ambient_effect(array, \"join\", io)` took the same audit 0 -> 2, and the existing positive control stayed green only because it happens to map to `rd_local`. (3) The class-initialiser branch: `may_throw` HAS NO CONSTRUCTION EDGE - `function g() { return new C(); }` with `class C { x = boom(); }` throws and `may_throw(g)` is FALSE, because the propagation rides `nearest_v` and a field initialiser`s call has no enclosing function to hang on. WHAT THAT DOES TO THE HALF OF THE ORACLE THIS FINDING KEPT: `may_throw_only[audit]` = 0 was offered above as the direction a propagation defect would take, and it is now known to mean `this layer lost nothing THAT ORACLE CAN SEE` rather than `this layer lost nothing`. It is kept, because it is still the only INDEPENDENT relation the fifth layer has and a partial oracle is not a wrong one; it is no longer LEANED ON, and what test/js-ambient.test.ts asserts positively instead is `eff_exn_unexplained[audit]`, which compares the effect layer against itself and has a live mutant (M8, killed on a probe). ALL THREE BRANCHES LEFT `may_throw` UNREPAIRED and each said so: the repair is one item, the decision is the owner`s, and three agents quietly patching one relation in three worktrees is the merge this repository has already paid for once. THE TRANSFERABLE PART is not about JavaScript: when three independent pieces of work find three holes in one oracle in one day, the oracle is not the thing to fix first - the thing to fix is that it was the ONLY one, and every claim that leaned on it was leaning on a single point of measurement.").

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE TWO SURFACE ITEMS, 2026-09-09 (w_ambient_prototype_effects,
-- w_ambient_es_intrinsic_effects). One question from two sides, and the two
-- answers are opposite - which is what makes the pair worth recording rather
-- than either half.

finding(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, insight).
recorded(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, "`w_ambient_prototype_effects` ASKED WHETHER THE ARRAY MUTATORS CAN BE READ FROM A DECLARATION FILE RATHER THAN TYPED AND SAID THE HONEST ANSWER MAY BE NO. IT IS YES, AND THE SOURCE HAD ALREADY WRITTEN IT DOWN IN A PLACE NOBODY THOUGHT TO SUBTRACT. TypeScript declares a `Readonly` TWIN of every mutable collection interface in lib.es*.d.ts, and the twin is the same surface WITH THE MUTATING MEMBERS REMOVED - so the closed list is a SET DIFFERENCE over two interfaces in files scanners/ts_lib.ts already parses. MEASURED over the ecmascript lib files, all three twins: `Array` minus `ReadonlyArray` is `copyWithin fill pop push reverse shift sort splice unshift`, which is EXACTLY the nine the item`s own note writes out; `Map` minus `ReadonlyMap` is `clear delete set`; `Set` minus `ReadonlySet` is `add clear delete`. THE REVERSE DIFFERENCE IS EMPTY IN ALL THREE and that is the property the subtraction rests on rather than a curiosity: the readonly view declares nothing the mutable interface does not, so a member missing from the view is missing BECAUSE IT MUTATES. `lib_readonly_only[audit]` is that direction as a row. THE SUBTRACTION IS IN THE RULES AND NOT IN THE SCANNER, which is the transferable half: the generated pack carries what the declaration SAYS - this prototype has a readonly view, these are its members - and the one INFERENCE of the whole item is a rule body anyone can argue with. A scanner emitting `lib_mutator` directly would have hidden the criterion in the layer with no query surface, which is CLAUDE.md`s own `a filter`s danger is that its criterion is invisible while you look through it`. AND WHAT THE SOURCE CANNOT SAY IS A ROW: five of the six builtin prototypes - `string`, `number`, `boolean`, `regexp`, `bigint` - have no twin at all, so this construction is SILENT about them rather than saying they have no mutators. The rule is guarded on `lib_readonly_view(P, _)` having a row for exactly that reason; without the guard the negation inverts the source wholesale and calls `RegExp.prototype.test` a mutator, which MUTANT M10 plants. THE GENERAL SHAPE: before concluding that a semantic property is not in the source, ask whether the source states its COMPLEMENT somewhere - a readonly view, a deprecation tag, a missing constructor interface. Three of the four rows this branch derived came from a complement rather than from the thing itself.").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, w_js_ambient, "lib_mutator(P, Key)", 9).
witness_absent(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, w_js_ambient, "lib_readonly_view(string, V)", "lib_readonly_view(array, V)").
addressed_by(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, "scanners/ts_lib.ts").
addressed_by(f_a_readonly_view_is_the_mutating_half_already_written_down, "rules/js-ambient.rofl").

finding(f_a_surface_grain_in_tray_empties_for_a_row_that_joins_nothing, defect).
recorded(f_a_surface_grain_in_tray_empties_for_a_row_that_joins_nothing, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_surface_grain_in_tray_empties_for_a_row_that_joins_nothing, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_surface_grain_in_tray_empties_for_a_row_that_joins_nothing, "`ambient_owed[flow](Origin, Surface)` READ `concrete_effect(_, S, _), not amb_surface_attributed(S)` - NO MEMBER OF THIS SURFACE IS ATTRIBUTED - AND THAT FORM CAN BE EMPTIED WITHOUT ANSWERING ANYTHING. `w_ambient_es_intrinsic_effects` proposes a floor of exactly that shape and calls it the derivable half: `lib_static_shape` separates a PropertySignature from a MethodSignature, so a data static of a frozen intrinsic is `total` with no judgement at all. IT WOULD HAVE BEEN 87 ROWS AND NOT ONE OF THEM WOULD EVER JOIN. An OPERATION in this model comes from `selects[flow]` on the CALLEE of a call, so a data static can only be an operation if a program calls `Math.PI(...)` - which is the very thing the data/method split exists to say nobody does. Measured over w_js_ambient: of the operations a call site reaches on an ES surface, every one is `callable` and none is `data`. The rows would have moved exactly ONE thing - `Math`, `Object`, `Promise`, `Symbol`, `Error`, `Array`, `Map` and `String` out of the in-tray - while `Math.max`, `JSON.parse` and `Object.keys` stayed as unanswered as before. THE FAILURE MODE IS NOT DISHONESTY, IT IS THAT THE RELATION`S GRAIN WAS COARSER THAN ITS SUBJECT. A queue in-tray keyed by SURFACE answers `has anybody started` and reads as `is anybody still owed`, and the two come apart the first time an attribution is partial. Six days of this relation being right were six days of every attribution being all-or-nothing per surface. THE REPAIR IS ONE JOIN AND IT WAS MEASURED BEFORE IT WAS USED: `ambient_owed` now ranges over `concrete_unmapped[flow](S, Op)` - a surface with at least one operation A CALL SITE REACHES and no map - and on the tree before any attribution the two forms agree ROW FOR ROW, sixteen surfaces, so the redefinition is not what moves the set. That control is the whole difference between a repair and a redefinition that hides its own effect. They disagree in BOTH directions now: `JSON`, `Math`, `Reflect` and `array` are attributed in part and still owe, and `Error` is unattributed and owes nobody anything. THE REFUSED RULE IS PLANTED AS A MUTANT rather than argued about - with it in, `ambient_owed` does not move by a row - so the strengthened relation is immune to the trick that motivated it and the immunity is executable. GENERAL FORM, and this repository already has half of it: a COUNT that two branches move is not a pin. This is the other half - a SET whose membership test is coarser than the thing it is a set of will shrink for work nobody did, and it shrinks silently, because every row in it is still true.").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_surface_grain_in_tray_empties_for_a_row_that_joins_nothing, w_js_ambient, "ambient_owed[flow](Origin, Surface)", 2).
witness_absent(f_a_surface_grain_in_tray_empties_for_a_row_that_joins_nothing, w_js_ambient, "ambient_owed[flow](O, \"Error\")", "ambient_owed[flow](O, \"Math\")").
addressed_by(f_a_surface_grain_in_tray_empties_for_a_row_that_joins_nothing, "rules/js-ambient.rofl").

finding(f_an_ambient_surface_whose_receiver_is_in_this_programs_heap, insight).
recorded(f_an_ambient_surface_whose_receiver_is_in_this_programs_heap, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_an_ambient_surface_whose_receiver_is_in_this_programs_heap, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_an_ambient_surface_whose_receiver_is_in_this_programs_heap, "`ambient_effect(Surface, Member, Name)` HAS NO HEAP COLUMN AND A BUILTIN PROTOTYPE IS THE ONE SURFACE THAT NEEDS ONE. rules/js-ambient.rofl section 3 decides the ambient heap once and defends it in a sentence: an ambient surface is BY DEFINITION not allocated in this program, so an ambient read is `read<global>` with no arm and no exception. That is true of `node:fs` and of `console` and it is FALSE of `Array.prototype` - the surface is ambient and the RECEIVER is a value in this program, so `[3,1,2].sort()` writes a cell this program made. A surface-grain row would have to name one heap for every site, and there is no right one. THE ANSWER IS NOT A SITE RULE, IT IS THAT THE HEAP IS DECIDABLE PER PROTOTYPE AND STRUCTURALLY, for some of them. `eff_heap_of` says `local` exactly when `may_be_node[flow]` reaches the receiver; every arm of `prototype_of[flow]` except the first IS a `may_be_node` join, so the only untraced receiver comes from the receiver`s own KIND - and a prototype every one of whose `kind_prototype` kinds is a `node_value_kind` can have none. `array` and `regexp` are those two, `string`, `number`, `boolean` and `bigint` are not, and `\"ab\".concat(x)` really is a read of an untraced receiver. So nine `ambient_effect` rows exist for the Array prototype and none for the String one, and the reason is a property of two of this model`s own tables rather than of the .d.ts. THE ARGUMENT IS ALSO A ROW: `amb_proto_heap_split[audit]` is a receiver whose prototype this pack calls heap-decided and whose heap the effect layer calls global - the exact contradiction, per site. AND IT IS BLIND HERE, which is the part worth carrying: w_js_ambient reaches the `string` prototype only through `new String(s)`, whose receiver `may_be_node` traces, so the audit has NO SITE that could contradict a wrong claim. MUTANT M11 - every prototype heap-decided - SURVIVES the four fixtures and dies on a two-line probe. A survivor against that corpus would have been a statement about the fixtures and not about the guard. THE GENERALISATION IT PAID FOR: section 3`s landmark closure was written over an ATOM and is now written over (LABEL, HEAP), because two callers needed the same Moore closure on a heap the first one refuses. `wr_local` appears nowhere in the pack; `eff_of_label(write, H, E)` computes it, and it moves if the lattice does.").
witness_in_atleast(f_an_ambient_surface_whose_receiver_is_in_this_programs_heap, w_js_ambient, "amb_proto_unsplit[flow](P)", 5).
witness_absent(f_an_ambient_surface_whose_receiver_is_in_this_programs_heap, w_js_ambient, "amb_proto_heap(string, H)", "amb_proto_heap(array, H)").
addressed_by(f_an_ambient_surface_whose_receiver_is_in_this_programs_heap, "rules/js-ambient.rofl").

finding(f_a_debt_can_be_discharged_by_showing_it_belongs_to_somebody_else, insight).
recorded(f_a_debt_can_be_discharged_by_showing_it_belongs_to_somebody_else, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_debt_can_be_discharged_by_showing_it_belongs_to_somebody_else, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_debt_can_be_discharged_by_showing_it_belongs_to_somebody_else, "ONE SURFACE LEFT `ambient_owed[flow]` ON 2026-09-09 AND NOTHING ABOUT IT WAS ATTRIBUTED. `es_intrinsic/Error` was owed for exactly one operation, `Error.captureStackTrace` - which is V8`s, declared in @types/node and NOWHERE in lib.es*.d.ts. `lib_static(\"Error\", \"captureStackTrace\", _)` does not exist, so no table of ECMAScript effects will ever map it, and reporting the ECMAScript surface as owing an effect for a member the ECMAScript surface DOES NOT DECLARE is false about the surface and hides whose debt it is. `ambient_off_surface[flow](S, Op)` is the relation and that pair is its one row. WHAT MAKES IT DERIVED RATHER THAN AN EXCUSE: the same two arms range over `lib_static` for a binding and `lib_member` for a prototype, and every OTHER operation a call site reaches in this world is on its surface - `JSON.parse`, `Math.max`, `array.toSorted`, `regexp.test` all have a declaration row, so exactly one thing falls out and it is the one the fixture put there as a positive control for a different audit. The two operation WORDS are excluded because `itself` and `construct` name a call and a `new` and no declaration carries them as keys; MUTANT M12 removes that guard and five surfaces leave the in-tray at once, quietly and in the direction that makes the queue look shorter. THE TRANSFERABLE PART is about queues and not about JavaScript. This repository already knows that an item can be BLOCKED on somebody else and that a blindness can have no cell; what it did not have was the third move - a debt that is discharged by showing the creditor was wrong. `f_an_invisible_call_is_two_different_blockers_and_only_one_is_ours` splits an item by WHO OWES; this splits a ROW the same way, and it needs a relation of its own, because a row that simply disappears from the in-tray is indistinguishable from a row that was answered.").
witness_in_atleast(f_a_debt_can_be_discharged_by_showing_it_belongs_to_somebody_else, w_js_ambient, "ambient_off_surface[flow](S, Op)", 1).
witness_absent(f_a_debt_can_be_discharged_by_showing_it_belongs_to_somebody_else, w_js_ambient, "ambient_off_surface[flow](\"JSON\", Op)", "ambient_off_surface[flow](\"Error\", Op)").
addressed_by(f_a_debt_can_be_discharged_by_showing_it_belongs_to_somebody_else, "rules/js-ambient.rofl").

-- ...and back to `f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one
-- _seed`, whose notes bracket the four findings above because the surface items
-- were written into the middle of that block. Nothing in this pack depends on
-- order; the marker is here so a reader following the oracle`s five notes does
-- not think one went missing.

-- FOURTH NOTE, 2026-09-09. The half of the oracle this finding KEPT turned out
-- to be vacuous in the world that most needed it, and its own control is what
-- found that - which is the fourth thing wrong with one relation in one day.
finding_note(f_an_oracle_that_agreed_row_for_row_was_a_statement_about_one_seed, "`may_throw_only[audit]` = 0 WAS OFFERED AS THE HONEST HALF AND IN w_js_ambient IT IS AN EMPTY SET AGREEING WITH AN EMPTY SET. `may_throw[code]` is ZERO over that world`s four fixtures - test/fixtures/js-host`s three files and test/fixtures/js-globals` one contain not a single `throw_statement` between them - so the relation the fifth layer`s only independent oracle is built on has NOTHING TO SAY there, and the green line meant nothing about this pack. IT WAS FOUND BY A CONTROL AND NOT BY READING. The assertion `may_throw[code](F) >= 1` was added to the test as a liveness check in the same edit that recorded the two holes other branches had found, purely because a claim about an oracle should say the oracle can speak - and it went red immediately. Without it the file would have carried a vacuous green through review, in a test written specifically about the oracle`s honesty. THE REPAIR IS A PROBE AND NOT A FIXTURE, on the pattern rules/js-effects.rofl already uses for the recursion seed: two lines that throw, scanned into the world as `throws.mjs`, so `may_throw` has rows and the direction can actually be measured - and it is 0 there, live. The four shared fixtures keep their named sets untouched, which is why a probe rather than a fifth file. SO THE SCORE ON ONE ORACLE IN ONE DAY IS FOUR: it cannot see a label that did not arrive through `resolves` (this item), one planted row moves it 0 -> 2 (the coercion branch), it has no construction edge (the class-initialiser branch), and its honest direction is VACUOUS on the corpus where the ambient surface lives (this note). The first three are about the relation; the fourth is about the WORLD it is asked in, and that is the one a reader would never have suspected from a passing test.").

