-- 2026-09-09, w_destructuring_hides_a_call second pass: the DENOMINATOR of a
-- cell that had already closed, and the layer below it that never got the row.
finding(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, "FOUR CELLS CLOSED ON A RULE THAT FIRES AT NINE OF TWENTY-FOUR SITES, AND THE ARITHMETIC WAS NEVER DONE. `w_destructuring_hides_a_call` closed object_pattern, array_pattern, rest_element and spread_element at the control-flow layer against an EXECUTION ORACLE reporting `missed` empty - which says the model has no edge the runtime contradicts, and says NOTHING about a position where the rule derives no edge at all. Measured 2026-09-09 over the five call fixtures: 24 destructuring positions, `pattern_accessor` and `pattern_iterates` fire at 9. WHETHER THAT RATIO IS A LIE OR A COMPLETE MODEL DEPENDS ENTIRELY ON THE OTHER FIFTEEN, and the layer could not say which. `const [a, b] = plainArray` runs the BUILT-IN Array iterator and no user code at all - a positive statement, the model is right to be silent. `const [a, b] = whateverThisIs` where the value layer traced nothing is an ABSENCE - the model may be missing an edge. Both produce zero rows and nothing told them apart. THIS IS EXACTLY THE HOLE `eff_conv_untraced` FILLS ONE SECTION OVER, and the argument transfers verbatim: section 5b of rules/js-effects.rofl decomposes 369 coerced operands into 223 primitive, 5 in-program objects overriding nothing and 141 the value layer never traced, and REFUSES to seed the top of the lattice for the 141 because that destroys the layer`s sharpest statement to say something nobody can use. `hidden_call_*` in rules/js-controlflow.rofl is the same decomposition for a binding: pos / src / user / builtin / primitive / untraced / unsourced, with a totality audit that is an IDENTITY rather than a corpus fact. THE ANSWER IS 24 = 9 + 15 + 0 + 0 + 0, SO THE CELLS STAY CLOSED - and they are closed on the residue being empty rather than on the ratio, which is the only ground that ever licensed them. THE FIFTH DOOR IS IN THE SAME CENSUS AND ITS RESIDUE IS NOT EMPTY, and that is the transferable half. `for_of_statement` reaches the identical protocol: 4 positions = 2 fire + 1 builtin + 1 UNTRACED. facts/js-callgraph.rofl describes both of the unresolved ones in one clause - `iterating an array literal or a generator calls a BUILT-IN [Symbol.iterator], a function that is not in the program` - and the census splits them. The array literal is exactly right. THE GENERATOR IS NOT: a generator object`s `next` runs the GENERATOR BODY, which is a node in this program, so `for (const drawn of pick())` may well be missing an edge and the sentence said it could not be. A clause that reads as one answer was two, and only a relation could show it. THE GENERAL SHAPE: an oracle that checks what the model SAYS cannot check what it DECLINES to say, and every may-set therefore needs its denominator written down beside it. A cell closed against recall alone is closed against half the question.").
witness_in(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, w_js_corpus, "hidden_call_unaccounted[audit](N)", 0).
witness_in_atleast(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, w_js_corpus, "hidden_call_builtin[flow](N, O)", 1).
witness_in_atleast(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, w_js_corpus, "hidden_call_untraced[flow](N, S)", 1).
addressed_by(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, "rules/js-controlflow.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, "facts/js-controlflow.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, "facts/js-callgraph.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_rule_that_derives_nothing_is_saying_one_of_two_opposite_things, "test/js-destructuring-transfer.test.ts").

finding(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, "THE EFFECT LAYER`S LEDGER ASSERTED THE HOLE AWAY IN ONE SENTENCE AND THE AUDIT BUILT TO CATCH IT WAS STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK. facts/js-effects.rofl said of the four destructuring kinds: ``pattern_iterates` and `pattern_accessor` turn each into a `resolves` row, so at this layer they are ordinary calls`. `pattern_iterates` does. `pattern_accessor` DOES NOT and deliberately does not - a rest over two getters is two true calls at one node, and two `resolves` rows at one node is what `ambiguous_call[audit]` exists to report - so the getter side reaches `calls[code]` and nothing else. `eff_latent` closes over `resolves`. MEASURED WITH A PROBE, one getter that writes and throws: `gate.hot` gives the enclosing function read<local>, write<local>, exn and alloc; `const { hot } = gate` gives NOTHING; `const { ...rest } = gate` gives NOTHING; `{ ...gate }` gives the literal`s own alloc and no part of the getter`s. THREE OF THE FOUR KINDS CONTRIBUTE NOTHING WHATEVER while the cell says they are ordinary calls. AND THE SAME HOLE ONE HOP FURTHER ON THE ITERATOR SIDE: `pattern_iterates` IS a `resolves` row and the `next()` it implies is a `calls` row, so an iterator whose `next` writes is dropped by all three doors - the array pattern, the spread argument and the for-of loop - while the call graph carries every one of those edges by name. The two layers disagree and only one of them is right. `eff_join_short[audit]` IS THE GATE FOR EXACTLY THIS - it says a caller`s row must contain its callee`s, joins the two names and demands the caller`s back - AND IT RANGES OVER `resolves`, THE SAME RELATION THE PROPAGATION RANGES OVER. A gate that looks where the propagation looks cannot see an edge the propagation never took. It was green while the layer was built, audited and closed, and it is green today in the very world where `eff_edge_unclosed[flow]` reports the drop, caller by callee by label. THAT CLAUSE IS THE FINDING and the missing arm is not: a gate sharing its blind spot with the thing it guards is the third instrument defect of that shape on this branch in one day, beside `may_throw` seen from two directions. THE REPAIR IS ONE LINE, IS WRITTEN DOWN, AND IS REFUSED. Section 5b already has it - `eff_conv_call` propagates a coercion`s hidden callee exactly this way - and it was built, measured and REVERTED on the owner`s decision of 2026-09-09: the repair belongs to the call-graph and effect packs together and is not to arrive inside a destructuring merge. `w_effect_reads_only_what_resolves` owns it. WHAT THE REFUSAL IS WORTH IS THAT IT CARRIES NUMBERS: a test PLANTS the repair and measures what it would buy - all three bindings gain the control`s row, the residue empties, the shared corpus moves by ZERO rows because every getter in the five fixtures is pure, and no function reaches the top of the lattice. It would also light `eff_exn_only[audit]` at readsByPattern, readsByRest and readsBySpread, because `may_throw[code]` closes over `resolves` and `throw_statement` and cannot follow a getter reached through a BINDING - a fifth face of the relation three branches met the same day and all three left alone. A refusal with a measurement beside it is a decision; a refusal without one is an omission. THE TRANSFERABLE PART: when you write an audit for a propagation, range it over a WIDER relation than the propagation reads, or it certifies the propagation`s own blind spot.").
witness_in(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, w_js_effects, "eff_edge_unclosed[flow](F, G, L, H)", 0).
witness_in_atleast(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, w_js_effects, "eff_hidden_call[flow](N, M)", 1).
addressed_by(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, "rules/js-effects.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, "facts/js-effects.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_gate_that_reads_what_the_propagation_reads_cannot_see_a_dropped_edge, "test/js-effects.test.ts").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026-09-09. Two OPERATIONAL traps met while four agents shared one machine.
-- Left OPEN and demanding a doc, because the place they belong is HANDOFF.md's
-- own list of operational traps and that file was being edited by another
-- session at the time — a finding merges by name and a race on a prose file
-- does not.
finding(f_the_shared_scratchpad_and_the_page_size_both_lie_quietly, pitfall).
recorded(f_the_shared_scratchpad_and_the_page_size_both_lie_quietly, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_the_shared_scratchpad_and_the_page_size_both_lie_quietly, doc).
finding_note(f_the_shared_scratchpad_and_the_page_size_both_lie_quietly, "TWO TRAPS, ONE MACHINE, AND BOTH FAIL BY PRODUCING A PLAUSIBLE NUMBER RATHER THAN AN ERROR - which is the family HANDOFF.md`s operational list already collects (the node-24 PATH, the pipe into `head`, the neighbour`s `pkill`). FIRST: THE SCRATCHPAD IS SHARED BETWEEN SIBLING AGENTS. The per-session scratchpad path is keyed by the PARENT session, so four subagents of one wave write into ONE directory; two of us independently chose `t2.txt` for a test log and the file ended up holding my run`s console.log lines interleaved with a sibling`s spec-reporter output. I read `57 tests` off it and spent several minutes reconciling worklist and note-witness test names against a file I had told node to fill with ONE test file, before noticing that my invocation passed no `--test-reporter=spec` and could not have produced an `ℹ tests` line at all. THE TELL IS THE REPORTER, and it is the same shape as `a difference between two INSTRUMENTS read as a difference between two ANSWERS`: the log was well-formed, internally consistent, and about somebody else`s tree. Remedy is one line - a private subdirectory named for the item - and it costs nothing. SECOND: `vm_stat` ON THIS MACHINE REPORTS A 16384-BYTE PAGE, NOT 4096. The memory floor handed round the fleet computes `pages * 4096 / 1048576` and calls the result MB; it is a QUARTER of the real figure. Measured here: 4494 free pages is 70 MB and the formula prints 17. Nothing was decided wrongly, because everyone applies the same wrong constant so the floor and the reading are consistent with each other - but the number is not megabytes, and the first person to compare it against `top` or against `hw.memsize` will conclude the box is four times worse off than it is. A THRESHOLD AND ITS MEASUREMENT MUST BE IN THE SAME UNIT, which is the sentence HANDOFF.md already writes about the 500 000-row space wall being read as facts when it counts ROWS - the identical error, in the identical direction, eleven hours apart and about a different quantity.").

-- ==========================================================================
-- 2026-09-09, from the w_directives / w_meta_property / w_export_specifier_forms
-- re-audit, and it is about the HARNESS rather than about the model.
finding(f_a_worktree_without_node_modules_fails_two_tests_that_look_like_resolver_defects, defect).
recorded(f_a_worktree_without_node_modules_fails_two_tests_that_look_like_resolver_defects, "2026-09-09").
demands(f_a_worktree_without_node_modules_fails_two_tests_that_look_like_resolver_defects, doc).
finding_note(f_a_worktree_without_node_modules_fails_two_tests_that_look_like_resolver_defects, "HANDOFF SAYS `SEVEN PRE-EXISTING FAILURES ... ANY EIGHTH IS YOURS`, AND A FRESH WORKTREE HAS NINE. Measured 2026-09-09 in .claude/worktrees/agent-ac52ddc2295f03aa8, a worktree created with no `node_modules` of its own: test/js-modules.test.ts fails `ORACLE: node resolves; the model resolves; set for set, both directions` (1 !== 2, `bare and subpath, both declared, both real`) and `OPEN CELL #sub: the subpath specifier resolves INSIDE the tree and the bare one does not`, where the oracle returns `@babel/parser -> THROWS MODULE_NOT_FOUND` against an expected `@babel/parser -> OUTSIDE`. Both are green the moment a `node_modules` symlink to the main checkout exists: 42 pass / 2 fail before, 44 pass / 0 fail after, with nothing else in the tree touched and the run repeated in both directions. THE TWO STATES ARE NOT THE SAME MEASUREMENT AND THE TEST IS RIGHT TO SEPARATE THEM. `OUTSIDE` means node resolved the specifier to a path the scanned tree does not contain, which is a fact about module resolution; `THROWS MODULE_NOT_FOUND` means node could not resolve it at all, which is a fact about the machine. The oracle correctly refuses to call the second the first, and that is why the repair is the environment rather than the assertion. WHY IT BITES A WORKTREE AND NOT THE MAIN CHECKOUT: the source imports of `@babel/parser` succeed either way, because node walks up from the test file and finds the repository root`s package directory; the ORACLE resolves from a scoped fixture root instead, and there the walk does not reach it. So the parser works, every scan works, 44 of 46 assertions in that file work, and exactly two rows report a resolver defect that does not exist. THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT EXPENSIVE: HANDOFF`s `any eighth failure is yours` rule sends the next worktree agent hunting inside rules/js-modules.rofl, and the cause is a missing directory one level up. THE REPAIR IS ONE SYMLINK AND IT CANNOT BE COMMITTED BY ACCIDENT: the ignore pattern for `node_modules` in this repository deliberately carries NO trailing slash, which is f_a_gitignore_pattern_with_a_slash_does_not_match_a_symlink - a slash-suffixed pattern matches a directory only, and a symlink of that name once rode into a merge commit. The fix for the old incident is what makes the fix for this one safe. WHAT WOULD REFUTE THIS: a worktree with no node_modules in which test/js-modules.test.ts reports 44 pass, or a main checkout that reports these two failures with node_modules present.").
addressed_by(f_a_worktree_without_node_modules_fails_two_tests_that_look_like_resolver_defects, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding(f_model_the_task_in_rofl_because_a_model_can_say_none, decision).
