-- MIGRATED OUT OF HANDOFF.md, 2026-09-10, on the owner's instruction.
--
-- HANDOFF.md is 888 lines of process facts written where nothing can check
-- them, which is failure mode 17 in docs/failure-modes.md and the subject of
-- docs/claims-outside-the-store.md. It was deleted by the owner on 2026-09-07
-- for exactly that reason and recreated by a nightly loop the next day. This
-- block is the migration the comment brief prescribes, applied to the largest
-- single instance in the tree: every claim in it that the ledger did not
-- already hold, sent to the relation that can carry a date, a witness and a
-- supersession.
--
-- WHAT THE READING FOUND, reported separately as the brief demands: most of
-- the file WAS already in the ledger - peakRows, DEFAULT_SPACE and the
-- read-only public surface, the paired-counters trap, the SIGPIPE rule, the
-- two mutant-anchor expiry findings. Eleven claims were not, and three of
-- those are live. Four claims were STALE and are corrected here rather than
-- carried.

-- 1 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_the_moving_comparison_rule_failed_a_third_time_and_the_third_is_red_in_ci, defect).
recorded(f_the_moving_comparison_rule_failed_a_third_time_and_the_third_is_red_in_ci, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_moving_comparison_rule_failed_a_third_time_and_the_third_is_red_in_ci, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_moving_comparison_rule_failed_a_third_time_and_the_third_is_red_in_ci,
  "MIGRATED FROM HANDOFF.md, WHERE THE DIAGNOSIS OF A CURRENTLY-RED CI TEST HAS BEEN SITTING UNRECORDED. `MUTANT C'` in test/js-layer-cost.test.ts compares a LIVE firing share against the literal `6.593`, and `6.593` is the BASELINE world`s firing share copied in by hand. A census moved the baseline to 6.712 and C' went red at 6.399 - still catching its mutant, by 0.313 pp instead of 0.288, but against a reference that had drifted out from under it. TWO MOVING NUMBERS AGAIN, with one of them only LOOKING still because somebody had written it down. THE NOTE BESIDE THAT ASSERTION ALREADY RECORDED TWO PRIOR OCCURRENCES, both on `perFiring`; this is the third and the first on `firingShare`. So the record has now failed THREE TIMES to prevent the thing it records, and the answer was never going to be a better-written warning. THE PRESCRIBED REPAIR, and it is the one that makes the class impossible rather than the instance green: COMPUTE THE REFERENCE IN THE SAME RUN - measure the baseline world`s share alongside the mutant`s and assert the DELTA against a band - so nothing is written down which the corpus can move, and a census moves both sides together. Restating the literal is a stopgap that leaves the mechanism intact for a fourth time. WHY THIS IS URGENT RATHER THAN ARCHIVAL: test/js-layer-cost.test.ts is one of the three things red in CI on this branch today, and this is its diagnosis - so the repair was known, written down, and reachable only by opening an 888-line handoff document that the owner had already deleted once. THE SISTER FAILURE IN THE SAME FILE IS A DIFFERENT ANIMAL AND IS NOT THIS: `L.world.total` pinned at 87535 against a measured 41419 is a PREDICTION the file itself flags as unverified, not a drifted reference, and it wants an investigation rather than this repair.").

-- 2 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_the_fifth_layer_question_is_the_owners_and_lived_only_in_a_handoff, question).
recorded(f_the_fifth_layer_question_is_the_owners_and_lived_only_in_a_handoff, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_fifth_layer_question_is_the_owners_and_lived_only_in_a_handoff, decision).
finding_note(f_the_fifth_layer_question_is_the_owners_and_lived_only_in_a_handoff,
  "THE ONE GENUINELY OPEN THING HANDOFF.md HELD THAT NEITHER LEDGER DID: whether to declare `layer(scheduling)`. It is not a finding, it is not a work item, and `layer(L)` is declared by the owner alone - so a question addressed to the owner was stored in the one place the owner had said he would not trust line by line. THE MECHANICS ARE MEASURED and are not the open part: `await thenable` calls `.then` one microtask turn later on a stack of depth ONE, foreign microtasks run between the two halves of one await, while for-of, getters and generator resumption are synchronous and nothing interleaves. THE COST IS WHAT MAKES IT A DECISION: a scheduling layer`s cells would be answered almost entirely by BUILTINS - setTimeout, Promise.prototype.then - and the queue already knows that chain, `w_prototype_of_a_value` (49) -> `w_env_api_surface` (17), which `w_effect_layer` declares it needs. Declaring the layer first would open about 80 cells whose honest verdict is BLOCKED ON THE STANDARD LIBRARY, and this repository already prices a green matrix over cells nobody can answer. THE CHEAPER ALTERNATIVE, offered in the same breath and also undecided: a PERSPECTIVE costs nothing extra and is checkable today, since await, for-of and the generator are all in the corpus. Recorded as a question with `demands(decision)` so it appears in the backlog at every session start until the owner settles it, which is the whole difference between a ledger row and a paragraph.").

-- 3 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_a_pin_on_bsd_grep_kept_ci_red_for_twelve_commits_reported_green, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_pin_on_bsd_grep_kept_ci_red_for_twelve_commits_reported_green, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_pin_on_bsd_grep_kept_ci_red_for_twelve_commits_reported_green, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_pin_on_bsd_grep_kept_ci_red_for_twelve_commits_reported_green, "test/example-yak.test.ts").
finding_note(f_a_pin_on_bsd_grep_kept_ci_red_for_twelve_commits_reported_green,
  "RECORDED LATE AND ON PURPOSE: THE REPAIR EXISTS AND THE FINDING NEVER DID. `example-yak` fragment 04 asks grep about a file with three exports and one NUL byte. With `-I`, BSD grep on macOS prints NOTHING AT ALL and exits 1; GNU grep on Linux prints `0` and exits 1. Same incident, two renderings, and the assertion was pinned to the FIRST - so it could only ever pass where it was written. CI WAS RED ON THE NODE JOB FOR AT LEAST TWELVE COMMITS AND EVERY ONE OF THEM WAS REPORTED GREEN, because the suite passes on the author`s machine and fails on the runner. That is `f_ci_is_the_referee` measured at its full cost rather than stated: not one wrong verdict, twelve, each written into a commit message by somebody who had run the tests. THE REPAIR (commit 0331631) asserts the PROPERTY - the `-I` column disagrees with the `-c` column about the same file, and says so with a failing exit - and leaves the rendering to the host, with a NEGATIVE CONTROL that the row where the two instruments AGREE does NOT match, because a widened pattern that also accepted the passing case would have been a green light rather than a gate. Verified 2026-09-10 on Linux: fragment 04 matches, and the whole file passes here. WHY IT IS WRITTEN DOWN NOW: it is a textbook instance of a gate inheriting the scope of its INCIDENT - the incident is an instrument answering `none` about a file with three, both spellings are that incident, one was written down - and the ledger held the principle and not this instance. HANDOFF.md CARRIED IT AS A LIVE DEFECT (`example-yak passes here and fails on Linux`) FOR TWO DAYS AFTER IT WAS FIXED, and its `seven pre-existing failures, any eighth is yours` rested on that. A DOCUMENT CANNOT BE SUPERSEDED; a finding can, and this one is settled by the test that now proves it.").

-- 4 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_a_lesson_placed_near_the_code_does_not_prevent_its_own_repetition, insight).
recorded(f_a_lesson_placed_near_the_code_does_not_prevent_its_own_repetition, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_lesson_placed_near_the_code_does_not_prevent_its_own_repetition, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_lesson_placed_near_the_code_does_not_prevent_its_own_repetition, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_lesson_placed_near_the_code_does_not_prevent_its_own_repetition,
  "TWO MEASUREMENTS OF THE SAME PROPERTY AT TWO SCALES, AND THEY AGREE, WHICH IS WHY THE PAIR SAYS SOMETHING NEITHER SAYS ALONE. FOUR HUNDRED LINES: the moving-comparison rule is recorded in a paragraph of HANDOFF.md and violated four hundred lines below it, in `MUTANT C'`, by a reader who had the paragraph. TWELVE LINES: the same bug - a mutant querying the relation whose only rule it deletes - was found, fixed AND COMMENTED in the c2 census mutant, and then written again in another section of THE SAME FILE, in the same session, BY THE SAME AUTHOR, twelve lines away. Distance from 12 to 400 changes nothing, so proximity is not the variable, and `write the warning more prominently` is therefore not a remedy - it is the thing that was already tried at the strongest dose available. THE CONCLUSION IS THE ARGUMENT FOR STRUCTURE: a rule that has been read, re-recorded and still not applied is asking to be made IMPOSSIBLE TO VIOLATE, not to be restated. In the C' case that is computing the reference in the same run; in the mutant case it is judging from downstream by construction. THIS FINDING IS ITSELF AN INSTANCE OF WHAT IT DESCRIBES: it existed as prose in a document, next to both of its own violations, and had no effect until it became a row. That is the argument for the ledger over the handoff in one sentence, and it is measured rather than asserted.").

-- 5 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_a_mutant_that_removes_a_rule_must_be_judged_from_downstream, insight).
recorded(f_a_mutant_that_removes_a_rule_must_be_judged_from_downstream, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_mutant_that_removes_a_rule_must_be_judged_from_downstream, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_mutant_that_removes_a_rule_must_be_judged_from_downstream,
  "MUTANT `d14b` DELETES THE `pattern_next` RULE AND THEN QUERIES `pattern_next` - it asks the deleted rule whether it was deleted. The shared query helper refuses correctly with `nothing in this world can populate it`, which is `unpopulatable` doing exactly its job, and the refusal is the only reason the mutant did not read as a clean kill. THE RULE: judge a removal by what DOWNSTREAM LOST - here the three edges - never by the hole itself. A query over the removed relation has no measurement in it; it can only report the removal it performed, which is a tautology wearing an assertion`s clothes, and it looks identical to a real oracle in a passing run. This is the same family as `a mutant anchored to an item name expires when the item closes`, one level down: there the anchor outlived its subject, here the anchor IS its subject. Migrated out of HANDOFF.md, where it sat twelve lines from a second violation of it.").

-- 6 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_a_mutants_oracle_must_know_which_half_of_a_union_it_removes, insight).
recorded(f_a_mutants_oracle_must_know_which_half_of_a_union_it_removes, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_mutants_oracle_must_know_which_half_of_a_union_it_removes, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_mutants_oracle_must_know_which_half_of_a_union_it_removes,
  "MUTANT M16 DELETES THE `pattern_accessor` ARM OF `eff_hidden_call` AND ASSERTED THE RELATION EMPTIES. It does not: `eff_hidden_call` is a UNION OF TWO ARMS and the iterator`s `next` hop survives - three rows, exactly `pattern_next`. THE ORACLE KNEW LESS ABOUT THE RELATION THAN THE RELATION DID, which is the same sentence this repository writes about a gate inheriting the scope of its incident, arriving in a mutation oracle. REWRITTEN AS AN IDENTITY RATHER THAN A NUMBER, and the replacement is STRONGER THAN `=== 0` WOULD HAVE BEEN EVEN IF ZERO HAD BEEN RIGHT: `eff_hidden_call == pattern_next` (only the iterator hop remains), `pattern_next > 0` (the surviving arm has rows), and on the base world `eff_hidden_call > pattern_next` (the removed doors were the difference). It says WHICH doors went and that the rest stayed, and it cannot be satisfied by the relation merely vanishing - which a zero-assertion can, and which is how a mutant that breaks the whole pack passes as a mutant that removed one arm. THE GENERAL MOVE is the one already recorded for pins: prefer a named set to a count, applied to the oracle of a mutation rather than to a measurement.").

-- 7 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_all_three_reds_of_that_session_were_oracles_and_not_rules, insight).
recorded(f_all_three_reds_of_that_session_were_oracles_and_not_rules, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_all_three_reds_of_that_session_were_oracles_and_not_rules, doc).
addressed_by(f_all_three_reds_of_that_session_were_oracles_and_not_rules, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_all_three_reds_of_that_session_were_oracles_and_not_rules,
  "WORTH SAYING PLAINLY, BECAUSE A GREEN LINE AT THE END OF A SESSION OTHERWISE IMPLIES THE TESTS WERE RIGHT FIRST TIME. Every red of 2026-09-09 on that branch was a TEST being wrong about its subject, and not a rule: C' compared against a frozen copy of a moving number; M16 asserted a union empties when one arm goes; the c2 and c5 census mutants would have queried a relation whose only rule the mutant deletes. THREE FOR THREE, each a test knowing less about its subject than the subject did. THAT IS THE BETTER DIRECTION TO FAIL IN - a wrong oracle costs a diagnosis, a wrong rule costs an answer - AND THREE IN ONE SESSION STILL SAYS WHERE THE NEXT HOUR OF REVIEW BELONGS. The reason to record it rather than enjoy it: a repository that measures its rules with mutants and never measures its ORACLES has moved the unchecked thing one level out rather than removed it, and the only signal that has ever caught one here is a red that arrived for the wrong reason.").

-- 8 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_a_pin_without_its_alarming_shape_is_a_number_nobody_can_read, insight).
recorded(f_a_pin_without_its_alarming_shape_is_a_number_nobody_can_read, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_pin_without_its_alarming_shape_is_a_number_nobody_can_read, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_pin_without_its_alarming_shape_is_a_number_nobody_can_read,
  "NEXT TO EVERY PIN, WRITE THE ALARMING SHAPE AND WHAT TO TURN OFF. Found from both sides at once on 2026-09-09. A pinned number is only worth having if the reader knows which DELTA should alarm them: the peer session moved 70/60/139 -> 63/54/129 and the useful part was not the drop, it was that the move was UNEVEN across three worlds where the previous move had been a uniform +4. An uneven move is a claim about which world changed; a uniform one is a claim about the instrument. A pin with no such note hands the next reader a number and no way to tell those apart, so every change reads as `something moved` and the only available response is to restate it. THE SECOND HALF - WHAT TO TURN OFF - is the part that is always omitted: a pin should say which flag, pack or fixture to remove in order to make the number move on purpose, because a reader who cannot move it deliberately cannot tell a real drift from a measurement artefact. This is the pins discipline (`f_a_pin_that_moves_with_the_corpus_is_measuring_the_corpus`) continued past the point that finding stops: that one says WHAT to write down, this one says what a reader needs beside it to act.").

-- 9 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_a_pkill_by_a_flag_every_session_shares_kills_a_neighbours_run, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_pkill_by_a_flag_every_session_shares_kills_a_neighbours_run, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_pkill_by_a_flag_every_session_shares_kills_a_neighbours_run, doc).
addressed_by(f_a_pkill_by_a_flag_every_session_shares_kills_a_neighbours_run, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_pkill_by_a_flag_every_session_shares_kills_a_neighbours_run,
  "EXIT 144 HAS TWO CAUSES AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THE HANDOFF ENTRY NAMED ONE. One of the observed kills was a neighbouring session`s `pkill -f test-concurrency`, which matched THIS tree`s js-directives run as well as its own, because `--test-concurrency` is a flag every session`s node carries. So a run can die at 144 because of a pipe (`f_never_pipe_a_test_run_into_head`) or because a neighbour`s pattern was wider than its intent, AND BOTH LOOK IDENTICAL FROM INSIDE - a run that vanishes with no failure line. The only tell is that the pipe case sits at 0 per cent CPU first. THE RULE: if you pkill on a shared machine, match your own scratchpad PATH, never a flag every session shares. DEMONSTRATED AGAIN THE DAY IT WAS MIGRATED: the session doing this migration ran `pkill -f test-concurrency` to stop its own superseded run, from a tree it believed it had to itself, without having read this entry - which is the same pattern the entry names, done by the one person who was reading the document it lived in. A WRONG CAUSE IN A HANDOFF IS WORSE THAN NO ENTRY, which is why the correction was written beside the original rather than edited over it, and why both causes are carried here.").

-- 10 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_the_attestation_fingerprint_cannot_see_the_fixtures_it_certifies, defect).
recorded(f_the_attestation_fingerprint_cannot_see_the_fixtures_it_certifies, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_attestation_fingerprint_cannot_see_the_fixtures_it_certifies, doc).
finding_note(f_the_attestation_fingerprint_cannot_see_the_fixtures_it_certifies,
  "CLAUDE.md`S TREE-FINGERPRINT RITUAL FINDS `*.ts` AND `*.rofl` AND NOTHING ELSE, so it cannot see `alpha.mjs` or `shapes.ts.txt` change under a running suite - and those are FIXTURES, the inputs the JS-model tests are measurements OF. A run whose fixture moved would attest `TREE STILL - result valid` and be wrong about the only files whose content the result is about. THIS IS THE THIRD RECORDED HOLE IN THE SAME ATTESTATION and they are all one shape: it was written for the files the author was editing at the time, so it inherited the scope of its incident. The first was that it did not certify the run FINISHED (repaired with the arithmetic guard). The second was that `./.claude/*` was 92 per cent of the fingerprint once worktrees appeared (repaired with an exclusion). This is the third, it was named in HANDOFF.md as `extend its find to *.mjs and *.txt`, and it was never carried into either CLAUDE.md or the ledger - so the snippet a reader copies is still the narrow one. FAILS IN THE UNSAFE DIRECTION, unlike the worktree hole: a foreign worktree moving made a valid run read DISCARD, which merely costs a re-run, while a fixture moving makes an invalid run read VALID. NOT REPAIRED HERE ON PURPOSE: the fix is one line in CLAUDE.md`s snippet, and the finding is written first because the snippet has now been narrowed by omission three times and a fourth extension without a note would leave the next reader with no idea which files were deliberate and which were forgotten. The session recording this used a widened `find` (`*.md`, `*.mjs`, `*.txt`, `*.json`) for its own runs and says so rather than quietly shipping it.").

-- 11 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_the_perspective_doc_is_narrower_than_the_practice_and_planes_fail_its_litmus, question).
recorded(f_the_perspective_doc_is_narrower_than_the_practice_and_planes_fail_its_litmus, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_perspective_doc_is_narrower_than_the_practice_and_planes_fail_its_litmus, decision).
finding_note(f_the_perspective_doc_is_narrower_than_the_practice_and_planes_fail_its_litmus,
  "HANDOFF.md SAYS IN SO MANY WORDS `THAT GAP IS WORTH A FINDING OF ITS OWN` AND NOBODY WROTE ONE, which is the cheapest possible instance of a process fact stored where nothing can chase it. THE GAP: docs/choosing-perspectives.md is NARROWER THAN THE PRACTICE. Its discipline is that a perspective is a LEDGER - `in whose book is this entry` must answer cleanly, and its own last litmus is `would you ever need two X on one literal? then at least one of them is not a perspective`. But `[code]` and `[flow]` are PLANES rather than ledgers with rival writers: one AST fact and one dataflow fact about the same node are not competing entries in two books, they are two kinds of statement about one thing, and a reader applying the document`s first litmus to them gets the wrong answer. THE MODEL USES BOTH SENSES AND THE DOCUMENT DESCRIBES ONE, so the discipline that binds all modelling here is silently inapplicable to the largest model in the tree. WHY IT IS A DECISION AND NOT A DOC FIX: either the document widens to admit planes as a second legitimate use, and then its litmus needs a second clause saying which sense is being tested, or the practice is wrong and `[code]`/`[flow]` should not be perspectives at all - which is a change to every js-*.rofl pack. The first is cheap and weakens a rule that has been load-bearing; the second is expensive and the document currently implies it. Neither is a session`s to take.").

-- 12 -----------------------------------------------------------------------
finding(f_the_handoff_was_a_process_fact_written_where_nothing_could_check_it, defect).
recorded(f_the_handoff_was_a_process_fact_written_where_nothing_could_check_it, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_handoff_was_a_process_fact_written_where_nothing_could_check_it, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_handoff_was_a_process_fact_written_where_nothing_could_check_it, "facts/findings.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_handoff_was_a_process_fact_written_where_nothing_could_check_it,
  "HANDOFF.md IS 888 LINES OF PROCESS FACTS WRITTEN WHERE NOTHING CAN CHECK THEM, which is failure mode 17 and the exact subject of docs/claims-outside-the-store.md and docs/comment-migration-brief.md - both of which arrived on this branch in the SAME MERGE as this migration (PR #4, ec745a0). THE OWNER DELETED IT ON 2026-09-07 with the reason stated: `a document that has to be distrusted line by line is worse than no document; the ledger, the scanners and the docs/ files carry the same content and are checked`. A nightly loop recreated it on 2026-09-08. This block is the migration, on the owner`s instruction of 2026-09-10. WHAT THE READING FOUND, reported the way the brief demands - the count of blocks found FALSE is the return on the work, not the count migrated. ALREADY IN THE LEDGER, and this is the larger half: peakRows and DEFAULT_SPACE including the read-only public surface, the paired-counters trap and its sharpening, the SIGPIPE rule, both mutant-anchor expiry findings, the pins conversion. NOT IN THE LEDGER: eleven claims, of which three were live - the diagnosis of a currently-red CI test, a standing question addressed to the owner, and a platform-dependent failure count. STALE AND CORRECTED RATHER THAN CARRIED, four of them: `w_computed_key_names is still the sharpest open thing` (DONE 2026-09-09, one day after the document was written); `66 work items, 54 done` (82, 67 done, 15 open on 2026-09-10); `seven pre-existing failures` (eight on Linux, which is what CI runs - see f_example_yak_pins_bsd_grep_and_every_ci_runner_is_gnu); `develop and push on modeljs only` (merged into nextver at c30daa8, and the PR closed at ec745a0). FOUR STALE CLAIMS OUT OF ROUGHLY FORTY, IN A DOCUMENT TWO DAYS OLD, AND THE FIRST SENTENCE OF IT NAMES A BRANCH THAT NO LONGER RECEIVES WORK. That is the measurement the owner`s deletion was an unmeasured judgement about, and it agrees with him. THE DECISION LEFT OPEN IS HIS AND IS NOT `delete it`: it is whether a handoff should EXIST at all here, given that every honest thing in one is either already a row or ought to become one, and that the nightly loop which recreated it will recreate it again unless something says not to.").

-- AND IT HAPPENED A SECOND TIME, ONE HOUR LATER, TO THE PERSON WHO HAD JUST
-- WRITTEN THE FINDING. Recorded as a note on the finding rather than as a new
-- one, because it is the same defect and the value is in the repetition.
finding_note(f_the_warning_about_line_anchors_lives_below_the_damage_it_prevents,
  "SECOND OCCURRENCE, SAME SESSION, SAME AUTHOR, ONE HOUR LATER. Adding three documents to README`s index - twelve lines, in the section nearest the top - unfounded all thirteen `duty[readme]` citations again, and every one moved by EXACTLY +12. The author had written the paragraph above about this trap, had re-anchored the thirteen by hand once already, and walked into it again while editing the very file the finding is about. THAT IS THE THIRD DATA POINT FOR `f_a_lesson_placed_near_the_code_does_not_prevent_its_own_repetition`, and it is the sharpest of the three: the other two were four hundred lines and twelve lines away from their own violation, this one was ZERO lines away - the lesson and the violation are the same edit session on the same file, by the reader who wrote the lesson. Proximity is now measured at 400, 12 and 0, and it changes nothing. WHAT THE SECOND OCCURRENCE ADDS THAT THE FIRST DID NOT: the shift was UNIFORM. All thirteen moved by the length of the inserted block, because the insert was above all of them and no anchor text changed - so the re-anchoring is not merely derivable in principle, it is a single addition, and the gate that caught it could have OFFERED the repair instead of only reporting the breakage. `checkCitation` already computes, for a failing duty, the line its anchor is actually on; printing that number turns a red gate into a patch. THAT IS CHEAPER THAN THE SCHEMA CHANGE THE FIRST NOTE PRICES and does not need the `conclusion_tense` ambiguity resolved, because it reports rather than resolves - the human still confirms. Neither has been taken; both are recorded, and the count of occurrences is now the argument.").

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE BLAST-RADIUS MODEL, 2026-09-10. Asked for by the owner as a refactoring
-- instrument AND as a test of whether ROFL is fit for refactoring at all.
-- Both answers are below, and the second is more interesting than the first.

finding(f_the_blast_radius_model_was_wrong_three_times_and_the_edge_count_was_right_each_time, insight).
recorded(f_the_blast_radius_model_was_wrong_three_times_and_the_edge_count_was_right_each_time, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_blast_radius_model_was_wrong_three_times_and_the_edge_count_was_right_each_time, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_the_blast_radius_model_was_wrong_three_times_and_the_edge_count_was_right_each_time, "rules/depends.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_blast_radius_model_was_wrong_three_times_and_the_edge_count_was_right_each_time,
  "`WHAT BREAKS IF I DELETE THIS FILE` WAS MODELLED IN ROFL AND THE MODEL WAS WRONG THREE TIMES BEFORE IT WAS RIGHT. Every error was the same shape and NONE of them would have been caught by checking the facts: the edges EXISTED, the counts were correct, and the closure took each edge to mean something it did not. (1) THE FULL CLOSURE DID NOT FIT. `needs(A,B) :- needs(A,X), edge(X,B,_)` over 679 artifacts and 3 201 edges reaches 88 472 rows and dies with `hole($rule(...), space_exhausted)` against the 500 000-row wall - and EVERY QUERY THEN ANSWERS 0, which reads exactly like a clean negative result. The repair was not a bigger wall: nobody asks for the closure, they ask what breaks if they delete THIS, and seeding the walk from a `target[dep](X)` fact makes it linear in the reachable set. A quantity that CONTAINS the answer is not the answer and can cost more than the question. (2) PROSE MADE THE GRAPH ONE COMPONENT. Seeded from three unrelated targets - boot.rofl, facts/rule-shape.rofl, scanners/rule_shape.ts - it answered 322 and 411 FOR ALL THREE, because `doc_ref` and `ledger_ref` run both ways through text: facts/findings.rofl names most of the tree and README names findings. A closure over a near-total relation answers `all of it` and looks like an answer. THE CONTROL THAT CAUGHT IT was asking three targets instead of one, which cost nothing and is the whole reason it was caught. Repair: prose is ONE HOP and never a closure, and it gets its own verdict - a deletion either breaks CODE or makes PROSE wrong, and those want different work. (3) `generates` POINTED BACKWARDS. A generator does not break when its output is deleted, it recreates it - but with `generates` in the closure `test/rule-shape.test.ts` appeared in the blast radius of `facts/rule-shape.rofl`, which is the exact opposite of the truth, since that test was written to re-derive from source PRECISELY so that it would not read the generated pack. THE SAME LITERAL WAS ALSO PRODUCING A SPURIOUS `reads_path`, because a path inside `writeFileSync` is a write and the scanner recorded it twice. THE TRANSFERABLE PART: three errors, three different wrong MEANINGS, one correct edge count each time. A dependency graph is checked by asking whether its answers DIFFER for things that differ, not by counting its edges.").

finding(f_the_first_real_refactoring_question_asked_of_rofl_did_not_fit, defect).
recorded(f_the_first_real_refactoring_question_asked_of_rofl_did_not_fit, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_first_real_refactoring_question_asked_of_rofl_did_not_fit, decision).
finding_note(f_the_first_real_refactoring_question_asked_of_rofl_did_not_fit,
  "THE OWNER ASKED FOR A DEPENDENCY MODEL AND SAID, IN THE SAME BREATH, THAT IT WOULD TEST WHETHER ROFL IS FIT FOR REFACTORING. It is, and the way it failed first is the finding. THE NAIVE TRANSITIVE CLOSURE OVER THIS REPOSITORY`S OWN FILE GRAPH - 679 nodes, 3 201 edges, which is a SMALL graph by any standard - does not fit in the engine`s default space. It reaches 88 472 rows and stops at `space_exhausted`. `DEFAULT_SPACE` is a hard 500 000 and `Rofl`'s constructor takes no `space` option, so A USER CANNOT RAISE IT FROM THE PUBLIC API - already recorded, and this is the first time it has been hit by a question somebody actually wanted answered rather than by a benchmark. WHAT SAVES IT IS THAT THE SEEDED FORM IS BOTH CHEAPER AND MORE HONEST: 256 ms, no hole, and it answers the question that was asked instead of a superset of it. So the verdict on fitness is not `ROFL is too small`, it is TWO THINGS THE PRODUCT OWES A USER. FIRST, THE WALL MUST BE RAISABLE, or the first non-toy query anybody writes dies for a reason they cannot fix. SECOND, AND WORSE: WHEN IT DIED, EVERY QUERY RETURNED ZERO ROWS AND `partial: true` - and a caller who does not check `partial` reads a complete-looking empty answer. That is the failure this language exists to prevent, arriving through its own public surface: the `hole` was there and the ANSWER did not carry it where a caller looks. A user writing their first real query will get `0` and believe it. THAT is the fitness problem, and it is a kernel decision rather than a rule fix.").

finding(f_the_cleanup_ratchet_moved_four_times_in_an_hour_and_the_tree_never_changed, insight).
recorded(f_the_cleanup_ratchet_moved_four_times_in_an_hour_and_the_tree_never_changed, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_cleanup_ratchet_moved_four_times_in_an_hour_and_the_tree_never_changed, decision).
finding_note(f_the_cleanup_ratchet_moved_four_times_in_an_hour_and_the_tree_never_changed,
  "THE OWNER ASKED FOR THE INSTRUMENT THE JS MODEL RUNS, POINTED AT THE REPOSITORY: not a list of candidates somebody walks with their eyes, but a TABLE that generates work and says when it is finished. Built as (role x duty) -> earns | waived(reason) | unmet, with `open_cell[audit]` as the queue, the role vocabulary and the waiver reasons declared by the OWNER ALONE on the model of `layer(L)`, and a CEILING rather than a count as the CI invariant - a ratchet that only comes down, because a count over a growing tree measures the tree while a ceiling is a claim about the WORK and merges when two branches lower it. THE FIRST RUN OPENED 127 CELLS AND THE NUMBER THEN MOVED FOUR TIMES IN AN HOUR - 127, 117, 140, 128, 120 - WITHOUT ONE FILE BEING CLEANED. Every move was the MODEL: (1) the table`s own four files gave ten artifacts consumers they did not have, so the queue fell for the worst possible reason; (2) `test_file` named by DIRECTORY rather than by the `*.test.ts` glob credited `test/cleanliness.check.ts` - the one file deliberately held OUT of CI - as run by the referee; (3) seeding that glob at the VERDICT instead of into the `ci_runs` CLOSURE left every test helper and fixture reading as unrun, thirteen rows of work nobody could do; (4) `str_pre(P, \"/\") = \"\"` parses and never fires, because the language has exactly one term-producing builtin form and a destructor on the LEFT of `=` is a relational literal over a relation nothing populates - the `unpopulatable` shape, silent, and it cost 18 roleless rows including boot.rofl AND made `test_file` empty in the depends pack, which in turn made `regenerated_by_test` empty. THE OWNER STOPPED THE CI STEP BEFORE IT LANDED, and the record now says he was right: a ratchet that ratchets on its own instrument would have gone red three times in one afternoon over nothing, and CLAUDE.md`s own sentence is that a gate red on an honest checkout gets switched off and then its absence is invisible. THE RULE THIS PRODUCES, and it is general: A RATCHET MEASURES THE WORK ONLY ONCE THE MODEL UNDER IT HAS STOPPED MOVING. Until then the number is about the instrument, and promoting it to CI converts a measurement into a nuisance. The table is therefore shipped as `npm run cleanliness` and `npm run cleancheck`, with the invariant file named `.check.ts` rather than `.test.ts` SO THE GLOB DOES NOT PICK IT UP - promotion is a rename, once the queue has been read once and the false rows are gone. WHAT THE QUEUE SAYS TODAY, unvalidated and stated as such: run_by_ci 82 (scripts and gates the referee never runs), has_consumer 28 (part real orphans, part files loaded through a path built at runtime and therefore indistinguishable from orphans here), read_by_rules 6 (a scanner whose facts no rule pack reads - CLAUDE.md calls that a report with extra steps and nothing checked it until now), indexed 3, regenerable 1.").

finding(f_the_engine_already_knows_nothing_about_the_library_and_nobody_had_measured_it, insight).
recorded(f_the_engine_already_knows_nothing_about_the_library_and_nobody_had_measured_it, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_engine_already_knows_nothing_about_the_library_and_nobody_had_measured_it, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_engine_already_knows_nothing_about_the_library_and_nobody_had_measured_it, "rules/layering.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_engine_already_knows_nothing_about_the_library_and_nobody_had_measured_it,
  "THE OWNER RULED 2026-09-10 THAT ROFL IS AN ENGINE AND THE JS MODEL IS A LIBRARY FOR IT - one of many to come - and asked for the tree to be re-laid-out accordingly. THE MEASUREMENT SAYS THE SPLIT IS ALREADY THERE AND NOBODY HAD CHECKED. Of the 28 imports the JS model makes into `src/`, TWENTY-SIX GO TO `src/api.ts`; the two that do not are both in ONE test file, `test/js-ast.test.ts`, reaching `src/parser.ts` and `src/unify.ts` for a parser and a TYPE the public surface does not re-export. AND THE DIRECTION THAT MATTERS MORE IS EXACTLY ZERO: `inversion[audit]` - the engine naming a library - is EMPTY over src/, adapters/, rust/, boot.rofl, policy.rofl and safety.rofl. That is the kernel grep test`s promise one level out, and it turns out to hold without anybody having required it. SIZES: engine 75 artifacts, library 112 (49 264 lines, a fifth of the tree), harness 504. 56 crossings, 2 of them deep. THE FIRST RUN WAS WRONG AND THE TELL WAS IN THE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER: it reported 29 deep crossings and 26 named ONE destination, `boot.rofl`. An audit whose rows are mostly a single target is usually wrong about that target rather than right about the sources - and it was: loading the semantics is not reaching into the engine`s insides, it is the front door in its other form, and a library that could not load boot.rofl could not use the language. TWO MORE DEFECTS THE MODEL FOUND IN ITSELF. The first draft was UNSTRATIFIABLE and the engine refused it before a rule fired, naming the whole stuck set - `side(P, harness) :- artifact(P), not sided(P)` with `sided(P) :- side(P, _)` is a cycle through negation, harness-ness defined by the absence of a side while `side` contains harness; fixed by giving each side its own relation and composing `side` last. And the default LOAD budget does not cover this program: the first complete run left `hole($load(4), budget_exhausted)` with `side` at 17 of 75 and `harness` at ZERO, so every downstream count was a number about the budget. WHAT IS OWED IS NOW A SHORT LIST RATHER THAN A REORGANISATION: the two deep crossings, and a decision about the 8 harness artifacts `moves_with[audit]` says would travel with the library - runtime/ingest.ts, scanners/materialize.ts, scanners/volume_locality.ts, scripts/witness_check.ts and four tests. THE STRUCTURAL POINT THE MEASUREMENT MAKES: the engine`s surface is `src/api.ts`, `src/store.ts` and `boot.rofl`, and it is small because the tree already treats it as small. Declaring it makes it A THING WITH A SHAPE - to keep small, document and version - instead of whatever happens to be exported, which is the difference between a repository with an engine in it and an engine with users.").

finding(f_load_takes_an_options_object_and_evaluate_takes_a_number_and_one_of_them_says_nothing, defect).
recorded(f_load_takes_an_options_object_and_evaluate_takes_a_number_and_one_of_them_says_nothing, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_load_takes_an_options_object_and_evaluate_takes_a_number_and_one_of_them_says_nothing, kernel_change).
finding_note(f_load_takes_an_options_object_and_evaluate_takes_a_number_and_one_of_them_says_nothing,
  "`Rofl.load(text, { who, budget })` AND `Rofl.evaluate(budget: number)` - TWO ENTRY POINTS ON ONE CLASS, TWO SHAPES, THREE LINES APART. Writing `r.evaluate({ budget: BUDGET })` is the obvious thing to do after writing the `load` above it, and THE RUNTIME TOOK IT IN SILENCE: an object where a number is expected, so the caller believed it had raised the wall and had not. Only `tsc` said so, and only because this repository happens to typecheck in CI - a plain JS user of the published surface gets no signal at all. IT IS THE SAME FAMILY AS THE FINDING BESIDE IT about a query returning zero rows with `partial: true`: the API`s failure mode is to look like it worked. The repair is a decision rather than a patch, because both spellings are now in callers: either `evaluate` grows an options object and keeps the number for compatibility, or it refuses a non-number loudly. What it may not stay is a parameter that silently ignores what it is handed, in a language whose whole argument is that an inability must be stated.").

finding(f_the_cleanup_table_gave_a_perfect_score_to_the_thing_that_had_to_go, defect).
recorded(f_the_cleanup_table_gave_a_perfect_score_to_the_thing_that_had_to_go, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_cleanup_table_gave_a_perfect_score_to_the_thing_that_had_to_go, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_cleanup_table_gave_a_perfect_score_to_the_thing_that_had_to_go,
  "I BUILT A TABLE TO FIND WHAT DOES NOT EARN ITS PLACE, AND ASKED IT ABOUT `scripts/kernel_grep.ts`: ZERO OPEN CELLS. Measured by putting the deleted artifact back into the world as facts and re-running: it had a consumer (`npm:grepcheck`, `test/phase3.test.ts`), it was run by CI, it was not a generated pack, it was not a scanner. Perfect health, by every duty I had written. THE OWNER DELETED IT THE SAME AFTERNOON, and it was fifteen commits of appeasement, three per cent coverage, and a whitelist still carrying `in_perspective` — A RELATION THE KERNEL HAD REMOVED. THE DEFECT IS IN THE AXES AND IT IS ONE SENTENCE: every duty I wrote was derived from the DEPENDENCY GRAPH, so every one of them asks WHO POINTS AT THIS - has_consumer, run_by_ci, regenerable, read_by_rules, indexed. That measures INTEGRATION, NOT USEFULNESS. And the two run opposite ways over time: the longer a useless thing survives, the MORE things point at it, so it scores BETTER on my table the worse it actually is. A table built on reachability is a seniority ranking wearing a health check. WHAT THE OWNER DID THAT I DID NOT, and it was two questions I never asked of anything: `what happens if it is not there` and `how exactly does it decide`. The first is a COUNTERFACTUAL - my table only ever asked what IS, never what would change. The second asks the instrument to state its own criterion, and the answer was that it had none: 120 whitelisted words and 39 hand-typed names, compared with indexOf. NEITHER QUESTION IS ANSWERABLE FROM A DEPENDENCY GRAPH, which is why building a better graph would not have found it. THE MISSING AXIS IS EVIDENTIAL RATHER THAN STRUCTURAL, and its sources are already in the tree and my model reads NEITHER: the findings ledger, which records what caught what through `addressed_by`, and git history, which records whether a check`s commits ACTED on its output or WIDENED it to shut it up. kernel_grep`s ratio is fifteen to zero. FOUR QUESTIONS, in the order that opens it: (1) WHAT DID IT CATCH - not what does it check; a gate that has never gone red on a real defect has never done its job. (2) HOW MANY TIMES WAS IT WIDENED TO SILENCE IT - the appeasement ratio, and it is computable from git. (3) WHAT CRITERION DOES IT ACTUALLY JUDGE BY, and is that the criterion the claim is about - the proxy test, and it has a computable smell: A CHECK WHOSE EXCEPTION LIST IS LARGER THAN ITS SUBJECT LIST IS MEASURING THE WRONG THING, 72 against 48 here. (4) WHO ELSE HOLDS THIS DUTY IF IT GOES - which `guards[map]` in facts/spec.rofl already models, and which my depends model never read. THE GENERAL FORM, and it is this repository`s own sentence arriving in the instrument I built to apply it: A CHECK INHERITS THE INDEX OF THE THING IT WAS BUILT TO COUNT. I counted edges, so only edge-shaped uselessness was visible, and the most useless thing in the tree was the best-connected.").

finding(f_a_file_that_is_fed_rather_than_developed_is_visible_in_git_and_two_measures_died_finding_it, insight).
recorded(f_a_file_that_is_fed_rather_than_developed_is_visible_in_git_and_two_measures_died_finding_it, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_file_that_is_fed_rather_than_developed_is_visible_in_git_and_two_measures_died_finding_it, decision).
artifact_removed(f_a_file_that_is_fed_rather_than_developed_is_visible_in_git_and_two_measures_died_finding_it, "rules/history.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_file_that_is_fed_rather_than_developed_is_visible_in_git_and_two_measures_died_finding_it,
  "THE OWNER`S POINT WAS THAT THE FOUR EVIDENTIAL QUESTIONS BELONG TO EVERY FILE, NOT ONLY TO GATES, and the one of them that is mechanical is git. THREE MEASURES WERE TRIED AND TWO DIED, which is the useful half. DEAD: `does it only ever grow`. Measured over this tree, `scripts/kernel_grep.ts` is 93 per cent additions - and so are `src/store.ts` (93) and `boot.rofl` (93), while `src/unify.ts` is 97 and `scripts/text_check.ts` is 100. In a young repository everything grows and the measure says nothing. DEAD: co-change direction, `A changes only when B does`. 331 non-merge commits is not enough history to separate a follower from a busy neighbour. ALIVE, AND IT SEPARATED ON THE FIRST TRY: WHERE THE ADDED LINES LAND. Strip the string literals out of an added line; if what is left is only commas, brackets and space, that line carried no logic - it carried A NAME SOMEBODY ADDED TO A LIST. AND IT FOUND, WITHOUT BEING TOLD, EXACTLY WHAT THE TREE ALREADY KNEW WAS WRONG: test/rule-shape.test.ts 39 per cent (RULES_BASELINE, one of the three things red in CI today), scripts/kernel_grep.ts 29 per cent (deleted the same day), test/spec-coverage.test.ts 15 per cent (the closed gate list, hand-edited twice this session), test/skill-bundle.test.ts 14 per cent (the ledger records it two behind its own directory), scanners/rule_shape.ts 16 per cent - against 0 to 1 per cent for src/engine.ts, src/api.ts, scripts/text_check.ts, scanners/spec.ts and everything else healthy. Four of the top five were known bad for reasons nobody derived from this. AND MY FIRST THRESHOLD WAS AN ASSUMPTION WEARING A TEST`S FACE. The rule said `the population is bimodal, everything healthy is 0-1 and the flagged set starts at 11, with nothing in between`, and that sentence came from A HAND SAMPLE OF EIGHT FILES. Measured over all 533 files with more than twenty added code lines it is a CONTINUUM: 375 at 0, 63 at 1-2, 37 at 3-5, 14 at 6-8, 32 at 9-14, 7 at 15-24, 5 above 25. This repository already writes that sentence about a sampled byte boundary; I wrote the same error into the instrument built to catch that class. Corrected in place rather than re-tuned quietly. SO THE MEASURE RANKS AND DOES NOT CLASSIFY, and the reason is a second question it cannot answer: IS THE LIST DERIVABLE. A whitelist of relation names is - every relation in the tree is computable - and that one should go. A named set of expected globals in a test is NOT: it IS the oracle, and writing it as a set rather than a count is this repository`s own prescribed practice. Both look identical from git. The cut is therefore DECLARED (`fed_cut(15)`) and the output is a queue for a person, and NO DUTY IS WIRED IN - a duty whose rule cannot separate the good case from the bad opens a cell nobody can close, which this table has now done to itself five times. ONE MORE INCIDENT, SMALL AND EXACT: a NUL byte reached scanners/history.ts through a heredoc, `npm run textcheck` caught it correctly, and THE OBVIOUS FIX - delete the byte - silently joined two paths into one string, broke every co-change pair, and left the gate green. A SEPARATOR IS NOT A CHARACTER YOU CAN REMOVE; it is a character that must not occur in the operands. The gate that caught it has a `fed` share of ZERO, which is the other half of the day`s argument: the checks that earn their place look different in git from the ones that do not.").

finding(f_the_language_has_no_nullary_relation_and_nobody_decided_that, question).
recorded(f_the_language_has_no_nullary_relation_and_nobody_decided_that, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_language_has_no_nullary_relation_and_nobody_decided_that, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_language_has_no_nullary_relation_and_nobody_decided_that, "src/parser.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_language_has_no_nullary_relation_and_nobody_decided_that, "rust/rofl/src/rofl_parse.rs").
finding_note(f_the_language_has_no_nullary_relation_and_nobody_decided_that,
  "`ready.` AND `ready().` BOTH FAIL TO PARSE - `expected '('` and `expected a term, got ')'` - AND NOTHING ANYWHERE SAYS WHY. Not START.md, not LIMITS.md; README carries one sentence, `Relations take >= 1 argument`, and no reason. The grammar is `terms := term (\",\" term)*`, which cannot produce an empty list, and the refusal comes from the PARSER rather than from any check. THE STORE ALREADY HOLDS ONE: measured 2026-09-10, `store.add('ready', 'main', [])` is accepted, `relPersp` returns the fact with empty args, `canonicalState()` contains it and `relCount` is 1. So the restriction is one production in the grammar and nothing beneath it. WHAT IT MEANS SEMANTICALLY, and it is not `a relation to nothing`: a relation of arity n is a set of n-TUPLES, and there is exactly ONE 0-tuple, the empty one. So a nullary relation`s extension is either the empty set or the singleton - IT IS A TRUTH VALUE. A nullary relation is a PROPOSITION, which is the degenerate case that makes propositional logic a special case of predicate logic. This language is first-order and currently EXCLUDES ITS OWN BASE CASE. AND THE OBJECTION DISSOLVES UNDER PERSPECTIVES. The one real argument against is docs/choosing-perspectives.md: a fact is an entry in a book and must be ABOUT something, so a subjectless fact strains the discipline. But a proposition IS about something - it is about the BOOK IT LIVES IN. `ready[deploy]` and `ready[test]` are two different propositions about two different ledgers, and `in whose book is this entry` answers cleanly. Without perspectives you would have a global boolean; with them you have a per-book proposition, which is exactly what perspectives are for. WHAT IT COSTS TODAY, MEASURED: of the FIFTEEN cross products in the engine`s own rule packs, THREE exist only because a guard has nothing to hang on - `wf_inadmissible(Rel) :- wf_declared(yes), demand_rel(Rel)`, `any_unblocker(W) :- takeable(W), unblocking_work(_)`, `ci_runs(T) :- test_file(T), edge(W, \"npm:test\", ci_step)`. And rules/kernel-policy.rofl shows the shape at its purest: `wf_declared(yes) :- semantics(well_founded).` IS A PROPOSITION WEARING A DUMMY ARGUMENT. `yes` carries no information; it is there because the grammar demands a term. rules/worklist.rofl says so in its own comment, in as many words: `this kernel has no nullary head, and a body that only asks does anything at all unblock still has to name the item it is about`. WHAT IT WOULD OPEN. The chief one is hoisting an existential OUT of a join: `anything_unblocks :- unblocking_work(_).` then `any_unblocker(W) :- takeable(W), anything_unblocks.` - the product survives but its right side is ONE ROW, so a quadratic becomes a constant multiplier. Also a clean negation over an existential (`not anything_unblocks` says nothing unblocks, where today you must carry a variable through the negation), and honest propositional flags where `semantics/1` and `sealed/1` already carry arguments that matter but `wf_declared/1` carries one that does not. WHAT IT DOES NOT COST: range restriction is trivial (nothing to bind), `derived_by($fact(rel, persp, $nil), R, T)` is representable since `$nil` exists, the rounds are untouched, and the store is measured above as already able. A MINIMAL OPENING IS AVAILABLE IF THE OWNER WANTS ONE: allow a nullary head only for DERIVED relations and not for base facts, so no book ever holds an entry about nothing while a derived proposition - which is what a guard is - gets the head it wants. THE DECISION IS THE OWNER`S. What is recorded here is that it was never made: the restriction is a grammar production written the obvious way, its cost is three of fifteen and one dummy argument, and the discipline that seemed to forbid it in fact supplies the subject.").

finding(f_reflection_can_derive_which_heads_want_to_be_nullary_and_the_model_was_wrong_twice, insight).
recorded(f_reflection_can_derive_which_heads_want_to_be_nullary_and_the_model_was_wrong_twice, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_reflection_can_derive_which_heads_want_to_be_nullary_and_the_model_was_wrong_twice, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_reflection_can_derive_which_heads_want_to_be_nullary_and_the_model_was_wrong_twice, "rules/nullary.rofl").
finding_note(f_reflection_can_derive_which_heads_want_to_be_nullary_and_the_model_was_wrong_twice,
  "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER REFLECTION COULD DERIVE WHICH RULES ARE EXPRESSIBLE WITH A NULLARY HEAD RATHER THAN A PERSON READING BODIES. IT CAN, ENTIRELY FROM THE PUBLIC SURFACE, and the model was wrong twice on the way. MEASURED OVER 48 RULE PACKS: 13 relations are PROPOSITIONS WEARING A DUMMY ARGUMENT (`wf_declared(yes)`, `program_negates(yes)`, seven in rules/parse-cost.rofl, `sound` and `unfinished_any` in rules/profile.rofl, `forged` in rules/self-audit.rofl, `slow_because` in rules/eval-cost.rofl) and 36 rules carry a PASSENGER - a head variable whose sole binder is a premise disconnected from the rest of the body. THE FIRST DRAFT READ `premise_var` AND `slot_arity` AND ANSWERED ZERO, and the zero was meaningless: those two are seeded by the host into the kernel`s OWN private store for safety.rofl and are EMPTY in an ordinary one - measured, 0 rows beside 29 rules and 29 `concludes`. A model over an unpopulated relation returns a clean-looking negative, which is the exact failure this repository has a word for, arriving in the model built to answer a question about the language. THE PUBLIC SURFACE IS ENOUGH AND THE SHAPE HAD TO BE MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED: `conclusion_lit`/`premise_lit` are reserved but READABLE, `$` IS writable in a rule body (safety.rofl already matches `$builtin(_, _)`), the reified literal is `$lit(Rel, Persp, Args, Tense)` with FOUR arguments and K is ONE-BASED. The first probes asked for `$lit(Rel, _, _)` at K = 0 and got nothing from that too. TWO DEFECTS THE POSITIVE CONTROL FOUND, and both are the same family. FIRST: a ONE-PREMISE body is always `disconnected` because there is nothing to be apart FROM, so the first run reported 28 passengers including every recursive walk in the model file itself; `multi_premise` fixed it and the audit went to 2 real rows on the pack that has 2. SECOND, AND IT IS A SENTENCE I WROTE AND THEN DISPROVED: the draft documented `a negated premise binds nothing, so ignoring $not is SAFE IN THIS DIRECTION`. True about BINDING, false about CONNECTEDNESS. `coverage_gap(I, Con) :- pack(I, ...), concern(..., Con), not concern_covered(I, Con).` joins its two positive premises THROUGH the negation, and with `$not` invisible they read as disconnected and both head variables reported as passengers. The sweep was 103 passengers with the blind spot and 36 without it - SIXTY-FIVE PER CENT OF THE FINDING WAS THE INSTRUMENT. Repaired by splitting the question: `binds` is positive only, because that is what binding means, and `mentions` is positive plus negated, because sharing a variable with a negated premise is still sharing. THE REMAINING KNOWN HOLE IS NAMED AND DELIBERATELY NOT PATCHED: a BUILTIN premise is `$builtin(Op, ...)` and this file does not open it, so `being_fed(F, Pct) :- fed_share(F, Pct), fed_cut(C), Pct >= C.` reads as two disconnected premises although the comparison joins them, and `Pct` is reported as a passenger it is not. Same shape as the `$not` defect - and left open on purpose, because that one was ASSUMED safe and was not, so this one gets measured before it gets fixed. AND THE TWO MODELS MEET: a disconnected premise that is the sole binder of a head`s variables is the same set `scanners/rule_shape.ts` reports as a CROSS PRODUCT, seen from the other end. It is not an accident of join order; it is a head that wanted to be nullary. The two were built for different questions on different days and agree, which is worth more than either alone.").

finding(f_a_normalizer_anchored_on_the_first_bracket_ate_the_head_and_the_rule, insight).
recorded(f_a_normalizer_anchored_on_the_first_bracket_ate_the_head_and_the_rule, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_normalizer_anchored_on_the_first_bracket_ate_the_head_and_the_rule, test).
addressed_by(f_a_normalizer_anchored_on_the_first_bracket_ate_the_head_and_the_rule, "rust/run_corpus.sh").
finding_note(f_a_normalizer_anchored_on_the_first_bracket_ate_the_head_and_the_rule,
  "THE NARROWED ORACLE WENT 150/150 GREEN AND WAS COMPARING ALMOST NOTHING, and the only thing that said so was a mutant set with a PLANTING CONTROL. The narrowing is sound in principle: a witness is one support among equals, the semantics does not fix which is recorded, so the comparison should keep the rule, the tick and the support CARDINALITY and drop the tuple. The implementation anchored on `index($0, \"[\")` — the FIRST bracket in the line — and a witness line is `wit ab1[main](archdruid,...) <- rb6dc800c@0 [fact:...]`, so the first bracket is in the HEAD. Everything from `ab1[` onward, head and rule and tick together, was replaced by a count. The harness reported a clean sweep over an oracle that compared `wit ab1[N]`. THE PLANTING CONTROL IS WHAT CAUGHT IT AND THE FIRST RUN WITHOUT ONE DID NOT. The first mutant sweep used nested sed with hand-escaped anchors; two of its six substitutions silently failed to apply and printed `зелёный` — a mutant that was never planted reads exactly like one that survived. Adding one column, `подсажен: да/нет`, turned two false survivors into two real ones (a witness naming a different RULE, and one at a different TICK) and named the defect in a single run. MEASURED AFTER THE REPAIR, anchoring on `^wit .* <- r[0-9a-f]+@[0-9]+ \\[`: eight mutants planted, SEVEN go red — different rule, different tick, different head, support one element larger, a deleted fact, a renamed fact, a deleted witness line — and ONE survives, which is the cost this narrowing was chosen to pay: the same rule at the same tick with a support of the same size and a different tuple. `--strict-witness` restores the old comparison and reproduces the four historical reds exactly (146/4 on both oracles), so the cost is not only stated but runnable.").

finding(f_deprecation_had_no_mechanism_and_the_agreements_file_proved_it_twice, defect).
recorded(f_deprecation_had_no_mechanism_and_the_agreements_file_proved_it_twice, "2026-09-11").
demands(f_deprecation_had_no_mechanism_and_the_agreements_file_proved_it_twice, code).
addressed_by(f_deprecation_had_no_mechanism_and_the_agreements_file_proved_it_twice, "scripts/render_docs.ts").
addressed_by(f_deprecation_had_no_mechanism_and_the_agreements_file_proved_it_twice, "facts/commands.rofl").
finding_note(f_deprecation_had_no_mechanism_and_the_agreements_file_proved_it_twice,
  "THE OWNER: `the deprecation mechanism for stale material is not worked out`. It was not, and CLAUDE.md proved it inside twenty-four hours of being rewritten FOR THAT REASON. Three hand-patches in two days, and the third carried a line that was actively harmful: `npm test` described as the whole suite, half an hour on four cores, to be run ONLY WHEN ASKED BY NAME — when it had become the eleven-second golden check over 81 worlds. The file told the next reader to avoid the cheap primary check and offered nothing in its place. Also `npm run history` listed after deletion, and four commands missing. WHAT ALREADY WORKED WAS ONE THING: a block GENERATED from the source of its fact, with a regeneration compare — facts/deviations.rofl to README, built the day before. Everything else in every document was prose asserting facts about a tree that moves. SO THE MECHANISM IS THAT ONE, GENERALISED. scripts/render_docs.ts takes NAMED BLOCKS: a block declares its source in its own opening marker, is rendered from that source, and `--check` fails naming the block. The commands block of CLAUDE.md is now generated from package.json plus facts/commands.rofl — THE SET FROM THE CODE, which cannot go stale, and the NOTE from a person, which must be written. A name in the pack that package.json does not have fails the render; a script with no note fails it too. AND THE SIMPLEST FORM OF THE SAME DISEASE IS CHECKABLE WITH NO JUDGEMENT AT ALL: a backticked repo path in a document must exist. NINE were dangling, every one from a single afternoon of deleting — `scripts/kernel_grep.ts` twice, `bench/mem_scale.ts`, two `docs/dogfood/` pages, two scanners, a brief, a fixture. A trailing `:123` is stripped and a glob is skipped; a path either is there or is not. BOTH CHECKS RUN INSIDE `npm test`, which costs a second on twelve, because a document that lies about the tree is as red as a fact that moved. Measured with planted defects: an edited note in the file reads STALE, a script removed from package.json reads BROKEN naming the reason, a reference to a file that does not exist reads DANGLING, and exit is 1 in each. ONE THING THE REPAIR TAUGHT IMMEDIATELY: three of my nine fixes introduced NEW dangling references, because I named the deleted files in prose WITH BACKTICKS. The name of a deleted file is not a path, and backticking it makes it a claim the check correctly reads as live.").

finding(f_readme_was_the_source_and_the_ledger_pointed_at_it, defect).
recorded(f_readme_was_the_source_and_the_ledger_pointed_at_it, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_readme_was_the_source_and_the_ledger_pointed_at_it, code).
addressed_by(f_readme_was_the_source_and_the_ledger_pointed_at_it, "facts/deviations.rofl").
addressed_by(f_readme_was_the_source_and_the_ledger_pointed_at_it, "scripts/render_docs.ts").
finding_note(f_readme_was_the_source_and_the_ledger_pointed_at_it,
  "THE OWNER NAMED IT EXACTLY: citing a line is `the man in the third chair from the door is the director` instead of `the director is Ivan Ivanovich, he wears a black suit and gives the orders`. A `duty` carried (File, LINE, sentence) and scanners/spec.ts read THAT LINE to check the sentence stood there — identity by POSITION. THIRTEEN README citations were reported unfounded while every one of those sentences was still in the file; the paragraphs above them had grown. Every failure was false, and the repository had already written the lesson down once, in test/policy-ladder.test.ts: `BY NAME, NOT BY RANGE`, after four assertions pinned src/engine.ts line ranges. TWO CHANGES, AND THE SECOND IS THE OWNER`S INVERSION. First, the line left the data: `duty[P](Id, Kind, File, Anchor)`, 131 rows rewritten, and the check asks whether the anchor is IN the file — EXACTLY ONCE, because an identity that matches twice is not one. That last clause found five ambiguous anchors immediately: `Nothing` (three times in LIMITS.md), `breach[audit](R)`, `collected[audit](X)`, `malformed[audit](R)`, `unmoded[audit](R)` and `conclusion_tense` (four times in README). All were lengthened to something unique. SECOND: README IS NOW GENERATED FROM THE LEDGER RATHER THAN CITED BY IT. `facts/deviations.rofl` owns all twenty deviation entries verbatim — `\\n` survives a ROFL string round trip, so the text is stored exactly and rendered byte-identically — `scripts/render_readme.ts` splices them between markers, and `npm run readme -- --check` is a regeneration compare, the same shape every generated pack in facts/ already has. The thirteen duties now cite `deviation(<id>`, which is a KEY and unique by construction, so there is no prose to match at all. AND THE INVERSION IMMEDIATELY SHOWED SOMETHING THE OLD DIRECTION HID: of twenty documented deviations, SEVEN carry no duty. They are written down and nothing holds them — `Budget accounting`, `Arithmetic that cannot be evaluated`, `why embeds finite-failure demos`, `whynot recurses`, `File layout ... exceeded by 3.5x`, `tm.rofl uses one @next rule`, `Node >= 22.6`. ONE MORE DEFECT FELL OUT, AND IT WAS TODAY`S OWN: the test census in scanners/spec.ts matches `^test(`, and this morning`s marker migration turned 251 tests into `mutant(`. test/bridges.test.ts marked ALL NINETEEN of its tests and went silently EMPTY to the census, taking `s7_no_nondeterministic_iteration` and `s_canonical_order` out of `covered` with it. A census that names one spelling of `a test` goes blind the day a second one is introduced — which is the same sentence as the line numbers, one level up.").

finding(f_the_feature_list_covered_one_modality_of_three, defect).
recorded(f_the_feature_list_covered_one_modality_of_three, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_feature_list_covered_one_modality_of_three, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_feature_list_covered_one_modality_of_three, "scripts/goldens.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_feature_list_covered_one_modality_of_three, "scanners/features.ts").
finding_note(f_the_feature_list_covered_one_modality_of_three,
  "TWO CORRECTIONS IN ONE, BOTH THE OWNER`S. FIRST: I WAS PARSING `.rofl` WITH REGEXES AGAIN, in the same session in which I catalogued that as the class to remove and counted seven existing sites. I added TEN more — three in scanners/features.ts and seven in scripts/goldens.ts — reading `guards[map]`, `cited_check[map]`, `check_world`, `check_file`, `check_opt`, `demo_unhashable`, `golden_state`, `golden_rel` and `golden_host` by matching text in a repository whose whole claim is that rules are data and `query` is how you ask. All ten now load the pack and query it; a regex reads what it was told to expect, the loader reads what is written and REFUSES what is malformed instead of silently missing it. `npm test` is unchanged at 11.4 s, so the objection that loading is dearer does not survive measurement. The eleventh was a regex over `src/api.ts` for the method list, now an AST walk — and its first version was wrong in both directions, taking every ClassProperty in the file (`store`, `diagnostics` and four option FIELDS read as entry points) while excluding the static `fromSnapshot`, which is as public as anything there. SECOND, AND LARGER: THE FEATURE LIST WAS ONE MODALITY OF THREE. `Rofl` is the TypeScript class; the port has `Session` with fourteen public methods, and `rofl-serve` speaks fifteen verbs over newline-delimited JSON with `runtime/port.ts` as its client. Measured, the two LIBRARY surfaces share FOUR NAMES — `assert`, `evaluate`, `fork`, `load`. Thirteen are TypeScript-only (`why`, `whynot`, `excise`, `retract`, `query`, `holds`, `save`, `run`, `factKeys`, `strataPlan`, `fromSnapshot`, `assertClauses`, `tickAdvance`) and TEN are Rust-only (`ask`, `cool`, `cool_many`, `cool_trail`, `reheat`, `reheat_trail`, `volume`, `open`, `fresh`, `tick`). Some of that is spelling — `ask` is `query` — but two whole FAMILIES are genuinely absent on one side each: the port has no explanation (`why`/`whynot`) and no retraction, and this host has no residency (`cool`/`reheat`/`volume`). The two engines are not one surface in two languages, and the corpus that holds them byte-identical says nothing about it, because it only ever asks them to evaluate. THAT IS ALSO THE FIRST PLACE GRAFEMA`S TAXONOMY PAYS OFF HERE. `SHARES_BEHAVIOR_WITH` was empty over the TypeScript class because one capability was exposed once; the service and the Session are the same capability exposed twice and share twelve of fifteen verbs, which is exactly the equivalence class the model is for.").

finding(f_i_reported_an_orphan_capability_the_language_does_not_have, defect).
recorded(f_i_reported_an_orphan_capability_the_language_does_not_have, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_i_reported_an_orphan_capability_the_language_does_not_have, code).
addressed_by(f_i_reported_an_orphan_capability_the_language_does_not_have, "scanners/features.ts").
addressed_by(f_i_reported_an_orphan_capability_the_language_does_not_have, "examples/checks/operators.rofl").
finding_note(f_i_reported_an_orphan_capability_the_language_does_not_have,
  "THE OWNER SAID THE SIX ORPHAN CAPABILITIES SHOULD BE SORTED OUT. Sorted: THREE OF THE SIX WERE THE INSTRUMENT AND THE SHARPEST OF THEM WAS A FEATURE THAT DOES NOT EXIST. `op:%` — I hardcoded the arithmetic set as `['+','-','*','/','%']` in scanners/features.ts instead of deriving it, and THIS LANGUAGE SPELLS MODULO `mod`. The lexer answers `unexpected character '%'`. So the census invented an operator, then reported that no demo uses it, which was true and meaningless — a clean-looking negative about nothing, which is the failure this repository has a word for, arriving in the model built to enumerate the system`s own capabilities. `src/unify.ts` EXPORTS `ARITH_OPS` and has all along; `CMP_OPS` in src/parser.ts is now exported for the same reason, and every list in the census comes from the code that dispatches on it. `flag:error` and `flag:unpopulatable` — not flags at all. They are fields of `QueryResult { rows, partial, error?, unpopulatable? }`, OUTPUTS, and my regex took any optional property of any interface in the file. Both are used — src/repl.ts reads `q.error`, test/query-unpopulatable.test.ts is named after the other. scripts/flag_census.ts already owns the definition (`an optional property of an options object taken by a public method`) and already parses for it; the census now imports `declaredFlags` rather than re-deriving it badly. A second crude pass also truncated `maxTicks` to `max` and `onFixpoint` to `on`. THREE WERE REAL AND ARE NOW COVERED. `str_pre` (8 packs), `str_seg` (4) and `str_segs` (4) are live in `rules/` and were reachable by no demo and no duty, so nothing held either engine to them — which is exactly how the port carried four collapsed reason atoms unnoticed. `examples/checks/operators.rofl`, seventeen lines, puts all four under both engines. Unreachable-by-any-demo went 7 -> 1. THE ONE THAT REMAINS IS NOT A REMOVAL CANDIDATE: `assertClauses` loads already-parsed clauses, bypassing the parser, and three tests use it to reconstruct a past tick from clauses read back out of a store. A public method with no worked example is a documentation gap, not dead weight.").

finding(f_the_bridge_from_a_promise_to_a_capability_was_already_two_thirds_built, insight).
recorded(f_the_bridge_from_a_promise_to_a_capability_was_already_two_thirds_built, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_bridge_from_a_promise_to_a_capability_was_already_two_thirds_built, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_bridge_from_a_promise_to_a_capability_was_already_two_thirds_built, "rules/features.rofl").
addressed_by(f_the_bridge_from_a_promise_to_a_capability_was_already_two_thirds_built, "facts/feature-map.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_bridge_from_a_promise_to_a_capability_was_already_two_thirds_built,
  "ASKED WHETHER THIS SYSTEM HAS GOALS AND USE CASES AND WHETHER THEY MAP TO FEATURES. It has 129 DUTIES in facts/spec.rofl — 28 `guaranteed`, 16 `acceptance`, 11 `prohibited` and 5 `done` from START.md, 31 `limit` from LIMITS.md, 13 from README, plus decided/boot/perf/wfs — and 118 `guards[map]` rows already saying which CHECK stands for which duty, and 76 `cited_check[map]` rows naming the FILE each check lives in. Two thirds of the bridge existed; the missing third is that a file`s features are the same parse a demo gets. So duty -> check -> file -> feature is a JOIN over what was already there, with nothing labelled by hand. MEASURED: 842 duty-feature pairs, 92 of 129 duties reach a feature, 28 of 56 features are stood over by one. SIX ORPHAN CAPABILITIES — no demo shows them AND no duty promises them: `flag:error`, `flag:unpopulatable`, `op:%`, `op:str_pre`, `op:str_seg`, `op:str_segs`. Nothing documents what they are for and nothing goes red when they change meaning. SIX PROMISES NO CAPABILITY ANSWERS, and one of them is the day`s own doing: `s_a_kernel_grep` — the duty whose guard was DELETED this morning as fifteen commits of appeasement — plus `s_two_runtimes` and `s_done_ci_green`, which are about the build rather than about a feature, `s_vocabulary_closed`, `r_stratum_in_kernel_source` and `r_vocabulary_additions`. AND TWENTY-FOUR OF THE TWENTY-EIGHT `unpromised` ROWS ARE THE BRIDGE, NOT THE TREE. They are every language construct — the operators, the destructors, negation, the perspectives, both tenses, the nullary head — and the reason is exact and checkable: ZERO of the 76 cited checks live in a `.rofl` file. 73 are `.ts` and 3 are `.yml`, so the bridge parses TypeScript and the constructs live in the `.rofl` those tests load. Four of the 28 are genuine and they are all flags — `error`, `onFixpoint`, `space`, `unpopulatable` — parsed by exactly the same pass that mapped the flags which did land. Stating which part of a gap is the instrument is the whole difference between a finding and a number.").

finding(f_a_feature_is_an_equivalence_class_not_a_name, insight).
recorded(f_a_feature_is_an_equivalence_class_not_a_name, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_feature_is_an_equivalence_class_not_a_name, decision).
addressed_by(f_a_feature_is_an_equivalence_class_not_a_name, "rules/features.rofl").
addressed_by(f_a_feature_is_an_equivalence_class_not_a_name, "scanners/features.ts").
finding_note(f_a_feature_is_an_equivalence_class_not_a_name,
  "THERE WAS NO ENUMERATION OF THIS SYSTEM`S FEATURES and the three things that came closest are each about something else: facts/spec.rofl lists 122 OBLIGATIONS with citations, README`s tables list the kernel`s VOCABULARY, scripts/flag_census.ts lists the API`s optional FLAGS. The owner pointed at Grafema`s `_ai/research/feature-taxonomy.md`, and the model there is not a list at all. SIX ENTITIES. ENTRY_POINT is a concrete access modality at one call site, identified by (modality, name, file, line). INTERFACE is its pure structural surface. CONTRACT is INTERFACE plus behavioural guarantees and belongs to the ENTRY_POINT, not to the feature — one product unit exposed via CLI and MCP has TWO contracts over one behaviour. BEHAVIOR is the bounded transitive call closure rooted at the handler, keyed `sha256(sort(transitive_call_targets))`. FEATURE is the EQUIVALENCE CLASS of entry points under SHARES_BEHAVIOR_WITH, and is a derived projection rather than a node. COMPONENT is a cluster of features. SO MY FIRST ENUMERATION WAS WRONG TWICE. It was a flat list of 56 names that conflated an ENTRY_POINT (`why`), a language construct (`str_len`) and a CONTRACT modifier (`retainTicks`) — three different levels of the taxonomy counted together. And its first version derived the list FROM THE DEMOS and reported 41 of 41 covered, which it could not have failed to (f_a_corpus_built_for_a_table_cannot_surprise_it). Taking the list from the SYSTEM instead — the destructor table, the operator set, the API surface, the flags — gives 56, of which 10 demos reach 49 and SEVEN are reachable by no demo at all: `api:assertClauses`, `flag:error`, `flag:unpopulatable`, `op:%`, `op:str_pre`, `op:str_seg`, `op:str_segs`. THE TAXONOMY APPLIED HERE, in rules/features.rofl over the JS model pointed at src/: 17 entry points, 1 113 call edges, 7 444 reach pairs, behaviour sizes from 5 (`factKeys`) to 206 (`load`) — AND ZERO SHARING. Every public method of `Rofl` has a distinct closure. That is the model`s own prediction rather than an empty result: the equivalence class pays off when ONE capability is exposed by SEVERAL modalities, and this API has one. AND THE HASH IS NOT NEEDED. Grafema keys a behaviour by a sha256 because a graph database wants a key; Datalog wants none. `A and B reach the same set` is the absence of a witness that they differ, in both directions — two rules and a negation, and the equivalence is derived rather than encoded. THREE INSTRUMENT DEFECTS ON THE WAY, ALL ALREADY IN THIS LEDGER BY NAME. `private_name(addClause)` as an ATOM against facts carrying `\"addClause\"`, so the exclusion never fired and all 34 methods read as entry points. A name-matched graph whose two sides do not join — the caller is qualified `Rofl.why`, the callee is written `E.activate`, and 0 of 557 callees matched a caller until both were normalised to the last segment (204 of 220 then join, and the over-approximation costs exactly FOUR method names carried by more than one class, measured before the model was built on it). And `str_seg(Q, \".\", 1)` on a name with no dot does not RETURN empty, it REFUSES with `str_index_error`, so a guard written as `N != \"\"` never fired and every unqualified name fell out of the graph — the same shape as `str_pre(P, \"/\") = \"\"` earlier the same day. Counting the segments first is the form that works.").

finding(f_the_demos_are_the_host_contracts_only_oracle, insight).
recorded(f_the_demos_are_the_host_contracts_only_oracle, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_demos_are_the_host_contracts_only_oracle, none).
addressed_by(f_the_demos_are_the_host_contracts_only_oracle, "scripts/goldens.ts").
finding_note(f_the_demos_are_the_host_contracts_only_oracle,
  "THE OWNER ASKED WHETHER THE DEMOS COULD JUST BE REWRITTEN OR WHETHER THEY ARE LIKE THAT ON PURPOSE. Measured: TWENTY-ONE OF TWENTY-THREE call `why`, `whynot`, `excise`, `retract`, `fromSnapshot`, `tickAdvance` or a runtime `assert`. Only `reach` and `yak` are load-evaluate-query and could be plain `.rofl`. So the 17 818 lines of TypeScript around 3 722 lines of ROFL are not scaffolding: they are the demonstration of the HALF A WORLD CANNOT REACH — explanation, retraction, excision with a blast radius, forking a store, advancing a tick — and that half had no oracle at all. The same surface 54 test files touch. SO THEIR STDOUT BECOMES THE GOLDEN. `npm run test:hosts`, one engine because they are TypeScript, 21/21 in 96 s, with the EXIT CODE part of the answer — `moot` exits 1 because it found a disagreement and says so, and a demo that stopped disagreeing would be a change worth seeing. THE MASK TOOK FOUR VERSIONS AND THAT IS THE LESSON. `N ms` left five demos moving. Every time unit left `npc` moving on `7.2 ticks/s`, where the number touches no unit. Enumerating formats is widening a guess, so the rule became one sentence — A LINE REPORTING A DURATION, A RATE, A RATIO OR AN ENVIRONMENT READING LOSES ALL ITS NUMBERS — plus the whitespace on that line, because `153` and `1` mask to the same character and the column padding carried the timing the number no longer did. `cram` printing the machine`s load average is the environment clause; the fourth version is what made it stable. TWO ARE DECLARED UNHASHABLE WITH REASONS RATHER THAN CHASED: `npc` prints a benchmark TABLE whose columns carry no units, so a timing and a row count cannot be told apart line by line, and `slop` measures itself against a headless LibreOffice and answers about whatever is installed. Both in facts/checks.rofl beside the reason. WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, stated: it is a REGRESSION oracle. It says the output changed, never that it is right. Blessing is what makes it right and blessing is a person`s judgement — which is why `npm run bless` prints every world and demo it moves.").

finding(f_a_load_evaluates_here_and_does_not_there, insight).
recorded(f_a_load_evaluates_here_and_does_not_there, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_load_evaluates_here_and_does_not_there, decision).
addressed_by(f_a_load_evaluates_here_and_does_not_there, "facts/checks.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_load_evaluates_here_and_does_not_there,
  "GIVING `rofl-load` A BUDGET FLAG TOOK ONE LINE — `Session::fresh(budget)` always took it and the binary hard-coded 200 000 000 — AND THE FIRST WORLD THAT USED IT FOUND A CONTRACT DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE TWO HOSTS. At budget 50 over six facts crossed three ways, TypeScript records TWO holes, `hole[$kernel]($load(1), budget_exhausted)` and `$load(2)`, naming which load ran out; Rust records ONE, `hole[$kernel]($adhoc, budget_exhausted)`. Same wall, same reason atom, different attribution — because `Rofl.load` EVALUATES what it can with the budget it is handed, and `rofl-load` loads every file and evaluates once at the end. Whether a load evaluates is a decision about the host contract, so the world declares `check_opt(\"budget_wall\", one_engine, 1)` with the reason written beside it, rather than standing as a permanent red — which is the state this repository has already recorded a check getting switched off in. AND THE FIRST DIVERGENCE I REPORTED FROM THIS WORLD WAS MY OWN INSTRUMENT, for the third time today. I passed the budget to `evaluate()` alone, saw TypeScript complete a world Rust walled on, and read it as an engine disagreement. In this host the budget belongs on every `load` as well: with it there, both engines wall at 50 and agree on the reason. The runner now passes it everywhere, which is what makes the comparison one. THE GENERAL FORM, and it is the owner`s point rather than mine: a check whose subject can be written IN THE LANGUAGE should be, because then two engines answer it instead of one host asserting about itself — and the disagreements surface in seconds. Four worlds of four to six lines each (`refused`, `destructor_hole`, `sensors_t3`, `budget_wall`) produced one real port defect (four reason atoms collapsed into one) and one contract difference nobody had written down, against 150 corpus cases that had found neither.").

finding(f_a_four_line_world_found_four_reason_atoms_the_port_never_had, insight).
recorded(f_a_four_line_world_found_four_reason_atoms_the_port_never_had, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_a_four_line_world_found_four_reason_atoms_the_port_never_had, code).
addressed_by(f_a_four_line_world_found_four_reason_atoms_the_port_never_had, "rust/rofl/src/engine.rs").
addressed_by(f_a_four_line_world_found_four_reason_atoms_the_port_never_had, "examples/checks/destructor-hole.rofl").
finding_note(f_a_four_line_world_found_four_reason_atoms_the_port_never_had,
  "THE OWNER ASKED WHY A REFUSAL CANNOT BE A GOLDEN — write the program that must be refused, and the refusal is the output. It can, and asking it found a port defect in the first run. TWO KINDS OF REFUSAL AND ONLY ONE WAS EVER OBSERVABLE. A LOAD refusal is host text: `res.diagnostics` = [`line 6: expected ')', got 'eof'`], outside the store, so nothing but a test could assert it — and the corpus DROPPED such a file and carried on, which is how `examples/ring1/l1.dense.rofl` hid the whole ring1 world for as long as the corpus existed. A RUNTIME refusal was already golden material and nobody had looked: `str_len` handed an atom does not guess, it refuses, and the kernel records `hole[$kernel]($rule(rb4de30f0), str_type_error)` IN THE CANONICAL STATE. So the fix is two lines of shape rather than a new mechanism — the load diagnostics join the golden as `refused <file>` lines, and the hole was always there. THE MESSAGE IS NOT HASHED AND THE FACT OF THE REFUSAL IS. Two implementations word a syntax error differently and always will; that a file is refused, and which one, is the part both must agree on. The wording is printed by `npm run bless` so a person still reads it. AND THE FOUR-LINE WORLD `examples/checks/destructor-hole.rofl` FOUND A REAL DIVERGENCE IN ONE RUN. TypeScript records `str_type_error`; Rust recorded `arith_type_error`. Located exactly: `hole_reason_of` in rust/rofl/src/engine.rs had TWO arms where src/reflect.ts has SIX, so `str_type_error`, `str_index_error`, `str_empty_separator` and `atom_unwritable` ALL came out as `arith_type_error` — which is precisely the collapse the reference refuses to make and explains in a comment: the repair a reader needs is about the string operation, and an arithmetic reason sends them looking at an expression that is not there. Four reason atoms the port never had, invisible to a corpus of 150 cases because not one of them reached a string destructor. Fixed in the port (four symbols in Vocab, four match arms) and 78/78 green on both engines in 11.6 s. THE GENERAL POINT IS THE OWNER`S: a check whose subject can be written IN THE LANGUAGE should be, because then two engines answer it instead of one host program asserting about itself.").

finding(f_the_four_corpus_reds_are_witness_choice_and_not_disagreement, defect).
recorded(f_the_four_corpus_reds_are_witness_choice_and_not_disagreement, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_four_corpus_reds_are_witness_choice_and_not_disagreement, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_four_corpus_reds_are_witness_choice_and_not_disagreement, "rust/run_corpus.sh").
finding_note(f_the_four_corpus_reds_are_witness_choice_and_not_disagreement,
  "THE PORT HARNESS HAS PRINTED `fail 4` FOR AS LONG AS ANYBODY HAS LOOKED, NOTHING WAS EVER RECORDED ABOUT IT, and I repeated `all pre-existing` today without checking. Checked now, and the four are not disagreements at all. MEASURED over drip, spat, sus and wtf: every differing line in the strict oracle is a `wit` line and every one in the loose oracle is a `d` line — ZERO fact lines in either, on any of the four. The non-witness part of `canonicalState` is BYTE-IDENTICAL in all four. The witness counts are equal (2317/2317, 4254/4254, 1396/1396, 1756/1756). And the loose diffs are BALANCED substitutions (3/3, 27/27, 87/87, 4/4) whose differing lines name the SAME derived facts on both sides. So the two engines derive exactly the same facts by exactly the same number of derivations, and choose a different supporting tuple for some of them. THE HARNESS IS THEREFORE RED ABOUT THE ONE THING THE SEMANTICS DOES NOT FIX. A fact derivable more than one way has no distinguished support; which one is recorded falls out of iteration order, and two engines with different internal order will differ forever. No amount of work on the port can turn these green — you would have to make the two iterate identically, which is a stronger contract than the one docs/port-surface.md states. This is the repository`s own sentence about a gate red on an honest checkout: it gets switched off, and then its absence is invisible. Four permanent reds in a harness whose whole value is that a red means something. AND ONE SENTENCE IN rust/run_corpus.sh IS FALSE. The loose oracle is described there as `the whole support hypergraph, every firing, not one witness and a count` — stronger than strict on provenance. If that were so a witness CHOICE could not appear in it, because both choices would be present on both sides. It appears in all four, balanced, over the same facts. Whatever `derivations.txt` holds for this class, it is not every firing. WHAT TO DO IS A DECISION AND NOT A PATCH, which is why this is recorded rather than fixed: either the contract pins a witness (and then the port must reproduce an order, which is the expensive reading), or the oracles stop comparing witness choice (and then the harness loses whatever provenance coverage it had, and that loss should be measured before it is taken).").

finding(f_what_a_store_owes_is_that_a_derived_fact_can_say_why, insight).
recorded(f_what_a_store_owes_is_that_a_derived_fact_can_say_why, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_what_a_store_owes_is_that_a_derived_fact_can_say_why, none).
addressed_by(f_what_a_store_owes_is_that_a_derived_fact_can_say_why, "scripts/port_corpus.ts").
finding_note(f_what_a_store_owes_is_that_a_derived_fact_can_say_why,
  "I CALLED IT A DEFECT AND THE OWNER CALLED IT NOTHING, and the owner was right: `it depends and it depends; what matters to us is the PRESENCE of provenance on a fact — computing every support is a different job`. Measured under that criterion and the thing I recorded as a reference defect dissolves. THE NUMBERS. `rules_js-controlflow`, direct load against restore-from-seed: base facts 2 715 = 2 715, DERIVED FACTS 433 = 433 WITH NONE LOST, witnesses 434 = 434 in the canonical state. The single differing row is a SURPLUS `derived_by` the direct run records for `guard_named[code](spread_element)` — a fact that is BASE in facts/js-controlflow.rofl and that a rule also happens to derive. The seed run does not re-derive a fact it already holds, so it records nothing, and that is the CLEANER of the two: provenance on a base fact is surplus, not owed. The reference was never wrong; my comparison was asking a question nobody had. WHAT REPLACED IT IS THE INVARIANT WITH MEANING: every fact NOT in the seed and not itself a `derived_by` row has a witness. Measured over all 75 worlds of the corpus: ZERO orphans. And the zero is a measurement rather than an absence, because the control was run — deleting one `wit` line from the state reports exactly 1 orphan, deleting every seventh reports 61. THE GENERAL FORM, and it is the third time today: a comparison inherits the shape of whatever the tool happened to emit. `canonicalState()` is a total dump, so comparing it compares everything a store records — including the choice among equal supports, which the semantics does not fix, and surplus provenance on a base fact, which nothing owes. Both of today`s corpus repairs are the same sentence: ask what the contract fixes, then compare that.").

finding(f_provenance_of_a_fact_that_is_both_base_and_derived_depends_on_history, defect).
recorded(f_provenance_of_a_fact_that_is_both_base_and_derived_depends_on_history, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_provenance_of_a_fact_that_is_both_base_and_derived_depends_on_history, code).
addressed_by(f_provenance_of_a_fact_that_is_both_base_and_derived_depends_on_history, "scripts/port_corpus.ts").
finding_note(f_provenance_of_a_fact_that_is_both_base_and_derived_depends_on_history,
  "TWO DISPOSITIONS, AND THE FIRST RUN OF `two_minds[audit]` FOUND THEM 2026-09-11. This finding carried `addressed_by(scripts/port_corpus.ts)` AND a bare `dismissed(wrong_on_its_facts)` at the same time, written by different sessions, and nothing in the tree could say so: `settled` is a disjunction, so a finding settled twice in opposite directions reads exactly like a finding settled once. THE DISMISSAL IS THE ONE THAT GOES, on the repository's own rule that a refusal carries a NAMED reason: `wrong_on_its_facts` is an atom with no note beside it, against a measurement that pins 3 599 facts to 3 598, names the surviving row, and says which 23 of 24 keep their provenance. It may still be right - nobody wrote down why - so it is kept here as prose rather than deleted, and the finding stands addressed.").
finding_note(f_provenance_of_a_fact_that_is_both_base_and_derived_depends_on_history,
  "WIDENING THE CORPUS TO THE RULE PACKS MADE THE GENERATOR REFUSE ONE WORLD BY ITS OWN ADMISSION TEST: `rules_js-controlflow` fails `reference does not round-trip its own seed`. The TypeScript engine cannot reproduce that world`s canonical state from a snapshot of it, and that is a defect here rather than a target for any port. PINNED EXACTLY. Direct load: 3 599 facts. Snapshot with the derived layer cleared, restore, evaluate: 3 598. ONE fact lost, ZERO gained, and it is not a domain fact — it is a provenance row, `derived_by[$kernel]($fact(guard_named, code, $cons(spread_element, $nil)), r7dec22eb, 0)`. The domain answer is identical: `guard_named[code]` is 29 rows on both paths. The 3 217 differing LINES are one deletion and the shift it causes. THE CAUSE IS THAT THE FACT IS BOTH ASSERTED AND DERIVABLE. All 29 `guard_named[code]` rows are base facts in facts/js-controlflow.rofl and ride into the seed as such; a rule ALSO derives some of them. On a direct load 24 of the 29 pick up a `derived_by` row; from the seed only 23 do. The restored world already holds the fact, so the rule`s firing records nothing for it — but that suppression is not uniform, 23 of 24 still get theirs, so the survivor set falls out of which round each fact first appears in. PROVENANCE FOR A FACT THAT IS BOTH BASE AND DERIVED IS A FUNCTION OF EVALUATION HISTORY, not of the program. SCOPE IS WIDER THAN ONE WORLD: any program that asserts a fact its own rules can also derive has this, and the corpus only sees it where the round-trip is checked. It is the same family as the four witness reds recorded beside this — both are the store recording ONE derivation of a thing derivable more than once — and both were invisible until the corpus was widened past the examples.").

finding(f_the_rule_packs_were_never_in_the_conformance_corpus, defect).
recorded(f_the_rule_packs_were_never_in_the_conformance_corpus, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_rule_packs_were_never_in_the_conformance_corpus, code).
addressed_by(f_the_rule_packs_were_never_in_the_conformance_corpus, "scripts/port_corpus.ts").
addressed_by(f_the_rule_packs_were_never_in_the_conformance_corpus, "rust/run_corpus.sh").
finding_note(f_the_rule_packs_were_never_in_the_conformance_corpus,
  "THE CORPUS THAT HOLDS THE SECOND ENGINE TO THE FIRST COVERED THE EXAMPLES AND NOTHING ELSE, and the examples are the smaller half of the language here. MEASURED 2026-09-10: 40 example files against 46 rule packs and 12 464 lines, and `rules/` is the ONLY user in the whole tree of `str_pre`, `str_seg` and `str_segs`. So the Rust engine had never evaluated a rule pack, which is why rust/rofl/src/engine.rs carries `No corpus case exercises these` and `nothing in the corpus reaches atom_of` as bare statements with no reason attached. AND ONE FILE THAT IS NOT A PROGRAM COST SEVEN DESTRUCTORS THEIR ONLY CASE. `port_corpus.ts` built a directory world by loading every `.rofl` in it and refusing the world on the first refusal; `examples/ring1/l1.dense.rofl` is a DENSE ENCODING of l1.rofl`s rules — `r(r1, l(len, [v(\"N\")]), ...)` — checked for reproducibility by test/example-ring1.test.ts and not loadable as a program. So `ring1` was absent from every run, and ring1 is the only user among the examples of `str_char`, `str_sub` and `atom_of`. Now a file that will not load drops ITSELF and is named; the world is refused only when nothing in it loaded. AND THE HARNESS WAS HIDING THE ABSENCE. rust/run_corpus.sh sent the generator`s stdout to /dev/null, so the line `skip ring1` never reached the person reading `loose pass 54 / fail 4`. A harness that discards what its oracle does not cover reports on a corpus whose edges nobody can see. It now prints every skip and drop before the table. THE RESULT, measured: 58 cases -> 148. Rust-only 5 s -> 12.6 s; with regeneration 22 s -> 40 s. 144 pass and 4 fail on BOTH oracles (drip, spat, sus, wtf), all pre-existing. ring1 passes, so the four string destructors the Rust engine was never held to are now held and green — proven rather than assumed. ONE NEW WORLD IS REFUSED BY THE REFERENCE ITSELF: `rules_js-controlflow` fails `reference does not round-trip its own seed`, which is the generator`s own admission test — the TypeScript engine cannot reproduce that world`s canonical state from a snapshot of it. That is a defect in the reference, not a target for anyone else, and widening the corpus is what found it. It is open. THE SHAPE THIS BUYS, and it is the owner`s point rather than mine: a whole-repository liveness check in twelve seconds, with the break LOCATED. The loose oracle is the derivation hypergraph, so a disagreement diffs to one line naming the rule id, the fact it derived and the support tuple that differs — `r247a5d68 | live73[main](e_honor) | ... | co5[main](archdruid,white...)` against `ty4[main](archdruid,creat...)`. A failing test name says which assertion; this says which firing.").

finding(f_the_agreements_file_became_the_thing_it_warns_about, insight).
recorded(f_the_agreements_file_became_the_thing_it_warns_about, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_agreements_file_became_the_thing_it_warns_about, decision).
addressed_by(f_the_agreements_file_became_the_thing_it_warns_about, "CLAUDE.md").
finding_note(f_the_agreements_file_became_the_thing_it_warns_about,
  "THE OWNER CUT CLAUDE.md FROM 403 LINES TO 54 AFTER I SPENT AN HOUR OF A SESSION BLOCKED ON THE FULL TEST SUITE, and the file was a cause rather than a bystander. MEASURED BEFORE CUTTING: 403 lines, 3 945 words, eight sections, and THE LARGEST SECTION IN THE FILE - 98 lines, a quarter of it - was `A measurement must certify its own conditions`, the ritual for running the full suite, carrying three nested amendments (`AMENDED`, `THE FIRST TWO ARMS WERE ADDED`, `./.claude/* IS EXCLUDED AND WAS NOT`). It gave a very detailed recipe for HOW to run and not one line about WHEN NOT TO. A guidance file with four thousand words on how to measure and nothing on the price of measuring will produce exactly one behaviour, and it produced it. AND IT CARRIED A DIRECT CONTRADICTION THAT PICKED THE WRONG SIDE. In bold: `The development loop is `npm test` (node) only.` Six lines later, in plain text at the end of the same paragraph: `While iterating, run the single test file you are touching; run the full node suite once before you report.` The first is a slogan and the second is the rule, and I followed the slogan - two full runs, the first invalidated because I edited the tree at minute 17 (the exact failure the ritual exists to catch, narrated by me in the same message in which I committed it), the second killed by the owner at 37 minutes. The hour produced zero information: the commit that ended it says `full suite not run`, which was available at minute 17. THE SLOGAN IS A GATE INHERITING THE SCOPE OF ITS INCIDENT, which is the file`s own sentence: the paragraph was written against somebody ALSO running bun, and `the development loop is npm test only` is true against bun and false as a statement about the loop. Twenty minutes on the same line as the words `development loop` is an invitation. THREE OF ITS FACTUAL CLAIMS WERE ALREADY FALSE. Line 4 said `the kernel grep test in CI scans it` - deleted by the owner earlier the same day. The command list offered `npm run grepcheck`, a script that no longer exists, and omitted `loop`, `test:engine`, `test:library`, `nullary`, `cleanliness`, `layering`, `depends` - which ARE the working loop. The quoted cost `19 m 43 s` is low: on a four-core machine the run passed 37 minutes without finishing. THE CUT IS SAFE AND WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED - every section has a home outside the file: the mutant-set brief and `a gate inherits the scope of its incident` (22 ledger mentions) and `ask where the check cannot look` (8) are in the ledger; `a measurement must certify its own conditions` and `model the task in ROFL because a model can say none` are findings by name; `every flag must be exercised by a demo` is ENFORCED by scripts/flag_census.ts and `run the witness query first` by scripts/witness_check.ts. Prose in a guidance file was the third copy, and it was the copy nobody could run. WHAT REPLACED IT IS A LIMIT RATHER THAN A RITUAL: no single run longer than two minutes, no more than three turns in a row on testing, `npm test` only when the owner asks for it by name, and waiting for a run is not work and never counts as progress in a report. Plus the standing goal the owner stated with it - code simple, clear, minimal, MINIMUM COMMENTS, and anything worth writing down that is not code goes in the ledger. That last one reverses the practice this tree has followed all along, and this note is the first artifact written under it.").

finding(f_working_a_models_queue_is_the_only_measurement_of_the_model, insight).
recorded(f_working_a_models_queue_is_the_only_measurement_of_the_model, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_working_a_models_queue_is_the_only_measurement_of_the_model, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_working_a_models_queue_is_the_only_measurement_of_the_model, "test/nullary.test.ts").
addressed_by(f_working_a_models_queue_is_the_only_measurement_of_the_model, "rules/nullary.rofl").
finding_note(f_working_a_models_queue_is_the_only_measurement_of_the_model,
  "THE OWNER SAID FINISH `wants_nullary` BEFORE STARTING ANYTHING ELSE - `we are galloping across Europe and breeding debt; shrink the surface the debt can surface on`. THE QUEUE WENT 49 TO 0 AND SIX OF THE SEVEN STEPS WERE THE INSTRUMENT NARROWING, NOT WORK LANDING. Measured, in order: 49 first sweep; 45 after three guards on `dressed_constant`; 36 after the nine real constants were converted; 24 after `$builtin` operands were opened; 9 after the verdict was made per RULE instead of per VARIABLE; 1 after the `$lit` PERSPECTIVE slot was walked and a passenger was required to have a premise that feeds nothing into the head; 0 after the last conversion. TEN of the forty-nine were work. THIRTY-NINE WERE THE MODEL. AND EVERY ONE OF THE SIX WAS FOUND BY TRYING TO DO THE WORK THE ROW NAMED, never by re-reading the rules. That is the transferable part and it sharpens the practice this repository already writes down. `ask where the check cannot LOOK` is a question you put to an instrument; ACTING ON ITS OUTPUT is the experiment that answers it, because a false row cannot survive somebody sitting down to convert the rule it points at. The six, each with the row that exposed it: (1) TWO CONSTANT TUPLES ARE A TABLE - `slow_because(fixpoint, barren_rules)` and `(fixpoint, late_filter)` have no variable between them and the argument is the whole content of the relation; `not varying_head` asked only whether some rule writes a VARIABLE there. (2) AN ARGUMENT A CONSUMER BINDS IS DATA - `saves_nothing(reuse_plan)` feeds `pure_overhead(P) :- saves_nothing(P), ...` and `reuse_plan` travels out as the answer`s name in `slow_because(parse, reuse_plan)`. (3) A VARIABLE NESTED IN A HEAD TERM IS INVISIBLE - `forged[audit]($fact(R, P, A))` names three and the `$cons` walk sees `$fact(...)` on the spine. (4) A BUILTIN JOINS WITHOUT BINDING - the hole this model recorded and left open on purpose the day before, and opening it correctly needed the question SPLIT: `mentions` (positive and negated) counts premises, `joins` (those plus builtin operands) decides connectedness. Feeding builtins into one relation killed three rows and OPENED NINE, because two builtins pushed one-positive-premise rules like `test_file(T) :- artifact(T, ts), D is str_pre(T, \"/\"), D = \"test\".` over `multi_premise`. (5) ONE PASSENGER IS NOT A NULLARY HEAD - `share(R, P)` has R reading as a passenger and P not, and dropping R narrows the relation rather than emptying its head; the verdict has to be per rule. (6) THE PERSPECTIVE IS A SLOT - `mechanism_seen[audit](M) :- env_ran(E), resolve_via[E](_, M, _).` joins THROUGH THE BOOK, and every walk in the file read `Args` and skipped `Persp`. That is the third time the same defect arrived in a different slot of the same term, after `$not` and `$builtin`. AND A SEVENTH THAT IS NOT A SLOT BUT A CRITERION: a passenger needs something to be a passenger ON. `cell[audit](Lang, K, L) :- node_kind(Lang, K), layer(L).` is the JS model`s matrix - two disconnected premises, three head variables, and the head IS the product - and the audit called all three passengers and recommended emptying the head that is the whole point of the relation. So a passenger now requires a POSITIVE premise that puts nothing into the head, which is what a cross-product factor asking an existential looks like. POSITIVE ONLY, because range restriction means a negation can never feed the head and counting negations would make every `p(X) :- q(X), not r(_).` a passenger rule. THE TEN THAT WERE REAL, and they are the point of the exercise: `wf_declared(yes)` and `program_negates(yes)` in rules/kernel-policy.rofl, `saves_no_firing(yes)`, `same_store(yes)`, `cost_tracks_store(fixpoint)`, `both_factors_grow(fixpoint)` and `scans_what_it_reads(fixpoint)` in rules/parse-cost.rofl, `sound[profile](this_world)` and `unfinished_any[profile](this_world)` in rules/profile.rofl - each now a nullary head - plus three hoists that remove a cross product outright: `any_unblocker(W) :- takeable(W), unblocking_work(_).` became `anything_unblocks()` read under a negation, `ci_runs` split its `npm test` lookup into `ci_suite_runs[dep]()`, and `scanned[audit](Lang)` split `ast_node[code](_, _, _, _)` into `corpus_scanned[audit]()`. THREE OF THOSE WERE THE THREE ROWS facts/cross-products.rofl PRICED UNDER `no_nullary_head`, so that reason is retired from the closed list and its rows are DELETED rather than renamed - a reason that names a language limit is a reason with an expiry date. rules/profile.rofl carried the limit as a comment (`Zero-arity is not in this language... so the subject is named`) and both halves of that sentence were repaired the same day. THE POSITIVE CONTROL IS test/nullary.test.ts AND IT IS THE PART WITHOUT WHICH NONE OF THE ABOVE IS A CLAIM. A number that falls because the instrument narrowed looks exactly like one that falls because the work was done, and this model narrowed six times in one session. Thirteen tests: two positive controls that must fire (a dummy argument, a head variable riding an existential), seven mutants that plant each narrowing and must stay silent, and TWO SURVIVORS written down rather than left to be discovered. Survivor 1: a variable inside an ARITHMETIC operand is invisible, and it can never be fixed - `str_pre(S, \"/\")` IS writable as a term pattern and could be named the way `$fact` is, but an OPERATOR functor is not, `*(A, B)` being refused with `expected a term, got '*'`. Half-closing that hole would make it read closed. What it costs is bounded and stated: a false `passenger` row, which cannot reach the verdict unless it is the rule`s only head variable. Survivor 2: a USER functor in a head reads as a constant, for the reason safety.rofl already gives in as many words - `a term carries an arbitrary functor and Datalog cannot destructure one it does not name` - which is why the kernel seeds `premise_var` from the host. There is no type test to fall back on: measured, `str_len` refuses an atom, a number and a compound alike.").

finding(f_the_nullary_head_landed_and_the_second_engine_was_the_thing_that_could_have_broken, insight).
recorded(f_the_nullary_head_landed_and_the_second_engine_was_the_thing_that_could_have_broken, "2026-09-10").
demands(f_the_nullary_head_landed_and_the_second_engine_was_the_thing_that_could_have_broken, rule_and_test).
addressed_by(f_the_nullary_head_landed_and_the_second_engine_was_the_thing_that_could_have_broken, "examples/flag/flag.rofl").
finding_note(f_the_nullary_head_landed_and_the_second_engine_was_the_thing_that_could_have_broken,
  "THE OWNER SAID ALLOW IT FULLY AND MAKE SURE IT DOES NOT BLOW UP LATER, so the map of surfaces was walked BEFORE believing the change. WHAT THE CHANGE IS: two lines in src/parser.ts and two in rust/rofl/src/rofl_parse.rs. `terms` may now be empty, and `ident ( )` is taken in the body element dispatcher BEFORE the expression attempt. THE SPELLING IS `p()` AND NEVER A BARE `p`, and that is the whole of the caution: a positive body literal is parsed FIRST as an expression and only rewound if that produced a functor, a term is also an atom, so a bare `p` would need lookahead into what follows at every site forever, while `ident ( )` is unambiguous with nothing. WHERE IT COULD HAVE BROKEN, walked one at a time. WORKED UNCHANGED: canonicalState (`flag[main]()`), the reification (`$fact(flag,main,$nil)` and `$lit(flag,main,$nil,$now)` — the existing `$nil` did the whole job), `concludes`, `derived_by`, `why` (explains to the axiom), `whynot` (`no rule concludes`), perspectives (`ready[a]()` and `ready[b]()` are TWO rows, which is the semantic point), chains of propositions, and the guard actually gating - a true flag lets 2 rows through and a false one lets 0. BROKE AND WAS REPAIRED: `flag()` as a POSITIVE body premise died with `expected a term, got ')'` while `not flag()` parsed fine, because the negation arm takes `lit` directly and the positive one goes through `expr`. Found by the first probe set, three of six shapes. FALSE ALARM, checked with a control: `excise` looked wrong at 2 -> 2 until the same probe was run on a program with no nullary relation at all and behaved identically - excise is counterfactual and my probe called `evaluate()` after it. THE ONE THAT MATTERED IS THE SECOND ENGINE. `rust/rofl/src/rofl_parse.rs` has the identical `args()` shape, so the change made the TypeScript host accept a program the Rust one refuses - AND THE BYTE-IDENTICAL CORPUS COMPARISON BETWEEN THE TWO IS WHAT NOW HOLDS THE `semantics as data` DUTY, since scripts/kernel_grep.ts was deleted this morning. A grammar the two hosts disagree about is the one change that could quietly unmake the thing that replaced the check. Changed in the same commit; cargo builds; 12 pass 4 fail, and the SAME 4 fail with the change reverted, so they are pre-existing. THE FULL ENGINE-SIDE SUITE IS A STRICT SUBSET OF WHAT IT WAS: 996 tests, 5 failures, and every one of the five was failing before the parser was touched (the other two of the previous seven were fixed earlier today). AND THE DEMO EARNED ITS PLACE IN THE FIRST MINUTE, which is CLAUDE.md`s rule paying out: `examples/flag/` was written because a grammar production nothing uses cannot go red - and running `npm run nullary` over it immediately reported `deploy_blocked` as a `dressed_constant`, THE MODEL RECOMMENDING THE THING IT WAS LOOKING AT. A nullary head has `$nil` args and therefore no variables, so it read as a constant head; `head_args(R, $cons(_, _))` is the repair. The destination had been mistaken for a candidate, and only a demo that actually used the feature could have shown it.").

-- ============================================ THE PINS, AND WHAT THEY MEASURE
finding(f_a_pin_whose_prediction_was_overwritten_is_a_changelog, pitfall).
recorded(f_a_pin_whose_prediction_was_overwritten_is_a_changelog, "2026-09-11").
demands(f_a_pin_whose_prediction_was_overwritten_is_a_changelog, decision).
finding_note(f_a_pin_whose_prediction_was_overwritten_is_a_changelog, "THE TESTS SAY IN THEIR OWN MESSAGES THAT THEIR NUMBERS ARE NOT PREDICTIONS. test/js-model.test.ts, read line by line: `assert.equal(coarse.cell, 184, 'predicted 134 coarse cells')`, `assert.equal(f.cell, 184, 'predicted 134')`, `assert.equal(counts(r).cell, 276, 'predicted 201 coarse')`, `assert.equal(f.cell, 302, '158 + 67 unrefined none-cells')` -- 158 + 67 is 225 -- `assert.equal(f.cell, 227, 'predicted 158 + 16')` -- 174 -- `assert.equal(f.cell, 135, 'predicted 158 - 51')` -- 107 -- `assert.equal(f.cell, 118, 'predicted 158 - 67')` -- 91 -- and `assert.equal(every.split('\\n').length, 74, 'positive control: 28 waivers were generated')`. EIGHT PINS WHOSE PROSE STATES A DIFFERENT NUMBER FROM THE PIN. In every one the prediction was written first, the run disagreed, and the LITERAL was edited to match the run while the sentence explaining it was left alone. A pin arrived at that way is not a test of anything: it records what the code did on the day somebody ran it, and it will be edited again the same way the next time it moves. THE TELL IS FREE AND MECHANICAL -- an assertion whose message contains a number different from the one asserted -- and nothing in this tree looks for it. WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE EIGHT: 514 numeric literals sit in assertions across test/, and the only way to tell a claim from a recording is whether somebody could have said the number BEFORE the run. The eight that carry both numbers are the ones that answered out loud.").

finding(f_a_cost_gate_prices_only_the_packs_that_were_in_its_world, insight).
recorded(f_a_cost_gate_prices_only_the_packs_that_were_in_its_world, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_a_cost_gate_prices_only_the_packs_that_were_in_its_world, "REMOVED test/js-layer-cost.test.ts, test/js-fixpoint-cost.test.ts and test/volume-locality.test.ts -- 1893 lines -- AND THE LEDGER HAD ALREADY WRITTEN THE ARGUMENT FOUR TIMES. f_both_cost_gates_refuse_the_pack_they_would_have_priced: `a per-layer cost gate whose worlds are pinned can only price the layers that were in the world when the pins were written, so EVERY pack added after a pin is unpriced by construction`. f_a_mutant_that_plants_a_bad_body_order_expires_when_the_engine_stops_caring: three of that file's four kill criteria had already fallen below their thresholds. f_the_cost_pin_measures_a_fixpoint_that_never_finishes: the number was taken on a world ending in `hole($load(3), budget_exhausted)`, so appending six functions read as a 27.6 per cent saving. f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of records a rule REFUSED because adding it `would move numbers measuring something else`. THE LAST ONE IS THE CHARGE: an instrument that forbids repairs to its own subject has stopped being an instrument. AND THE FIGURE THAT FINALLY FAILED SAYS IT PLAINLY IN ITS OWN COMMENT -- `the share's sensitivity is proportional to the neighbour's size, which means it is a detector of large neighbours and not a detector of neighbours` -- written at the previous recalibration, 0.5 to 0.2, and it broke again by 0.004. WHAT IS LOST, SAID RATHER THAN IMPLIED: nothing now measures rows handed out, so a badly ordered body is once more invisible until somebody notices the wall clock. That is a real hole and it is smaller than a gate that vetoes rules. The replacement, when it is wanted, is a number taken over a FIXED corpus declared as a world, not over this repository.").
artifact_removed(f_a_cost_gate_prices_only_the_packs_that_were_in_its_world, "test/js-layer-cost.test.ts").

finding(f_a_settlement_must_cite_something_that_exists, defect).
recorded(f_a_settlement_must_cite_something_that_exists, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_a_settlement_must_cite_something_that_exists, "THE LEDGER WAS AS ROTTEN AS THE DOCUMENTS, AND NOTHING LOOKED. `scripts/render_docs.ts --check` grew a walk over every `addressed_by(F, Path)` and the first run found FIFTY-ONE closed findings citing a file that is not in the tree -- bench/ (4), docs/dogfood/ (17), eight deleted scanners, scanners/tasks/, scripts/kernel_grep.ts, rules/history.rofl, two deleted test files -- almost all of them from ONE afternoon of deleting, the same afternoon that left nine dangling paths in the prose. The prose check was built that day; the closure was not taken, so a third of the settled ledger read settled on evidence that had been removed. THE FIX IS A WORD RATHER THAN A REPOINT. Aiming the dead citations at `facts/findings.rofl` would have made the gate green and toothless in one edit, which is the failure mode the gate exists for. `artifact_removed(F, What)` settles the finding and says WHAT went, `note_lost[audit]` catches one with no note left to hold the knowledge, and `two_minds[audit]` catches a finding that is both repaired and refused -- which the sweep itself wrote three times before the file was read back.").
finding_note(f_a_settlement_must_cite_something_that_exists,
  "THE OWNER'S CORRECTION, THE SAME HOUR: a deleted file is not a lost file, so a dead citation should change ADDRESS rather than be given up. `removed_in(Path, Sha)` names the commit that deleted it and the artifact is at `<sha>^:<path>`, one `git show` away. Thirty-three graves, and TWENTY-NINE OF THEM ARE ONE COMMIT - the afternoon of deleting, which the ledger could not previously state as a number at all. THE CHECK IS THE POINT, because a sha is exactly as good as whatever dials it: `scripts/render_docs.ts --check` runs `git cat-file -e <sha>^:<path>` for every removed artifact, so a grave with no body and a grave with no address are both red. Both arms proven by planting - a bad sha reads `not in 7fb522f^`, a missing row reads `(no removed_in)` - and the tree is clean on neither. WHAT THIS MAKES POSSIBLE and is the reason it is worth a relation instead of a comment: `artifact_removed` was a full stop, and it is now a POINTER. A finding settled by work that left the tree can be read again by anyone who wants the measurement back.").
addressed_by(f_a_settlement_must_cite_something_that_exists, "scripts/render_docs.ts").

-- ================================================== WHERE THE REMOVED ARE KEPT
-- A deleted file is not a lost file, and an `artifact_removed` without this row
-- is a dead end where a `git show` would do. The commit is the one that DELETED
-- it, so the artifact is at `<sha>^:<path>`; `scripts/render_docs.ts --check`
-- verifies exactly that, which is why the sha is here rather than in prose.
-- Twenty-nine of the thirty-three are one commit: the afternoon of deleting.
edb(removed_in).
