finding(f_the_tree_owned_a_decision_procedure_and_i_answered_in_prose, defect).
recorded(f_the_tree_owned_a_decision_procedure_and_i_answered_in_prose, "2026-09-11").
demands(f_the_tree_owned_a_decision_procedure_and_i_answered_in_prose, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_the_tree_owned_a_decision_procedure_and_i_answered_in_prose, "THE OWNER FOUND rules/decisions/production-readiness.rofl AND ASKED WHETHER I REMEMBERED IT. I did not. For a whole session the question `is this ready for 0.8 or 1.0` was answered in paragraphs, while 48 lines in the tree derive `go`, `conditional_go` and `no_go` from nine concern axes, and 250 more lines under rules/inquiry/ and rules/policies/ carry the epistemic grades, the confirmation grades, the staleness policy and the authority gate for accepting a residual gap. THE WORSE HALF IS THAT BOTH OF ITS GOLDENS WERE GREEN AND NEITHER ANSWERED ANYTHING. `scripts/goldens.ts` walks rules/ loading each pack beside `facts/<same name>.rofl` -- there is none -- so the decision pack ran with its data undefined, carrying `undefined_premise[audit]` at TEN and passing; and it walks examples/ loading a directory's `.rofl` files and no rules, so `examples/atlas-launch/` ran as eight claims and six observations with nothing to read them. Two halves of one instrument, each green in isolation, and the verdict in neither. That is the repository's own sentence about a clean-looking negative over an unpopulated relation, committed by the check built to prevent it. THE FIX NEEDED NO CODE: `check_file` is a relation, so a declared world already takes as many files as it is given, and facts/checks.rofl now assembles both -- `readiness_atlas` reproduces the fixture's own predicted outcome (no_go, blocked on aggregate_capacity and billing, delivery_rate contested, three open obligations) with `undefined_premise` at ZERO. AND THE SAME PACK NOW ANSWERS ABOUT THIS TREE. examples/rofl-release/ states seven claims decidable by command, with the journal naming the command for each: `recommendation(rofl_1_0, no_go)`, go_blocked on `port_surface_complete` ALONE, `docs_derived_from_ledger` unknown rather than false because nothing was observed either way, `kernel_has_no_runtime_dependency` CONTESTED because both polarities are true of different things -- nothing under src/ imports outside node:, and package.json declares @babel/parser -- and five coverage gaps. THE VERDICT AGREES WITH THE PROSE AND THAT IS THE LEAST INTERESTING PART. What the prose could not do is move when the tree moves: the verdict is a golden now, so closing d_port_owes_why turns `no_go` into a diff.").
addressed_by(f_the_tree_owned_a_decision_procedure_and_i_answered_in_prose, "examples/rofl-release/frame.rofl").

finding(f_a_concern_nobody_can_cover_reads_as_one_nobody_covered, insight).
recorded(f_a_concern_nobody_can_cover_reads_as_one_nobody_covered, "2026-09-11").
demands(f_a_concern_nobody_can_cover_reads_as_one_nobody_covered, decision).
finding_note(f_a_concern_nobody_can_cover_reads_as_one_nobody_covered, "FIVE OF THE NINE PRODUCTION-READINESS CONCERNS ARE UNMAPPABLE FOR A LANGUAGE AND THE PACK CANNOT SAY SO. `coverage_gap(I, Con) :- pack(I, production_readiness), concern(production_readiness, Con), not concern_covered(I, Con)` is a closed-world read over a fixed list, so `load`, `monitoring`, `rollback`, `billing` and `customer_acceptance` come out as gaps against a library the same way an unmeasured load test would against a service. The verdict is unaffected here -- `port_surface_complete` blocks on its own -- but a CONDITIONAL GO is unreachable for this inquiry for a reason that has nothing to do with its readiness, and that is a silent floor under the answer. THE SHAPE IS THE REPOSITORY'S OWN: a check inherits the index of the thing it was built to count, and this one is indexed by the concerns of a deployed service. TWO HONEST FORMS EXIST and the choice is the owner's: `not_applicable(I, Con)` asserted in the frame and subtracted in the pack, which puts the judgement where the authority is and leaves it visible; or a second decision pack whose concern list is a library's. The dishonest third -- mapping `rollback` to `fromSnapshot` because the engine happens to have one -- would answer the pack's question with a different question's evidence, which is the `borrowed reason` this ledger already records three times.").

finding(f_the_census_could_not_see_the_port_so_no_port_duty_could_be_guarded, defect).
recorded(f_the_census_could_not_see_the_port_so_no_port_duty_could_be_guarded, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_the_census_could_not_see_the_port_so_no_port_duty_could_be_guarded, "`d_port_owes_why` WOULD HAVE READ UNCOVERED ON THE DAY IT WAS DISCHARGED. scanners/spec.ts builds the census -- the list a duty's guard is checked against -- from `test/*.test.ts` and nothing else, so a check written in Rust cannot be cited: name it and `speccheck` calls the citation dangling; do not name it and the duty stays unguarded. Either way a duty ABOUT THE PORT has no reachable green state, and nothing said so, because `UNCOVERED` is also what an undone duty looks like. THE SAME BLINDNESS ONE LANGUAGE FURTHER OUT as the `mutant()` miss recorded three days earlier: the census names one spelling of `a test` and goes silent on every other. It now walks `rust/rofl/tests/*.rs`, keyed on the FUNCTION name because that is what `cargo test` prints and therefore the only thing a citation could be checked against. Uncovered guaranteed duties 11 -> 10, dangling citations 0. THE RESIDUE IS THAT NOTHING RUNS THEM: `npm test` is the golden loop and `cargo test` is nobody's step, so the census can now see a check the tree never executes. That is a smaller lie than the previous one and it is still one.").
addressed_by(f_the_census_could_not_see_the_port_so_no_port_duty_could_be_guarded, "scanners/spec.ts").

finding(f_two_engines_that_explain_differently_are_two_languages, insight).
recorded(f_two_engines_that_explain_differently_are_two_languages, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_two_engines_that_explain_differently_are_two_languages, "THE PORT OWED EXPLANATION AND THE DEBT IS PAID, AND THE INTERESTING PART IS WHAT THE GATE HAD TO BE. The corpus asks both engines to EVALUATE and compares a byte-identical `canonicalState`, which says nothing about whether either can be ASKED anything afterwards -- so a port could pass every world in the tree and be a table. `why` and `whynot` were therefore gated on their TEXT rather than on their shape: the sixteen expected strings in rust/rofl/tests/explain.rs came out of src/api.ts on the same program and were pasted in unedited. WHAT THAT FORCED INTO AGREEMENT IS MORE THAN THE TREE SHAPE. The content-addressed rule ids (`rb0ab574d`), the `@tick` numbers, the `?B#1` and `?B#3` suffixes the evaluator assigns when it renames a clause, the two spaces before `<=`, the `-- blocked: K holds` clause naming the fact that defeated a negation, the `[cycle]` cut that fires from the PATH rather than from the depth cap, and the single-step `whynot` that `why` inlines under `[finite failure]` -- a negation rendered without it is a claim with its evidence removed. All sixteen match. THE ONE PLACE THE TWO HOSTS COULD STILL DRIFT is where they already have: `failing_premises` must explore the body IN THE ORDER THE EVALUATOR SOLVES IN, because `whynot` is top-down and reads a negation with the head's arguments BOUND where the bottom-up run read the same negation with them free. src/api.ts carries that comment from an incident; the port carries the same call to `plan_body` for the same reason, before the incident.").
addressed_by(f_two_engines_that_explain_differently_are_two_languages, "rust/rofl/tests/explain.rs").

finding(f_a_snapshot_at_a_boundary_lost_the_provenance_of_what_crossed_it, defect).
recorded(f_a_snapshot_at_a_boundary_lost_the_provenance_of_what_crossed_it, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_a_snapshot_at_a_boundary_lost_the_provenance_of_what_crossed_it, "THE FIRST TEST THAT EVER TOOK A SNAPSHOT AT A TICK BOUNDARY FOUND IT. `counter(M) @next :- counter(N), N < 5, M is N + 1` concludes at the boundary, and the boundary takes `counter(1)` out of the world with the tick. The witness of `counter(2)` still names it, and MUST -- that is what the fact was derived from. The reference store keeps a premise as a STRING, so it survives a snapshot with nothing to point at; this store keeps a `FactId`, which needs a record to exist, and `seed::restore` was counting the miss as `dangling` and dropping the whole firing. MEASURED IN ONE LINE OF DIFFERENCE: save, open, canonical state -- `counter[main](2) ... support=1` became `support=0` and the `wit counter[main](2) <- raf4205f4@1 [fact:counter[main](1); bi:1 < 5; bi:2 is +(1,1)]` line was simply gone. The reference round-trips the same world byte for byte. THE SEED'S `dangling` COUNTER IS WHY IT SURVIVED: its comment reads `Zero on every corpus seed (the derived layer is cleared before the snapshot is taken)`, which is true and is the whole blindness -- every seed the port corpus has ever read was taken with the derived layer CLEARED, so the one case where a witness outlives its premise had no world to appear in. A counter that is always zero is not evidence that the case does not arise; it is evidence about which cases were tried. THE REPAIR IS A FIELD OF ITS OWN, `ghosts`: the dead records travel with the snapshot, are re-entered and killed again on the way in, and appear in no answer and no canonical state -- the witness simply has its id back. Separate from `facts` because the reference's `restore` would read them out of it and make them LIVE, and a snapshot that resurrects five ticks of history is worse than one that forgets a witness. AND THE OWNER'S QUESTION IS NOW ANSWERED BY A GATE RATHER THAN BY A CLAIM: three boundaries, a snapshot, a session rebuilt from it, three more boundaries -- byte-identical to six taken without stopping.").
addressed_by(f_a_snapshot_at_a_boundary_lost_the_provenance_of_what_crossed_it, "rust/rofl/src/seed.rs").

finding(f_an_accessor_over_an_empty_relation_answers_none_forever, insight).
recorded(f_an_accessor_over_an_empty_relation_answers_none_forever, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_an_accessor_over_an_empty_relation_answers_none_forever, "`strataPlan` WAS WRITTEN FOR THE PORT AND THEN DELETED, and the deletion is the finding. It reads `readStrata()`, which reads the `stratum` relation -- and measured on BOTH hosts, with boot.rofl loaded and without, that relation holds ZERO rows in a settled store. The reference answers `level: 1` for `up(X) :- down(X), not gone(X)` only because `Rofl.strataPlan` calls `prepared()` first and reads the table the evaluation builds WHILE IT IS STILL STANDING. So a port accessor over the settled store answers `None` for every rule in every world, forever, and a test written against it passes. THE TELL WAS THE TEST: the first expectation asserted `is_some()` and went red, the second asserted `is_none()` without boot and green `is_some()` with it and went red again -- two failures in a row where the CONTROL moved and the subject did not. THIRD INSTANCE THIS SESSION of the same sentence: a check inherits the index of the thing it was built to read, and this one was built to read a table that only exists mid-flight. Shipping it would have added a green entry point that cannot be wrong because it never says anything, which is worse than the gap. The honest form re-runs the stratifier and is a piece of work rather than an accessor; it is not owed by any duty and is not queued.").
dismissed(f_an_accessor_over_an_empty_relation_answers_none_forever, "not shipped: an entry point that can only answer None is a gap wearing a green test").

finding(f_a_replay_without_the_budget_is_a_history_that_never_happened, insight).
recorded(f_a_replay_without_the_budget_is_a_history_that_never_happened, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_a_replay_without_the_budget_is_a_history_that_never_happened, "`evals` WAS THE LAST FIELD THE PORT'S SNAPSHOT WENT OUT EMPTY ON, and the reason it mattered is not bookkeeping. docs/time-and-continuity.md: a past tick is reconstructable EXACTLY -- same program, same dated inputs, bit-identical fixpoint -- but only if the replay is given the SAME BUDGET. A tick cut short at 100 000 steps and replayed at 500 000 derives more, and the replay then disagrees with history while claiming to be it. So the per-tick record of what the standing evaluation was allowed and what it spent is not a statistic, it is part of the world. `Store::eval_log` now keeps it and the snapshot carries it both ways. WHERE IT IS WRITTEN IS THE PART THAT NEEDED CARE: both exits of `Eval::run`, AND the budget wall in `Session::evaluate`, which unwinds PAST those exits -- and the tick that ran out is precisely the one whose budget a replay must be given, so the one path that skips the note is the one that must not. MEASURED AGAINST THE REFERENCE on the counter world, ticks 0/1/2 at budget 100 000 and 2 steps each, identical on both hosts and identical across a save. TWO OF THE FOUR TESTS WENT RED ON THEIR FIRST RUN AND BOTH WERE THE EXPECTATION, NOT THE CODE: a wall at budget 1 spends 2 steps rather than 0, and `assert(\"marker(1).\")` moved the step count by NOTHING because no rule reads `marker` -- a control that cannot move is not a control, and it was replaced with a fact `emit(N) :- counter(N)` consumes. THE SURFACE IS NOW TWO NAMES OUT OF SIXTEEN, both refused with a reason: `strataPlan`, which reads a relation empty in a settled store on both hosts, and `assertClauses`, which no demo reaches. Whether that is COMPLETE is a decision and not a measurement, so both polarities are in the release journal and `port_surface_complete` reads CONTESTED rather than being settled by whoever wrote the last line.").
addressed_by(f_a_replay_without_the_budget_is_a_history_that_never_happened, "rust/rofl/src/store.rs").

finding(f_the_pack_falls_silent_where_it_should_say_conditional, defect).
recorded(f_the_pack_falls_silent_where_it_should_say_conditional, "2026-09-11").
demands(f_the_pack_falls_silent_where_it_should_say_conditional, decision).
finding_note(f_the_pack_falls_silent_where_it_should_say_conditional, "THE AUTHORITY CLEARED THE BLOCKER AND THE PACK STOPPED ANSWERING AT ALL. `port_surface_complete` moved from `blocking` to `requires` on the owner's judgement that two names of sixteen are not what a base version owes; `go_blocked` went to zero and `violated_blocking` with it, so `recommendation(I, no_go)` no longer fires -- and neither positive verdict can. `go` wants no open obligation, no contested one and no coverage gap; `conditional_go` wants no contested one and no coverage gap. This inquiry has one open, two contested and five gaps, so the answer is SILENCE. FOUR STATES AND ONLY THREE HAVE A NAME. `no_go` is derived, `go` and `conditional_go` are derived, and `nothing blocks and it is not clean either` is the state a reader meets as an empty relation -- which reads exactly like a pack that was never activated. rules/decisions/production-readiness.rofl carried `undefined_premise[audit]` at ten for six days for the same reason: an empty answer and an unasked question are the same shape. WHAT THE THREE REMAINING ITEMS ACTUALLY ARE, because the silence hides that too: five coverage gaps that are a deployed service's concerns and not a language's (f_a_concern_nobody_can_cover_reads_as_one_nobody_covered, awaiting the `not_applicable` decision); `kernel_has_no_runtime_dependency` contested because both polarities are true of different things, which is a claim that needs splitting rather than settling; and `docs_derived_from_ledger` unknown and unaccepted, which is w_docs_from_ledger and is real work. NONE of the three is a reason to withhold a verdict from a reader -- they are the CONTENT of the verdict. The pack should say `conditional_go` and name them, or say `no_verdict` and name why; an empty `recommendation` says neither.").

finding(f_a_requirement_measured_false_is_invisible_to_the_verdict, defect).
recorded(f_a_requirement_measured_false_is_invisible_to_the_verdict, "2026-09-11").
demands(f_a_requirement_measured_false_is_invisible_to_the_verdict, rule_and_test).
finding_note(f_a_requirement_measured_false_is_invisible_to_the_verdict, "THE PACK SAYS `conditional_go` WHILE FIVE OF ITS REQUIREMENTS ARE MEASURED FALSE. Demonstrated rather than argued: with `accepted_gap_by(rofl_1_0, docs_derived_from_ledger, owner)` added to the frame, `recommendation(rofl_1_0, conditional_go)` fires with `refuted_obligation` carrying every_duty_has_a_guard, every_feature_has_a_world, package_declares_what_it_ships, release_contract_is_current AND used_by_somebody_other_than_its_author -- the last of which means NOBODY OUTSIDE THE AUTHOR HAS USED IT. THE RULE NEVER LOOKS. `conditional_go` reads `not go_blocked`, `not contested_obligation`, `not coverage_gap`, `open_obligation(I, _)` and `not unaccepted_open`; `refuted_obligation` appears in no positive verdict's body, and `unaccepted_open` is defined over OPEN obligations only, so authority-acceptance cannot even be demanded for a refutation. A non-blocking requirement that is UNKNOWN therefore costs more than one that is KNOWN FALSE, which is the ordering exactly inverted. WHY IT SURVIVED SIX DAYS: examples/atlas-launch, the only fixture, carries its refutation on a BLOCKING claim (`billing_e2e_verified`), where `violated_blocking` catches it and the verdict is no_go. The one arrangement the fixture never had is the one a real inquiry reached on its first outing -- a refuted claim that merely `requires`. TWO HONEST FORMS, and the choice is the pack's owner's: `refuted_obligation` joins the exclusion list of both positive verdicts, which makes a measured-false requirement as strong as a blocking one; or `unaccepted_open` widens to cover refutations, so the authority must ACCEPT each one by name and the acceptance is on the record. The second is better -- it keeps the shape the pack already has for residual risk -- and it is a decision because it changes what every existing GO in this repository would have meant.").

finding(f_five_axes_called_inapplicable_and_three_of_them_were_not, pitfall).
recorded(f_five_axes_called_inapplicable_and_three_of_them_were_not, "2026-09-11").
finding_note(f_five_axes_called_inapplicable_and_three_of_them_were_not, "I DECLARED FIVE OF NINE CONCERNS INAPPLICABLE IN ONE BREATH AND THE OWNER TOOK THREE BACK, one question each. LOAD: `why not applicable?` -- and it is the engine's CENTRAL question, with 5 676 864 facts over a 128-file world at 198 B/fact and 45.7 s already measured in docs/the-target.md, plus two walls a caller sets and reads, `budget` in steps and `space` in rows, which examples/reach exists to tell apart. MONITORING: `do we have any metrics?` -- `partial`, `steps`, `peak_rows` against `space` per run; `scanned`, `probed`, `micros` per question; and a stop writes `hole(...)` naming why INTO THE WORLD, so the record is a fact rather than a log line. CUSTOMER_ACCEPTANCE: `what even is that?` -- sign-off by whoever asked for the thing, which for a language at 1.0 is whether anybody outside the author has used it. Nobody has. That axis is not inapplicable, IT IS THE ONE THIS RELEASE FAILS, and I had hidden it in a list of things that did not apply. Only rollback and billing survived. THE MECHANISM OF THE ERROR IS WORTH MORE THAN THE CORRECTION: I read the axis NAMES against a template of `deployed web service` and answered about the template. Every one of the three has a library form, and two of them were already MEASURED IN THIS REPOSITORY -- the evidence existed and the question was never asked of it. A checklist read as a checklist scores the checklist; read as questions, three of five had answers lying in docs/ the whole time.").
